{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00698","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00698","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00698","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Diller","name":"Diller, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anne","familyName":"Esslinger","name":"Esslinger, Anne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Piotr","familyName":"Gorczyca","name":"Gorczyca, Piotr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evi","familyName":"Hartig","name":"Hartig, Evi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lia","familyName":"Kacholdt","name":"Kacholdt, Lia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hannes","familyName":"Strass","name":"Strass, Hannes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Supporting Cybersecurity Risk Management for Medical Devices via the SECUMAN Ontology and Shapes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:55:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:36:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose the SECUMAN ontology and shapes for representing and analysing cybersecurity risk-management documentation for medical devices. Cybersecurity risks are increasingly relevant for connected medical devices and may have direct consequences for patient safety. Current risk-management files are often maintained as semi-structured natural language text, which makes consistency checking, certification review, and reuse difficult. SECUMAN provides a formal OWL-based vocabulary for modelling security-risk context, assessment, control measures, and residual-risk evaluation, and uses SHACL constraints to check structural completeness and conformity with the intended documentation model. The ontology is aligned with VDE Spec 90025 and the related RISKMAN ontology and shapes, while extending their safety-oriented approach to concepts relevant to cybersecurity risk documentation such as threat scenarios, protection goals, attacker profiles, exposure levels, assets, and secure design arguments. SECUMAN is intended to support automated first-pass validation, traceability, and integration of cybersecurity and safety risk-management documentation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00698","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:45:20Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:45:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:45:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00696","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00696","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00696","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boris A.","familyName":"Malomed","name":"Malomed, Boris A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Solitons in optical couplers: introduction and perspectives"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Gases (cond-mat.quant-gas)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Pattern Formation and Solitons (nlin.PS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:52:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:36:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This minireview provides a brief summary and a discussion of directions for further development of theoretical and, chiefly, experimental studies of bright solitons in optical couplers, i.e., dual-core waveguides which combine the linear inter-core coupling (tunneling of light between the parallel cores with the intra-core group-velocity dispersion and self-focusing Kerr (cubic) nonlinearity. Following a short introduction to the field, the article focuses on a brief review of relatively recent experimental results for the switching of solitons in dual-core nonlinear optical fibers and the spontaneous emergence of stable asymmetric two-core solitons in the couplers with the symmetric dual-core structure."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"to be published in the inaugural issue of the journal Photonic and Quantum Waves"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00696","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:45:17Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:45:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:45:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00695","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00695","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00695","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianfeng","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Jianfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Peiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueliang","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xueliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sihang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Sihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanning","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Yanning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FreqAnchorAD: Language-Free Zero-Shot Anomaly Detection via Frequency-Deviation Anchoring"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:48:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:36:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Zero-shot anomaly detection (ZSAD) aims to detect anomalies and localize defective regions in unseen target domains without target training data. Recent ZSAD methods build on pretrained vision models, particularly CLIP, and construct normal and anomaly references from textual prompts or learnable visual representations. These methods perform anomaly discrimination primarily in spatial feature spaces, where subtle changes in texture, boundaries, and local structures can be confused with normal appearance variations. Although inconspicuous spatially, such defects can disrupt local texture regularity or boundary continuity, inducing response deviations across frequency bands. However, existing ZSAD methods do not explicitly model these frequency-dependent characteristics. Our image-domain analysis reveals that local defects exhibit spatial-frequency deviations from normal references across low-, middle-, and high-frequency bands, indicating that anomaly evidence is not universally dominated by high-frequency responses. Motivated by this observation, we propose FreqAnchorAD, a frequency-aware framework that organizes frequency-enhanced responses for anchor-relative anomaly discrimination. Specifically, the Local Frequency Compensation Module (LFCM) enhances intermediate patch tokens with local spatial-frequency cues. The Frequency-Deviation Anchor Projector (FDAP), our core discrimination module, organizes enhanced responses along a source-derived channel coordinate and measures anomaly evidence through relative similarity to normal and anomaly anchors. Finally, Asymmetric Anchor Supervision (AAS) stabilizes normal-anchor alignment while preserving diverse anomaly patterns. Experiments on thirteen industrial and medical benchmarks show that FreqAnchorAD achieves state-of-the-art mean performance in image-level anomaly recognition and pixel-level defect localization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages,4 figures,7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00695","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:45:16Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:45:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:45:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00694","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00694","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00694","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhenyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongqi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zongqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shijie","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Shijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"E2Pano: Learning Event-to-Panorama Image Reconstruction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:45:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:36:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Event cameras offer microsecond-level temporal resolution and high dynamic range, potentially facilitating motion-blur-free panoramic imaging from fast rotational scanning. Nonetheless, existing optimization-based methods remain computationally demanding, while prior learning-based reconstruction methods are largely designed for perspective imagery and lack geometry-aware support for panoramic outputs. We present E2Pano, a geometry-guided event-to-panorama pipeline with an end-to-end learnable photometric reconstruction stage. Our framework preserves real spherical coordinates from geometric mapping throughout the pipeline, employs a lightweight enhancement module with frequency-domain supervision to bridge the event-image domain gap, and leverages a spherical Transformer with 3D positional embeddings for photometric reconstruction. Experiments on synthetic data and captured rotational scans show improved reconstruction quality and lower photometric reconstruction cost than optimization-based baselines, together with encouraging transfer to real captures under our acquisition protocol despite training purely on synthetic data. Additionally, we construct PanoScan, a dataset with 4,370 synthetic and 30 real-world panoramic scenes paired with event streams. Our dataset and code will be released."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00694","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:45:14Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:45:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:45:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00693","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00693","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00693","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwang","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Jinwang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haowen","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Haowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiangheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongying","familyName":"Zan","name":"Zan, Hongying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AttnLink: Turning Attention into Schema Links for Text-to-SQL"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:44:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:36:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Schema linking is a critical component of Text-to-SQL systems, but existing approaches often trade off contextual modeling capacity, score-based controllability, and inference efficiency. We introduce AttnLink, an attention-based framework that converts LLMs' internal attention into continuous relevance scores for schema items. AttnLink extracts the attention from the generation-start position to candidate schema spans, enabling all candidates to be ranked in a single prefill pass without autoregressive decoding. We develop two variants: AttnLink-U, which directly probes pretrained attention without parameter updates, and AttnLink-S, which aligns the attention distribution with gold schema items through direct supervision. To improve coverage of multiple relevant schema items, AttnLink-S combines a set-mass objective with an adaptive probability-floor regularizer. The resulting scores support post-hoc precision-recall control through temperature scaling and cumulative-mass selection. Experiments on Spider, BIRD, and Spider2-SQLite show that AttnLink-S achieves mAP scores of 99.22%, 95.95%, and 83.29%, respectively, with millisecond-scale schema-linking latency. It also yields the best or tied-best execution accuracy for downstream SQL generation in seven of nine generator-dataset settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00693","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:45:13Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:45:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:45:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00692","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00692","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00692","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tan","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, Tan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ferdian","familyName":"Thung","name":"Thung, Ferdian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunpeng","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Yunpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Penghao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Penghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Lo","name":"Lo, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vul4Py: Benchmarking Automated Vulnerability Repair in Python with Paired Exploit and Functional Oracles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:40:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:36:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated Vulnerability Repair (AVR) has advanced rapidly across program analysis, machine learning, and Large Language Models (LLMs), but a verifiable, head-to-head comparison of AVR approaches on Python is still missing. Python underpins critical web, data, and machine-learning infrastructure, yet existing Python benchmarks accept a patch on the strength of a proof-of-concept exploit alone, or apply a functional test only on the subset of entries whose upstream project happens to ship one. Both therefore miss functional regressions, in which a patch defeats the exploit but breaks unrelated behavior. We present Vul4Py, a Python AVR benchmark in which every entry carries a paired oracle: an exploit oracle that must fail on the vulnerable revision and pass on the fixed one, together with a project-native pytest functional oracle that must pass on both. Vul4Py comprises 100 real vulnerabilities from 60 open-source projects, spanning 60 distinct CWEs and the years 2017 to 2025, each packaged with a pinned, reproducible per-instance environment. Using Vul4Py, we compare six approaches in three categories: a specialized vulnerability repair tool, directly prompted LLMs, and software engineering agents. The agents dominate: OpenHands repairs 41 of 100 vulnerabilities, against 4 for the strongest directly prompted LLM and 2 for the specialized tool, despite all three sharing the same backbone model. The paired oracle is what makes these counts trustworthy: it rejects 15 of the 119 patches that an exploit-only oracle would accept, and 98 of the 104 patches it admits are manually confirmed to be semantically equivalent to the developer's patches"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00692","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:45:12Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:45:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:45:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00691","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00691","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00691","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuyan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shuyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Odd-Cycle Span Defect: A Polynomial Lower Bound and a Square-Root Upper Bound"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"05C15 (Primary) 05C35, 05C38, 94B75 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:35:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:36:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For a graph $G$, let $ψ(G)=\\max\\{χ(G[V(C)]):C$ is an odd cycle of $G\\}$, with $ψ(G)=0$ when $G$ is bipartite. For positive integers $N$, set $F(N)=\\max\\{χ(G)-ψ(G):|V(G)|\\le N\\}$. The function $F$ measures the finite-order additive gap arising from an open problem of Erdos and Hajnal. We prove $N^{1/6-o(1)}\\le F(N)\u0026lt;\\sqrt{6N}$. The lower bound raises the finite-order scale supplied by the Cameron-Clow path-colour construction from $\\log N/\\log\\log N$ to a fixed power of $N$. Its proof constructs a palette-code graph from a binary covering code $\\mathcal{C}\\subseteq\\{0,1\\}^p$ and establishes the exact identities $χ(G)=2p+\\ell-ρ(\\mathcal{C})$ and $ψ(G)=2p$. Near-middle Hamming coverings yield the exponent $1/6$. The upper bound combines Polavarapu's connectivity theorem, the Chvatal-Erdos Hamiltonicity theorem, and maximum-independent-set stripping."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00691","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:45:10Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:45:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:45:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00690","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00690","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00690","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmad","familyName":"Sarlak","name":"Sarlak, Ahmad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Amin","name":"Amin, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abolfazl","familyName":"Razi","name":"Razi, Abolfazl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Assisted Coalition Formation for Cooperative Perception in Autonomous Driving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:31:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:36:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cooperative perception (CP) enables connected autonomous vehicles (CAVs) to share complementary observations for safer navigation, but practical deployment is limited by bandwidth constraints, unreliable links, and redundant information exchange. Existing CP methods often assume predefined participants and merely focus on collective perception. Likewise, recent LLM-based cooperative driving frameworks facilitate multi-vehicle reasoning but do not regulate participation criteria to select more beneficial vehicles. To bridge this gap, we propose an LLM-assisted coalition formation framework that selects the most informative helper vehicles before LLM reasoning. The approach jointly optimizes perceptual diversity using a determinantal point process (DPP) over multimodal vehicle embeddings and communication-aware reliability. This leads to a joint coalition selection and power allocation problem, which we solve efficiently via a relaxed convex reformulation and an ADMM-based optimization strategy that decouples diversity-aware selection from network-aware resource allocation. The selected coalition is then summarized and provided with an LLM reasoning module for efficient and less redundant multi-vehicle decision support. Experimental results show that our approach outperforms other baselines in overall coalition value, while maintaining high diversity and improved networking efficiency. The framework achieves a better balance between task performance and safety across OPV2V and V2V4Real datasets, demonstrating its effectiveness for cooperative autonomous driving with communication constraints."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for presentation at IEEE Global Communications Conference (GLOBECOM 2026), Cognitive Radio and AI-Enabled Networks Symposium"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00690","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:45:09Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:45:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:45:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00689","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00689","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00689","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siran","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Siran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jianing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuantu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yuantu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Existence of Weak Solutions to a Power-Law Model for Compressible Non-Newtonian Fluids on 1D Unbounded Domain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary: 35Q30, 76A05, 35D30, 35K55, Secondary: 35B40, 35B45, 76N10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:29:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:36:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper is concerned with the analysis of a one-dimensional power-law model for compressible fluid dynamics on $\\mathbb{R}$, in which the shear stress takes the form $μ|\\partial_{x}u|^{p-2}\\partial_{x}u$, where $μ$ is the viscosity coefficient and $u$ is the velocity. We prove that, in the singular limit $p\\rightarrow\\infty$, the solutions converge to functions $(ρ,u)$ satisfying $|\\partial_{x}u|\\leq 1$, $τ= π\\partial_{x}u$, $π\\geq 0$, and $π(1 - |\\partial_{x}u|) = 0$ a.e. on $\\mathbb{R}$. Moreover, we rigorously justify the existence of weak solutions to the limiting equation. The convergence as $p \\to \\infty$ is obtained via domain truncation and compactness arguments, of which the key challenge is to show that the density remains bounded away from zero and infinity on any compact subset. This extends the recent result of Bresch, Burtea, and Szlenk [Nonlinearity 26 (2026), no. 5, Paper No. 055010.] from one-dimensional periodic domain to the whole real line."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00689","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:45:07Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:45:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:45:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00688","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00688","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00688","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dezi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dezi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spectral Microlensing of Extragalactic H II Regions by Stellar-Mass Black Holes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:28:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:36:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Most of the Milky Way's predicted stellar-mass black holes remain hidden, especially at high Galactic latitudes or in the Galactic halo, where traditional dense-field stellar microlensing is ineffective. We propose an alternative method to map this isolated population via the spectral microlensing of compact, extragalactic H II regions. Projected onto the source plane, the physical Einstein radius of a Galactic black hole can match the typical core sizes of H II regions in distant galaxies. Microlensing triggers an achromatic magnification, producing distinct narrow emission-line excesses in integrated galaxy spectra. Because gravitational lensing is wavelength-independent, intrinsic line ratios are preserved, offering a robust discriminant against false-positive astrophysical transients. Notably, the efficiency of this method depends critically on the size of the H II regions: while extended regions suffer from low optical depth, compact regions with a physical size $\\lesssim 10$ pc offer significantly higher magnifications. These compact cores, however, are heavily dust-obscured at optical wavelengths, making infrared and radio observations the primary windows for this method. Even so, the spatial sparseness of background H II regions and the stringent alignment requirement for high magnification limit the expected event rate to $\\sim 10^{-6}$ per year. Nevertheless, this method offers a unique opportunity to detect stellar-mass black holes and constrain their abundance in such low-density environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 3 figures, and 1 table; ApJ Letters accepted"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00688","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:45:06Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:45:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:45:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00687","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00687","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00687","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihan","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Yihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodong","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xiaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baoquan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Baoquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hailong","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Hailong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Proteus: A Truncation-Robust Entropy Model for Progressive LiDAR Compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:24:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:36:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LiDAR point clouds provide explicit, deterministic physical boundaries critical for collaborative safety-critical perception. However, wireless channels inherently impair and corrupt transmitted signals. Existing robust frameworks (such as deep JSCC or MDC) attempt to counter these channel impairments through statistical or parametric estimation, turning exact physical measurements into unverified algorithmic estimates. To address this, we propose Proteus, a learned LiDAR codec operating on 2D range images. By decoupling the frame representation into independent coders for the \\textbf{sig}nificant range bit-planes (SIG) and the \\textbf{ins}ignificant range bit-planes and attributes (INS), Proteus achieves overall stream-level truncation robustness. The non-truncatable SIG block encodes the most significant range bit-planes to establish a necessary, self-contained perceptual lower bound, below which the reconstructed point cloud is severely degraded. Meanwhile, INS employs bit-plane slicing representation and coding, ensuring that range truncation mathematically maps to a deterministic spatial precision degradation. Subordinate attributes are reconstructed via a hybrid lossless-predictive method, leveraging the decoded geometry as a strong structural prior for fine-grained approximation. Furthermore, strategic ordering within INS prioritizes geometry over attributes under bandwidth drops. Experimental results on the Waymo Open Dataset and SemanticKITTI demonstrate that Proteus tolerates up to approximately 70\\% bitstream truncation, while outperforming established standards (G-PCC, Draco, and JPEG XL) and the representative learned compressor Unicorn under ideal channel conditions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00687","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:45:05Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:45:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:45:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00686","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00686","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00686","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erhan","familyName":"Bayraktar","name":"Bayraktar, Erhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibrahim","familyName":"Ekren","name":"Ekren, Ibrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xihao","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantitative Particle Approximation for Controlled Nonlinear Filtering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:16:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:36:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We estimate convergence rates of value functions for particle approximations of a controlled nonlinear filtering problem. The state is a McKean--Vlasov diffusion on the flat torus, driven by hidden idiosyncratic noise and observed common noise. The filter---the conditional law of the state given the observations---serves as the state variable of the control problem, and the associated value function solves a second-order Hamilton--Jacobi--Bellman equation on the Wasserstein space. We approximate this problem by a centralized \\(N\\)-particle control problem with independent idiosyncratic noises and a common observation noise. The framework accommodates nonseparable rewards and controlled drifts. Since a single control is applied to the entire population, the Hamiltonian is defined by an optimization performed after integration over the population. Under smoothness of the data, uniform ellipticity, and regularity of this Hamiltonian, we establish uniform value-function error bounds of order \\(N^{-1/6}\\) for \\(d=1\\), \\(N^{-1/6}(\\log N)^{1/3}\\) for \\(d=2\\), and \\(N^{-1/(3d)}\\) for \\(d\u0026gt;2\\). The proof combines a translation lift in the common-noise direction, Fourier--Wasserstein inf- and sup-convolutions, viscosity comparison, and particle derivative estimates uniform in \\(N\\)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00686","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:45:03Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:45:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:45:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00685","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00685","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00685","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Leins","name":"Leins, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nico","familyName":"Pelleriti","name":"Pelleriti, Nico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jana","familyName":"Gonnermann-Müller","name":"Gonnermann-Müller, Jana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Pokutta","name":"Pokutta, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Does LLM Orchestration Pay Off? A Controlled Evaluation of Accuracy, Cost, and Task Difficulty"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:14:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:36:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM orchestration is often assumed to improve reasoning by allocating additional inference-time computation, yet its gains may not justify its cost. Existing comparisons also frequently overlook differences in optimization effort, making it difficult to isolate the value of orchestration itself. We conduct a controlled evaluation of Self-Refine, Best-of-$N$, and Debate against task-only and chain-of-thought (CoT) single-call baselines across five LLM backbones and three domains: competitive programming, chess puzzles, and mathematics. For comparability, we optimize each method with GEPA under the same optimization budget and evaluate all methods on the same difficulty-stratified benchmark items. Orchestration yields moderate but benchmark-dependent gains: averaged across backbones within each benchmark, the largest improvement is 4.6 percentage points over optimized CoT inference and 4.5 points over task-only inference, while requiring approximately 2 to 4 times the mean total tokens of task-only inference. Human-derived difficulty is associated with lower absolute accuracy in all three benchmarks, but within-benchmark analyses do not indicate that orchestration effects increase with task difficulty. By contrast, exploratory mixed-effects analyses reveal strong interactions between orchestration method and backbone model across all three benchmarks, showing that orchestration effectiveness depends substantially on the underlying model. Our results suggest that orchestration decisions should be model-specific and account for whether moderate accuracy gains justify the additional inference cost. More broadly, evaluations of LLM orchestrations should control optimization effort and report model-specific accuracy--cost trade-offs rather than treating additional inference-time structure as uniformly beneficial."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00685","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:45:02Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:45:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:45:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00684","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00684","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00684","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masoud","familyName":"Sabzevari","name":"Sabzevari, Masoud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Holomorphic normal forms of six-dimensional totally nondegenerate CR manifolds in C^5"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Complex Variables (math.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"32V40, 58K50, 53A55","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:12:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:36:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The class of six-dimensional totally nondegenerate CR submanifolds in $\\mathbb C^5$ contains an infinite number of models parameterized by nonzero pairs $(a, b)\\in\\mathbb C\\times\\mathbb R$ appearing in their defining equations. Employing the equivariant moving frame method, we construct normal forms of this class. The applied normalizations yield into fourteen biholomorphically inequivalent subclasses, each with its own normal form. For each subclass, we determine the Lie algebra of infinitesimal CR automorphisms. Finally, using the method of involution, we prove the convergence of the constructed normal forms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"38 pp"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00684","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:45:00Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:45:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:45:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00683","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00683","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00683","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Allison","familyName":"Youngblood","name":"Youngblood, Allison","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"France","name":"France, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Fleming","name":"Fleming, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim H.","familyName":"Hellickson","name":"Hellickson, Tim H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alan C.","familyName":"Hoskins","name":"Hoskins, Alan C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James Paul","familyName":"Mason","name":"Mason, James Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dolon","familyName":"Bhattacharyya","name":"Bhattacharyya, Dolon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hannah","familyName":"Diamond-Lowe","name":"Diamond-Lowe, Hannah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Girish M.","familyName":"Duvvuri","name":"Duvvuri, Girish M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cynthia S.","familyName":"Froning","name":"Froning, Cynthia S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vinay L.","familyName":"Kashyap","name":"Kashyap, Vinay L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tommi T.","familyName":"Koskinen","name":"Koskinen, Tommi T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuta","familyName":"Notsu","name":"Notsu, Yuta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seth","familyName":"Redfield","name":"Redfield, Seth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David J.","familyName":"Wilson","name":"Wilson, David J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ute V.","familyName":"Amerstorfer","name":"Amerstorfer, Ute V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tracy M.","familyName":"Becker","name":"Becker, Tracy M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Borsa","name":"Borsa, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David A.","familyName":"Brain","name":"Brain, David A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Fossati","name":"Fossati, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Briana","familyName":"Indahl","name":"Indahl, Briana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Graham S.","familyName":"Kerr","name":"Kerr, Graham S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam F.","familyName":"Kowalski","name":"Kowalski, Adam F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rachel A.","familyName":"Osten","name":"Osten, Rachel A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tom L.","familyName":"Patton","name":"Patton, Tom L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steven V.","familyName":"Penton","name":"Penton, Steven V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ignazio Francesco","familyName":"Pillitteri","name":"Pillitteri, Ignazio Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kurt D.","familyName":"Retherford","name":"Retherford, Kurt D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Astrid M.","familyName":"Veronig","name":"Veronig, Astrid M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aline A.","familyName":"Vidotto","name":"Vidotto, Aline A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jennifer G.","familyName":"Winters","name":"Winters, Jennifer G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adina D.","familyName":"Feinstein","name":"Feinstein, Adina D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guillaume P.","familyName":"Gronoff","name":"Gronoff, Guillaume P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey L.","familyName":"Linsky","name":"Linsky, Jeffrey L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R. O. Parke","familyName":"Loyd","name":"Loyd, R. O. Parke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susan E.","familyName":"Mullally","name":"Mullally, Susan E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jorge","familyName":"Sanz-Forcada","name":"Sanz-Forcada, Jorge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter J.","familyName":"Wheatley","name":"Wheatley, Peter J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amber V.","familyName":"Young","name":"Young, Amber V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ESCAPE: a small explorer mission to study the stellar drivers of exoplanet evolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:08:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The long-term stability of exoplanetary atmospheres depends critically on the extreme-ultraviolet (EUV) photon and high-energy particle fluxes from the host star, which are poorly constrained. To address this key gap in our understanding of atmospheric retention, we present the Extreme-ultraviolet Stellar Characterization for Atmospheric Physics and Evolution (ESCAPE) mission, a NASA Small Explorer concept proposed in 2026. ESCAPE employs extreme- and far-ultraviolet spectroscopy (80 - 1650 Ang) to provide the first comprehensive study of the stellar EUV history and stellar coronal mass ejection (CME) environments that control atmospheric mass-loss and determine the habitability of rocky exoplanets. This paper outlines both the primary science goals of the mission, the breadth of future general observer investigations, and a detailed design study of the mission's instrumentation. The ESCAPE instrument comprises a grazing incidence telescope that feeds multiple diffraction gratings and a photon-counting detector. We describe a demonstration of the Hettrick-Bowyer telescope, etched silicon diffraction gratings, the microchannel plate detector and housing, and gold and zirconium coatings. We present a STOP analysis that verifies ESCAPE's ability to meet its structural integrity, thermal stability, and optical performance requirements throughout the mission environment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00683","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":2,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:59Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:45:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:45:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00682","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00682","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00682","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Organizational","name":"Si-Bao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BRIC-Net: Boundary-Reliable Illumination-Color Interaction for Remote Sensing Image Deshadowing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:07:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Shadows in remote sensing images obscure surface appearance and disrupt radiometric continuity, reducing the reliability of visual interpretation and downstream analysis. Remote sensing image deshadowing is an ill-posed inverse problem that requires spatially varying illumination recovery while preserving chromatic and radiometric consistency in non-shadow regions. Existing methods commonly rely on hard shadow masks for compensation or directly regress RGB intensities. Hard masks may inadequately model gradual penumbra variations and are sensitive to localization errors, often producing residual shadows or halo artifacts; direct RGB regression entangles illumination recovery with chromatic reconstruction and can introduce color casts. To this end, we propose the Boundary-Reliable Illumination-Color Interaction Network (BRIC-Net), which decouples these failures at different representation levels. A Lightness Reliability Prior (LRP) derives reliability-aware guidance from CIELAB statistics. Boundary-Adaptive Gated Mixing (BAGM) performs gated interpolation between shallow RGB and lightness features around uncertain transitions, while Spatial-Channel Mutual Modulation (SCMM) coordinates deeper spatial and channel responses for appearance-preserving illumination recovery. BRIC-Net achieves 29.46~dB full-image peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR) on AeroDS-Syn and 27.96~dB on SRGTA. It also obtains the lowest Perception-based Image Quality Evaluator (PIQE) scores on AISD and AeroDS-Real. Region-wise evaluations and component ablations further support its effectiveness in shadow recovery and non-shadow preservation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00682","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:58Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00680","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00680","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00680","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdalla","familyName":"Doleh","name":"Doleh, Abdalla","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ratna Babu","familyName":"Chinnam","name":"Chinnam, Ratna Babu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Dimensional Assessment for AI Cognition (MAAC): A Theoretical Framework for Process-Oriented Cognitive Evaluation of Text-Based AI Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:01:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluating artificial intelligence systems has historically relied on outcome-based benchmarks that measure task accuracy, robustness, or fairness. While indispensable, these benchmarks provide limited diagnostic insight into the underlying cognitive processes that generate performance-leaving critical questions unanswered about how AI systems reason, integrate memory, manage complexity, or avoid generating false information. This paper introduces the Multi-Dimensional Assessment for AI Cognition (MAAC), a theoretically grounded framework for shifting evaluation from what text-based AI systems produce to how they think. MAAC defines nine cognitively motivated dimensions: Cognitive Load, Tool Execution, Content Quality, Memory Integration, Complexity Handling, Hallucination Control, Knowledge Transfer, Processing Efficiency, and Process-Outcome Alignment. Each dimension is grounded in established cognitive science theory-drawing on Marr's tri-level hypothesis, Baddeley's working memory model, Sweller's cognitive load theory, and unified theories of cognition. Five theoretical analyses provide initial support for the framework's coherence and empirical testability: dimension-to-theory mapping; a coverage matrix assessing breadth and non-redundancy; a formal gap analysis relative to current evaluation practice; a worked diagnostic illustration; and a set of a priori interdependency predictions for future empirical testing. MAAC provides a theoretical and operational framework for principled process-level cognitive assessment of text-based AI systems, complementing existing outcome-based benchmarks with cognitively grounded, multi-dimensional evaluation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00680","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:55Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00679","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00679","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00679","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiangwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanduni","familyName":"Nimalsiri","name":"Nimalsiri, Nanduni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saman","familyName":"Halgamuge","name":"Halgamuge, Saman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HetGPS: Scalable Graph Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Physics-Anchored Adaptive Safety for EV Charging"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:59:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Safety interventions for large populations of network-coupled agents must protect shared constraints without unnecessarily overriding task-oriented policy decisions. We present HetGPS, a hybrid graph-control framework synergizing learned graph risk with physics-anchored correction by separating intervention magnitude from corrective direction. An action-conditioned graph residual model schedules state-dependent intervention authority, while a physics model determines its direction. For electric vehicle (EV) charging, we couple this filter with a parameter-shared heterogeneous graph soft actor-critic policy, enabling topology-aware coordination with a learned model size independent of fleet size. Across five nested distribution networks with 200--3,218 EVs and 100 evaluation days, Adaptive Authority reduces bus--step voltage violations from 3.93--7.74\\% without filtering to 0.52--3.44\\%, while maintaining 99.06--100\\% departure success. Relative to the same physics-directed projection with fixed authority, it improves mean reward on all five networks and lowers the mean safety score on four. The deployed policy-and-risk model contains 383,702 learned parameters at every scale; at 3,218 EVs, a matched centralized SAC actor is about $170\\times$ larger. A policy trained on the eight-transformer system transfers zero-shot to the 16- and 32-transformer systems, attaining 0.57--0.75\\% violation rates and at least 99.99\\% departure success. These results show that learned graph risk can allocate intervention authority at scale while feeder physics anchors corrective action."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00679","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:54Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00678","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00678","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00678","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaihua","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Kaihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqing","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Ziqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xiaoxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael Bi","familyName":"Mi","name":"Mi, Michael Bi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dave Zhenyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Dave Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Breaking the Horizontal Prior: From Long-Tailed Orientation Bias to Roll-Robust Monocular Depth Estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:56:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite recent advances in Monocular Depth Estimation, state-of-the-art depth foundation models remain vulnerable to robustness issues. Particularly, even slight camera rolls can result in substantial degradation in depth estimations. We attribute this problem to a previously overlooked phenomenon, termed the Horizontal Prior, which is a manifestation of long-tailed distribution bias: most training images are captured in approximately horizontal orientations due to human visual preferences and photographic habits. While intuitive remedies such as re-balanced data augmentation and horizon leveling provide partial improvements, they fail to fully address the issue. In this paper, we introduce Invariant Depth Constraint (ID-Constraint), a training-time supervision strategy that improves roll robustness by fine-tuning and jointly regularizing the depth backbone with a series of geometric and spatial reasoning tasks. These auxiliary objectives encourage the backbone to learn rotation-stable, depth-relevant representations, while the auxiliary prediction heads are discarded after training, leaving the original inference architecture unchanged. Extensive experiments on five benchmark datasets across four roll settings demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed method."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The code is publicly available on GitHub: https://github.com/KaihuaTang/Horizontal-Prior"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00678","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:52Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00677","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00677","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00677","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yunhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yixu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Teng","name":"Teng, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingjun","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xingjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xia","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Gang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yu-Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OpenART: Scaling Agent Red Teaming via Open-Ended Environment Evolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:51:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI agents operate in persistent environments where early state changes can influence decisions far into the future. Unlike conventional language-model interactions, agent behavior is mediated through a shared state that is repeatedly modified and reused across long-horizon workflows. Current safety benchmarks often fail to capture these cumulative risks because they focus on short, static tasks. To address these limitations, we introduce OpenART, an open-ended arena for scalable agent red teaming through environment evolution. OpenART provides over 10,000 validated stateful scenarios across 50 domains, drawing from a pool of more than 500,000 tools and skills. These tasks require a median of 97 tool calls and enable unified evaluation across 75 different agent-model configurations. To systematically explore these evolving attack surfaces, we propose the Evolutionary Markov Hypergraph Attack (EMHA). EMHA is a black-box policy that performs feedback-driven environment evolution by coordinating authorized state transitions without requiring parameter updates. Throughout the evaluation, task objectives remain fixed while only the environment state changes. Across all configurations, EMHA achieves a pooled Attack Success Rate (ASR) of 85.0%. Its advantage over instruction-only evolution increases from approximately 2% on simple environments to over 17% on the most complex ones, demonstrating that environment evolution increasingly exposes safety failures as task complexity grows. Furthermore, our analysis shows that the specific runtime implementation of an agent explains a significant portion of safety variation beyond the underlying model's capabilities. These results establish OpenART as a scalable foundation for studying agent safety in complex, evolving environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00677","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:51Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00676","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00676","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00676","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabio","familyName":"Renda","name":"Renda, Fabio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Separable and Frobenius Cowreaths of type $(A \\otimes H^{\\mathrm{op}}, H,ψ)$"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Algebra (math.QA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"16T05, 15A66, 18M05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:50:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cowreaths of type $(A \\otimes H^{\\mathrm{op}},H,ψ)$ have been investigated in [10,19-21,27] as examples of (h-)separable and Frobenius coalgebras in monoidal categories. They are entwining structures built from a Hopf algebra $H$ and an $H$-comodule algebra $(A,ρ_A)$. In this article we develop a general theory that allows us to recover results from the aforementioned papers in an easier way and also to extend them to cowreaths in higher dimension. Focusing on separability, the crucial observation is that, when $H$ has bijective antipode, one should work with integrals on the coalgebra $(H,ψ)$ in place of Casimir morphisms, for they are easier to classify. On the other hand, in dealing with Frobenius properties, we obtain that a cowreath $(A \\otimes H^{\\mathrm{op}},H,ψ)$ is Frobenius if and only if the morphism $(\\mathrm{Id}_A \\otimes μ)ρ_A$ is inner ($μ$ is the distinguished grouplike element in $H^*$). While Frobenius cowreaths are always (h-)separable, examples of (h-)separable cowreaths that are not Frobenius will be presented at the end of this article."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"44 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00676","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:49Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00674","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00674","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00674","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiqi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ruiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zuxuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CopyCat: Improving Fine-Grained Subject Consistency in Subject-to-Image Models within Seconds"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:49:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent subject-to-image models have achieved impressive progress in personalized image generation, yet they still struggle to preserve fine-grained subject-specific details. A major reason is the lack of high-quality fine-grained identity supervision: real paired data are expensive to collect, while synthesized training pairs often preserve only coarse subject appearance and fail to capture subtle subject-specific details. In this work, we propose CopyCat, a lightweight model-refinement framework that improves fine-grained subject consistency within only a few seconds. CopyCat performs a one-time refinement of a pretrained subject-to-image model by attaching a lightweight Fine-grained Consistency LoRA (FCLoRA) and optimizing it using a single proxy image, which is used as both the conditioning image and the reconstruction target. This exact self-reconstruction objective substantially simplifies the optimization task, enabling effective fine-grained refinement within only a few seconds. The refinement is performed only once; the resulting model can be directly applied to diverse unseen reference subjects and prompts without further subject-specific optimization. We further revisit subject-to-image LoRA training in double-stream diffusion transformers and find that adapting only the visual stream consistently improves subject consistency. Extensive experiments on DreamBench and XVerseBench demonstrate consistent improvements in fine-grained subject consistency across representative subject-to-image models under both single- and multi-subject settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00674","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:47Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00673","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00673","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00673","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Rodriguez","name":"Rodriguez, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergio","familyName":"Torregrosa","name":"Torregrosa, Sergio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alicia","familyName":"Cordero","name":"Cordero, Alicia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan R.","familyName":"Torregrosa","name":"Torregrosa, Juan R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mustapha","familyName":"Ziane","name":"Ziane, Mustapha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francisco","familyName":"Chinesta","name":"Chinesta, Francisco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A plausible Parametrization of Modal Basis for Dynamical Systems Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:48:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the field of solid dynamics, knowing the corresponding modal basis of the system is capital, in order to improve design with respect to a desired dynamical behavior, such as avoiding natural frequencies at specific values or designing mechanical systems that can account for desired frequency spectrum. However, the determination of the modal basis involve the resolution of an eigenvalue problem, which can be expensive to perform for large systems, especially when dealing with a optimization of a parametric system design. In the present work, we propose to determine the parametrization of modal basis by considering an advanced Deep Learning technique based on the Rank Reduction AutoEncoder (RRAE). The RRAE is based on an autoencoder whose latent space is constrained through a truncated Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) approximation. This formulation enables the latent space to capture the dominant features of the data efficiently. As a result, the autoencoder is guided toward learning the underlying physical behavior represented across the dataset, mitigating overfitting and spurious predictions. The main idea consists of identifying a reduced parameter space using the RRAE for the first eigenvector, while the remaining modes are subsequently reconstructed through neural networks that take the same reduced parameter space as input, thereby coupling all modes in a nonlinear parametric framework. The proposed architecture is validated through the parametrization of the modal basis in 1D and 2D problems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00673","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:45Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00672","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00672","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00672","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Thurner","name":"Thurner, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nadine","familyName":"Jost","name":"Jost, Nadine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan Albert","familyName":"Horstmann","name":"Horstmann, Stefan Albert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian","familyName":"Ising","name":"Ising, Fabian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lea","familyName":"Groeber","name":"Groeber, Lea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alena","familyName":"Naiakshina","name":"Naiakshina, Alena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Schinzel","name":"Schinzel, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Chasing Ghosts to Missed Attacks: Perspectives and Perceptions of SOC Practitioners on LLM Integration, Risks, and Readiness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:47:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Security Operations Centers (SOCs) process large volumes of security events, requiring analysts to accurately detect and assess ongoing cyberattacks under time pressure. Recent advances in Large Language Models (LLMs) suggest potential benefits for security operations, yet their practical suitability for real-world SOC workflows remains poorly understood. To address this gap, we conducted 25 semi-structured interviews with SOC practitioners who had prior experience with LLMs, complemented by interactive scenarios to anticipate challenges and identify opportunities for the responsible integration of LLM-based tools into SOC workflows. We identified 15 LLM use cases grouped into six functional categories. While LLMs are valued for automating repetitive, low-level tasks such as report automation, practitioners rate high-impact tasks such as incident analysis as not yet feasible, reporting limitations in technical depth, context awareness, and organization-specific knowledge. They locate these limitations less in the models than in the readiness of their SOCs and human factors driving over-reliance. Despite concerns, practitioners express a strong willingness to adopt LLMs, describing competitive pressure that leaves few alternatives. This work contributes an empirical, practitioner-driven analysis of LLM use across SOC roles and organizations and derives concrete design and integration requirements for human-centered, operationally safe LLM-assisted security operations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00672","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:44Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00671","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00671","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00671","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas D","familyName":"Sauer","name":"Sauer, Lukas D","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Ritz","name":"Ritz, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meinhard","familyName":"Kieser","name":"Kieser, Meinhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How to optimize dynamic borrowing in basket trials - A utility-based framework and results of a comparison study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:46:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Basket trials are clinical trials in which one treatment is investigated in multiple subpopulations within a single trial. The subpopulations are called strata in the following. Stratified cohorts are typical but not limited to early oncological trials, where targeted therapies can be investigated in different strata defined by tumor tissue. In terms of statistical methodology, the stratification of the trial can be leveraged using information borrowing. The idea is that the strata will be analyzed separately if they respond differently to treatment, but that a stratum may share information from another if their response rates are similar. This can increase power while keeping type-I error inflation moderate. The borrowing methods can be tuned with respect to possible outcome scenarios. In this paper, we provide a framework for tuning basket trials: The compromise between power and type-I error rate is defined by a utility function which is then optimized using optimization algorithms. We investigated this framework by performing a pre-specified comparison study. Part I of the study compared optimization algorithms in terms of reliability and efficiency, part II compared the statistical performance of utility functions in simulated scenarios. This study shows how targeting different utility functions based on measures such as local power in each stratum or the number of correct decisions leads to different borrowing behavior of the optimized design. Furthermore, we discuss a mathematical counterexample which shows that no uniformly most powerful (UMP) test exists for basket trials, thus showing the limitations of basket trials. While information borrowing may result in power gains for some scenarios, there is no design which is optimal for all possible scenarios. Hence, a transparent optimization procedure is crucial when planning basket trials."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"47 pages, 18 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00671","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:43Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00670","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00670","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00670","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaetan","familyName":"Bardy","name":"Bardy, Gaetan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthieu","familyName":"Saubanere","name":"Saubanere, Matthieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adrian","familyName":"Tanasa","name":"Tanasa, Adrian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Coordinate space representation for quantum simulation of scalar field theory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:44:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantum computing provides a promising framework for the simulation of quantum field theories, where the computational cost depends both on the quantum algorithm employed and on the representation of the Hamiltonian. We investigate a formulation of the $ϕ^4$ model based on the harmonic-oscillator basis in coordinate space. We derive the lattice $ϕ^4$ Hamiltonian in this representation and analyze the structure of the resulting one-body matrix and interaction tensor. We show that both exhibit an effective band-diagonal structure, allowing controlled truncations of the Hamiltonian while preserving the low-energy spectrum. We validate this formulation by comparing low-energy observables obtained from numerical diagonalization with those computed in the standard harmonic-oscillator momentum-space representation. Finally, we estimate the resources required to encode the Hamiltonian on a quantum computer using both binary and unary boson-to-qubit mappings. By exploiting effective locality, the coordinate-space representation reduces the resources required for quantum simulation over a broad range of parameters."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00670","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:41Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00669","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00669","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00669","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongliang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Hongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinlan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jinlan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hua","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dianbo","familyName":"Sui","name":"Sui, Dianbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dianhui","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Dianhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiying","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Zhiying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GARDRec: Decision-Level Graph Grounding for Large Language Model Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:44:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) offer new opportunities for recommendation by interpreting item descriptions, user instructions, and external knowledge through natural-language prompts. However, existing graph-augmented LLM recommenders often use knowledge graphs mainly as prompt-level evidence, leaving ranking decisions weakly constrained by structured user-item relations. This is problematic for next-item recommendation, where the model must compare candidates under the same user context while preserving temporal preference, collaborative signals, and attribute matches. To address this issue, we propose \\emph{GARDRec}, a Graph-grounded Adaptive Reasoning and Decision-aware Recommendation framework for LLM-based next-item ranking. GARDRec constructs semantic-structural item representations from textual node features and graph propagation, derives personalized graph contexts from temporally weighted histories and first-order neighborhoods, and aligns graph-derived representations with a frozen LLM through continuous multimodal prompts. Explicit interaction and matching features are injected through late-stage decision branches, while inter-candidate attention and restricted generative likelihood support final ranking. Experiments on three public benchmarks with multiple LLM backbones show that GARDRec generally improves candidate-ranking performance over representative baselines. Ablation and diagnostic analyses verify the contributions of graph projection, neighborhood retrieval, explicit decision features, ranking loss, and generative calibration."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00669","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:40Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00668","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00668","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00668","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Itamar","familyName":"Nir","name":"Nir, Itamar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Faster Deterministic Integer Root Finding for Integer Polynomials"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Symbolic Computation (cs.SC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:43:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We give a deterministic algorithm for finding all integer roots of a square-free polynomial $f\\in\\mathbb Z[x]$ of degree $n$ with $\\lVert f\\rVert_\\infty\u0026lt;2^b$. The running time is $$ \\tilde{O}(n^{3/2}b), $$ improving the $\\tilde{O}(n^2b)$ bound of Harvey and Hittmeir (Research in Number Theory, 2022).\n The algorithm follows the classical $p$-adic framework: find roots modulo a prime $p$, lift them modulo a high power of $p$, and verify the lifted candidates. The main new idea is to avoid searching for a prime for which $f\\bmod p$ is square-free. Instead, we find a prime for which the total multiplicity of repeated roots modulo $p$ is small. This requires lifting repeated roots, which we handle using a weighted lifting tree. We also give a faster deterministic candidate-verification algorithm: given $n$ candidate integers smaller in absolute value than $2^b$, we decide which are roots of $f$ in $$ \\tilde{O}(nb+\\min(n^2,nb^2)) $$ bit operations. Together, these ingredients give the first deterministic subquadratic-in-$n$ improvement for integer root finding in the square-free case."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00668","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:39Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00667","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00667","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00667","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minhui","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Minhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joshua D.","familyName":"Reiss","name":"Reiss, Joshua D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Band-Count Dense Modal Estimation with Fixed-Frequency Differentiable Resonator Refinement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:42:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Task B of the 1st DAFx Parameter Estimation Challenge requires estimating the frequencies, decay rates, gains, and number of modes in a dense plate-reverb impulse response. Weak and overlapping modes make sparse peak detection prone to severe undercounting. We train an ExtraTrees regressor on simulator-generated data to predict mode counts in four frequency bands. These counts define dense frequency grids, after which a differentiable all-pole resonator model refines decay and gain while keeping frequency fixed. On two separate synthetic validation sets, the system reduces a local challenge-style error by about 66% relative to the official default peak-picking baseline. The improvement is mainly associated with lower mode-count mismatch, while decay and gain remain the largest error sources. These findings support separating modal-density estimation from continuous parameter fitting."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as a challenge paper at the 29th International Conference on Digital Audio Effects (DAFx 2026), Cambridge, MA, USA, September, 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00667","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:37Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00666","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00666","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00666","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Gebele","name":"Gebele, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timm","familyName":"Mutzel","name":"Mutzel, Timm","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"Matthes","name":"Matthes, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Executable Arbitrage and Market Efficiency in Prediction Markets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:42:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deterministic payoff identities imply no-arbitrage bounds in winner-takes-all prediction markets, but violations of these bounds need not be exploitable before settlement. We distinguish payoff-space no-arbitrage, which follows from terminal payoffs, from protocol-executable no-arbitrage, which depends on the position transformations available to traders. Polymarket's negative-risk markets make this distinction observable: linked binary markets represent mutually exclusive outcomes, while the NegRisk Adapter operationalizes only the NO-to-YES direction before settlement. We reconstruct depth-aware executable portfolio values and combine them with actor-level transaction histories and on-chain conversion traces to measure payoff-bound violations and exploitation. Our reconstruction estimates \\$1.12 million in arbitrage profit across two realization channels: \\$1.086 million from converter-enabled strategies and \\$32 thousand from settlement-based basket formation. In the CLOB sample, positive violations concentrate on the unsupported YES side, whereas adapter-supported NO-side violations are substantially less frequent and shorter-lived. These patterns are consistent with the view that pre-settlement conversion strengthens enforcement by reducing capital lock-up and enabling inventory recycling. Finally, we implement a prototype bidirectional extension of the NegRisk Adapter that makes the reverse path executable before settlement. Together, our findings show that market efficiency depends not only on payoff structure, but also on whether protocols expose payoff equivalences as executable primitives."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00666","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:36Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00665","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00665","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00665","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin-Li","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Xin-Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun-Xia","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jun-Xia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Defu","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Defu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hai-Cang","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Hai-Cang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spectral Functions of $J/ψ$ Meson in Rotating Thermal Background from Holography"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:39:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate the spectral functions of the $J/ψ$ meson in a rotating thermal background within the soft-wall holographic model. The global rotation is implemented through a rotating AdS-like metric, while a local inertial frame is introduced in which the vector field can be decomposed into different spin states. We solve the equations of motion of the vector field in the bulk with incoming wave condition near the horizon and compute the retarded Green function, from which we extract the invariant-mass spectral functions for $J/ψ$. When the momentum is parallel to the rotation axis, the peak energies shift by $-ΩJ_z$, as expected by a coupling between rotation and angular momentum, while the widths are nearly independent to $Ω$. When the momentum is in perpendicular direction, the spectral functions for spin-$\\pm1$ states deviate significantly from the single-peak behavior and the energy shifts depart from $-ΩJ_z$. The resulting triplet splittings of the spectral functions provides a holographic perspective on spin-dependent vector meson properties in rotating systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00665","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:34Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00664","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00664","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00664","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["UCM"],"givenName":"Aaron A.","familyName":"Maldonado","name":"Maldonado, Aaron A.","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["IPARCOS,UCM"],"givenName":"Alberto","familyName":"Domínguez","name":"Domínguez, Alberto","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adithiya","familyName":"Dinesh","name":"Dinesh, Adithiya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alba","familyName":"Rico","name":"Rico, Alba","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pablo","familyName":"Peñil","name":"Peñil, Pablo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sara","familyName":"Buson","name":"Buson, Sara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Ajello","name":"Ajello, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evidence for a bursty $γ$-ray QPO in the neutrino-associated FSRQ PKS 1424$-$418 using Fast Template Periodograms and Machine Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:35:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Standard frequency-domain searches for quasi-periodic oscillations (QPOs) in active galactic nuclei generally assume sinusoidal variability. However, $γ$-ray blazar emission often shows asymmetric flares and localized bursts, causing spectral leakage that can reduce the sensitivity of conventional methods to non-sinusoidal periodic signals. We analyze a blind sample of 100 high-cadence (7-day binned) Fermi-LAT blazar light curves. After baseline detrending with Singular Spectrum Analysis, we apply the Fast Template Periodogram using three template families: a sinusoid, a Gaussian burst, and an empirical asymmetric template derived from PG 1553+113. Candidate signals are then evaluated with a Random Forest classifier trained on $5\\times10^4$ simulated light curves to distinguish genuine periodic phase structure from stochastic red noise. We identify nine $\u0026gt;3σ$ periodicity candidates. Three persistent QPOs, including PG 1553+113, are recovered by all template models, whereas six additional candidates are strongly suppressed under the standard sinusoidal assumption but become detectable with morphology-aware templates. Machine learning validation rejects five of these as lacking stable phase coherence. The remaining candidate, PKS 1424$-$418, shows a structurally stable 4.8-year periodicity with a source-specific significance of $3.76σ$ (approximately $2.4σ$ after accounting for the trial factor of the 100-source sample). These results demonstrate that morphology-aware periodograms, combined with structural machine learning validation, improve the detection of burst-dominated QPOs that are difficult to identify with traditional harmonic searches."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to A\\\u0026amp;A on April 24 (currently under review)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00664","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:33Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00662","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00662","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00662","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"V.","familyName":"Troude","name":"Troude, V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L.","familyName":"Takayasu","name":"Takayasu, L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R.","familyName":"Maskawa","name":"Maskawa, R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"W.","familyName":"Suda","name":"Suda, W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D.","familyName":"Sornette","name":"Sornette, D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H.","familyName":"Takayasu","name":"Takayasu, H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Takayasu","name":"Takayasu, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"No persistent circadian oscillator at genome resolution: pseudo-coherence in gut microbiome dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Biological Physics (physics.bio-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Adaptation and Self-Organizing Systems (nlin.AO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Genomics (q-bio.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:33:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diurnal rhythms in the gut microbiome are commonly read as evidence of host-driven entrainment or of microbial oscillators that synchronise to a common clock. We reanalyse hourly genome-resolved (MAG-level) mouse-gut time series with diagnostics tailored to test that interpretation. At this resolution and for both animals in the dataset, the time-frequency representation carries no persistent ridge; the time-averaged spectrum is enhanced at low frequencies and depleted at intermediate frequencies; the lagged covariance is markedly time-asymmetric, with a global imbalance peak near tens of hours; and an amplitude-adjusted Fourier surrogate test identifies a weak time-averaged construction in the candidate circadian band, never as a fixed time-frequency ridge. The two functional guilds that carry the inferred non-normal amplification are identified independently by the rankings of two inferred dynamical modes (the reaction mode, into which fluctuations are transiently amplified, and the non-normal mode, which injects them), and recover the primary polysaccharide degraders of Bacteroidota and the secondary butyrate and propionate fermenters of Bacillota A without invoking any phase information. The conjunction of these signatures matches a stable but strongly non-normal stochastic regime, that is, pseudo-coherence: geometric amplification reshapes stochastic fluctuations onto a low-dimensional reaction subspace, producing intermittent synchronisation-like episodes, broken time-reversal symmetry, and emergent time-averaged characteristic scales without an underlying oscillator. We propose a falsifiable test via high-resolution clock-gene-knockout cohorts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 9 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00662","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:31Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00661","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00661","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00661","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rodrigo Pato","familyName":"Nogueira","name":"Nogueira, Rodrigo Pato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Vieira","name":"Vieira, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"João R.","familyName":"Campos","name":"Campos, João R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unreliable in Practice? A Comprehensive Study of Errors in LLM-Generated Code"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:31:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are being widely used for coding, with reports indicating that AI now generates an increasing share of production code. Studies show that LLMs can significantly improve developer productivity, yet they still struggle with more complex coding tasks. Just as understanding error modes in human-written code has been central to improving software quality, identifying and characterizing the errors in LLM-generated code is critical for setting realistic expectations and designing mitigation strategies. Prior research has been limited in scope, often focusing on a single language, a small number of problems, or a limited selection of models. As a result, there is still no comprehensive understanding of which errors are common and which are specific to certain models or languages. To address these gaps and develop a deeper understanding of the quality of LLM-generated code, we analyzed a corpus of 86,726 code samples that contained compilation or runtime errors. These samples were generated by seven LLMs across four compiled languages. We classified errors by their underlying causes using an LLM, manually validated these classifications, and performed a comparative analysis. This labeled data is then used to measure error prevalence by model, language, and problem difficulty, to identify common error patterns. Results show that, although error types vary strongly across languages and models, even the largest models frequently make simple mistakes. We also observe that generated code often omits basic input validation or memory-safety checks, which can lead to overflows, resource exhaustion, or other reliability/security issues."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication in the 37th IEEE International Symposium on Software Reliability Engineering (ISSRE) 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00661","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:29Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00660","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00660","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00660","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikos","familyName":"Fotiou","name":"Fotiou, Nikos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lefteris","familyName":"Georgiadis","name":"Georgiadis, Lefteris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ignacio","familyName":"Lacalle","name":"Lacalle, Ignacio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"George C.","familyName":"Polyzos","name":"Polyzos, George C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vasilios A.","familyName":"Siris","name":"Siris, Vasilios A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving the Security of Containerized Workloads using Transparency and Traceability Services"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:25:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Containerized workloads are commonly built via CI/CD pipelines, stored in registries, and executed across heterogeneous infrastructures, including cloud and edge environments. A single compromised build step or credential can turn routine automation into large-scale distribution of malicious artifacts, motivating integrity, transparency, and enforceable deployment-time checks. In this paper, we present an architecture for verifiable container image distribution that addresses key-management challenges and enables policy-enforced admission-time verification. A transparency service generates one-time signing keys bound to authenticated identities, records signing events in an append-only transparency registry, and returns cryptographically verifiable proofs of inclusion. These proofs and identity attributes are attached to image metadata and evaluated by policy-as-code at admission time, so only compliant artifacts are deployed. We implement a proof-of-concept integrated with GitHub Actions and GitLab Runners and evaluate how the resulting pipeline mitigates common supply-chain attacks under a realistic threat model."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"in IEEE CSR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00660","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:28Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00659","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00659","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00659","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antoine","familyName":"Ducros","name":"Ducros, Antoine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"François","familyName":"Loeser","name":"Loeser, François","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Nash structure of curves over a valued field and tame henselian rationality"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Commutative Algebra (math.AC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic (math.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"03C98 14G22 14H99","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:22:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We provide a new proof, model-theoretic and geometric in nature, of the tame henselian rationality theorem of Kuhlmann. The proof takes place within the framework of stable completions of algebraic varieties over a valued field. It relies on the proof of two main results of independent interest: a Nash structure theorem for the stable completion of an algebraic curve and a tame descent theorem for abstract polydiscs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"74 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00659","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:27Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00658","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00658","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00658","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenming","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Chenming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Select-And-Extract: A Lightweight Plugin for Retrieval-Augmented Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:21:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) for language model (LM) systems fundamentally has two failure modes: retrieval failure and reading failure. The former fails to recall the right pieces of information from the external corpus, and the latter fails to produce the correct answer although the right information is retrieved. Some methods perform structured indexing for retrieval failure, but may suffer from limited generalization of the fixed structures. Some methods perform query-time structuring for reading failure, but typically require a lot of LM calls and rely heavily on the LM's capability. To this end, we propose Select-ANd-Extract (SANE), a simple yet effective plugin for RAG. For the retrieval failure, we retrieve a wide set of candidates with a semantic retriever, and leverage the LM to select the top candidates based on their synopses, which yields better recall than the original retriever. For the reading failure, we perform blueprint-guided query-time evidence extraction, which allows the generator LM to use only compact and structured key information so that it can perform better reasoning. Empirical results confirm that SANE brings solid improvements, while only introducing modest extra overhead. As a lightweight plugin for RAG, SANE offers a simple alternative to heavier approaches, and suggests a high-performance RAG framework need not be overly complex."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Pre-print"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00658","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:25Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00657","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00657","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00657","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin Christian","familyName":"Wibisono","name":"Wibisono, Kevin Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Causal Inference with Unstructured Treatments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:21:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Causal inference usually concerns a scalar treatment, yet in many problems the treatment is unstructured: a text, an image, or a sequence of clinical decisions. Consider an instructor writing a course description to attract more students: the treatment is the course description, and the outcome is enrollment. The standard target, the average treatment effect of fixing the treatment to one exact value versus another, runs into two problems. It cannot be estimated, because almost no exact description recurs across courses, leaving no comparable group from which to measure its effect; and it would be of little use even if it could, since no one wants every course to carry the same description. What the instructor actually wants to know is which features of a description raise enrollment, and which of those features can be acted on across many courses. To this end, we propose a causal query for unstructured treatments: the maximally influential feature (MIF), the feature of the treatment that most strongly influences the outcome. We formalize the MIF as a binary feature of the treatment, defined by a feature-scoring function, constrained so that both of its values stay well populated, and chosen to maximize the causal effect it induces. Turning the feature on shifts the distribution of treatments toward those that display it, turning it off shifts away, and the MIF effect contrasts the two average potential outcomes. We study identification conditions for the MIF, develop algorithms to estimate it, and make it actionable through a nudging algorithm that revises a treatment along the MIF into an outcome-improving version. We illustrate the MIF algorithm across applications in text, image, and dynamic treatment sequences."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"74 pages, 16 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00657","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:24Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00656","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00656","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00656","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minhui","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Minhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joshua D.","familyName":"Reiss","name":"Reiss, Joshua D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Simulation-Based Plate-Reverb Parameter Estimation from a Single Impulse Response"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:20:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a simulation-trained, non-iterative estimator for Task A of the 1st DAFx Parameter Estimation Challenge. Each unnormalized plate-reverb impulse response is summarized by amplitude, spectral, and decay descriptors, and an ensemble of tree regressors estimates the six target parameters in one pass. Across two independent synthetic validation sets, the normalized models outperform the training-set mean and an earlier raw-regression baseline. On a shared set, the final ensemble also outperforms a single run of the official default PSO at substantially lower inference cost. Since the official labels are hidden, parameter accuracy is measured on simulator-matched data, and the released responses support only audio-side consistency checks. The estimator returns point estimates without uncertainty."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as a challenge paper at the 29th International Conference on Digital Audio Effects, Cambridge, MA, USA, 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00656","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:22Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00655","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00655","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00655","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adedayo S.","familyName":"A","name":"A, Adedayo S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Blind Source Separation Can Distort Behavior and Connectivity Analyses of Calcium Transients"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:19:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Denoising is often treated as a technical prelude to calcium transients analysis, but it can redefine the variables used for behavior decoding and causal structure learning. An inspectable motorneuron recording with a visible artefact motivated this study: removing artefact-linked blind source separation (BSS) components also changed trace dynamics outside the targeted frame. We therefore tested whether BSS denoising preserves behavior and causal evidence on already extracted calcium transients. In a synthetic benchmark with known lagged graphs, raw corrupted traces retained graph recovery, whereas component-removing BSS variants collapsed median graph F1 to 0 across estimator families. We then evaluated four BSS methods: FastICA, Infomax, SOBI, and JADE, on four larval zebrafish recordings of v2a reticulospinal neurons (v2a-RSNs) with tail behavior. BSS sometimes improved behavior decoding, but gains depended on fish, method and retained clusters; matched PCA and low-pass controls often matched or exceeded BSS in fold-audited comparisons. Connectivity effects were more consistent: c-GC and c-GC* graphs inferred from BSS-cleaned traces were much denser than raw-trace graphs. BSS should therefore be treated as an intervention on the measured process, not a neutral cleanup step."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00655","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:21Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00654","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00654","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00654","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"János","familyName":"Végh","name":"Végh, János","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Is K+ current in Action Potential real?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Biological Physics (physics.bio-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:17:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The correct description of ion traffic during an Action Potential in neurons has fundamental importance from its theoretical description to practical clinical applications. The classical Hodgkin-Huxley theory hypothesized that a delayed K+ current causes the hyperpolarization of the Action Potential. The paper shows that the membrane's capacitive current was misinterpreted as K+ current, and that the direct evidence for that current is an experimental artifact, both arising from an incomplete understanding of the electrical model. After introducing the Axon Initial Segment (discovered decades after constructing the classical model) and using the correct cross-disciplinary physical laws for living nature (instead of using laws valid for inanimate nature), one can construct the correct model for neuronal operation, which provides the perfect description of neuronal operation, including the Action Potential, in full accordance with the laws of science."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00654","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:20Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00653","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00653","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00653","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuhiko","familyName":"Tamesue","name":"Tamesue, Kazuhiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shotaro","familyName":"Yamaguchi","name":"Yamaguchi, Shotaro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiroyuki","familyName":"Kasai","name":"Kasai, Hiroyuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wataru","familyName":"Kameyama","name":"Kameyama, Wataru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toshio","familyName":"Sato","name":"Sato, Toshio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutaka","familyName":"Katsuyama","name":"Katsuyama, Yutaka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takuro","familyName":"Sato","name":"Sato, Takuro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takeshi","familyName":"Maesaka","name":"Maesaka, Takeshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Machine Learning-based Non-precipitating Clouds Estimation for THz Dual-Frequency Radar"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:13:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/cama57522.2023.10352672","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate measurement of non-precipitable clouds is important for early prediction of heavy rainfall disasters caused by extreme weather events. However, microwave cloud radar cannot observe the early stages of cloud development from non-precipitation clouds (cumulus) to cumulonimbus. In this paper, we propose a terahertz dual-frequency cloud radar using 150 GHz and 95 GHz bands to detect cloud particles in cumulus smaller than 10 μm. Using a dataset generated by the ITU-R radio propagation model, we estimate the liquid water content of non-precipitation clouds and water vapor content in atmospheric gases, respectively, by using a machine learning-based approach. The effectiveness of using the dual wavelength ratio as an explanatory variable is examined."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00653","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:18Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00652","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00652","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00652","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir","familyName":"Belder","name":"Belder, Amir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gonçalo Dias","familyName":"Pais","name":"Pais, Gonçalo Dias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Refael","familyName":"Vivanti","name":"Vivanti, Refael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omri","familyName":"Carmi","name":"Carmi, Omri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"DeTone","name":"DeTone, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oren","familyName":"Shrout","name":"Shrout, Oren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ido","familyName":"Gattegno","name":"Gattegno, Ido","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayellet","familyName":"Tal","name":"Tal, Ayellet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Assistant Placement Aria: A Benchmark for Egocentric Placement Assistance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:13:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Human assistance in robotics spans around several tasks such as navigation, object manipulation, and placement, where a key challenge is selecting target destinations that align with human intentions or preferences. We focus on this challenge in the context of Virtual Placement (VP), the task of identifying all plausible target locations given scene context and human-centric constraints. This differs from traditional placement tasks that typically focus on a single, predefined target location. The VP problem is complex, as it requires both global and local reasoning about the scene's geometry, semantics, and plausibility. To address this gap, we introduce {\\bf Assistant Placement Aria}, the first benchmark to explore diverse aspects of VP, including global, local, and human-centric constraints. It contains both synthetic and real indoor scenes annotated for three tasks: (i)~2D Panel Placement, (ii)~Sitting Suggestion, and (iii)~TV Placement. Each scene includes 2D images, a 3D point cloud, and a textual description of the objects within the scene. By contributing this benchmark, we aim to encourage further research in this underexplored and challenging field that is critically dependent on relevant data. We also evaluate several foundation models for object detection and segmentation on our benchmark."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00652","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:17Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00651","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00651","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00651","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diogo","familyName":"Dória","name":"Dória, Diogo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"João R.","familyName":"Campos","name":"Campos, João R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding Online Failure Prediction in Linux Through Complementary Multi-View Explainability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:12:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate Online Failure Prediction (OFP) has been shown to be feasible in Operating Systems (OSs) settings, but prediction alone is not sufficient for practical adoption. Without diagnostic insight, operators have limited basis to trust alerts or decide how to respond. Moreover, even when predictive accuracy is high, it is often unclear whether models are capturing meaningful failure processes or merely exploiting workload-specific noise and incidental correlations in telemetry. This paper reports a practical experience building and evaluating an explainable OFP pipeline for Linux OSs. We combine consensus-based feature selection for detection with temporal onset analysis, subsystemlevel causal analysis, and complementary diagnostic mechanisms to support failure interpretation. Evaluated under strict crossworkload conditions with frozen training artifacts, it achieved 91-94% detection on unseen workloads without retraining, while maintaining false alarm rates below 1%. However, failure mode diagnosis proved substantially more sensitive to workload shift, and several diagnostics mechanisms showed limited effectiveness for specific failure types. Our experience highlights three main lessons: i) detection generalizes more robustly than diagnosis across workload changes; ii) early-warning capability depends strongly on the failure mode, ranging from 38 to 215 seconds in our study; and iii) unseen failure modes are not reliably diagnosable from related training modes alone, providing 0% accuracy under Leave-One-Mode-Out (LOMO) evaluation. Taken together, these results show the value of complementary explainability mechanisms for interpreting accurate failure predictions, revealing when predictive signals reflect transferable failure structure and when diagnostic generalization breaks down under workload variation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication in the 37th IEEE International Symposium on Software Reliability Engineering (ISSRE) 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00651","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:15Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00650","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00650","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00650","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xufei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengcheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Pengcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yitong","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anqi","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Anqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jijun","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Jijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yidi","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Yidi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangxu","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Guangxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoliang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Guoliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanhe","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xuanhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TEngineDB-V: An OLAP-Native Vector Search System for Large-$k$ Workloads at Tencent"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:09:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vector search systems are essential infrastructure for modern data-driven applications. Large-$k$ analytical vector search, which retrieves $k=10^3$--$10^5$ results for analytics (e.g., aggregation, filtering, joins), is increasingly important for emerging workloads, including LLM data management and advertising analysis at Tencent. Existing systems remain inadequate: specialized vector databases often cap $k$ (e.g., $k \\leq 10^4$) to satisfy tail-latency constraints and offer limited analytical support, while OLAP systems typically embed per-segment vector indexes as black boxes, causing severe read/compute amplification and preventing native query optimization.\n This paper presents TEngineDB-V, an OLAP-native vector search system for large-$k$ workloads. TEngineDB-V makes vector search a first-class analytical primitive in Tencent's OLAP engine through a global segment-decoupled index materialized as relational tables, eliminating scatter-gather execution, reducing amplification, and enabling native storage optimizations. It decomposes IVFPQ-based search into relational operators, integrates OLAP optimizations, and introduces DPPQ, which combines direction-aware quantization with hierarchical residual refinement to improve recall while preserving relational efficiency. TEngineDB-V further incorporates index-aware query rewriting and a distributed-aware cost model for efficient distributed execution. Experiments show that TEngineDB-V achieves up to a $145\\times$ speedup over competitive systems such as StarRocks, and up to a $52\\times$ improvement in 10-billion-scale production deployments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00650","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:14Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00649","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00649","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00649","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto","familyName":"González-Sanz","name":"González-Sanz, Alberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gilles","familyName":"Mordant","name":"Mordant, Gilles","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shunan","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Shunan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Empirical optimal transport potentials: fast rates and a functional central limit theorem"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62G35, 62G30","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:09:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Optimal transport potentials are fundamental objects in statistics, economics, and machine learning: their gradients generate optimal transport maps, while the potentials themselves act as location-dependent dual prices and sensitivity variables. We study the estimation of the quadratic optimal transport potential when a fixed absolutely continuous reference distribution $μ$ is transported to an unknown distribution $ν$, accessed to via its empirical measure. Our main ingredient is a stability inequality that controls the $L^1(μ)$ distance, modulo additive constants, between a strongly convex potential $φ$ and a convex potential $\\widetildeφ$ by a weak dual norm of $(\\nabla\\widetildeφ)_\\#μ-(\\nablaφ)_\\#μ,$ together with a second-order Wasserstein remainder of logarithmic type. This separation between the leading empirical-process term and the Wasserstein remainder yields faster convergence for potentials than for the corresponding transport maps.\n Under smoothness and uniform convexity assumptions, the exact semidiscrete Brenier potential converges in $L^1(μ)$ at rate $n^{-1/2}$ for $d\\leq3$, at rate $n^{-1/2}(\\log n)^{5/2}$ for $d=4$, and at rate $n^{-2/d}(\\log n)^{(d+2)/d}$ for $d\\geq5$. The polynomial exponents are sharp. In dimensions $d\\leq3$, we further establish a nondegenerate function-space central limit theorem and prove consistency of the nonparametric bootstrap. These results yield joint root-$n$ inference for every fixed finite collection of normalization-invariant weighted contrasts of the potential, including regional shadow premia in reference-based risk problems. Finally, we prove matching upper and lower bounds of order $\\varepsilon\\log(1/\\varepsilon)$ for the normalization-invariant sum of the entropic dual potentials."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00649","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:13Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00648","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00648","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00648","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Licheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Licheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingtao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Mingtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MDGAM-Based Cooperative Task Scheduling for Communication-Constrained Distributed Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:08:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cooperative task scheduling in communication-constrained distributed multi-agent systems is challenging because each agent must make decisions from partial and dynamic observations while satisfying complex practical constraints. Existing heuristics rely on handcrafted bidding rules and repeated consensus, whereas many learning-based methods assume global observations and lack explicit communication-based coordination. To address these limitations, this paper proposes a neural scheduling framework for distributed multi-robot task allocation (MRTA), consisting of a multi-decoder graph attention model (MDGAM) policy model and a critic-free group relative multi-agent policy gradient (GRMAPG) training algorithm. MDGAM uses an extended graph attention mechanism to jointly update node and edge features, and employs multiple decoders to generate task-selection decisions and communication messages. GRMAPG constructs group-relative advantages from equivalent task-planning instances to replace the critic network used in conventional MARL algorithms, thereby reducing training difficulty and improving convergence performance. Experiments under different problem scales and communication ranges show that the proposed method improves task-completion performance over existing heuristic and learning-based methods, while ablation, complexity, and generalization tests further validate the proposed innovations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00648","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:11Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00647","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00647","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00647","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maksym","familyName":"Nechepurenko","name":"Nechepurenko, Maksym","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Axient: On-Chain Credit and Loss Allocation for Leveraged Event Markets: A Venue-Agnostic Protocol for Traders, Credit Providers, Market Makers, and Liquidation Backstops"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Trading and Market Microstructure (q-fin.TR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Finance (q-fin.CP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Risk Management (q-fin.RM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:07:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A physically backed leveraged event position requires real credit: if collateral C receives leverage L, the protocol supplies (L-1)C and uses the combined amount to acquire recognized event exposure. This paper develops a venue-agnostic on-chain credit architecture for that capital layer and an endogenous model of its capital market. It separates traders, Senior Credit LPs, market makers, liquidators, and Liquidation Backstop Providers; formalizes pool and debt shares, utilization- and risk-sensitive interest, collateral-locked position accounts, venue capabilities, market-maker commitments, withdrawal queues, isolated pools, non-redeemable reserves, and a deterministic loss waterfall; and models endogenous provider participation, leverage demand, liquidity withdrawal, liquidator entry, reserve replenishment, runs, and common-factor contagion. Formal results establish balanced real- and integer-unit accounting, settlement-confirmed debt priority, trader residual ownership, idempotent partial settlement, non-dilutive share issuance, junior-before-Senior impairment, loss-participating withdrawal queues, utilization-equilibrium conditions, and loss-allocation and contagion bounds. The release preserves 28 exact fixtures and 31,082 deterministic checks and adds fixed-seed agent-based experiments with 70,207,488 scalar invariant evaluations and zero failures. The experiments show that layered protection reduces but does not eliminate Senior loss, that 5x leverage materially increases capital pressure, and that market-maker capacity can raise aggregate shortfall if admission expands too quickly. Results are synthetic mechanism comparisons under author-specified behavior, not forecasts of APY, defaults, venue liquidity, or production safety."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"90 pages, 17 figures. Formal protocol design and fixed-seed synthetic agent-based validation; no live Axient or venue data"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00647","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:10Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00646","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00646","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00646","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiwen","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PixelSR: Efficient Screen Content Super-Resolution via Pixel Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:03:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Screen content images are generally composed of texts and graphics. Compared to natural images, these man-made images contain a large quantity of sharp but repetitive structures. However, existing works in screen content super-resolution underutilize the special characteristics of screen content, leaving a large room to improve model performance and speed up. In this paper, we propose PixelSR, a simple yet effective method to improve super-resolution performance but with faster inference speed. To improve model performance, we classify pixels via pixel binning to compute content attention in the training phase. Specifically, after binning pixels into content-dependent groups, content attention is aggregated from pixel features within each group to introduce a content-dependent and non-local receptive field for every pixel. In the testing phase, we utilize the properties of self-repetitiveness and redundancy in screen content to speed up inference without the loss of model performance. We divide targeted high-resolution pixels into three types, which are unique pixels, repeated pixels, and background pixels for each test image. We conduct conventional network processing on unique pixels and cache their predictions in the on-the-fly lookup table. For repeated pixels which have appeared in unique pixels, we directly retrieve prediction results from the lookup table without network processing. For background pixels, we use the nearest neighbor algorithm to generate high-resolution pixels. The on-the-fly lookup table is cleaned and repeats the procedure above for the next test image. Experiments show our PixelSR achieves state-of-the-art performance with shorter inference time in screen content super-resolution."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00646","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:08Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00645","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00645","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00645","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prakash","familyName":"Chaki","name":"Chaki, Prakash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takumi","familyName":"Ishihara","name":"Ishihara, Takumi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shinya","familyName":"Sugiura","name":"Sugiura, Shinya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Precoded Non-Orthogonal Frequency Division Multiplexing with Subcarrier Index Modulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T13:00:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/vtc2022-spring54318.2022.9860537","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we propose a novel precoded non-orthogonal frequency division multiplexing (NOFDM) with subcarrier index modulation (SIM) to increase spectral efficiency for multicarrier systems. The proposed NOFDM-SIM scheme is constituted by a filterbank multicarrier system under reduced subcarrier spacing, where precoded index modulated symbols are mapped onto the subcarriers. The detrimental effects of inter-carrier interference (ICI) resulting from the non-orthogonality between subcarriers are efficiently mitigated with the aid of eigenvalue-decomposition-based precoding. Our simulation results demonstrate that the proposed precoded NOFDM-SIM system exhibits a similar peak-to-average power ratio to OFDM and OFDM-SIM counterparts while achieving the bit error ratio limit of zero-ICI OFDM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 4 figures, Preprint (accepted version) for publication in IEEE 95th Vehicular Technology Conference (IEEE VTC2022-Spring), 2022"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00645","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:07Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00644","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00644","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00644","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatsuro","familyName":"Kawakami","name":"Kawakami, Tatsuro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teppei","familyName":"Takamatsu","name":"Takamatsu, Teppei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A cusp surface singularity is Frobenius liftable"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Commutative Algebra (math.AC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"13A35, 14B05, 14F10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:58:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We show that a cusp surface singularity is Frobenius liftable. As a consequence, we determine all the Frobenius liftable surface singularities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00644","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:06Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00643","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00643","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00643","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weizhe","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minhong","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Minhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinhao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jinhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangquan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Guangquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Domain Decoupling Attack: Exploiting the Validation Gap Between Protective DNS and Shared Edge Routing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:56:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:34:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Network attackers often conceal malicious communication within legitimate Internet traffic. Existing CDN-based evasion techniques rely on SNI--Host inconsistency, insufficient domain ownership verification, or provider-specific routing rewrites, which limit their applicability in modern CDN environments. We identify a validation gap in DNS-based authorization, where permission derived from an allowed domain applies to a shared IP and can be reused to reach another tenant in both CDN and non-CDN shared-hosting environments. This paper presents the Domain Decoupling Attack (DDA), which resolves an allowed domain to obtain permission for a shared edge IP and subsequently connects to the same address while presenting the hidden domain consistently in both TLS SNI and HTTP Host. Measurements of 1,069,048 domains across six continents produce 18,025,068 successful probes and identify exposure rates of 95.8% overall, 99.26% for CDN domains, 92.75% for non-CDN domains, and 97.7% for non-CDN cross-tenant IPs, while laboratory experiments reveal a structural limitation of DNS-bound access control on shared addresses. These results clarify the security risks of DNS-derived IP authorization and support the evaluation and improvement of access-control mechanisms in CDN and non-CDN shared-hosting environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00643","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:04Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00642","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00642","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00642","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alison M.","familyName":"Fernandes","name":"Fernandes, Alison M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hermes I.","familyName":"Del Monego","name":"Del Monego, Hermes I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruno S.","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Bruno S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anelise","familyName":"Munaretto","name":"Munaretto, Anelise","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hélder M.","familyName":"Fontes","name":"Fontes, Hélder M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui L.","familyName":"Campos","name":"Campos, Rui L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WiFuse: An Attention Mechanism for Human Activity Recognition using Fused CSI Amplitude and Delay-Doppler Channel Features"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:54:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recently, Wi-Fi sensing has played a significant role in Human Activity Recognition (HAR), as it enables the detection of various activities using only Wi-Fi signals, ensuring privacy and remaining non-intrusive for the user. However, environmental characteristics such as reflective surfaces, hardware offsets, and other physical impairments affect recognition by the neural network, subsequently causing errors and significantly reducing model accuracy. To overcome this problem we present the WiFuse framework, a dual-stream Channel State Information (CSI) framework for human activity recognition (HAR) that pairs denoised time-domain amplitude variations with 2D-FFT-derived Delay-Doppler motion representations computed from the sanitized channel phase. The fused representation feeds a hybrid ResNet-Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN) neural architecture augmented with channel and spatio-temporal attention, where the ResNet extracts spatial-spectral features and the TCN models long-range temporal dependencies; a decoupled two-stage transfer learning strategy is employed to improve optimization stability and feature reuse. We conduct extensive experiments on two public datasets, including comparisons against state-of-the-art methods and alternative hybrid architectures, ablation studies, and cross-dataset and domain-adaptation evaluations. The proposed framework reaches an overall accuracy of up to 95.28% across the four environments of the XRF55 dataset and up to 98.20% on the multi-user Wi-MIR dataset. Overall, the results indicate that combining amplitude and Delay-Doppler representations within a dual-stream strategy, enhanced by transfer learning, improves recognition performance under conditions that typically degrade deep neural networks, such as class overlap, multipath propagation, noise, and interference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00642","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:03Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00640","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00640","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00640","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Linyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weili","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Weili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Organizational","name":"Guanquecairang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongbin","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yongbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingye","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Jingye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nyima","familyName":"Tashi","name":"Tashi, Nyima","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gadeng","familyName":"Luosang","name":"Luosang, Gadeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TreeProbe : A Tibetan Medicine Benchmark for Cultural Bias in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:53:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are increasingly viewed as a potential means of mitigating global health inequities, yet their outputs often reflect dominant high-resource medical traditions and provide limited coverage of traditional medical knowledge systems. Tibetan medicine, one of the world's four major traditional medical systems, has an independent and highly structured theoretical framework. When models lack grounded understanding of Tibetan medicine, they may fall back on dominant epistemic systems and distort the native knowledge structure during reasoning. However, quantitative tools for evaluating cultural bias in Tibetan medicine remain largely absent. To address this gap, we introduce TreeProbe, the first cultural-bias benchmark organized around the native Tree of Medicine framework in Tibetan medicine. It contains 4,719 expert-adjudicated items covering 467 diseases and 10 subtasks along the three roots. Experiments on representative LLMs show that current models remain limited in native Tibetan medical contexts and exhibit systematic external ontology drift. Further analysis reveals that models diverge in whether they drift toward biomedical or TCM reasoning, shaped by pretraining data composition and surface resemblance between TCM and Tibetan medicine. TreeProbe provides a diagnostic benchmark for developing medical AI systems that are both linguistically inclusive and epistemically fair. Code and data are available in an anonymous repository at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/TreeProbe/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00640","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:44:00Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:44:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:44:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00639","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00639","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00639","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaehyun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jaehyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seokhun","familyName":"Jeong","name":"Jeong, Seokhun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sehyuk","familyName":"Ahn","name":"Ahn, Sehyuk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haechan","familyName":"Kwon","name":"Kwon, Haechan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sukyoung","familyName":"Ryu","name":"Ryu, Sukyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"P4-SpecTec: Integrating a Language Mechanization Framework into the Real-World P4 Specification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:51:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Programming languages evolve, but often without a complete and unambiguous definition of their syntax and semantics. Ambiguities and inconsistencies are silently introduced into specifications, and manifest as divergences between the specification, implementations, and formalizations that constitute the language ecosystem. Even in rare cases when a normative specification exists, keeping the ecosystem in sync is a daunting task. Language mechanization frameworks address this problem by treating a mechanized specification as the single source of truth, from which implementations and documents are generated. Recently, this approach has been integrated into the actual JavaScript and WebAssembly (Wasm) specifications with ESMeta and Wasm-SpecTec, respectively. Despite these successes, it remains an open question how to extrapolate ESMeta and Wasm-SpecTec to other language specifications. As a first step towards addressing this question, we present P4-SpecTec, a language mechanization framework for the P4 programming language, as a case study of real-world adoption of language mechanization. P4 introduces unique challenges, in particular the requirement that its type system mechanization should be executable, which is not supported by either ESMeta or Wasm-SpecTec. To address this challenge, we introduce algorithmic inference rules as the primary instrument for mechanization, enabling the mechanized P4 static and dynamic semantics to be executed as a P4 type checker and interpreter, respectively. We mechanized the most recent P4 specification, and utilizing its executability, identified 24 bugs across the official P4 specification and the reference compiler. Furthermore, P4-SpecTec derives a specification document as prose algorithms, making it accessible to P4 developers. P4-SpecTec is conditionally adopted as the official P4 specification authoring toolchain."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00639","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:59Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00638","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00638","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00638","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Work Statistics of Autonomous Quantum Energy Pumps"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:51:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop a terminal-resolved theory of work statistics for autonomous quantum energy pumps. By modeling the systems that supply and receive energy as explicit quantum terminals, the energy exchanged with each terminal is defined directly from its Hamiltonian change. When the terminals are modeled by ideal clocks, these full-space observables admit exact representations on the pump Hilbert space and recover the conventional phase-derivative currents of periodically and quasiperiodically driven systems. The framework resolves transported work from energy accumulated in the pump. It also incorporates arbitrary initial pump--terminal correlations and identifies when correlations can enhance directional energy transfer under uncertain driving phases. For periodic pumps, we derive finite-cycle work statistics in Floquet eigenstates, relate their fluctuations to Floquet quantum geometry, and show that the long-time terminal currents become mutually compatible. An exactly solvable two-terminal qubit exhibits noise matching, in which transport becomes sharp through cancellation of common-mode terminal fluctuations even though the individual terminal energies remain noisy. Finally, for physical terminals beyond the ideal-clock limit, we introduce a positive work-variance gap that quantifies the fluctuations missed by a pump-only description. A coherent-cavity benchmark shows systematic convergence toward the ideal-clock regime with increasing occupation while demonstrating that agreement of the mean current alone does not guarantee accurate work statistics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00638","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:57Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00637","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00637","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00637","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cesar R.","familyName":"de Oliveira","name":"de Oliveira, Cesar R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Remarks on Regular Approximations to the Robin Aharonov-Bohm Hamiltonian"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Spectral Theory (math.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"primary 35J10, 81Q10. Secondary: 81Q15, 47A55, 35J10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:46:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1142/s0129055x26500157","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the space $\\mathbb R^3$, for Robin parameter $L\u0026gt;0$, it is shown that there is a family of Schrödinger operators with penetrable toroidal solenoid and variable conductivity that approximates the magnetic Aharonov-Bohm operator with a Robin boundary condition at the solenoid (border). It is also shown that approximations via smooth potentials and then a barrier in the solenoid interior give Dirichlet boundary conditions. The approximations are in the strong resolvent sense and obtained through the $Γ$-convergence technique, and they hold for the more general setting of smooth, closed and compact surfaces and continuous and bounded magnetic potentials."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in Reviews in Mathematical Physics"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00637","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:56Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00636","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00636","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00636","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G. P.","familyName":"Wilmot","name":"Wilmot, G. P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J.","familyName":"Chappell","name":"Chappell, J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D. K.","familyName":"Abbott","name":"Abbott, D. K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Clifford Algebra Calibration Post-Quantum Cryptography"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Rings and Algebras (math.RA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:44:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The double lock scenario allows a secret to be transferred from one person to another without having previously exchanged secrets such as private/public keys. This can be realised by rotations of generalised calibrations in Clifford algebra. Calibrations map to Cayley-Dickson algebras and generalising this provides the mathematical structure enabling the enormous code space needed for a cryptosystem. Commuting rotations in large dimensional spaces are used but the complexity comes from the message being embedded in generalised calibrations. This system uses ideals of Clifford algebras and quasi-algebras that are sometimes embedded in Cayley-Dickson algebras making it a candidate for post-quantum cryptography. Understanding whether the new mathematics of generalised calibrations can produce a secure cryptosystem is the challenge presented in this manuscript."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00636","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:54Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00635","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00635","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00635","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Runqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ding","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengyuan","familyName":"Min","name":"Min, Pengyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinhong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xinhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Fu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FlowPilot: Real-Time World-Action Modeling for Agile UAV Navigation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:43:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present FlowPilot, a compact world-action model for real-time onboard UAV navigation from depth. Unlike map-then-optimize pipelines that require local reconstruction or end-to-end policies that lack explicit scene prediction, FlowPilot jointly denoises future depth observations and executable trajectories with flow matching. A dual-stream mixture-of-transformers couples video and action experts through shared attention, allowing future-scene prediction and trajectory generation to inform each other. At deployment, the model runs action-centrically and outputs only a trajectory. To ensure trackability, actions are parameterized as degree-7 Bernstein polynomials: the current state constrains the initial control points, and the network predicts five free control points, yielding C^2-continuous references with closed-form velocity, acceleration and jerk. FlowPilot is trained on a three-level depth pyramid spanning high-throughput simulation, photorealistic simulation, and real onboard data. In closed-loop simulation, it outperforms learning- and optimization-based baselines under increasing clutter and commanded speeds up to 8m/s. On a physical quadrotor, the full perception-to-action pipeline runs in under 18ms on a Jetson Orin NX and reaches 5.5m/s in cluttered indoor and forest environments using only onboard sensing and computation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 9 figures, 2 tables, submitted to IEEE Robotics and Automation Letters (RA-L)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00635","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:53Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00634","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00634","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00634","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gerhard","familyName":"Hensler","name":"Hensler, Gerhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yannick","familyName":"Hein","name":"Hein, Yannick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Boselli","name":"Boselli, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"the VESTIGE","familyName":"consortium","name":"consortium, the VESTIGE","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Formation of extragalactic Star Clusters"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:41:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The outskirts of galaxies show their beauty by an assembly of Globular Clusters and satellite galaxies, but also by interactions with their environment. Galaxies in clusters experience dynamical effects by mutual encounters and by the intra-cluster gas. This latter exerts a drag to remove gas from the outskirts. These stripped clouds are capable to form stars and open fundamental questions on astrophysical processes, as e.g. the survival of intergalactic clouds, the star-formation process, the evolution of isolated star clusters, etc. Our studies target at star clusters around the Virgo cluster spiral galaxy NGC 4254, observed within the VESTIGE survey. 60 young clusters are identified in the GALEX/FUV with different masses, ages, and separations from the mature galactic disk. Only half of them are also emitting H-alpha as long as the clusters are still encompassed by gas until ram pressure strips it off leading to star-formation quenching and LyC escape."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"4 pages, 2 figures, Proceedings of the IAU Symposium No. 403, The Hidden Beauty of Galactic Outskirts"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00634","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:52Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00633","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00633","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00633","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Shishido \u0026 Associates, Hachioji, Tokyo, Japan","SeemeData Labs, Inc., Nakano-ku, Tokyo, Japan"],"givenName":"Tomoyuki","familyName":"Shishido","name":"Shishido, Tomoyuki","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Ootaki \u0026 Architects, Sayama, Saitama, Japan"],"givenName":"Masahiro","familyName":"Ootaki","name":"Ootaki, Masahiro","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Naru Science Software, Fujimi-shi, Saitama, Japan"],"givenName":"Hiroyuki","familyName":"Narusawa","name":"Narusawa, Hiroyuki","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"bFaaaP: An Inclusive, Head-Angle Piano-Pedal Interaction that Quantitatively Reproduces a Pianist's Intended Pedalling -- Foot-Free, for Acoustic and Electronic Pianos"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.5.2; K.4.2; I.2.9; J.5","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:41:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Expressive piano performance depends on the sustain (damper) pedal, operated by foot, excluding players who cannot readily use their feet: wheelchair users and others with lower-limb impairments, small children, and some elderly or disabled players. We present bFaaaP (barrier-Free assist as a Pedal), an inclusive, foot-free interaction that operates the pedal from the angle of the player's head: a smartphone tracks head pose with on-device augmented-reality (AR) face tracking and streams a compact command over Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) to a pedal device. Supported by patent examination, our central claim is not the head-to-pedal architecture (anticipated by prior art) but a quantitative, user-tunable control law -- the patentable \"key\" to a natural, expressive result: the player presets a small angular dead-zone (offset 3-10 degrees) and a multiplier (10-50), which together fix a secondary, pre-adjustable response speed that reproduces the pianist's intended pedalling. An engineering trick decouples the fast AR sampling from the slower BLE rate. Two co-equal realizations share one controller: a non-destructive robotic actuator for acoustic pianos (Pro), anchored by a pneumatic \"airback\" (our coined term for an inflatable air-braced anchor) that absorbs the reaction force without modifying the instrument; and an electronic sustain switch for digital pianos (Switch). In a human-subject Auxiliary Pedal Effect Evaluation (APEE) with 15 participants, bFaaaP significantly increased sustained-tone energy (p\u0026lt;0.01) and was statistically indistinguishable from a player's own foot (p\u0026gt;0.05), with no significant difference across classes; one participant with a leg disability and a tracheostomy performed successfully. With nothing worn on the face and fast setup, bFaaaP has run in formal public concerts (2018-2025). We release the full hardware and software as open source."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, 10 figures, 9 tables. Open hardware/software release. Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00633","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:50Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00632","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00632","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00632","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiwei","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang Young","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yang Young","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning the Pareto Frontier of Predictive Models under Distribution Shift"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:40:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern machine learning pipelines increasingly rely on reusing pretrained and foundation models across downstream tasks. These pretrained models can differ not only in performance but also in how they can be used: some only provide black-box predictions, while others may permit white-box access to internal representations that can be probed or fine-tuned. When deployed to the target domain in the presence of distribution shift, no single strategy, including zero-shot application, fine-tuning, or directly training a target-specific model, is uniformly the best.\n In this work, we propose Frontier Learning, a framework that treats a library of candidate models spanning different training histories and access regimes as complementary sources of information rather than mutually exclusive alternatives. Frontier Learning constructs a unified target-domain feature by concatenating internal representations from white-box candidates as well as prediction outputs from black-box candidates, then fits a lightweight, regularized supervised learner on this concatenated representation using labeled target data. Because the resulting hypothesis class contains predictors obtained by zero-shot reuse, fine-tuning, and direct training as special cases, empirical risk minimization over the frontier learner is guaranteed to be no worse, on the training sample, than any individual baseline.\n We evaluate the framework in simulations spanning varying degrees of source-target compatibility and in two real-world distribution-shift settings: visual domain adaptation on DomainNet/VisDA and clinical mortality prediction across intensive care unit domains using MIMIC-IV-Notes. Across all settings, Frontier Learning matches or outperforms the strongest individual reuse strategy, with the largest gains arising precisely when no single baseline is reliable across the range of shift considered."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, 4 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00632","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:49Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00631","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00631","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00631","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maksym","familyName":"Nechepurenko","name":"Nechepurenko, Maksym","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Axient: Debt-Free Finality for Leveraged Binary Event Markets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Trading and Market Microstructure (q-fin.TR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Risk Management (q-fin.RM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:39:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Leveraged event positions combine a repayable loan with an outcome claim that may become non-tradable before oracle payout is final. This paper specifies Axient, a physically backed margin layer for binary event markets that separates leverage maturity from claim maturity and makes the hard-flat decision under explicit execution uncertainty. The model distinguishes quoted book proceeds, matched proceeds, settled proceeds, and redemption. At decision time, the protocol selects the smallest sale whose lower settled-proceeds envelope covers an upper bound on debt at the settlement horizon plus a buffer. We prove robust ex-ante debt clearing, pathwise debt-extinguishment and debt-free-finality invariants, maximal residual spot exposure, payout-vector and dispute-duration invariance of lender principal after debt extinction, and an impossibility boundary when execution, signer control, settlement, or market closure leave the registered operating set. We also derive a book-dependent leverage envelope, aggregate hard-flat capacity without double-counting shared liquidity, and scenario-conditional reserve bounds. A deterministic verifier covers step books, partial fills, settlement delay, adversarial book transformations, shared-book liquidation, reserve allocation, zero liquidity, and multiple payout vectors. The operating and stress sets are author-specified; empirical calibration is separate. The contribution is a conditional mechanism-design result and reference-implementation boundary, not a production-safety claim."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"65 pages, 7 figures. Mechanism-design paper with deterministic verification; no external dataset and no production-safety claim"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00631","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:47Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00630","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00630","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00630","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Malavika","familyName":"Suresh","name":"Suresh, Malavika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ikechukwu","familyName":"Nkisi-Orji","name":"Nkisi-Orji, Ikechukwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nirmalie","familyName":"Wiratunga","name":"Wiratunga, Nirmalie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Relative Parameter Importance in Task-Agnostic Replay-Free Continual Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:38:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Achieving continual learning (CL) with deep neural networks requires balancing stability and plasticity while enabling knowledge transfer. In this work, we focus on offline learning algorithms under the constraints: (I) no access to training data from prior tasks (II) no access to task-id at inference time. We introduce a novel measure, the relative parameter-importance, which measures the relative importance of each parameter with respect to both the current and past tasks. Parameters with high relative importance are interpreted as more important for maintaining past-task stability and thus heavily regularised, whereas parameters with low relative-importance are allowed to be more freely updated. Unlike existing methods, our approach allows the update of parameters with high past-task importance when they have low relative-importance, thus enabling backward knowledge transfer in addition to tackling the stability-plasticity trade-off. We demonstrate improvements against state-of-the-art CL methods on both class-incremental and domain-incremental learning text classification problems and provide insights for extending our method to text generation problems. Code available at: https://github.com/itsmemala/LACL"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication at the SCL Workshop, ECML-PKDD 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00630","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:46Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00629","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00629","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00629","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhao","familyName":"Zhuge","name":"Zhuge, Junhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Junyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangzhan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kangzhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sipeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Sipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaogang","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Xiaogang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ParticleGen: A Multi-Agent System for Particle Effects Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:38:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Particle systems are widely used in digital entertainment to create dynamic scene elements and visual effects. However, authoring high-quality particle effects remains labor-intensive and demands specialized expertise, requiring practitioners to navigate complex procedural rules and high-dimensional parameter spaces. Recent large language models (LLMs) enable users to specify particle effects through natural language, yet reliably translating high-level creative intent into executable procedural logic and low-level parameters remains difficult. In this work, we present a multi-agent framework for the from-scratch synthesis of structured and editable particle systems from natural language descriptions. Given a text prompt, our framework first generates an initial particle configuration through a decoupled planning and parameterization pipeline, and then iteratively improves the result based on rendered feedback. To support precise and targeted adjustments, we further introduce a diagnostic mechanism that links observed visual artifacts to their underlying procedural causes. We validate our approach in Unreal Engine 5's Niagara system across a diverse set of scenarios, including elemental spells, dynamic natural phenomena, and fireworks. Quantitative and qualitative evaluations show that our method achieves high semantic fidelity and visual quality. By directly synthesizing structured particle simulation logic, our framework reduces the technical barrier to particle effect authoring and improves the efficiency of creative iteration."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00629","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:45Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00628","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00628","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00628","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"V.","familyName":"Delécluse","name":"Delécluse, V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Ivashkovych","name":"Ivashkovych, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Thullen-type Extension Theorem for Nakano Semi-Positive Holomorphic Vector Bundles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Complex Variables (math.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary - 32D15, , Secondary - 32J25, 32L05, 32L10, 14F17","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:35:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove a Thullen-type extension theorem for hermitian bundles with Nakano semi-positive curvature. As an intermediate result we establish the $L^2$-estimates for the $\\dbar$-equation in hermitian vector bundles over a non-complete Kähler manifolds."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00628","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:43Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00627","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00627","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00627","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinnan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Qinnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanyuan","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Yuanyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Metal sign of a large-scale AGN feedback in cool-core cluster MACS J1931.8-2634"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:35:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The spatial distribution of metals in the intracluster medium (ICM) is a sensitive tracer of the chemical and dynamical history of galaxy clusters. While most cool-core (CC) clusters exhibit a centrally peaked Fe abundance profile, several outliers show an anomalous central Fe drop, potentially associated with the AGN activities. We revisit the reported large-scale (sim 100 kpc) central Fe drop in the massive CC cluster MACS J1931.8-2634 using new XMM-Newton observations. We aim to verify this feature and search for imprints of AGN feedback on the ICM metallicity distribution. We analyzed sim 170 ks of new XMM-Newton observations and re-analyzed sim 100 ks archived Chandra observations. We derived radial and two-dimensional (2D) Fe abundance maps from CCD spectra. High-resolution RGS spectra were used to constrain the Ne/Fe abundance ratio to test the dust depletion scenario. Spectral fitting was performed in SPEX using an updated atomic database and both single- and multi-temperature collisional ionization equilibrium models. The previously reported central Fe drop is not confirmed in the radial profile from XMM-Newton. However, the 2D Fe distribution is clearly asymmetric: Fe-rich regions are elongated along the axis of the AGN cavities, extending beyond their immediate scale. The Ne/Fe ratio in the core is consistent with solar (${\\rm Ne/Fe} = 1.03^{+0.25}_{-0.23}$), arguing against the dust depletion scenario."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication in Astronomy \u0026amp; Astrophysics. 7 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00627","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:42Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00626","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00626","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00626","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcel","familyName":"Plocher","name":"Plocher, Marcel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernhard","familyName":"Schölkopf","name":"Schölkopf, Bernhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Geiger","name":"Geiger, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gege","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Gege","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Where Does Generative Difficulty Reside? An Empirical Study of Target Representations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:32:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The target representation defines the distribution an image generator must learn, yet it is often treated as an interchangeable interface. This assumption is particularly questionable for continuous masked generators, which combine contextual inference from visible tokens with conditional modeling of each missing token. We study raw pixels, SD-VAE latents and DINOv2 as well as MAE representation-autoencoder features within a unified masked autoregressive rectified-flow model. Under a shared ImageNet training budget, these spaces exhibit distinct optimization and inference regimes. DINOv2 converges fastest in both iterations and computation but benefits strongly from a wider local denoiser and direct context fusion. Pixels optimize substantially more slowly and require a different prediction, masking, and guidance configuration. MAE reconstructs images more faithfully and exhibits clear semantic clustering, yet produces generations substantially worse than DINOv2. The representations also respond differently to classifier-free guidance and occupy distinct precision-recall trade-offs. Together, our results show that compression, reconstruction fidelity, token dimensionality, and visible semantic clustering do not individually predict generative behavior. Instead, target representations redistribute difficulty across contextual modeling, per-token denoising, and inference-time distributional control."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"TL;DR: Across pixels, SD-VAE, DINOv2, and MAE, we find that target representations are not interchangeable: they shift difficulty between contextual modeling, per-token denoising, and guidance, producing distinct optimization and diversity trade-offs"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00626","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:40Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00625","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00625","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00625","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongyuan","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Zongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shalabh","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Shalabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shancheng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Shancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dehua","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Dehua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Gao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaming","familyName":"Ou","name":"Ou, Yaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongqiang","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Zhongqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikui","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Yikui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"C. L. Philip","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, C. L. Philip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning-Based Motion Planning for Dynamic Environments: From Foundational Algorithms to Emerging Paradigms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:30:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Motion planning in dynamic environments is a fundamental problem in robotics, aiming to generate safe and efficient paths, trajectories, or control actions in the presence of moving obstacles, uncertain predictions, and multi-agent interactions. It has broad applications in autonomous driving, service robotics, warehouse logistics, human-robot collaboration, crowd navigation, and multi-robot systems. This survey reviews representative works published primarily between 2015 and 2025, with a particular focus on how recent learning-based advances extend, complement, or interact with classical planning foundations. We first revisit classical planning methods as algorithmic foundations and reference frameworks for learning-based extensions. We then propose a role-of-learning taxonomy that categorizes existing methods according to how learning participates in the planning pipeline, including direct policy learning, learning-augmented classical planning, hybrid planning, and training enhancement methods. For each category, we summarize the main problem settings, representative algorithms, key ideas, integration mechanisms, strengths, and limitations. We further analyze how observation representations, prediction uncertainty, interaction modeling, planner integration, safety constraints, and training strategies shape learning-based motion planning in dynamic environments. Finally, we discuss open challenges and future directions, including sim-to-real gap, safe and certifiable planning, dense crowd navigation, perception-planning coupling, and embodied AI."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00625","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:39Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00624","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00624","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00624","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaokai","familyName":"Rong","name":"Rong, Xiaokai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammadali Sefidi","familyName":"Esfahani","name":"Esfahani, Mohammadali Sefidi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aashish","familyName":"Yadavally","name":"Yadavally, Aashish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rigby","familyName":"Peter","name":"Peter, Rigby","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tien N.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Tien N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Perplexity Serve as a Cognitive Signal for Code Understandability?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:29:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent work suggests that token-level perplexity from large language models can align with localized human confusion during code comprehension. This raises a natural question: can perplexity also serve as a snippet-level signal for code understandability? We conduct an empirical study of this question across multiple human-grounded datasets, including method-level understandability judgments, output-prediction tasks, and accepted understandability-improvement patches. Despite prior token-level evidence, we find that simple snippet-level aggregations of token perplexity, such as average, median, or peak perplexity, do not reliably correlate with human understandability. We then investigate why this happens. First, token perplexity is highly skewed and heavy-tailed across code structures; extreme spikes arise not only from semantically meaningful constructs, but also from identifiers, literals, types, separators, and tokenization artifacts. Second, human understandability labels often lack consensus, making whole-snippet difficulty a noisy target. Third, perplexity distributions and their alignment with human difficulty vary substantially across models and tokenizers. These findings explain why prior token-level perplexity--confusion alignment does not directly transfer to snippet-level understandability. Overall, our study positions perplexity as a promising but delicate cognitive signal: useful for localized code confusion, but requiring code-aware aggregation, consensus-aware evaluation, and model-sensitivity analysis before it can support reliable code-understandability measurement."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00624","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:38Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00623","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00623","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00623","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinlin","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yinlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xunkai","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xunkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Wang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Jin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wei-Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Miao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guocong","familyName":"Quan","name":"Quan, Guocong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Effective Federated Multimodal Graph Learning via Navigating Multifaceted Heterogeneity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:29:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal-attributed graphs (MAGs), where nodes carry heterogeneous semantic content across multiple modalities while edges encode relational dependencies, have been widely adopted across diverse domains. Federated multimodal graph learning (FMGL) extends federated graph learning (FGL) to MAGs, enabling collaborative optimization across decentralized MAGs without exposing raw data. However, naively applying existing FGL methods to FMGL is insufficient, as they fail to navigate the multifaceted heterogeneity inherent in decentralized MAGs, including task heterogeneity across diverse client objectives, modality heterogeneity from discrepant modality quality and semantic domains, and topology heterogeneity arising from divergent topological patterns with low cross-modality correlation. To address these challenges, we propose Federated multimodal graph learning with Topology-aware Cross-modal Routing (FedTCR), the first systematic algorithm designed for FMGL. To handle task heterogeneity, FedTCR employs a two-stage paradigm that comprises federated task-agnostic pre-training followed by isolated task-oriented fine-tuning. To jointly address modality and topology heterogeneity, FedTCR introduces a topology-aware cross-modal routing mechanism. Concretely, each client distills modality-specific knowledge into compact prototypes via topology-aware importance-weighted aggregation informed by graph structure; the server then evaluates cross-client cross-modal relationships among these structure-informed prototypes and routes informative ones as contrastive references, driving a tri-level cross-modal contrastive learning scheme that jointly aligns cross-client modalities while preserving discrimination. Experiments across 7 domains demonstrate that FedTCR outperforms state-of-the-art baselines on both graph-centric and modality-centric tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under Review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00623","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:36Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00622","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00622","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00622","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Tian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yemin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yemin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuancheng","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Chuancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zesheng","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Zesheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanglai","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Guanglai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangdong","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Xiangdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Heuristic Perspective on Debiasing Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:27:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language models (LMs) often acquire various biases during pre-training and may express them in interactions, potentially causing social harm. Existing methods often rely on counterfactual augmentation or representation projection. These strategies remain limited in practice due to their high computational costs and difficulty in scaling to larger models. Additionally, many of these strategies require manual data annotation, narrowing their scope to specific cultures and bias categories. To overcome these limitations, we propose HEIMAT, a HEurIstic-style autoMATic debiasing framework for LMs. HEIMAT consists of two main steps: bias disclosure and debiasing fine-tuning. In the first step, it uses simple templates to construct heuristic prompts, which are applied to reveal model biases and generate corresponding context prompts. In the second step, it fine-tunes the model by minimizing the Jensen-Shannon divergence of predictions on these context prompts to reduce bias. Extensive experiments show that HEIMAT effectively mitigates bias in different cultures while maintaining the model's natural language understanding (NLU) performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages in total, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00622","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:35Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00621","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00621","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00621","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinlin","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yinlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyu","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Ziyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zekai","familyName":"Chenm","name":"Chenm, Zekai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Wang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Miao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guocong","familyName":"Quan","name":"Quan, Guocong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RHEA: Reliability-Harmonized Reconstruction and Assignment for Robust Multimodal-Attributed Graph Clustering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:24:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal-attributed graphs (MAGs), whose nodes carry heterogeneous attributes such as text and images over a relational structure, have become a fundamental substrate for label-free entity grouping tasks, including community discovery and product segmentation. Existing MAG clustering methods effectively integrate complementary modalities when attributes are clean and complete, but degrade substantially under noisy or missing attributes because they implicitly assume equal modality reliability across all nodes. In practice, modality reliability is inherently node-specific: images may be corrupted or absent, while textual descriptions are incomplete or noisy. We argue that, under attribute homophily, graph neighborhoods naturally provide supervision-free evidence for estimating node-specific modality reliability. Based on this insight, we propose RHEA, a reliability-aware framework for MAG clustering that estimates node-specific modality reliability from neighborhood consensus and propagates this signal throughout the clustering pipeline. RHEA reconstructs unreliable or missing modalities from graph neighborhoods, adaptively weights modalities during reliability-aware fusion, and performs topology-aware optimal transport clustering with reliability-aware transport assignment and neighbor-consensus assignment distillation. Furthermore, the confidence of reconstructed representations is incorporated into the clustering objective, allowing uncertain reconstructions to contribute proportionally during optimization. Experiments on four MAG benchmarks under five attribute conditions show that RHEA consistently outperforms the strongest baseline, with NMI gains increasing as attribute quality deteriorates."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under Review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00621","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:34Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00620","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00620","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00620","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhu","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Yizhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gengbo","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Gengbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianghao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xianghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spatially Reconfigurable Antenna Systems for 6G: EM-based Channel Modeling, Measurements, and Orientation Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:21:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:33:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spatially reconfigurable antenna systems (SRASs) are recognized as a key physical-layer technology for sixth-generation (6G) systems. By dynamically adjusting each antenna element's spatial configuration, e.g., position and orientation, SRASs can revamp favorable channel conditions for reliable high-rate data transmission. However, in widely adopted channel models, antennas are typically modeled as ideal isotropic radiators, and the vectorial nature of electromagnetic (EM) propagation is neglected. This oversimplified model precludes full exploitation of the degrees of freedom offered by SRASs for performance enhancement. To address this issue, in this paper, by leveraging the theoretical framework of spherical vector wave expansion, we develop an EM-based channel model tailored for SRAS-enabled multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) systems. The proposed EM-based channel model is applicable to antennas with arbitrary structures and intrinsically accounts for the vectorial nature of EM propagation, thereby enabling accurate characterization of EM effects such as polarization mismatch on channel gain. Full-wave simulations and experimental measurements are conducted, and the results show excellent agreement with theoretical predictions. Simulation results also reveal that antenna orientation exerts a more pronounced influence on the achievable rate than antenna displacement. Therefore, building upon the derived channel model, a manifold optimization method is proposed to maximize the sum-rate of an SRAS-enabled multiuser-MIMO system by optimizing antenna orientations. Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed scheme improves the sum-rate by up to 16.6\\% and 19.3\\% compared to systems employing movable antennas and conventional fixed antennas, respectively."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00620","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:32Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00619","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00619","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00619","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusuke","familyName":"Kawabata","name":"Kawabata, Yusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukio","familyName":"Katsukawa","name":"Katsukawa, Yukio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masahito","familyName":"Kubo","name":"Kubo, Masahito","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takayoshi","familyName":"Oba","name":"Oba, Takayoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takuma","familyName":"Matsumoto","name":"Matsumoto, Takuma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryohtaroh T.","familyName":"Ishikawa","name":"Ishikawa, Ryohtaroh T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshihiro","familyName":"Naito","name":"Naito, Yoshihiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hirohisa","familyName":"Hara","name":"Hara, Hirohisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toshifumi","familyName":"Shimizu","name":"Shimizu, Toshifumi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fumihiro","familyName":"Uraguchi","name":"Uraguchi, Fumihiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toshihiro","familyName":"Tsuzuki","name":"Tsuzuki, Toshihiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuya","familyName":"Shinoda","name":"Shinoda, Kazuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomonori","familyName":"Tamura","name":"Tamura, Tomonori","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshinori","familyName":"Suematsu","name":"Suematsu, Yoshinori","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlos Quintero","familyName":"Noda","name":"Noda, Carlos Quintero","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose Carlos del Toro","familyName":"Iniesta","name":"Iniesta, Jose Carlos del Toro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David Orozco","familyName":"Suárez","name":"Suárez, David Orozco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"María Balaguer","familyName":"Jimenez","name":"Jimenez, María Balaguer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sami K.","familyName":"Solanki","name":"Solanki, Sami K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Lagg","name":"Lagg, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Achim","familyName":"Gandorfer","name":"Gandorfer, Achim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pietro","familyName":"Bernasconi","name":"Bernasconi, Pietro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Berkefeld","name":"Berkefeld, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Feller","name":"Feller, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tino L.","familyName":"Riethmüller","name":"Riethmüller, Tino L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto","familyName":"Álvarez-Herrero","name":"Álvarez-Herrero, Alberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H. N.","familyName":"Smitha","name":"Smitha, H. N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bianca","familyName":"Grauf","name":"Grauf, Bianca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Carpenter","name":"Carpenter, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Bell","name":"Bell, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentín Martínez","familyName":"Pillet","name":"Pillet, Valentín Martínez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francisco Javier","familyName":"Bailén","name":"Bailén, Francisco Javier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian Blanco","familyName":"Rodríguez","name":"Rodríguez, Julian Blanco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan Sebastián Castellanos","familyName":"Durán","name":"Durán, Juan Sebastián Castellanos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edvarda","familyName":"Harnes","name":"Harnes, Edvarda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johannes","familyName":"Hoelken","name":"Hoelken, Johannes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francisco A.","familyName":"Iglesias","name":"Iglesias, Francisco A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Azaymi L.","familyName":"Siu-Tapia","name":"Siu-Tapia, Azaymi L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanna","familyName":"Strecker","name":"Strecker, Hanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dušan","familyName":"Vukadinović","name":"Vukadinović, Dušan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Three-dimensional Magnetic Structures of Ellerman Bombs revealed by SUNRISE III/SCIP"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:20:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:32:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.3847/2041-8213/ae91e0","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ellerman bombs (EBs) are widely recognized as photospheric and chromospheric signatures of magnetic reconnection. However, the three-dimensional (3D) magnetic topology has remained elusive due to the lack of seamless height coverage in observations. Here, we present initial results from the SUNRISE III/SCIP (Sunrise Chromospheric Infrared spectroPolarimeter) observations of an emerging flux region. Exploiting the seeing-free, high-spatial-resolution observations provided by the 1-meter balloon-borne telescope, SCIP achieved seamless multi-line spectropolarimetry from the photosphere to the lower chromosphere. We analyzed the multi-line Stokes profiles of the photospheric Fe I and K I lines and the chromospheric Ca II lines, and applied the Weak Field Approximation to the K I and Ca II lines to reconstruct the 3D magnetic field structure. The blue- and red-wing brightenings of the Ca II 8542 Å line appear at spatially offset locations, indicating bi-directional reconnection flows. The reconstructed 3D magnetic field reveals that the opposite-polarity field structure reaches different heights in the two events analyzed. In one event, it is confined to the lower layers and is absent at the formation height of Ca II 8542 Å core, which shows no intensity enhancement, whereas in the other event it extends up to the Ca II 8542 Å core formation height, where enhanced line-core intensity is also observed. We interpret this as the reconnection current sheet reaching different altitudes. These results demonstrate that SCIP has successfully resolved the 3D structure of EBs, distinguishing magnetic reconnection events occurring at different atmospheric heights."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 5 figures. Accepted for publication in the Astrophysical Journal Letters. An animation of Figure 1 is included as an ancillary file"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00619","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:31Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00618","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00618","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00618","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guowei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generation and manipulation of multipole and vortex events in (1+1)-dimensional spacetime"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:17:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:32:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"An event is usually regarded as a zero-dimensional coordinate label in spacetime, rather than as an object with spatial extent, internal structure or topology. Here we show that in nonlinear photonic spacetime crystals, a complete energy-momentum gap ($ωk$-gap) suppresses extended radiation channels, while Kerr self-trapping localizes an optical occurrence in the physical $(x,t)$ plane. Imposing and relaxing symmetry constraints then yields multipole and vortex events with distinct internal structure. Their spacetime action of these states establishes a hierarchy of structural cost, Bogoliubov-de Gennes spectra distinguish phase-winding vortices from real-valued multipoles, and initial-value reconstructions provide an independent robustness check together with a finite preparation window. Using representative optical parameters, we predict that these structured events span transverse scales of 30-60 $μ$m and temporal duration of 85-170 fs. These results suggest that an event can be treated not only as a point label, but also as a localized wave object with controllable internal degrees of freedom, offering a route to wave control in time-varying nonlinear media."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00618","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:29Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00617","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00617","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00617","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanning","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yanning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Delu","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Delu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Paisley","name":"Paisley, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qibin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Qibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diagnosing Under-Development of Irreversible Processes in Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:16:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:32:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many physical attributes are \\emph{irreversible}: ice melts but does not re-freeze, paper chars but does not un-burn. Do video generators respect this? We show the question is hard to measure, and that what can be measured reliably is \\emph{development} rather than reversal. Metrics of local reversal are null-degenerate: a per-clip violation rate scores $0.50$ on pure noise, and a variance-normalized reversal residual sits at its noise ceiling. What survives null-testing is a two-part protocol: progress (a directional attribute correlation) and a stasis rate. Under this protocol, generated video separates cleanly from real footage, and the gap is human-validated. Across seven text-to-video models, real reference footage advances ($ρ{=}{+}0.40$, $35\\%$ static) while every generator shows near-zero progress and $92$--$100\\%$ stasis; nine annotators rate real footage far above generated ($2.75$ vs.\\ $0.99$ on a $0$--$4$ scale). The reliable finding is \\emph{under-development}: generators barely advance irreversible attributes rather than reversing them. As a complementary mechanism, we show that post-hoc readout guidance is gameable, whereas enforcing monotonicity by construction in a disentangled attribute latent removes the gameable readout, validated in controlled and semi-synthetic settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00617","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:28Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00615","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00615","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00615","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto","familyName":"Alfarano","name":"Alfarano, Alberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pablo","familyName":"Bianucci","name":"Bianucci, Pablo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matilde N.","familyName":"Lal\\'ın","name":"Lal\\'ın, Matilde N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Berend","familyName":"Ringeling","name":"Ringeling, Berend","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Machine learning the arithmetic of Boyd's Mahler measure conjectures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"11R06 (Primary) 11F67, 11-11, 68T05 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:11:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:32:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Boyd conjectured that the Mahler measure of $P_k(x,y)=x+y+\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{y}+k$ for $k$ an integer, is given by $r_kL'(E_k,0)$, where $E_k$ is the elliptic curve associated to the zero locus of $P_k$ and $r_k$ is a rational number. We study various arithmetic properties of $r_k$ using a dataset containing the first $250{,}000$ values of $k$, combining large-scale statistical analysis assisted by Claude with transformer-based experiments carried out using Axolver.\n We recover Boyd's observation that, apart from a few exceptions, $r_k$ is the reciprocal of an integer. The size of this integer is governed by the conductor of the elliptic curve. Moreover, its $p$-adic valuations display markedly different behavior according to the prime. For $p\\geq 5$, the probability of $v_p(r_k)=-m$ for $m\\geq 1$ appears to be $p^{-m}$. For the primes $2$ and $3$, however, we find additional arithmetic structure involving congruence conditions on $k$ and the primes of bad reduction of $E_k$. Although the neural networks do not predict $r_k$ exactly, they recover significant information about its magnitude and valuations. In particular, the experiments at the prime $2$ suggest arithmetic structure beyond the explicit predictor obtained from our statistical analysis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"36 pages, 19 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00615","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:25Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00614","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00614","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00614","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long Duc","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Long Duc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuyen","familyName":"Van Kieu","name":"Van Kieu, Tuyen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khanh","familyName":"Van To","name":"Van To, Khanh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compact SAT and MaxSAT Encodings for Business-to-Business Meeting Scheduling with Idle-Time Balancing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:09:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:32:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Business-to-business meeting scheduling assigns requested pairwise meetings to time slots and locations under availability, capacity, conflict, and precedence constraints. The published Boolean formulation retains meeting-slot assignments that propagation can eliminate and encodes precedence relations with pairwise clauses. We present compact SAT and MaxSAT encodings based on solution-preserving domain filtering, variables shared at selected precedence boundaries, and an objective that minimizes the range of participants' internal idle-slot totals. Experiments on 126 official and 100 higher-density derived instances examine domain filtering, precedence representation, transitive relations, and optimization method. Compared with an adapted published MaxSAT formulation using the same objective, the proposed encoding reduces the median clause count by 40.3% and median peak memory usage by 55.9%. Domain filtering alone reduces assignment variables by 24.1% and clauses by 16.2%. Sharing variables at selected precedence boundaries reduces clauses by 0.5%-1.0% on official precedence instances and by up to 5.5% at the highest derived density. The idle-time measure distinguishes one-slot interruptions from longer waits, while aggregate idle time provides a complementary measure. Compared with Gurobi, a leading commercial solver, all three SAT and MaxSAT methods solve every official instance with lower median total times."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00614","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:23Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00612","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00612","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00612","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehran","familyName":"Rahimi","name":"Rahimi, Mehran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ulrich modules : decomposition and relation with rings having minimal multiplicity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Commutative Algebra (math.AC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:07:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:32:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Some basic properties of Ulrich modules are studied and it is proved that Ulrich modules in Cohen-Macaulay local rings are decomposable. As a result, some characterizations proved for rings having minimal multiplicity, and some properties for ideals which are Ulrich as module is discussed."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"All comments are very welcome!"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00612","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:21Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00611","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00611","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00611","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arkadiy I.","familyName":"Syamtomov","name":"Syamtomov, Arkadiy I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Complex chromoelectric polarizability of a heavy-quarkonium resonance: pole definition and channel-complete pNRQCD matching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:03:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:32:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For a stable compact quarkonium, chromoelectric polarizability is generated by two E1 transitions through virtual color-octet states. An unstable quarkonium is instead defined by an isolated complex pole. We define its polarizability by the quadratic displacement of that pole. Combining the standard pole-residue definition of resonance properties with pNRQCD matching shows that the complete two-field vertex is normalized by the energy derivative of the full inverse propagator. Its numerator contains octet E1-E1 propagation, the field dependence of decay channels, and local hard matching terms, without double counting. The stable-state result is recovered with its standard sign and color factor. A minimal single-threshold model calibrated to the Psi(3770) pole gives, within its specified field convention, the residue factor 0.800-0.336i. This illustrates a potentially sizable normalization effect but is not a model-independent pole observable. The absolute polarizability still requires quarkonium and channel response inputs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00611","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:19Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00610","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00610","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00610","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuzhi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongjun","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Rongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shengyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huachi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Huachi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Chuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhicong","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Zhicong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Slides2MindMap: Reconstructing Cognitively Efficient Knowledge Hierarchies from Lecture Slides"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:59:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:32:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generating mind maps from lecture slides can help learners efficiently assimilate fragmented knowledge, promising substantial benefits for intelligent education. However, dedicated automatic generation and evaluation frameworks remain underexplored and challenging, requiring a global-local knowledge focus balance and handling large-scale, heterogeneous slides. We formulate the Slides2MindMap task, which aims to reconstruct cognitively efficient knowledge hierarchies from a course's slide deck collection. For systematic evaluation, we introduce S2M-Bench, a benchmark comprising 12,774 slide pages with expert-annotated mind maps spanning 24 university courses. S2M-Bench includes a cognitive-science-grounded evaluation framework that integrates ground-truth-based comparison, structure conformity analysis, and VLM-as-a-Judge. To address this task, we propose AutoMindMap, an agentic framework inspired by the Structure Building Framework. AutoMindMap comprises Skeleton Laying for global scaffold anchoring, Iterative Knowledge Integration augmented by context-aware summarization, and Dual-Stage Refinement with a local-global decoupling mechanism. The framework reconciles local knowledge faithfulness with global coherence, and adapts to slide-specific features. Experiments on S2M-Bench demonstrate that AutoMindMap outperforms baselines and achieves superior robustness across different models and scenarios, underscoring its pedagogical application value."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00610","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:18Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00609","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00609","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00609","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyan","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Kaiyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikai","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Signed Matrix Thinning and Projection Estimation for Integer-Valued Autoregressive Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:58:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:32:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Integer-valued time series are ubiquitous in fields such as finance, economics, and epidemiology. As spatiotemporal data structures in these domains grow increasingly complex and high-dimensional, the matrix integer-valued autoregressive (MINAR) model efficiently captures row-column cross-correlations to reduce dimensionality. However, it fundamentally fails to accommodate negative values, which is a critical flaw for analyzing real-world differenced data or financial tick fluctuations. To bridge this theoretical and practical gap, this paper introduces the Z-MINAR model, a novel matrix autoregressive framework defined on the full integer domain (Z). By pioneering a signed matrix thinning operator and utilizing an extended poisson distribution for the innovations, the Z-MINAR model elegantly handles both positive and negative integers while strictly preserving the crucial topological interactions inherent in matrix data. Furthermore, we employ a projection-based conditional least squares estimation procedure and rigorously establish the model's stationarity, causality, and asymptotic normality. Extensive simulations demonstrate the superior estimation accuracy, robustness, and adaptability of Z-MINAR over existing benchmark models. Finally, an empirical application focusing on crime count variations across different urban regions confirms the model's practical efficacy in uncovering dynamic spatiotemporal dependence structures in Z-valued matrix time series."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00609","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:17Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00608","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00608","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00608","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Viacheslav","familyName":"Barkov","name":"Barkov, Viacheslav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Schmidinger","name":"Schmidinger, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robin","familyName":"Gebbers","name":"Gebbers, Robin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Atzmueller","name":"Atzmueller, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From field-scale to large-scale spectral libraries: Tabular foundation models in soil spectroscopy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:56:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:32:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visible and near-infrared (vis-NIR) and mid-infrared (MIR) spectroscopy enable rapid, cost-effective prediction of soil properties. Yet, translating high-dimensional, highly collinear spectra into accurate soil property predictions remains challenging, particularly when employing machine learning. We systematically investigated regression models and dimensionality reduction approaches for spectroscopic modeling across 85 regression tasks from open benchmark datasets in pedometrics spanning field-scale digital soil mapping and a global soil spectral library. We compared an in-context learning tabular foundation model (TabPFN), a convolutional neural network (CNN), rule-based regression (Cubist), Random Forest, and partial least squares regression (PLSR) using full spectra as well as features derived from principal component analysis (PCA) and partial least squares (PLS) latent variables. TabPFN consistently delivered the best overall performance across scales, including large spectral library tasks with tens of thousands of soil samples. Notably, TabPFN applied directly to full spectra already surpassed all classical baselines, showing that explicit dimensionality reduction is not strictly required for strong performance. Further improvements were achieved through PLS, which proved to be an effective dimensionality reduction strategy for all models. Combining PLS latent variables with TabPFN yielded the best predictions overall. Our findings provide evidence-based guidance for spectroscopic calibration model selection across operational scales, demonstrating that the long-standing advantages of PLSR and modern tabular foundation models complement each other in chemometrics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00608","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:15Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00607","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00607","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00607","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D.","familyName":"Kliukin","name":"Kliukin, D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"V.","familyName":"Barbé","name":"Barbé, V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T. Klijn","familyName":"Velderman","name":"Velderman, T. Klijn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. B. E.","familyName":"Schokking","name":"Schokking, J. B. E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J.","familyName":"John","name":"John, J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K. S. E.","familyName":"Eikema","name":"Eikema, K. S. E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. C. J.","familyName":"Koelemeij","name":"Koelemeij, J. C. J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. -Ph.","familyName":"Karr","name":"Karr, J. -Ph.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spectroscopy of the Hyperfine Structure of HD$^+$ in Rotationally Excited States with 10-ppb Uncertainty"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Atomic Physics (physics.atom-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:56:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:32:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We report the first measurement of the total hyperfine interval in the manifold of the ($v=0,L=3$) rovibrational state of HD$^+$ using microwave spectroscopy of HD$^+$ ions in a linear Paul trap. To overcome the low (0.2%) occupancy of the quantum states involved, we employ a novel technique that combines intermittent Majorana depolarization of the HD$^+$ ensemble with redistribution of rotational-state population by blackbody radiation to effectively amplify the occupancy by a factor of 25. This enables the observation of a single field-insensitive magnetic subcomponent of the hyperfine transition, leading to a measured total hyperfine interval of ${1\\,050\\,804.511}\\pm 0.011\\,$kHz with an unprecedented relative uncertainty of 10 parts per billion. This differs from the theoretically predicted value ${1\\,050\\,802.78}\\pm 0.89\\,$kHz by 1.9$σ$. We show that our experimental value is consistent with the recently measured hyperfine structure of the ($v=0,L=0$) rovibrational state of HD$^+$ [C.M. König et al., High-precision Penning trap spectroscopy of the ground state spin structure of HD$^+$, Phys. Rev. Lett. $\\mathbf{136}$,143002 (2026)], for which a similar deviation from theory was found. Our work may help resolve puzzling discrepancies between the theoretical and experimental hyperfine structure observed previously in optical rovibrational spectra of HD$^+$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 3 figures. Supplemental Materials file available upon request to the corresponding author"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00607","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:14Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00606","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00606","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00606","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingwei","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Mingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruochong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ruochong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yadong","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Yadong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yubing","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yubing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey I.","familyName":"Bozhevolnyi","name":"Bozhevolnyi, Sergey I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Electro-Optic Active Metasurfaces for High-Speed Photonic Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:49:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:32:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Metasurfaces are artificially engineered ultrathin nanostructured surfaces, capable of flexibly manipulating light-matter interactions on compact platforms, and thereby of great significance for a wide range of applications within modern optics and photonics, including communications, computing, sensing, and quantum technologies. However, the inherently static nature of conventional metasurfaces severely limits their functionalities and thus range of possible applications. Benefiting from integration of the metasurface platform for shaping optical wavefronts with ultrafast electro-optic (EO) materials, active EO metasurfaces have emerged as a frontier research direction targeting advanced photonic devices. This paper systematically reviews the latest progress in this field, featuring a comprehensive comparison of performances and application scenarios of mainstream EO materials such as lithium niobate, barium titanate and organic EO polymers. Modulation mechanisms based on the Pockels and Kerr effects along with the corresponding active metasurface implementations are summarized. Furthermore, improvements in modulation efficiency enabled by advantageously exploiting resonant structural designs and associated phenomena, including Fabry-Perot resonances, Mie resonances, surface plasmon polaritons, quasi-bound states in the continuum, surface lattice resonances, and guided-mode resonances, are presented and summerized in detail. Current challenges related to metasurface design, nanofabrication, performance and heterogeneous integration are also discussed. Finally, future research directions are outlined, highlighting interdisciplinary developments, novel material engineering, and AI-assisted design as key pathways to enable practical use of active EO metasurfaces in modern optics and photonics, including quantum information technologies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"61 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00606","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:12Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00605","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00605","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00605","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhong","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Zhenhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanqing","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Hanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yatao","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Yatao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongcheng","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Rongcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liuyue","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Liuyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davide","familyName":"Modolo","name":"Modolo, Davide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daoyi","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Daoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dacheng","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Dacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Escaping Confidence Trap: Evolutionary Decoding for Mathematical Reasoning in Diffusion LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:48:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:32:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion large language models (dLLMs) have emerged as a promising alternative to autoregressive LLMs, offering efficient generation through block-wise progressive unmasking. However, their strong general-purpose performance does not necessarily translate into reliable mathematical reasoning, where correctness depends on preserving coherent numerical-symbolic reasoning trajectories. In this work, we analyze the decoding trajectories of LLaDA 2.0 and identify a recurring diffusion confidence trap: local token confidence can become misaligned with global reasoning correctness during progressive block decoding. Our analysis reveals two representative failure regimes: sampling-sensitive failures, where correct paths exist but are unstable, and sampling-consistent failures, where repeated sampling converges to repetitive high-confidence but incorrect continuations. Motivated by this observation, we propose Evolutionary Decoding, a training-free test-time scaling framework that views diffusion decoding as an evolutionary process over candidate reasoning states. The framework combines step-wise selection, which preserves useful numerical-symbolic signals and suppresses repetitive patterns, with block-wise mutation, which introduces structured alternatives to escape incorrect high-confidence basins. Experiments on multiple benchmarks show that Evolutionary Decoding improves LLaDA 2.0 over confidence-based decoding, leading to more reliable mathematical reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00605","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:11Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00604","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00604","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00604","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Lussardi","name":"Lussardi, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Morandotti","name":"Morandotti, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federico","familyName":"Stra","name":"Stra, Federico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Minimizers of one-dimensional regularization problems with linear and nonlinear total variation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Classical Analysis and ODEs (math.CA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"49K05, 26A45, 49J05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:43:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:31:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A study of the minimizers of one-dimensional Rudin-Osher-Fatemi-type functionals with linear or nonlinear total variation and fidelity term is undertaken. Conditions on the input datum and the parameters of the functional are found that force the input itself to be the minimizer or not. In the linear setting the discriminating conditions on the parameters are complementary highlighting the sharpness of the results. The nonlinear setting is substantially different: while the non-minimality of the datum is treated in analogy with the linear case, for the minimality of the input only partial answers are found. The results in the nonlinear setting hinge on auxiliary constrained or penalized minimization problems investigating the behavior of optimal transitions with prescribed height. Additionally, they are complemented by some numerical examples."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"48 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00604","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:10Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00603","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00603","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00603","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanh Trung","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Thanh Trung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minh-Binh","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Minh-Binh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spectral Algorithms for 3-Wave Kinetic and $C_{12}$ Quantum Boltzmann Equations with General Resonance Manifolds in $\\mathbb{R}^d$"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:40:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:31:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Following recent developments in numerical schemes for 3-wave kinetic equations [2, 7, 42, 44, 43], we develop spectral algorithms for multidimensional 3-wave kinetic equations and $C_{12}$ quantum Boltzmann equations with general polynomial dispersion relations. The principal numerical difficulty arises from the resonance constraint, supported on a nonlinear manifold in wave-vector space. We approximate the Dirac distribution by a truncated Fourier representation and derive two spectral discretizations of the collision operator. The first is a direct spectral method with complexity $\\mathcal{O}\\big(L(2N)^{3d}\\big)$, while the second exploits multidimensional FFTs to reduce the complexity to $\\mathcal{O}\\big(L(2N)^{2d}\\log(2N)\\big)$. Numerical tests show excellent agreement between the two methods, with the fast algorithm providing substantial computational savings. To suppress unresolved high-frequency modes, we combine the classical $2/3$-rule with exponential spectral filtering. Simulations in two and three dimensions capture the gain--loss dynamics of the $C_{12}$ quantum Boltzmann equation for both rapidly and algebraically decaying initial data. For the 3-wave kinetic equation, the computations exhibit strong oscillations and rapid spectral broadening, providing numerical evidence of an apparent energy cascade toward high frequencies. The results also show that the dispersion relation and spatial dimension strongly influence the transient resonant dynamics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00603","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:08Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00602","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00602","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00602","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shrey","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Shrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gowri","familyName":"Asaithambi","name":"Asaithambi, Gowri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Venkatesan","familyName":"Kanagaraj","name":"Kanagaraj, Venkatesan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Treiber","name":"Treiber, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ostap","familyName":"Okhrin","name":"Okhrin, Ostap","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harish Babu","familyName":"Kumara","name":"Kumara, Harish Babu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Lanes: Traffic Flow Dynamics in Disordered Conditions Based on High-Resolution Trajectory Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Physics and Society (physics.soc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:38:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:31:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Disordered traffic flow is characterized by weak or non-existent lane discipline in the presence of strong vehicle heterogeneity and continuous lateral interactions, challenging traditional lane-based modeling assumptions. This study presents an empirical study of macroscopic and microscopic aspects of disordered traffic using high-resolution UAV trajectory data collected on an urban arterial. A two-dimensional extension of Edie's framework is applied to quantify aggregate traffic variables and produce a two-dimensional fundamental diagram, revealing that traffic states cannot be adequately represented using one-dimensional formulations and highlighting the persistent role of lateral redistribution. The propagation of congestion is estimated directly from the spatiotemporal speed fields, demonstrating the emergence of coherent stop-and-go waves and showing a similar dynamics as conventional lane-based flow, in spite of the heterogeneous vehicle interactions. At the microscopic level, steady-state follower-leader identification is used to examine desired time gaps and minimum lateral spacing, vehicle dimension distributions, and kinematic characteristics, revealing pronounced inter-class heterogeneity that explains disordered traffic behavior. The study provides an empirical framework linking vehicle-level interactions and aggregate traffic dynamics and establishes a data-driven basis for the calibration and validation of traffic models for disordered mixed traffic systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages; submitted to Transp. Research Part B"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00602","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:07Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00601","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00601","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00601","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanxiang","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Shanxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Symplectic Barnes-Wall GKP Codes: Deterministic $O(N \\log^2 N)$ Decoding and Logarithmic Rate Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:36:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:31:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We construct an explicit symplectic realization of the Barnes-Wall lattice that yields a family of multimode Gottesman-Kitaev-Preskill (GKP) codes with encoding rate $R = \\frac{1}{2}\\log_2 N$ and a deterministic $O(N\\log^2 N)$ bounded-distance decoder. The recursive generator $G_{m+1} = \\bigl(\\begin{smallmatrix} G_m \u0026amp; 0 \\\\ G_m \u0026amp; R_m G_m \\end{smallmatrix}\\bigr)$ with $R_m = I + Ω$ simultaneously guarantees symplectic integrality for valid quantum stabilizers and preserves the exact Barnes-Wall decoding structure through a chain of isometric isomorphisms. The code distance is constant at $Δ^2 = 1$ (in units of $2π$), representing an explicit distance--rate tradeoff in which logarithmic encoding efficiency is achieved at the cost of non-scaling protection. This construction provides a deterministic, space-efficient paradigm for GKP error correction in platforms supporting non-local modular connectivity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00601","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:06Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00599","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00599","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00599","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luciano Jacopo","familyName":"D'Onofrio","name":"D'Onofrio, Luciano Jacopo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria Teresa","familyName":"Mercaldo","name":"Mercaldo, Maria Teresa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mario","familyName":"Cuoco","name":"Cuoco, Mario","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carmine","familyName":"Ortix","name":"Ortix, Carmine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spin selectivity induced by non-collinear spins in Rashba wires"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Other Condensed Matter (cond-mat.other)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applied Physics (physics.app-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:34:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:31:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We report a previously overlooked general mechanism to obtain highly efficient spin selectivity in conventional time-reversal symmetric one-dimensional systems without invoking phase decoherence. We reveal that Rashba quantum wires featuring non-collinear spin states at the Fermi level inherently possess spin-selective transport properties. We show that this spin noncollinearity can be systematically designed and engineered by introducing an additional pseudospin degree of freedom - such as valley, sublattice, or orbital angular momentum - into spin-orbit coupled systems. By applying this framework to multi-subband semiconducting quantum wires and oxide nanowires, we establish a generalized route toward quantum-coherent spin selectivity up to 10 %. Our findings offer practical design principles for spin-selective transport devices."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages (with supplemental material), 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00599","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:03Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00598","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00598","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00598","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibo","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Haibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongxin","familyName":"Huan","name":"Huan, Hongxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenwei","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Chenwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xian","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EmergencyBias: Bias in Text-to-Image Models under Emergency Scenarios"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:28:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:31:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Bias in Text-to-Image (T2I) generation has become an important problem in multimedia content creation and communication. However, existing studies have primarily focused on relatively static and explicit forms of bias, such as disparities in the representation of gender, race, and geo-cultural attributes. Less attention has been paid to behavioral bias in how different groups are portrayed acting, reacting, and occupying social roles. Emergency scenarios provide a revealing setting for studying such bias because they require models to depict not only who is present, but also who is at risk, who intervenes, and how responsibility is allocated. In this paper, we define EmergencyBias, a form of bias in T2I generation under emergency scenarios that includes both demographic bias and behavioral bias. We construct an evaluation framework to systematically study EmergencyBias across seven leading T2I models, six representative emergency scenarios, and three demographic dimensions. Our experimental results reveal three main findings. First, under blank prompts without demographic specification, T2I models exhibit clear demographic bias in emergency scenarios, reflected in the distributions of portrayed individuals across gender, age, and skin tone. Second, under controlled prompts, behavioral bias in emergency responses remains systematically associated with demographic variation, with particularly pronounced disparities along gender and substantial differences across models. Third, we introduce ActionAlign, a lightweight prompt-embedding calibration method that outperforms a representative prompt-based baseline in reducing behavioral disparities while largely preserving image quality. Overall, our work identifies emergency scenarios as an important setting for bias evaluation in T2I models and offers a practical direction toward fairer visual generation in socially consequential contexts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 5 figures, 7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00598","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:02Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00597","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00597","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00597","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reza","familyName":"Shaebani","name":"Shaebani, Reza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hashem","familyName":"Fatemi","name":"Fatemi, Hashem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamidreza","familyName":"Khalilian","name":"Khalilian, Hamidreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jalal","familyName":"Sarabadani","name":"Sarabadani, Jalal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Confinement-Induced Optimization of Fluctuation-Induced Forces in Active Fluids"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Soft Condensed Matter (cond-mat.soft)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:26:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:31:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Active matter generates nonequilibrium fluctuations that mediate effective interactions between immersed objects. While fluctuation-induced (FI) forces in active fluids depend on activity, density, and geometry, their dependence on confinement remains poorly understood. We study FI forces between fixed intruders in two-dimensional active fluids composed of self-propelled circular or rodlike particles using Langevin dynamics simulations. We find that the FI force exhibits a pronounced nonmonotonic dependence on intruder separation, reaching a maximum at an optimal gap size well beyond the depletion regime, in contrast to the commonly assumed monotonic decay. This optimal confinement is robust across parameters and is more pronounced for elongated particles. The effect arises from a confinement-controlled balance between particle transport and crowding: narrow gaps hinder exchange between inner and outer regions, whereas large separations effectively decouple the intruders. At intermediate distances, enhanced crowding around the intruders generates maximal collision-rate asymmetries, leading to the strongest effective interactions. These results identify confinement geometry as a key control parameter for FI forces in active matter."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00597","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:43:00Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00596","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00596","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00596","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hugo","familyName":"Levy-Falk","name":"Levy-Falk, Hugo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniele","familyName":"De Bernardis","name":"De Bernardis, Daniele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elena","familyName":"Fanella","name":"Fanella, Elena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Louise","familyName":"Morlaës","name":"Morlaës, Louise","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Costanza","familyName":"Toninelli","name":"Toninelli, Costanza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Breakdown of the optical saturation regime in molecular single-photon emitters"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:25:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:31:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Solid-state single organic molecules, such as dibenzoterrylene (DBT) in organic matrices, are prominent deterministic single-photon sources, usually modeled as effective two-level systems (TLS). We show that single DBT molecules in anthracene nanocrystals, under strong continuous-wave driving, depart from this picture: instead of the expected saturation, fluorescence is strongly suppressed at high resonant pump power, while the linewidth broadens beyond the TLS prediction -- an \\emph{anomalous saturation} regime. Comparing coherent and incoherent excitation and independently calibrating temperature via phonon-induced dephasing rules out laser-induced heating and intersystem crossing. Instead, a model of intensity-dependent excited-state absorption (ESA) toward a short-lived dark state quantitatively reproduces both the fluorescence suppression and the linewidth broadening. We further show that matrix quality mitigates this quenching, and that pulsed excitation schemes are strategic toward full population inversion, with direct implications for quantum nanophotonics and molecular optomechanics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00596","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:59Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:43:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:43:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00595","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00595","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00595","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reza","familyName":"Ansari","name":"Ansari, Reza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maciej","familyName":"Wielgosz","name":"Wielgosz, Maciej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Time-Multiplexed Spiking Neural Network Accelerator with Pipelined Readout for FPGA Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:21:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:31:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spiking Neural Networks (SNNs) provide a power-efficient neuromorphic alternative to traditional artificial neural networks by processing information through discrete temporal events. This paper presents the design and Field-Programmable Gate Array (FPGA) implementation of an inference-only SNN accelerator optimized for MNIST digit classification. To address the physical routing constraints and timing bottlenecks inherent in low-cost devices, we propose an optimized hardware microarchitecture featuring a time-multiplexed 1-bit spike-feeding mechanism governed by a finite state machine (FSM), localized distributed memory for weight storage, and an integer-based Leaky Integrate-and-Fire (LIF) neuron model with register widths selected to prevent overflow. In addition, a multi-cycle pipelined argmax and tie-breaker readout module eliminates the dominant combinational critical path. Implemented on an entry-level AMD Artix-7 FPGA (XC7A200T) using a 784-64-10 network topology, the proposed pipelined architecture increases the maximum operating frequency (Fmax) from 13.3 MHz to 167 MHz. Hardware evaluation demonstrates a sequential processing latency of 82 μs per image, enabling a 1,000-sample VHDL simulation batch to be completed in 0.082 s. Vivado post-implementation vector-based power analysis estimates the total on-chip power consumption at 0.336 W and the energy efficiency at approximately 36,300 samples per joule. These results demonstrate that the proposed microarchitecture provides a resource-efficient solution for real-time neuromorphic edge inference, provided that the network size remains within the practical limits of time-multiplexed execution."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00595","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:57Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00594","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00594","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00594","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roberto","familyName":"Bruno","name":"Bruno, Roberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ugo","familyName":"Vaccaro","name":"Vaccaro, Ugo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Information Theoretic Treatment of Yager's Probability Distribution Negation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:16:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:31:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s00500-026-11413-9","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the seminal paper (Yager 2015), Yager defined the negation of a probability distribution $\\mathbf{p}=(p_1,\\dots,p_n)$, as the distribution $\\overline{\\mathbf{p}} = (\\overline{p}_1,\\dots,\\overline{p}_n)$, where $\\overline{p}_i = ({1-p_i})/({n-1}),$ for $ i=1, \\ldots , n.$ In this paper, we present a comprehensive information-theoretic analysis of Yager's negation and its generalizations. Using tools from information theory and majorization theory, we unify, extend, and strengthen a number of previously known properties of Yager's negation within a common framework. Overall, our results offer strong theoretical justification for Yager's negation as the most natural and principled definition of probability distribution negation under various information theoretic criteria."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in Soft Computing (Section: Foundation, Algebraic, and Analytical Methods)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00594","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:56Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00593","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00593","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00593","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anand","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Anand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Puli","familyName":"Saikiran","name":"Saikiran, Puli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vineet","familyName":"Dawara","name":"Dawara, Vineet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koushik","familyName":"Viswanathan","name":"Viswanathan, Koushik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uncertainty-guided active learning for surrogate prediction of stream-finishing wear fields"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Analysis, Statistics and Probability (physics.data-an)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:12:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:31:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In stream finishing, the wear experienced by a workpiece depends strongly on its orientation within the rotating abrasive media. Determining suitable orientations to achieve uniform wear requires evaluating the wear-rate field over all feasible orientations. Although the discrete element method (DEM) accurately resolves particle interactions, simulating hundreds of feasible orientations for a new geometry is computationally expensive. We present an uncertainty-guided surrogate framework that predicts, directly from geometry, the three fields governing erosion: per-triangle normal impact velocity, tangential impact velocity, and particle impact flux. These fields are combined through the Finnie wear model to reconstruct the wear-rate distribution. The surrogate employs a deep ensemble whose disagreement estimates epistemic uncertainty, enabling an active-learning strategy that selectively performs DEM simulations for the most uncertain orientations. Trained using only $13\\%$ of the $696$ feasible orientations, the surrogate achieves Spearman rank correlations of $0.93$, $0.89$, and $0.93$ for the normal impact velocity, tangential impact velocity, and particle impact flux, respectively. Moreover, the predicted uncertainty is well calibrated, reliably anticipating prediction error and the fidelity of the reconstructed wear field, which matches DEM with a Spearman rank correlation of up to $0.97$ for low-uncertainty orientations and degrades in a controlled manner as uncertainty increases."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00593","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:55Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00592","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00592","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00592","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mian","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Mian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro","familyName":"Navas","name":"Navas, Pedro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susana","familyName":"López-Querol","name":"López-Querol, Susana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Study of Landslides through a Stabilised Semi-Implicit Material Point Method"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T11:12:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:31:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.62512/conf.ukacm2024.018","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this research, a new semi-implicit two-phase double-point material point method is proposed, in which the soil and water phases are modelled using two distinct sets of material points, both being stabilised with a novel approach. The Nor-Sand constitutive model is implemented to simulate more realistic soil behaviour. Some landslide numerical examples are presented to investigate the performance of the proposed method and highlight the importance of using the double-point approach. The formulation with two sets of material points shows significantly different but more reliable results in the cases of landslides, compared with the conventional single-point approach. Furthermore, this research shows that the additional computational cost given by the additional water material points is acceptable. Therefore, it is recommended to use two sets of material points for some large deformation geotechnical problems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00592","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:53Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00590","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00590","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00590","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis A.","familyName":"Anchordoqui","name":"Anchordoqui, Luis A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alek","familyName":"Bedroya","name":"Bedroya, Alek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dieter","familyName":"Lüst","name":"Lüst, Dieter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Houri-Christina","familyName":"Tarazi","name":"Tarazi, Houri-Christina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"IR Black Hole Instabilities Trigger Species-Scale Particle Production"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:58:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:31:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a novel UV-IR mechanism in quantum gravity in which black hole instabilities act as a bridge to the most ultraviolet sector of the theory. Specifically, we argue that when black holes reach a critical temperature associated with a light tower of states, they undergo a phase transition that sources particles near the species scale, a threshold beyond which any effective field theory of gravity fails. Using the existing numerical results, we show that production of such particles through Hawking radiation is always subdominant. We also extend our investigation to a large family of dyonic and dilatonic black holes and show how the conclusion depends on the origin of the gauge symmetry, its dilatonic coupling, and the charge under that gauge symmetry."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00590","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:50Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00589","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00589","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00589","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federica","familyName":"Giacchino","name":"Giacchino, Federica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Conrado A.","familyName":"Torres","name":"Torres, Conrado A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giorgio","familyName":"Galanti","name":"Galanti, Giorgio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bradley J.","familyName":"Kavanagh","name":"Kavanagh, Bradley J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria A.","familyName":"Pérez-García","name":"Pérez-García, Maria A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose M.","familyName":"Diego","name":"Diego, Jose M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gamma Rays from ALP-Photon Conversion and Inverse Compton Reprocessing in Neutron Star Magnetospheres"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:57:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:31:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Exploring axion-like particle (ALP) signatures from neutron stars (NSs) in the \\emph{Fermi}-LAT energy range remains largely unexplored. Neutron stars with exceptionally strong magnetic fields, such as magnetars and pulsars with magnetar-like magnetic fields, provide particularly promising environments for ALP--photon conversion. Magnetars are characterized by surface magnetic fields as large as $B_0\\sim(10^{14}$--$10^{15})\\,\\mathrm{G}$; however, despite their extreme magnetic fields, no steady magnetar emission has been firmly detected in the \\emph{Fermi}-LAT energy range, with high-energy activity generally associated with rare flaring episodes.\n In this work, we investigate ALP production in the interiors of different classes of NSs and the subsequent conversion of ALPs into photons in their magnetospheres. The ALP emissivity is determined by the stellar density and temperature $T$, while the conversion probability is enhanced by the strong magnetic fields surrounding the star. We further account for photon propagation through the Galactic magnetic field, which can provide an additional contribution to the observable photon flux. We investigate the resulting gamma-ray signatures and assess whether ALP-induced emission from NS magnetospheres could be detectable at energies $E\\gtrsim100,\\mathrm{MeV}$ in the \\emph{Fermi}-LAT band. In addition, we consider if the reprocessing of the magnetospheric photons through inverse Compton scattering can shift part of the emission to higher energies and provide an additional observational signature. We use the resulting fluxes to derive constraints from existing gamma-ray observations and to estimate the sensitivity of future MeV--GeV observations, taking COSI as a representative example."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 24 figures, 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00589","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:49Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00588","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00588","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00588","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaoyue","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chaoyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunfei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yunfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Si","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Si","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InstancePin: Instance-Addressable Layout-to-Image Diffusion via Coordinate Pinning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:54:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:30:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Layout-to-image diffusion models have achieved impressive semantic controllability by conditioning generation on category-level segmentation maps. However, such category-aligned control is not necessarily instance-addressable: multiple nearby objects from the same category are often treated as a shared semantic region, leading to ambiguous boundaries, averaged appearances, and feature confusion among instances. This limitation is particularly evident in urban scene synthesis, where small and crowded pedestrians or vehicles require fine-grained instance separation while preserving global scene consistency. In this paper, we propose InstancePin, an instance-addressable layout-to-image diffusion framework that pins each object instance with an explicit coordinate anchor. Instead of directly injecting instance masks into the pretrained backbone, InstancePin introduces an independent instance-aware adapter to preserve the category-level generation prior while learning instance-specific spatial control. For each instance, its center coordinate is encoded with Fourier features and projected into a coordinate token, which serves as a spatial anchor queried by latent image features through coordinate pinning attention. To make these anchors spatially meaningful, we further supervise the coordinate attention maps with instance regions, encouraging each coordinate token to activate its corresponding object area. Finally, an instance-mask guided fusion module routes pretrained backbone features to non-instance regions and adapter features to instance regions, enabling local instance refinement without sacrificing global semantic fidelity. Extensive experiments on Cityscapes demonstrate that InstancePin mitigates instance entanglement in dense layouts and improves both image fidelity and semantic consistency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to PRCV 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00588","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:48Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00586","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00586","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00586","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingya Alexa","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Mingya Alexa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Da","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Da","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lovre Antonio","familyName":"Budimir","name":"Budimir, Lovre Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivana","familyName":"Matovinovic","name":"Matovinovic, Ivana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sven","familyName":"Loncaric","name":"Loncaric, Sven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Myeong Jin","familyName":"Ju","name":"Ju, Myeong Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yukun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siegfried K.","familyName":"Wagner","name":"Wagner, Siegfried K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pearse A.","familyName":"Keane","name":"Keane, Pearse A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marinko V.","familyName":"Sarunic","name":"Sarunic, Marinko V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Representation Transfer of Foundation Models for Ultra-Widefield Retinal Imaging"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:53:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:30:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite the widespread adoption of foundation models as feature extractors for medical imaging, relatively little is understood about how different pretraining strategies influence the transferability of learned representations to weakly supervised ophthalmic imaging tasks. We investigate this question in ultra-widefield (UWF) retinal imaging by evaluating foundation model representations within a patch-based multiple instance learning (MIL) framework for disease classification on UWF images. We compare Vision Transformer encoders pretrained with supervised, Masked Autoencoder (MAE), and self-distillation objectives, while keeping the downstream aggregation architecture unchanged. Within a controlled comparison of ViT-B encoders pretrained on ImageNet-1k, the choice of pretraining objective substantially influenced frozen representation transfer, with supervised and self-distillation-based models outperforming MAE. A contemporary DINOv3 model pretrained at a larger scale achieved the strongest overall performance, with a quadratic weighted kappa of 0.863 for five-class diabetic retinopathy grading, comparable with DINOv1. Attention analysis further revealed distinct patch-aggregation behaviours associated with the different pretrained representations, while partial fine-tuning substantially reduced the performance gap for MAE. These findings suggest that pretraining strategy influences both representation transferability and the subsequent aggregation of patch-level evidence within MIL, resulting in differences in downstream classification performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00586","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:45Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00585","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00585","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00585","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Randhir","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Randhir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Verification Without Sufficiency: Per-Chunk Filtering Fails on Multi-Hop RAG, and Decomposition Repairs It"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; H.3.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:52:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:30:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Verification for retrieval-augmented generation usually scores each retrieved chunk and drops the ones that fail. We show this cannot work for multi-hop questions, and show what does. Per-chunk scoring assumes one chunk is a sufficient premise for the answer. Multi-hop questions are built so that none is, and the paragraph carrying the answer is the one the question does not name. Entailment scoring reaches 0.643, 0.523 and 0.560 AUC on HotpotQA, 2WikiMultihopQA and MuSiQue, against 0.951 on single-hop SQuAD. Seven controls rule out model capacity, premise length, hypothesis template, decision threshold, retriever, answer-matching criterion and prompt. End to end across three datasets, three generator sizes and two prompts, per-chunk gating is significantly worse than not filtering at all in every cell, and its penalty grows with generator capability. The repair is to condition verification on the decomposed sub-question rather than the original query. Using MuSiQue's gold decomposition, entailment on a later hop rises from 0.546, which is chance, to 0.840, a paired lift of +0.355 with a bootstrap interval of [0.331, 0.382]. An off-the-shelf Qwen2.5-7B decomposer, given the question and the top retrieved paragraph, reaches 0.637 and captures 31% of that ceiling; decomposing without retrieval reaches 0.533, below the original question. Iterative retrieval systems already produce such decompositions and discard them before verifying."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 5 figures, 8 tables, 1 algorithm. Code, per-question traces and analysis scripts: https://github.com/iamhero2709/verification-without-sufficiency"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00585","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:43Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00584","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00584","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00584","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingtong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jingtong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xue","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"ShaoGuo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, ShaoGuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Minghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Element-Aware Group Learning for E-Commerce Image Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:45:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:30:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in image generation and editing have made prompt quality a key bottleneck for e-commerce creatives. Vision-language models (VLMs) can generate image-editing prompts from product images and metadata, but further improving their prompt-writing capabilities requires post-training with feedback from the generated images. Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) is a natural framework for such outcome-level reward optimization. However, it assigns credit only at the full-prompt level, even though image quality often depends on specific design elements such as composition, background, and the presentation of selling points. Existing fine-grained credit assignment methods typically require step-level supervision or learned critics. To address this, we propose EAGLE-GRPO (Element-Aware Group Learning for E-Commerce Image Generation), which decomposes the group-centered reward over predefined elements. We cast element-level credit assignment as a kernel ridge regression problem and derive a closed-form solution, without additional rollouts or separate credit-assignment models. This yields interpretable per-element advantages and more precise policy updates. Experiments show that EAGLE-GRPO sustains performance gains over more training steps before plateauing and generates prompts that produce higher-quality e-commerce images than competitive VLM prompt-writing baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00584","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:42Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00583","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00583","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00583","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shikhar","familyName":"Shiromani","name":"Shiromani, Shikhar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leo","familyName":"Richter","name":"Richter, Leo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A False Average: Chain-of-Thought Monitors Collapse Where They Are the Only Defense"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:42:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:30:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Chain-of-thought (CoT) monitoring is meant to catch the reward hacks that look clean in the actions and betray themselves only in the reasoning. We show that this is exactly where an adversary who controls the reasoning can defeat it. Rewriting only an agent's reasoning to read as good-faith engineering, while copying every command and output verbatim so the exploit is unchanged, drops a held-out monitor's catch rate on that subset from about 95% to under 11% in one gradient-free shot. A monitor's aggregate accuracy is a false average: dominated by hacks the actions give away, it hides the near-total collapse this rewrite produces on the subset where CoT monitoring is the only signal. The attack transfers across monitor families and agent models, reproduces with live agents, though against a calibrated monitor evasion concentrates in the strongest agent. Trace-only defenses recover it only partially, even one primed on the attack, because the rewrite stays truthful about what happened and lies only about intent; only information from outside the trace helps substantially. A probe on an open-weight surrogate monitor's activations separates the hacks its verdict misses, but a causal control shows this is a detector, not evidence the monitor secretly knows."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00583","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:41Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00582","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00582","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00582","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Lviv Polytechnic National University"],"givenName":"Bohdan","familyName":"Didenko","name":"Didenko, Bohdan","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Writing-System-Level Tokenizer Adaptation for Byte-Level BPE"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:38:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:30:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pretrained byte-level BPE tokenizers can segment underrepresented languages inefficiently. Replacing a tokenizer changes the meaning of nearly every token ID, while vocabulary expansion enlarges the model's embedding and output matrices. We study post-hoc adaptation that keeps the model-vocabulary size fixed and preserves most existing token-to-ID assignments as a construction-time compatibility property. Directly transferring tokens from a language-specific tokenizer does not guarantee derivability through the target BPE merge graph: an inserted entry can conflict with the target's greedy merge ranks. We formalize this failure as the merge ordering problem and introduce BPE-guided insertion, which builds each transferred token through a target-reachable decomposition. Our pipeline uses script-aware row selection to limit collateral fragmentation, reconstructs target-script byte-level prerequisites, and applies guided insertion to maintain merge-graph reachability. On Ukrainian adaptations of Nemotron and GPT-OSS, it reduces token counts by 33.5% and 36.6%, keeps changes on English and the evaluated four-language European aggregate within 0.05%, and retains 78.5%/77.3% of original model-vocabulary rows at the same IDs. Constraint-matched global and frequency-based removal achieve similar Ukrainian compression but increase English/European token counts by 0.7-2.2%; fresh same-size retraining compresses Ukrainian slightly more but retains effectively no same-ID rows and increases English token counts by 7.6-8.6%. The reallocation increases token counts on the evaluated three-language Cyrillic micro-aggregate by 6.7%/10.1%. Structural audits find all 28,134/45,398 inserted BPE nodes reachable under ordinary rank-ordered merging and no retained same-ID model-vocabulary entry newly broken. We release all tokenizers and code."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages. Accepted for poster presentation at the Second Tokenization Workshop (TokShop) at COLM 2026 (non-archival)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00582","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:39Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00581","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00581","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00581","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bogdan","familyName":"Savelyev","name":"Savelyev, Bogdan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Loanword or Switch? The Annotation Boundary, Not the Model, Drives Kazakh-Russian Code-Switching Identification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:38:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:30:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Off-the-shelf LID and letter heuristics over-label Kazakh-Russian social text as mixed: Russian loanwords inside Kazakh look like code-switching under a shared Cyrillic script. We release a document-level gold LID set whose guideline keeps integrated borrowings as Kazakh and reserves mixed for clause-level switches, plus a mixed-only sentiment pool used after LID in a filter-first cascade. On a shared LID test, FastText, Lingua, raw and windowed HeLI, character-trigram NB, and XLM-R range from weak to strong performance. The gap shows the bottleneck is the loanword-vs-switch annotation boundary, not model class alone."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages. Preprint. Submitted to W-NUT 2026. Code and data: https://github.com/naadgob/KazNLP"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00581","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:38Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00580","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00580","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00580","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martina","familyName":"Luskova","name":"Luskova, Martina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nino","familyName":"Buliskeria","name":"Buliskeria, Nino","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Elminejad","name":"Elminejad, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomas","familyName":"Havranek","name":"Havranek, Tomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zuzana","familyName":"Irsova","name":"Irsova, Zuzana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stepan","familyName":"Jurajda","name":"Jurajda, Stepan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marek","familyName":"Kapicka","name":"Kapicka, Marek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Publication bias and p-hacking in the effect of COVID-19 on learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Economics (econ.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:35:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:30:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We revisit a central estimate in the economics of education: the human-capital loss associated with COVID-19 school closures. Estimates of pandemic learning loss may be affected by publication bias, p-hacking, and the mechanical correlation between standardized effect sizes and their standard errors. We conduct a comprehensive multi-method assessment of bias by applying a wide range of correction techniques - including PET-PEESE, three-parameter selection models (3PSM), Robust Bayesian Meta-Analysis (RoBMA), Meta-Analysis Instrumental Variable Estimation (MAIVE), Right-Truncated Meta-Analysis (RTMA), and multi-bias sensitivity analysis. Our preferred specifications, RoBMA and MAIVE, rely on different assumptions yet converge on an effect size of approximately -0.12 SD, equivalent to a learning loss of about 30% of a school year. Although some methods reveal signs of publication bias and selective reporting, these findings do not explain away the central finding: the COVID-19 learning deficit is economically meaningful and statistically robust."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"56 pages, 16 figures, 9 tables. Also circulated as CEPR Discussion Paper 21630 and EconStor Preprint 341461. JEL: I21, I24, I28, C18"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00580","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:36Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00578","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00578","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00578","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mian","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Mian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro","familyName":"Navas","name":"Navas, Pedro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susana","familyName":"López-Querol","name":"López-Querol, Susana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A semi-implicit double-point Material Point Method for both free-surface flow and seepage in deformable porous media"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:32:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:30:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s00366-026-02369-3","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A new semi-implicit, two-phase, double-point formulation of the Material Point Method (MPM) for soil-water interaction with seepage and free-surface flows under large deformation is presented in this paper. The approach advances the water phase implicitly while keeping the soil phase explicit, enabling stable, efficient time integration in problems that involve rapid seepage and strong free-surface motion. The proposed framework models high-Reynolds-number interphase drag through a non-linear Darcy's law implemented for the first time within an incremental fractional step MPM formulation without enlarging the implicit solve. This methodology also enhances the numerical stability for fast flows and wave breaking via a hyperelastic constitutive treatment of slightly compressible viscous water, and mitigates spurious oscillations through a new stabilisation approach for the velocity. Robustness of soil-water interface is achieved by combining nodal-based, free-surface detection, suited for higher-order spline functions with smooth porosity-permeability transitions that avoid constitutive divergence at sharp material boundaries. Validation against laboratory benchmark cases reported in the literature, including pure-water dam break, dam-break seepage through a porous barrier, two granular-collapse tsunami experiments, and a dam-break wave over a movable granular bed, shows accurate and stable free-surface evolution, pressure time histories, seepage fronts, and wave-gauge records. Using an advanced critical-state soil model (NorSand) further improves the reproduction of granular flow kinematics. The results demonstrate that the proposed formulation is a reliable and computationally efficient tool for geotechnical hazards involving intense soil-water coupling, seepage, sediment transport and free water."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This version of the article has been accepted for publication, after peer review but is not the Version of Record and does not reflect post-acceptance improvements, or any corrections. The Version of Record is available online at: https://doi.org/10.1007/s00366-026-02369-3"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00578","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:34Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00575","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00575","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00575","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John M.","familyName":"Campbell","name":"Campbell, John M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The finitude of the fibers of the complementary Bell numbers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"05A18, 11B73","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:29:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:30:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Subbarao and Verma introduced, in 1999, a number of open problems concerning the sequence $(f(n))_{n \\geq 0}$ of complementary Bell numbers, which may be defined via Bell polynomials $B_{n}(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\left\\{ \\begin{smallmatrix} n \\\\ k \\end{smallmatrix} \\right\\} x^k$ so that $f(n) = B_{n}(-1)$. Yang [Electron. J. Combin., 2001] subsequently solved the first two problems from Subbarao and Verma, but the third such problem has remained open, to the best of our knowledge. The first part of this third problem asks whether or not $f(n)$ takes any given value only a finite number of times. We solve this problem in the affirmative, through a combined application of finite difference-based methods, partial Motzkin paths, the completeness of the Tate algebra with respect to the Gauss norm, and Strassmann's theorem."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted for publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00575","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:30Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00574","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00574","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00574","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Ziang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qishen","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Qishen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Munan","familyName":"Ning","name":"Ning, Munan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peizhen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Peizhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Relax Within, Balance Across: Geometry-Guided Load Balancing for Vision-Language Mixture-of-Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:23:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:29:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language MoE batches contain different numbers of image and text tokens. Image resolution, image count, tiling, and prompt length all change this token mix. We call the standard token-level Switch auxiliary loss Std-Aux. Std-Aux balances only the mixed load, so large image and text load errors can cancel at one mix. On our main model, the same trained router shows more than a fivefold change in load imbalance across image resolutions. We hold the image and text load profiles fixed and derive the exact load curve as the token mix varies. The image-text load gap controls sensitivity to the token mix. Physical preprocessing can also change the conditional profiles. The fixed-profile law excludes such changes. To design a remedy, we examine the router input structure. Image and text occupy distinct regions, while visual tokens group strongly by source image. The modality boundary motivates separate image and text terms. The image boundary motivates one equal-weight routing instance per image. ReBA, or Relax Within, Balance Across, implements both choices. Across four split backbones, ReBA lowers load on every reported benchmark input while keeping mean task accuracy comparable to Std-Aux. ReBA also lowers average load over the tested range and worst physical load under resolution and tiling shifts. Code is available at https://github.com/ZiangWu-77/ReBA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, including appendices. Code available at https://github.com/ZiangWu-77/ReBA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00574","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:29Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00572","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00572","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00572","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junchuan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Junchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minh Duc","familyName":"Vu","name":"Vu, Minh Duc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AnyBand: Unified Multi-Bandwidth Speech Extension via Frequency-Aware In-Context Spectral Infilling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:14:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:29:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Bandwidth extension (BWE) aims to recover missing high-frequency content from band-limited speech. Existing methods often formulate BWE as a fixed or predefined bandwidth conversion problem, potentially requiring cutoff-specific models or retraining when the input bandwidth changes. This assumption limits their applicability to practical scenarios where speech may arrive with diverse cutoff frequencies. We propose AnyBand, a unified BWE framework that recasts bandwidth extension as in-context spectral infilling. Motivated by prompt-based zero-shot speech generation, AnyBand conditions high-frequency generation on the observed low-frequency spectrum, using the available band as a frequency-domain prompt that conveys content, speaker, prosodic, and spectral-envelope cues. This formulation enables a single model to perform cutoff-conditioned generation over a continuous range of input bandwidths. AnyBand is trained with missing-band conditional flow matching and an Easy-to-Balanced cutoff curriculum over continuously sampled cutoff frequencies. To better exploit the spectral prompt, we introduce a frequency-aware Diffusion Transformer that models cross-frequency interactions and long-range temporal dependencies, followed by a physically motivated multi-view adversarial refinement stage to enhance spectral realism, envelope coherence, and harmonic consistency. Experiments on multiple datasets and bandwidth settings show that AnyBand consistently improves spectral reconstruction over existing baselines while achieving competitive perceptual quality across both standard and irregular input cutoffs. Audio samples are available."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 11 figures, 7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00572","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:26Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00571","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00571","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00571","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baige","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Baige","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takaharu","familyName":"Yaguchi","name":"Yaguchi, Takaharu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CoSynFlow: Conformal Symplectic Neural Flows for Cross-System Prediction of Dissipative Hamiltonian Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:12:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:29:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learning solution operators for differential equations is a central problem in scientific machine learning. However, many neural operator methods optimize prediction accuracy without explicitly enforcing the geometric structure of the dynamics. Structure-preserving models such as SympNets and Symplectic Neural Flows address this issue for conservative Hamiltonian systems by preserving the symplectic form. In dissipative Hamiltonian systems with conformal symplectic structure, however, the symplectic form evolves according to a conformal factor determined by the dissipation. We propose CoSynFlow, a conformal symplectic neural flow for learning continuous-time solution maps of dissipative Hamiltonian dynamics. CoSynFlow composes symplectic shear maps with explicit conformal scaling, preserving the conformal symplectic structure by construction. By conditioning it on a finite-dimensional Hamiltonian descriptor and the dissipation parameter, a single trained model predicts solution maps for unseen systems without retraining. CoSynFlow keeps the structure error at machine precision, attains the lowest long-horizon error, and admits physics-informed training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00571","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:24Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00570","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00570","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00570","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muchen","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Muchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mechanism of State Transitions for the Vector Kuznetsov-Ma Breathers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Pattern Formation and Solitons (nlin.PS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Exactly Solvable and Integrable Systems (nlin.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:12:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:29:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study two types of mechanisms of state transitions for vector Kuznetsov-Ma breathers (KMBs) in the coupled Fokas-Lenells framework on unequal backgrounds. The amplitude imbalance breaks spectral reflection symmetry and generates richer breather morphologies. In the degenerate sector, KMBs approaching a non-self-conjugate degeneration curve from opposite sides yield different limiting solitons, revealing a discontinuous KMB-to-soliton transition. In the nondegenerate sector, the background plane waves become frequency matched at a special wavenumber, converting the KMB into a single- or two-soliton state. We determine the exact transition threshold and characterize how nearby solutions vary with the parameter. We further investigate special limiting mechanisms arising on the self-conjugate degeneration curve and on the real spectrum at the state-transition wavenumber. Numerical excitation provides further evidence for these results."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00570","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:23Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00568","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00568","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00568","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rachit","familyName":"Verma","name":"Verma, Rachit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Padala","familyName":"Manisha","name":"Manisha, Padala","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujit","familyName":"Gujar","name":"Gujar, Sujit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fairness Auditing: Lower Bounds on Company Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:11:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:29:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fairness audits are increasingly mandated in high-stakes applications such as hiring, lending, and automated decision-making. Recent work has established fundamental impossibility results for black-box fairness auditing, showing that sufficiently expressive models can evade any auditing strategy. We complement these results by quantifying the extent of unavoidable post-audit manipulation under finite audit resources. We formulate fairness auditing as a min-max optimization between a computationally unbounded company and a budget-constrained auditor. We study two auditing regimes: (i) a budgeted auditor that certifies fairness using a fixed-size audit set, and (ii) a budgeted α-tolerant auditor that additionally requires the audit set to estimate the fairness of the certified model within an α approximation. For both settings, we derive explicit lower bounds on the worst-case post-audit demographic parity deviation as functions of the audit budget, group imbalance, and fairness tolerance. Finally, we empirically illustrate these theoretical limits using simple audit-set construction heuristics with linear and neural network classifiers. Our results demonstrate that increasing audit resources reduces, but does not eliminate, the scope for post-audit manipulation, highlighting fundamental limitations of finite-budget fairness certification."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00568","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:20Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00567","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00567","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00567","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matej","familyName":"Opatrny","name":"Opatrny, Matej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Opatrny","name":"Opatrny, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomas","familyName":"Havranek","name":"Havranek, Tomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zuzana","familyName":"Irsova","name":"Irsova, Zuzana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mojmir","familyName":"Hampl","name":"Hampl, Mojmir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimal Inflation Rate: A Meta-Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Economics (econ.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:03:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:29:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We revisit the optimal long-run inflation rate using 777 estimates from 116 primary studies published between 1989 and 2026, the largest sample on the topic to date. To our knowledge, this is among the first economics meta-analyses in which primary-data extraction is done from start to finish through a documented and auditable large-language-model pipeline, calibrated against a hand-coded training set and released for replication. The literature points to an optimum of about 0.6 percentage points per year, well below the two-percent targets used by most advanced-economy central banks. The gap should not be automatically read as a verdict against the two-percent norm. Measurement error in published price indices could close, widen, or even reverse the gap, and the structural literature itself cannot pin down the sign of the required correction. Bayesian model averaging over the full set of structural moderators shows that cross-study variation is driven by real modelling choices rather than by selective reporting. The main drivers are the choice of monetary benchmark (Friedman rule vs. laissez-faire), the transactions-frictions technology, the assumed shock structure, and the class of nominal-rigidity contract. The non-parametric caliper test finds no upward bunching at the two-percent target. The paper contributes a reproducible LLM-assisted extraction pipeline for structurally calibrated literature and a quantitative decomposition of where the optimal-inflation literature disagrees."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"62 pages, 4 figures. Also available as CEPR Discussion Paper 21629. Data, code, and extraction prompts: https://meta-analysis.cz/inflation"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00567","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:19Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00566","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00566","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00566","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niraj","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Niraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harsh","familyName":"Kasyap","name":"Kasyap, Harsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Crushing the Evidence: A Dual-Penalty Evasion Framework for Fooling White-Box Explainable AI Auditors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:03:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:29:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Post-hoc model explainers such as LIME, SHAP, and Integrated Gradients are widely deployed to audit models in high-stakes sensitive domains, including finance, healthcare, and social welfare. This ensures the model's transparency and acceptability. However, a few studies have examined potential attacks in the explainability pipeline. Adversaries can attempt to conceal algorithmic biases or backdoors using adversarial explanation attacks. These attacks have relied on scaffolding out-of-distribution (OOD) detectors that toggle predictions when queried by an explainer. Consequently, defenses have been developed to successfully neutralize these black-box attacks by identifying their anomalous perturbation footprints. In this paper, we demonstrate a critical vulnerability by introducing a more potent white-box, gradient-regularized evasion attack framework. By employing a continuous-embedding dual-penalty framework, we directly penalize trigger feature gradients during training on in-distribution data. Since our approach embeds the evasion logic natively into the model parameters, without relying on OOD scaffolding wrappers, it generates smooth, in-distribution predictions that leave no anomaly footprint. Empirical evaluations across four benchmark tabular datasets (COMPAS, German Credit, IEEE-CIS, and Communities \u0026amp; Crime) confirm that our method systematically crushes target feature attribution to near-zero (\u0026lt;0.02), maintains \u0026gt;90% Attack Success Rates, and fundamentally bypasses Conditional Anomaly Detection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00566","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:18Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00565","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00565","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00565","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongru","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Hongru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Log Determinant of Sample Correlation Matrices under Gaussianity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62H10, 60B20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:02:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:29:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove a central limit theorem for the log determinant of a Gaussian Pearson sample correlation matrix as the dimension diverges. Only two conditions are imposed: the population correlation matrix is positive definite, and the sample degrees of freedom are at least the dimension. Both are necessary for the ordinary log determinant to be finite. To the best of our knowledge, no previous central limit theorem covers this full nonsingular domain. It covers every aspect ratio from dilute growth to the square hard edge. No uniform lower or upper bound is imposed on the eigenvalues of the population correlation matrices: the smallest may approach zero and the largest may diverge. The proof develops a coordinatewise Wiener chaos reduction for the random diagonal normalization and combines it with an exact Wishart transform comparison. Geometrically, the statistic is twice the log volume of a random parallelotope spanned by standardized Gaussian coordinate vectors."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"34 pages, 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00565","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:16Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00564","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00564","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00564","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"I. A.","familyName":"Zemlyakov","name":"Zemlyakov, I. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"I. L.","familyName":"Chuev","name":"Chuev, I. L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. V.","familyName":"Kotikov","name":"Kotikov, A. V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pion-photon transition form factor with analytic coupling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:01:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:29:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate the pion-photon transition form factor within the framework of analytic QCD. A comparison is performed between experimental data and perturbative QCD based on conventional and analytic versions of perturbation theory with the ``massive'' form of the twist-four contribution. We show that conventional perturbation theory fails to reproduce the data, while the analytic version demonstrates good agreement with experiment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00564","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:15Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00563","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00563","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00563","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ángel","familyName":"Castro","name":"Castro, Ángel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio J.","familyName":"Fernández","name":"Fernández, Antonio J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claudia","familyName":"García","name":"García, Claudia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Smooth nonradial stationary solutions to SQG via the half-Yamabe equation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:01:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:29:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove the existence of infinitely many smooth nonradial stationary solutions to the surface quasi-geostrophic (SQG) equation with finite kinetic energy. Our construction is based on a family of nonradial sign-changing solutions to the two-dimensional half-Yamabe equation, obtained via a Lyapunov--Schmidt reduction and concentrated at the vertices of a regular polygon. As the number of vertices tends to infinity, the associated stationary SQG solutions converge to a radial stationary profile centered at the origin, together with a lower-order vortex sheet correction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00563","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:14Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00562","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00562","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00562","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinhua","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jinhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yisong","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yisong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhang","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Shuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Token-Level Cross-Entropy: Fréchet Distributional Post-Training for Autoregressive Image Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T10:00:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:29:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autoregressive image generators are commonly pretrained with token-level cross-entropy under teacher forcing, yet evaluated by the distributional quality of decoded images. This creates an objective mismatch, because categorical errors have unequal image-level consequences, and a context mismatch, because inference conditions on model-generated histories. We introduce FD-loss post-training, which adapts a pretrained discrete generator using representation-space Fréchet distance as the sole objective. A dual-pass scheme first constructs detached rollout contexts through gradient-free generation under the model's native inference configuration, then performs differentiable replay with a probability-level straight-through estimator (STE) that preserves hard argmax decoding in the forward pass while propagating image-level gradients through temperature-scaled probabilities. Only the generator is updated, while the tokenizer and feature extractors remain frozen. Across eight completed configurations from four generator families on class-conditional ImageNet at $256\\times256$, FD-loss post-training reduces FID and $\\mathrm{FD}_{r6}$ by 41.4% and 52.0% on average. The strongest FID result improves from 2.42 to 1.43 without adding parameters or inference steps."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00562","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:12Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00561","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00561","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00561","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shalom","familyName":"Kachko","name":"Kachko, Shalom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raz","familyName":"Lapid","name":"Lapid, Raz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Margarita","familyName":"Vald","name":"Vald, Margarita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Almog","familyName":"Dubin","name":"Dubin, Almog","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moshe","familyName":"Sipper","name":"Sipper, Moshe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Through the LENS: Local Geometric Decomposition of Vision-Language Model Representations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T09:54:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:28:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models (VLMs) process image patches and text tokens in a shared residual stream, but the local geometry through which the two modalities interact remains poorly understood. Most interpretability methods identify global linear directions, which may miss representations that are globally high-dimensional but locally low-dimensional. We introduce LENS (Local Explanation of Neighborhood Subspaces), a method that decomposes VLM activations into local low-rank Gaussian neighborhoods using a Mixture of Factor Analyzers. Applied to LLaVA-1.5-7B and Qwen3-VL-8B, LENS reveals distinct depth-dependent fusion trajectories consistent with each model's fusion mechanism: LLaVA progressively mixes modalities at later layers, whereas Qwen3-VL mixes them early, partially re-segregates them, and recombines them near the output. An automated multimodal labeling pipeline assigns concise semantic descriptions to these neighborhoods. Interpolating activations toward neighborhood centroids causally redirects generation within and across modalities and outperforms difference-in-means and VL-SAE in most evaluated conditions; in one LLaVA vision-to-vision setting, MFA achieves 5.7 times the VL-SAE score. Human evaluation finds MFA steering competitive with prompting and substantially stronger than the other intervention baselines. Finally, the MFA coefficient space improves Qwen3-VL image-to-rendered-text retrieval at the deepest evaluated layer from 14.9% to 48.6% R@1. Ablations show that the reported fusion trajectories are stable across component counts, local ranks, and modality-purity thresholds. These results support local geometric neighborhoods as useful interpretable and causal units for analyzing cross-modal representations in the evaluated VLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00561","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:11Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00560","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00560","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00560","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ginanjar","familyName":"Utama","name":"Utama, Ginanjar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hermawan Kresno","familyName":"Dipojono","name":"Dipojono, Hermawan Kresno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive operator-generated subspaces for effective many-body Hamiltonians"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T09:51:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:28:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Electronic-structure and embedding workflows terminate in effective many-body Hamiltonians, whereas quantum eigensolver studies often start from hand-built qubit models. We present the Adaptive Clifford-Algebra Subspace Eigensolver (A-CASE), a single-reference, operator-generated Rayleigh--Ritz method. Overlap, Hamiltonian, observable, and response matrices are reconstructed from one shared Pauli-expectation bank, while adaptive growth scores overlap-aware local pencils and rejects symmetry leakage or near-linear dependence. A strict FCIDUMP adapter supplies the active-space boundary. For linear H$_4$ in STO-3G with CAS(4e,4o), the mapped sector agrees with independent determinant FCI to $3.1\\times10^{-15}$ Ha. At a nine-vector budget A-CASE has a $3.019$ mHa error; replacing the determinant reference by a two-operator ADAPT-VQE state reduces it to $0.342$ mHa, without implying a matched total-cost advantage. Under a matched contract, the fixed-reference route uses one state preparation versus ADAPT-VQE's ninety but measures roughly an order of magnitude more Pauli words. Exact fixed-angle ADAPT-GCIM gives $13.364$ mHa at the nearest size match and $10.674$ mHa at the iteration match, with its transition-pair burden reported separately. Across a broader benchmark ladder, fixed Krylov bases are generally more accurate and often narrower but substantially less well conditioned. A grouped bootstrap propagates finite-shot variability through thresholding, diagonalization, root matching, spectral weights, susceptibility, and broadening; its bands are explicitly heuristic and conditional, not finite-sample confidence certificates. The work establishes an executable path from an interchange Hamiltonian to energies, correlations, and response, without claiming materials accuracy, favorable scaling, or quantum advantage."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 4 figures, 8 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00560","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:09Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00559","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00559","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00559","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yiqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheyuan","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Zheyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangzhao","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Xiangzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zefeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zefeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingjia","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Jingjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxu","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Jiaxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuohuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shuohuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test-Time Curriculum for Open-Set AIGC Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T09:42:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:28:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI-generated image detectors deployed in open-world environments inevitably face distribution shifts as new and stronger generative models continue to emerge. Although existing methods improve cross-generator generalization through better representations or training data construction, they typically follow a static train-once-and-deploy paradigm and cannot adapt after deployment. In this work, we study open-set AIGC image detection from a test-time adaptation perspective. We propose Test-Time Curriculum (TTC), a simple and model-agnostic framework that adapts a detector on unlabeled test data through curriculum-based self-training. TTC starts from highly reliable pseudo-labeled samples and progressively incorporates harder yet informative cases, while enforcing class-balanced selection to reduce biased updates under generator shift. To further improve pseudo-label quality, we introduce Cross-Scale Pseudo-Label Refinement, which aggregates complementary evidence across multiple resolutions for more reliable adaptation, and applies noisy-or fusion at inference to strengthen final predictions. In addition, we construct AIGCGuard, a new benchmark containing 3,100 representative real images and 124,000 generated images from 40 of the most advanced open-source and proprietary text-to-image models. Extensive experiments on five benchmarks show that TTC substantially improves overall detection performance under diverse unseen-generator shifts, establishing a practical and effective test-time adaptation framework for open-set generated image detection."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00559","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:08Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00558","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00558","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00558","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qunhui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qunhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AiFlow: Token-Native Reactive Orchestration with Bounded Backpressure for Streaming LLM Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T09:33:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:28:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) applications increasingly operate as streaming workflows combining retrieval, tool calls, safety filters, and multi-agent coordination. Although contemporary frameworks expose provider deltas, workflow nodes often treat generation as coarse request-response steps, leaving queue management, worker allocation, ordering, and backpressure to ad hoc callback code. This paper presents AiFlow, a token-native reactive orchestration model that normalizes provider deltas into typed Context events propagated through a directed streaming graph. Each node is managed by a Node Guardian that declares and enforces local queue bounds, worker concurrency, ordering, overflow policy, cancellation propagation, and retry discipline. We formalize the bounded-memory property, present the compilation from a compact DSL and JSON graph form, and provide static validation for type safety, state concurrency, and injection compatibility. Controlled microbenchmarks, captured DeepSeek trace replay (30 runs), descriptive online runs, LangGraph baselines, a streaming RAG workload, and an Ollama local-backend check show that AiFlow does not alter provider-side Model TTFT but reduces Application TTFPT by 70.9-94.7\\% versus aggregation and keeps runtime-owned queue depth within declared bounds (93.7-96.5\\% MaxQ reduction versus unbounded policies). The supplementary artifact contains scripts, raw traces, machine-readable tables, checksums, and an API-free smoke test; the public implementation is available through the FIT Framework repository."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 5 figures, 12 tables, 1 algorithm, and 1 code listing. Public implementation: https://github.com/ModelEngine-Group/fit-framework"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00558","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:07Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00555","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00555","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00555","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Jiménez","name":"Jiménez, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"C. N.","familyName":"Lintzmayer","name":"Lintzmayer, C. N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Sambinelli","name":"Sambinelli, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Proper conflict-free 7-coloring of planar graphs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"05Cxx (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T09:20:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:28:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A proper conflict-free coloring is a proper vertex coloring in which every nonisolated vertex has a color occurring uniquely in its open neighborhood. We prove that every graph with neither a $K_5$-minor nor a $Q_6$-minor admits such a coloring with at most seven colors, where $Q_6=K_3\\vee\\overline{K_3}$. In particular, this improves the previous general upper bound of eight for planar graphs. The proof combines a previously developed iterated distance-three selector construction with a general anchor-contraction lifting principle. The first supplies independently colored witnesses in closed neighborhoods, while the second combines those witnesses with a proper coloring of a suitable minor. We also develop the parity analogue of the first mechanism and show that, whenever the $K_{k+1}$ case of Hadwiger's conjecture holds, every $K_{k+1}$-minor-free graph can be proper vertex colored with $2k-1$ colors such that every nonisolated vertex has a color occurring an odd number of times in its open neighborhood."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00555","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:02Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00554","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00554","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00554","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengcheng","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Shengcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaming","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Jiaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanlin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wanlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyuan","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Ziyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxi","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Chenxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DexMani: Human-Derived Manipulability Guidance for Dexterous Rotation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T09:17:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Dexterous object rotation is a sequential contact problem: each support, release, and re-contact decision must both produce the desired object motion, and prepare the hand configuration for continued rotation. Existing reinforcement learning methods discover such movement patterns through trial and error on specific robotic hand embodiments, without explicitly accounting for how each contact transition affects the hand's ability to sustain object rotation in subsequent steps. We introduce DexMani, a framework that transfers human demonstrations as contact-conditioned manipulability evolution. This prior captures how successful human contact transitions reshape the object-rotation directions available to the hand. DexMani then learns this manipulability evolution and uses it to guide downstream reinforcement learning, enabling rotation skills to be acquired across robot embodiments with distinct kinematics and active-contact configurations. Across the Shadow Hand, Allegro Hand, and XHand, DexMani achieves the highest success rates in every evaluated setting for both seen and unseen objects. DexMani reaches an average success rate of 57.5% on LEAP Hand, outperforming other baselines and producing smoother rotatory motions. Project site: https://dexmani.github.io"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 17 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00554","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:01Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00553","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00553","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00553","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pranami","familyName":"Baishya","name":"Baishya, Pranami","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhaskar Jyoti","familyName":"Hazarika","name":"Hazarika, Bhaskar Jyoti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mass spectra of Tetraquark States from the Spinless Salpeter Equation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T09:12:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The mass spectrum of tetraquark states is examined using the spinless Salpeter equation alongside the Cornell potential. Analytical wave functions are derived through a variational approach, resulting in a specific mass formula for the bound states. This study investigates the mass spectra of heavy tetraquark states within a constituent diquark-antidiquark model. We employ a spinless Salpeter equation framework, incorporating the Cornell potential to describe the interaction between the diquark and antidiquark constituents. To account for relativistic effects, we utilize first-order perturbation theory to include the leading-order kinetic energy corrections arising from the expansion of the relativistic kinetic energy operator. A variational approach is adopted to determine the ground state masses, where the optimal variational parameter $β$ is found by minimizing the system's energy and then for higher states. We systematically calculate the masses of several experimentally observed and predicted tetraquark candidates, across a range of strong coupling constant values. Our results demonstrate a sensitivity of the calculated tetraquark masses to, and the model provides theoretical mass predictions that are in reasonable agreement with experimental observations for these exotic hadronic states. This work contributes to a deeper understanding of the internal structure and dynamics of heavy tetraquarks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 7 tables, submitted to EPJC"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00553","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:42:00Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:42:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:42:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00552","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00552","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00552","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Ginosar","name":"Ginosar, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Journey in Shared Memory Land"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T09:09:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"I have greatly enjoyed spending many years in studying parallel computing. My journey goes thorough MP-C, PLURAL, Async Plural, HAL, RC64 and more. As a PhD student at Princeton I studied a combination of shared memory and message passing, motivated by algorithms and the ease of programming. While at the Technion, a brilliant MSc student figured out a better way for shared memory machines that further simplified parallel programming. He later founded Plurality and we developed the HAL machine. At Ramon Chips we reinvented RC64 and managed to make programming much easier. Theoretical computing models, especially PRAM, have constituted the foundation for my work. Fortunately, I spent many years learning the theory and practice of asynchronous logic, and applied some of these concepts towards improving, namely simplifying, parallel machines. Over the years I became disillusioned of inventive hardware designers and their innovative machines. That perspective helped crystalize this process. Still, much more remains to be done."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00552","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:58Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00551","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00551","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00551","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolun","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Xiaolun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kezhao","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Kezhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinxing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xinxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Genke","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Genke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PHA-Net: Prototype-based Hierarchical Alignment Network for Text-Video Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T09:08:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the emergence of large-scale image-text pre-training models, e.g., CLIP, text-video retrieval has experienced substantial advances in recent years. Existing best-performing methods involve aligning cross-modal semantics at individual, local, and global levels simultaneously, raising concerns about the intrinsic semantic mismatch between concise texts and rich videos. A canonical approach is to integrate multiple language-video attention modules into the hierarchical framework while this paradigm only optimizes visual representations with prohibitive computational costs. In this paper, we propose a new prototype-based hierarchical alignment network (PHA-Net) to align individual/local/global level representations across modalities. Concretely, we introduce multiple modality-shared prototypes as the bridge to efficiently optimize text and video representations for cross-modal alignment. Then, we argue that the imbalanced semantic distribution in clustered tokens may undermine retrieval performance, as tokens with weak semantics are of little interest. To reduce the impact of these tokens, a proposed prototype-supported token merge module is responsible for enhancing tokens with strong semantics and suppressing others with weak semantics via prototype semantics guidance. Moreover, we devise a prototype contrastive loss to encourage textual and visual prototypes to focus on different semantic information. The idea of this auxiliary loss is to ensure higher similarity between textual and visual prototypes from the same prototype than those from different prototypes. Extensive experiments on four benchmarks confirm the effectiveness of our PHA-Net, which achieves significant improvements in the sum of all recalls on MSR-VTT (8.8%), ActivityNet (19.2%), VATEX (0.7%), and Charades (4.9%). Code is available at https://github.com/JingXiaolun/PHA-Net."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00551","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:57Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00550","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00550","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00550","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songtao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Songtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shixun","familyName":"You","name":"You, Shixun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daisheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Daisheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shoukang","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Shoukang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multiparameter quantum estimation in a photon system induced by gravitational redshift"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T09:07:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As photons propagate through curved spacetime, gravitational effects become unavoidable. In particular, gravitational redshift can induce significant distortion in photon wave packets, making it es?sential to investigate parameter estimation within this context. While previous research has focused on single-parameter estimation using the quantum Cramer-Rao bound, the multiparameter scenario remains largely unexplored. In this work, we investigate multiparameter quantum estimation for a photon system subject to gravitational redshift under both amplitude-damping and Ohmic-like dephasing channels. Our analysis reveals that the quantum Cramer-Rao bound fails to provide a tight error bound for the two-parameter estimation involving the initial phase and weight parameters inboth types of noisy channels. To overcome this limitation, we numerically compute two tighter error bounds, i.e., the Holevo Cramer-Rao bound and the Nagaoka bound, when utilizing a semidefinite program. We demonstrate that the Nagaoka bound yields the tightest error bound among all considered bounds, consistent with the general hierarchy of multiparameter quantum estimation. Furthermore, for the three-parameter estimation, including the initial weight parameter, the phase parameter, and the strength of gravitational redshift, we observe significantly enhanced estimation precision in the strong-coupling regime compared to the weak-coupling regime under the amplitude-damping channel. Similarly, in the Ohmic-like dephasing channel, the sub-Ohmic regime consistently affords higher precision than the Ohmic and super-Ohmic regimes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00550","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:55Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00549","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00549","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00549","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liliana","familyName":"Ardissono","name":"Ardissono, Liliana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabio","familyName":"Ferrero","name":"Ferrero, Fabio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angelo Geninatti","familyName":"Cossatin","name":"Cossatin, Angelo Geninatti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claudio","familyName":"Mattutino","name":"Mattutino, Claudio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noemi","familyName":"Mauro","name":"Mauro, Noemi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Context-Aware Cultural Heritage Guide Powered by LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T09:05:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3774935.3812714","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present an extension of Triangolazioni (a Cultural Heritage webapp) to enrich curated content with context-dependent, external information provided by Large Language Models (LLMs) within a loosely-coupled architecture agnostic to the LLM. The system supports context-dependent information search and presentation within an architecture agnostic to the exploited LLM."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00549","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:54Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00548","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00548","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00548","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pu","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingye","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Qingye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuedan","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Xuedan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuekun","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xuekun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rupeng","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Rupeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qing","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DrawAI: Agentic Benchmark and Workflow for Making Raster Images Editable"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T09:05:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent image-generation models and multimodal agents can produce high-quality visuals for increasingly complex visual communication tasks. Yet their raster outputs remain difficult to use directly because meaningful content and relationships are flattened into pixels, preventing users from inspecting, modifying, rearranging, or reusing individual components. We formulate image-to-editable reconstruction, which recovers a structured, directly manipulable artifact from a raster image while preserving its visual and semantic content. The central challenge is to jointly satisfy Fidelity and Editability, which often trade off in practice. To study this task, we introduce DrawAI, comprising an agentic benchmark, DrawAI-Bench, and a reconstruction workflow, DrawAI-Flow. DrawAI-Bench spans scientific figures, presentation slides, posters, and diagrams, combining real and AI-generated images to reflect practical visual-creation scenarios. It evaluates Fidelity and Editability through a hybrid protocol of 39 criteria: deterministic rule-based metrics measure properties with direct correspondences, while asset-specific vision-language rubrics capture semantic and perceptual qualities for which exact matching is misleading. Besides, we propose DrawAI-Flow, a two-stage agentic workflow in which a Parser Agent turns extracted elements evidence into an explicit reconstruction plan, and a Reconstruction Agent realizes the plan as executable graphics code through an iterative code-render-validate-revise loop. On DrawAI-Bench, we systematically evaluate thirteen models across five agent harnesses to study the effects of model capability, harness choice, and workflow design. The results show that reconstruction quality and costs vary substantially across model-harness configurations, while DrawAI-Flow consistently improves editable structure."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project URL: https://drawai.renaissancemind.ai/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00548","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:53Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00547","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00547","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00547","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Brigham Young University"],"givenName":"Zihang","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Zihang","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Beijing Academy of Science and Technology"],"givenName":"Chaoyue","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Chaoyue","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Beijing Academy of Science and Technology"],"givenName":"Yingying","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yingying","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Disentangling Visuo-Tactile Foresight: Oracle-Guided Interface Discovery for World Action Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T09:04:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Contact-rich manipulation remains challenging because successful control depends on physical interaction cues that are often weakly observable from vision alone. Recent tactile world action models jointly model future visual observations and tactile signals to guide action generation, but how such futures should be structured for effective use by the action expert remains underexplored. Directly studying this question with learned world action models is difficult because end-to-end behavior entangles physically invalid visual futures, unreliable predictions, inaccurate or cross-modally inconsistent tactile forecasts, and an unreadable future-to-action interface. To make this interface independently studyable, we introduce Oracle Visuo-Tactile Foresight (OVTF), a controlled framework that supplies paired RGB and tactile futures from successful trajectories verified in simulation. By fixing the future provider, OVTF isolates the interface and asks a cleaner question: if the future is successful and physically executable, what representation allows the action expert to absorb its benefit? Within OVTF, we propose Asymmetric Phase-Local Future Memory (AFM), in which visual memory reads future vision, each tactile memory jointly attends to its own tactile stream and phase-aligned future vision, and cross-tactile access is blocked. We compare AFM with Modality-Isolated Future Memory (IFM), which removes visual-to-tactile access and processes each future modality independently. Across seven tasks on the UniVTAC simulation benchmark, AFM achieves 32.0% average success, compared with 23.7% for IFM and 14.9% for UniVTAC-ACT. This controlled comparison shows that selective phase-aligned visual-tactile routing provides a more actionable future-to-action bridge than complete modality isolation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 3 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00547","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:51Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00545","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00545","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00545","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianhao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xianhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianghao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jianghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Prompt Adherence: Auditing Attribute-Level Voice Control in Speech Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T09:04:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Natural-language descriptions have become a flexible interface for controlling generated speech. Existing evaluations largely assess whether an output matches a prompt, but prompt matching alone does not reveal whether characteristics outside the intended change remain stable. We examine this distinction through a controlled paired audit of three speech-generation systems: CosyVoice3, VoxCPM2, and Fish-Speech-S2. The evaluation contains 5,940 outputs spanning six reference speakers, ten texts, three random seeds, and eleven conditions. Using acoustic, prosodic, content, and speaker measurements, we find that responses in the expected target direction are frequently accompanied by changes outside descriptor-specific signal-level target sets. This pattern remains among outputs whose target response exceeds baseline seed variation, and the accompanying changes differ substantially across systems. We further introduce VoDER-Cal, a training-free candidate selector that retains sufficiently strong target responses while favoring smaller off-target deviations. A three-candidate pool raises the joint success rate from 4.8% under single-sample direct generation to approximately 14% for all candidate-selection policies. Within the matched three-candidate budget, VoDER-Cal reduces held-out off-target deviation from 0.344 under target-only selection to 0.276 and improves listener-rated preservation. Preservation-sensitive evaluation therefore complements prompt-adherence evaluation, while candidate reranking offers a practical inference-time improvement. Code, configuration files, and analysis scripts are available at https://github.com/intelland/VoDER"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00545","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:49Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00544","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00544","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00544","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sigrid","familyName":"Vila-Bagaria","name":"Vila-Bagaria, Sigrid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mar","familyName":"Teixidó","name":"Teixidó, Mar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miquel","familyName":"Piñol","name":"Piñol, Miquel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felip","familyName":"Vilardell","name":"Vilardell, Felip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Montal","name":"Montal, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Veronica","familyName":"Vilaplana","name":"Vilaplana, Veronica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Zero-Cost Virtual RNA: Approximating Immunotherapy Signatures via Cross-Modal WSI Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T09:02:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Identifying the ``Inflamed'' immunophenotype in Gastric Adenocarcinoma predicts immunotherapy response but requires an expensive 10-gene RNA signature. While deep learning on standard H\\\u0026amp;E slides offers a scalable alternative, conventional binary classifiers oversimplify continuous RNA data and introduce label noise. To resolve this, we propose VITA (VIrtual Transcriptomic Approximation). By aligning H\\\u0026amp;E and RNA into a joint latent space during training, VITA requires only standard H\\\u0026amp;E at inference to retrieve morphologically similar historical cases and approximate the continuous RNA signature. Achieving 0.72 classification accuracy and a 0.66 Spearman correlation, VITA provides a cost-effective ``virtual transcriptomics'' pre-screening tool that preserves the continuous phenotypic spectrum without requiring genomic sequencing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to MIDL 2026 Short Paper track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00544","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:47Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00543","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00543","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00543","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshuo","familyName":"Cong","name":"Cong, Tianshuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Pei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianrui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tianrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinlei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xinlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Anyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaoyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust Watermarks Meet Backdoored Models: Evading Diffusion Semantic Watermarks via Stealthy Backdoor"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:58:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although semantic watermarking is considered a promising safeguard for images generated by Latent Diffusion Models (LDMs), the reliance of the watermark detection pipeline on neural networks introduces a critical yet underexplored backdoor attack surface. To systematically study this vulnerability, we propose GhostVAE to plant a stealthy backdoor into the encoder of Variational Autoencoder (VAE), enabling reliable evasion of watermark detection. GhostVAE operates in two stages: it first constructs a universal trigger via power spectrum regularization to improve the trigger robustness, and then trains a backdoored VAE encoder with a parameter-aligned objective. Through extensive evaluations across three state-of-the-art semantic watermarking schemes and three widely adopted LDMs, we show that GhostVAE preserves watermark detection performance on benign images (achieving an average true positive rate of 94.4%), while simultaneously enabling highly effective evasion under trigger activation (achieving an average attack success rate of 94.6%). Moreover, we comprehensively analyze seventeen representative defenses and demonstrate that GhostVAE remains stealthy across the input space, parameter space, and latent space. Our work fundamentally undermines the trustworthiness of semantic watermarking systems and highlights that secure deployment of semantic watermarks requires end-to-end security considerations, particularly for neural network components."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in USENIX Security 2026, August 12-14, 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00543","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:46Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00542","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00542","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00542","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuoyi","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Zhuoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agentic Graph Token Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:56:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graphs model relational data throughout science and industry, from citation networks to product co-purchase graphs. Because the nodes of many such graphs carry rich text, a growing line of work applies large language models (LLMs) to graph analysis. The most graph-native of these methods use graph tokens: a graph encoder compresses a graph view, such as a node, its k-hop neighbourhood, or a cluster, into a short block of continuous tokens that jointly encodes node attributes and topology and is read directly by the model. Existing methods, however, use graph tokens in a static single-shot manner: they encode one predefined graph view before the model has even seen the target and never revise it, leaving the model's step-by-step reasoning ability unused. We introduce agentic graph token reasoning, which recasts graph tokenization as part of the reasoning process itself. At each step, the model chooses which graph view to encode and at what granularity; a graph encoder is invoked on demand to materialise the corresponding graph tokens; and the resulting block is spliced into the running context. The model thus reasons step by step in the graph token space, and the tokens it reads are trajectory-dependent. We realise this with a three-stage training pipeline: (i) self-supervised tasks that teach the model to read heterogeneous graph tokens, (ii) a token-robust trajectory stage with a graph-token consistency regulariser, and (iii) preference optimisation that rewards trajectories in which the graph-token evidence and the node-text evidence agree. Across evaluations spanning seven graph domains, our models outperform a broad set of baselines by a large margin and transfer zero-shot to unseen domains without any per-target fine-tuning. More broadly, this work pushes LLM-based graph analysis from static graph-token encoders towards a graph-native agent paradigm."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00542","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:45Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00541","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00541","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00541","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tobias","familyName":"Hoppmann","name":"Hoppmann, Tobias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leona","familyName":"Lassak","name":"Lassak, Leona","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. Angela","familyName":"Sasse","name":"Sasse, M. Angela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zinaida","familyName":"Benenson","name":"Benenson, Zinaida","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring Usability and Legal Practice: Insights from German Judicial Users of Digital Forensics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:54:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Digital forensics has become an integral part of modern criminal proceedings, yet its effective integration remains challenging because of increasing data volumes, evolving technologies, and complex interactions between technical and legal stakeholders. Although prior work has focused primarily on digital forensic tools and methods, its broader procedural and organizational context has received limited attention. Building on emerging perspectives inspired by usability research and human-centered security, we conceptualize digital forensics as part of a socio-technical system within criminal proceedings. We consequently investigate this perspective through a survey of 101 practitioners from the judiciary of the German federal state of North Rhine-Westphalia, including public prosecutors, judges, and digital forensic experts. The results indicate a strong demand for improved integration of digital forensics into workflows, enhanced cross-domain communication, and a closer alignment of stakeholder expectations. They also uncover great potential for the improvement of digital forensics usability, e.g., through stronger interdisciplinary cooperation, easier and faster access to evidential data and results, or improvement of stakeholder training and education."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00541","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:43Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00540","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00540","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00540","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"An","familyName":"Lanji","name":"Lanji, An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Mei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DiffuseAgent-MI: Distributionally-Grounded,Tool-Integrated Self-Evolving Agents for Faithful Visual Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:54:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Tool-integrated vision-language agents have made remarkable progress on compositional and multi-step visual reasoning. Yet their outputs frequently exhibit unfaithfulness: the stated reasoning path diverges from the computation that actually produced the answer, undermining reliability in safety-critical applications. We present DiffuseAgent-MI, a self-evolving agent whose perceptual grounding is governed by a KL-minimal energy model over feature units, providing a distributional view of visual mechanistic interpretability. The agent learns an energy landscape that softly constrains generated samples to lie near the native prior conditioned on the chosen interpretable unit, closing the gap between the explanation and the internal representation. A verifier then supplies trajectory-level faithfulness rewards, and a repair branch re-conditions the energy when the verifier flags an unfaithful step. On GeoQA, SciVis, VQA-v2 and an in-house multimodal reasoning set, DiffuseAgent-MI improves accuracy by up to 5.1 points over prior self-evolving agents while more than doubling mutual-information faithfulness and human-interpretability agreement. Our analysis shows the energy term and the verifier are complementary: the former guarantees distributional faithfulness, the latter trajectory-level faithfulness, and only their combination closes both gaps."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 9 figures, accepted by ICML 2026 manitrack"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00540","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:42Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00539","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00539","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00539","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo-Yu","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Bo-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hsin-Po","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hsin-Po","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Venkatesan","familyName":"Guruswami","name":"Guruswami, Venkatesan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Frequency Coding over Noisy Sampling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:49:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"DNA molecules are so small that it might be practical to use their frequency vectors to encode messages. More precisely, a sender can inject $M_X$ copies of the string $X =$ CATCATCAT into a pool and the receiver can recover $M_X$ by sequencing the pool. There are, however, two sources of uncertainty: (a) $M_X$ is usually too big to be counted exactly, but is estimated by sampling. (b) The DNA sequencer could be noisy; it may have difficulty distinguishing CATCATCAT from CATGATCAT.\n Recently, Tamir, Weinberger, and Guillén i Fàbregas clarified the amount of information the frequency vector can carry under (a). They showed that each string can carry about $\\log_4 R$ bits, where $R$ is the average number of times each string is read. They also showed that $\\log_4 R$ bits can be achieved by a low-complexity uncoded scheme under the condition that there are at least $\\sqrt R$ distinct strings. In this paper, we show that a low-complexity coded scheme can achieve the same $\\log_4 R$ bits unconditionally. We then generalize the scheme to handle sequencing noise, (b), and show that the noise penalizes the total number of bits by $\\log_2 \\det W$, together with a linear term due to the use of Fourier transforms in our proof. The former penalty $\\log_2 \\det W$ is asymptotically the same as that obtained by Gerzon, Shomorony, and Weinberger; our scheme trades a small amount of rate for practical complexity."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00539","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:40Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00538","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00538","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00538","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuankang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuankang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiangming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DE-NER : Zero-shot Named Entity Recognition via Dialogue Elicitation of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:46:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements of zero-shot Named Entity Recognition (NER) establish strong baselines by formulating sequence labeling into question answering where Large Language Models (LLMs) can be naturally adopted. However, existing LLM-based zero-shot NER methods suffer from the limitations of prompt and demonstration engineering. To address these issues with minimal human interventions, we introduce DE-NER, a dialogue elicitation framework which elicits the chatting ability of LLMs to fully extract the knowledge encoded in LLMs. Our experiments demonstrate that the proposed method outperform the competitive baselines in zero-shot settings across multiple benchmarks, with an average improvement of 3.75\\% F1 points. Codes are released in https://github.com/kkkenshi/DE-NER."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00538","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:39Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00537","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00537","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00537","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongjie","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Hongjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiping","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiancheng","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Jiancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid-Domain Posterior Sampling for Inverse Problems via Latent Flow Matching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:46:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3767308.3835243","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Latent Flow Models have revolutionized compressed-space image synthesis, yet their application to high-fidelity inverse problems remains bottlenecked. In this paper, we trace this dilemma to a fundamental geometric limitation of pre-trained autoencoders, which we term \\emph{First-Order Manifold Blindness}. Severe decoder compression (e.g., retaining only $\\sim\\!2\\%$ of the original degrees of freedom) produces a rank-deficient Jacobian, rendering high-frequency measurement residuals in its orthogonal complement invisible to latent gradients even when the decoder can represent the target image. To overcome this bottleneck, we propose Hybrid-Domain Posterior Sampling (HDPS), a decoupled inference framework that disentangles physical measurement consistency from semantic prior modeling. HDPS diverges into the pixel space, leveraging Langevin dynamics to absorb precise orthogonal measurement gradients, and subsequently projects these structural corrections back onto the generative manifold. An optimization-based latent alignment is introduced to filter pixel-space artifacts while avoiding the semantic drift of direct encoding. Extensive experiments on diverse inverse problems demonstrate that HDPS establishes a new state-of-the-art, successfully recovering the high-frequency structural precision that latent-only solvers inherently discard. The code is available at \\href{https://github.com/74587887/HDPS}{https://github.com/74587887/HDPS}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ACM Multimedia 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00537","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:38Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00535","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00535","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00535","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianle","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tianle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Torus-enriched Motivic Bruhat Complexes and Maximal Compact Groups"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:45:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Bruhat decompositions give cellular models for split algebraic groups, flag varieties, and maximal compact groups, but motivic boundaries retain orientation and torus-translation data lost in the flag quotient. Over a perfect field of characteristic zero, let the group be connected, split, semisimple, and simply connected. Fixing a Borel subgroup with split maximal torus and unipotent radical, we construct a torus-enriched motivic cellular complex for the basic affine space and compute its boundary in every degree. Each cover in Bruhat order contributes a two-face operator determined by a transported coroot, a tail determinant weight, and an explicit Milnor--Witt frame degree. Bott--Samelson purity proves the formula, while the unipotent torsor identifies the complex with that of the group. Over the real numbers, realization identifies it at chain level with the extended-Weyl complex of a maximal compact subgroup, while torus augmentation gives the flag complex. A single motivic complex therefore interpolates between the two incidence theories. A finite torus-support filtration makes this explicit; after inversion of two it splits by the characters of the component group of the real split torus, and the support spectral sequence degenerates. Calculations in the rank-three special linear and exceptional rank-two cases exhibit the first higher differentials beyond the previously known range."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00535","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:35Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00534","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00534","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00534","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John D. P.","familyName":"Evans","name":"Evans, John D. P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gendered Structural Roles in Indian Epics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Physics and Society (physics.soc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:44:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Mahabharata and the Ramayana constitute the two major Sanskrit epics of ancient India. Previous network analysis revealed substantial structural similarities between their character networks, alongside differences consistent with their contrasting forms of narrative organisation. Building upon these findings, we investigate the representation and structural positions of male and female characters within the two texts. In particular, we examine whether the genders differ systematically in their connectivity, prominence, community participation, and contribution to network cohesion, while accounting for the considerable imbalance between the numbers of male and female characters. We employ methods designed to identify characters who connect otherwise distinct regions of the networks and to assess the structural consequences of character removal. Through these analyses, we ask whether gendered patterns are consistent across the two epics or instead reflect the different narrative structures previously identified. In so doing, we find that male characters are considerably more prevalent among highly central and structurally consequential nodes in both epics, and targeted removal of central male characters produces substantially greater network disruption. However, once baseline structural position is taken into account, we find little residual difference in deletion effects between male and female characters. Furthermore, under selected Girvan-Newman partitions, we find that female characters in the Mahabharata exhibit greater cross-community participation than female characters in the Ramayana."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"2 figures, 10 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00534","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:34Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00533","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00533","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00533","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean Gip","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Sean Gip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William Chandra","familyName":"Tjhi","name":"Tjhi, William Chandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hai Leong","familyName":"Chieu","name":"Chieu, Hai Leong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Native Multilingual Chain-of-Thought Reasoning in Low-Resource Southeast Asian Languages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:44:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models have achieved substantial progress in reasoning capabilities. Yet in low-resource native settings, many suffer from cross-lingual collapse, reverting to English during intermediate steps that require complex logical reasoning. This presents a cold-start bottleneck for policy optimization, whereas standard fine-tuning risks catastrophic forgetting due to cross-lingual representation drift. To address these challenges, we introduce the Onramp-Sequence Cross-Distillation (OSCD), a post-training algorithm that projects high-resource reasoning trajectories into low-resource vocabulary subspaces during generative training rollouts via an integrated translator agentic loop, ensuring the stable and efficient translation of dynamically generated reference samples for fine-tuning. This is coupled with joint-embedding semantic alignment of both reference and target-language reasoning traces, thereby bridging the pairwise cross-lingual representational gaps. Comprehensive evaluations using the AIME25 and HMMT25 benchmarks demonstrate that OSCD yields up to 3.2 times overall improvements in native Southeast Asian languages for mathematical reasoning, of which the joint-embedding semantic alignment component contributes up to 6.4% improvements in linguistic debiasing over translation-only baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 16 figures, 12 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00533","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:32Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00532","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00532","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00532","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aleš","familyName":"Drápal","name":"Drápal, Aleš","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Terry S.","familyName":"Griggs","name":"Griggs, Terry S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew R.","familyName":"Kozlik","name":"Kozlik, Andrew R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Basics of DTS quasigroups: algebra, geometry and enumeration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"05B07 (Primary), 20N05 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:44:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1142/s0219498815500899","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A directed triple system can be defined as a decomposition of a complete digraph to directed triples $\\langle x,y,z\\rangle$. By setting $xy =z$, $yz =x$, $xz =y$ and $uu =u$ we get a binary operation that can be a quasigroup. We give an algebraic description of such quasigroups, explain how they can be associated with triangulated pseudosurfaces and report enumeration results."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00532","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:31Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00531","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00531","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00531","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aofan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Aofan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingxiang","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Jingxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangxin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Fangxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongbiao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yongbiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CURE: Local Uncertainty Repair for Block-Parallel Speculative Decoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:43:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speculative decoding mitigates the latency of sequential generation in autoregressive Large Language Models (LLMs) by interleaving draft generation with target verification. However, existing parallel drafting backends often suffer from rapid accuracy degradation over long horizons, leading to high rejection rates during verification and suboptimal wall-clock speedups. We observe that drafting errors are not uniformly distributed but typically stem from localized high-uncertainty tokens that destabilize downstream generation trajectories. Motivated by this token error pattern, we propose CURE, a budget-aware dynamic repair tree designed to repair errors at uncertainty focal points without incurring prohibitive tree-verification overheads. Specifically, our method uses predictive confidence margins to dynamically locate candidate error tokens within a block-parallel draft, expands bounded repair paths only at these fragile nodes, and employs a novel repair resynchronization mechanism to realign draft states post-verification. Evaluations on code-generation benchmarks (HumanEval, MBPP, and LiveCodeBench-lite) and mathematical reasoning benchmark (GSM8K) demonstrate that CURE increases the average accepted length by 4.2-7.5% over parallel baselines without repair, translating to an end-to-end speedup of $2.66-3.49\\times$ over target-only decoding. Furthermore, we provide a plug-and-play repair module compatible with standard parallel drafting frameworks. We also characterize the trade-off between draft compute and verification efficiency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 2 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00531","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:29Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00530","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00530","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00530","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["School of Computer Science, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi'an, China"],"givenName":"Axi","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Axi","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["School of Computer Science, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi'an, China"],"givenName":"Jieheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jieheng","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["School of Electrical Engineering, KAIST, Daejeon, Republic of Korea"],"givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kang","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["School of Computer Science, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi'an, China"],"givenName":"Qingsen","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Qingsen","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["School of Aeronautics and Astronautics, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi'an, China"],"givenName":"Jinqiu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jinqiu","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["School of Computer Science, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi'an, China"],"givenName":"Yanning","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanning","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unleashing the Power of Text: Text-Guided Flow Matching for Image Fusion under Complex Degradations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:43:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Infrared-visible image fusion under realistic degradation scenarios is a challenging task, as degradations not only cause a loss of reliable modality-specific information in observed images but also hinder the fusion process. Recent studies indicate that text can provide prior information about degradation characteristics, complementing the limited evidence available from corrupted input images and facilitating fusion. However, existing methods typically inject fixed global text representations into visual features, making it difficult for textual guidance to adapt to spatially varying degradations, local structures, and thermal saliency. To this end, we propose TGFusion, a text-guided latent-space flow matching framework that unifies degradation suppression and cross-modal fusion. TGFusion encodes task, degradation, and generation cues into structured prompts. To fully exploit these priors, we design a Prompt-conditioned Multi-stream Joint Flow Transformer that represents text as an independent semantic stream alongside fusion, visible, and infrared streams. Joint attention enables token-level bidirectional interaction and layer-wise updating among semantic and visual representations, allowing degradation semantics to dynamically guide reliable information selection and fusion latent generation. Extensive experiments on public benchmarks and complex degradation scenarios demonstrate that TGFusion achieves superior or competitive performance in perceptual quality, image naturalness, structural-detail preservation, and infrared-saliency retention, while remaining robust across diverse single and compound degradations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 9 figures, including supplementary material"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00530","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:28Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00529","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00529","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00529","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin-Jing","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Lin-Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fu","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Fu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Pei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai-Jia","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Kai-Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guo-Liang","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Guo-Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Gang","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yu-Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Schrödinger Generator for High-Dimensional Integration and Sampling on Quantum Many-Body States"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Nuclear Theory (nucl-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:41:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Integration and sampling in high dimensions are among central challenges in modern science and technology, underlying applications ranging from quantum many-body physics to Bayesian inference and artificial intelligence. Although conventional Monte Carlo methods are formally scalable, their efficiency deteriorates rapidly in the presence of strong correlations or sharp features in high-dimensional configuration space. Here we introduce a new framework, termed the Schr\"odinger Generator, for integration and sampling based on the explicit optimization of coordinate transformations. The method decomposes the total Jacobian into two complementary components, including an adaptive map that minimizes estimator variance by learning the marginal structure in each dimension, and a normalizing-flow-based transformation that captures non-factorizable correlations in the target distribution. A final resampling step guarantees unbiased sampling even when the learned transformation is imperfect. We demonstrate stable and scalable performance for nuclear quantum many-body states in dimensions exceeding 600. Short-range correlations among nucleons in finite nucleus are faithfully reproduced. The framework offers a physically transparent approach to high-dimensional stochastic integration and sampling, opening new possibilities for simulations of complex quantum systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00529","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:27Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00528","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00528","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00528","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialong","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jialong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"You","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, You","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kewei","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Kewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"S$^4$R: Selective Sampling, Subspaces, and Sparse Reconstruction for Compressed Long-Context KV Caching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:41:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The growth of context window lengths in Large Language Models (LLMs) significantly enhances their long-context capabilities but incurs prohibitive memory costs due to the Key-Value (KV) cache. Although low-rank compression of KV cache is a promising remedy, existing methods face a dilemma: offline approaches depend on external calibration data, whereas online approaches incur substantial compute for full-prompt decomposition and reconstruction. In this paper, we propose S$^4$R, which builds low-rank subspaces from selectively sampled tokens and computes attention over a sparsely reconstructed KV representation. S$^4$R uses prompt-aware initialization to build initial key/value bases from a representative prompt subset, trading off calibration-data dependence against prefilling cost. Because fully reconstructing the cache at every decoding step is prohibitively expensive and hurts throughput, we further adopt sparse reconstruction to retain only informative positions during decoding. Extensive experiments on LongBench and RULER with Llama and Qwen model families show that S$^4$R achieves up to 5$\\times$ KV compression with near full-cache accuracy, combining the efficiency of fixed compression with the adaptability of prompt-dependent methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00528","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:25Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00527","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00527","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00527","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daojie","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Daojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingtao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bingtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SSTG-Nav: Metric-Grounded Spatial-Semantic Topological Graphs for Reusable Object Navigation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:40:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Service robots operating for months in the same homes, offices, and facilities should become more reliable with experience instead of searching familiar space from scratch for every request. Yet ObjectNav is predominantly formulated as one-shot exploration, leaving a central deployment challenge unresolved: recognizing an object does not identify a reachable place to stop, and one confident map error can terminate the task. We introduce SSTG-Nav, a reusable metric-semantic memory that turns a one-time survey into actionable object goals, consolidates evidence across viewpoints, and retains spatially distinct recovery standoffs. On 1,000 HM3D-v2 episodes across 36 scenes, our goal-independent topology achieves a 99.4% geometric success ceiling. Holding semantic responses fixed, metric grounding raises SR/SPL from 0.835/0.560 to 0.920/0.603, and source-aware fusion reaches 0.926/0.586. Fusion-aware Top-3 recovery raises Success@1/2/3 to 0.928/0.965/0.975 and reaches 0.601 SPL@3. Model, field-of-view, density, and corruption controls identify where these gains originate, and a ROS2/Nav2 realization demonstrates the complete reusable query-to-execution pipeline. Together, the results establish pre-exploration as a powerful practical regime for dependable, repeated semantic navigation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00527","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:24Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00526","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00526","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00526","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niclas","familyName":"Tilgner","name":"Tilgner, Niclas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andres David Peña","familyName":"Unigarro","name":"Unigarro, Andres David Peña","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susanne","familyName":"Wolff","name":"Wolff, Susanne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mats","familyName":"Leandersson","name":"Leandersson, Mats","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Craig","familyName":"Polley","name":"Polley, Craig","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian","familyName":"Göhler","name":"Göhler, Fabian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sibylle","familyName":"Gemming","name":"Gemming, Sibylle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Seyller","name":"Seyller, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philip","familyName":"Schädlich","name":"Schädlich, Philip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spin- and Angle-resolved Photoelectron Spectroscopy Study of the Quantum Spin Hall Insulator Bismuthene and its Precursor Phase"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:38:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent studies have revealed that a confined bismuth layer at the graphene/SiC interface can be reversibly switched between a topologically trivial precursor phase and the quantum spin Hall insulator bismuthene. Here, we present a detailed spin- and angle-resolved photoelectron spectroscopy study of both structures, resolving the spin texture of their low-energy electronic states. Owing to the strong intrinsic spin-orbit coupling of bismuth and the asymmetric confinement potential at the interface, the valence bands of both structures are Rashba-split. We demonstrate the expected spin-momentum locking for both phases and Kramers' doublets through the investigation of the valence bands' spin polarization at multiple positions in reciprocal space."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00526","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:23Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00525","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00525","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00525","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Congpei","familyName":"An","name":"An, Congpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiannan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiannan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoming","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Xiaoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Pólya's Conditions to a Complete Characterization of the $L^2$ Convergence of Hyperinterpolation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"41A35, 41A17, 41A55, 65D15, 65D32","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:37:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:27:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"It has remained open to identify the necessary and sufficient conditions for the $L^2$ convergence of hyperinterpolation since it was introduced by Sloan in 1995. We show that the $L^1$-$L^2$ Marcinkiewicz-Zygmund (MZ) condition, together with the asymptotic functional approximation property for polynomials, is the answer. We further prove that the optimal $L^1$-$L^2$ MZ constant coincides with the operator norm of the hyperinterpolation operator, and it admits a natural Banach space duality interpretation. With an explicit construction, we also show that Pólya's classical conditions for quadrature convergence are not sufficient for the $L^2$ convergence of hyperinterpolation. This reveals a fundamental distinction between the convergence of linear functionals (quadrature formulas) and that of linear operators (hyperinterpolation operators). We establish a strict logical hierarchy for the stability and accuracy conditions governing the convergence of quadrature and hyperinterpolation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00525","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:21Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00524","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00524","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00524","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Talha","familyName":"Azfar","name":"Azfar, Talha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruimin","familyName":"Ke","name":"Ke, Ruimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cooperative Platoon Routing and Dispatching via Edge-Assisted Hybrid Quantum Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:35:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:26:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cooperative platooning can reduce the energy use of Connected and Autonomous Vehicle (CAV) fleets, but the routing problem becomes difficult when vehicles must meet on the same road segments at compatible times while moving through unstable urban traffic. This paper develops an edge-assisted, closed-loop evaluation pipeline for platooning-aware vehicle routing. Roadside Units estimate local traffic kinematics from video, classify segment-level flow stability, and activate platooning rewards only on road segments where close-gap coordination is physically appropriate. The resulting multi-vehicle routing problem is written directly as a Quadratic Unconstrained Binary Optimization (QUBO) model, so pairwise platooning interactions are represented as native quadratic Ising terms instead of requiring auxiliary MILP linearization variables. We evaluate the framework using a 24-hour microscopic SUMO simulation of Troy, NY, together with localized IBM Quantum hardware benchmarks. The SUMO study shows an $18.5\\%$ reduction in fleet tractive-energy demand relative to a non-cooperative baseline. On 25-active-qubit benchmark instances executed on $\\texttt{ibm_boston}$, Linear-Chain QAOA reduces two-qubit CNOT depth by $66.7\\%$ compared with dense QAOA and samples the exact classical ground state with $P_{\\text{feas}} = 38.6\\%$ and $P_{\\text{opt}} = 14.2\\%$ at $p=2$. These results suggest that edge perception and shallow quantum optimization can work together as a useful component of closed-loop CAV platoon dispatching."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00524","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:20Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00521","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00521","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00521","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["State Key Laboratory of Coal Combustion, School of Energy and Power Engineering, Huazhong University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, China"],"givenName":"Shuyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuyang","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["National Key Laboratory of Aerospace Physics in Fluids, Mianyang, China"],"givenName":"Weidong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weidong","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["National Key Laboratory of Aerospace Physics in Fluids, Mianyang, China"],"givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Ming","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Institute of Interdisciplinary Research for Mathematics and Applied Science, Huazhong University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, China"],"givenName":"Zhaoli","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Zhaoli","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A memory-efficient deterministic method for multiscale gas flows using an ensemble-of-subproblems strategy with stochastic discrete velocities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:31:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:26:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deterministic multiscale gas flow simulations have long suffered from the curse of dimensionality: the number of discrete velocities increases dramatically with the velocity space dimension and the Mach number, exhausting available memory and computational resources. To address this issue, this paper proposes a memory-efficient deterministic method based on an ensemble-of-subproblems strategy using stochastic discrete velocities. This strategy transforms the originally computationally expensive problem into a series of independently and efficiently solvable subproblems. To be concrete, the proposed method replaces the conventional large deterministic velocity set with multiple small random velocity sets. Each random set defines a subproblem, which is solved by a deterministic multiscale numerical scheme that computes macroscopic moments via Monte Carlo integration. The final flow field is obtained by arithmetic averaging over all sub-problems. In this work, we employ the discrete unified gas kinetic scheme (DUGKS) for spatial discretization and term the resulting method SDV-DUGKS. To validate the proposed method, several numerical test cases are conducted, including (a) the one-dimensional shock structure, (b) the two-dimensional cavity flow, and (c) supersonic flow around a square cylinder. The results of the one-dimensional shock structure confirm the feasibility of the proposed method. The two-dimensional cases demonstrate that, compared to its deterministic counterpart, the proposed method saves more than 80% of memory usage while maintaining comparable accuracy. These results indicate that the proposed method markedly reduces memory demand for multiscale flow simulations and exhibits strong potential to alleviate the curse of dimensionality that currently hinders deterministic multiscale numerical schemes from being applied to engineering problems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 31 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00521","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:16Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00520","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00520","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00520","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mu'allim","familyName":"Yakubu","name":"Yakubu, Mu'allim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Using High-Resolution Spectroscopy to Study the Composition, Temperature, and Dynamics of Exoplanet Atmospheres"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:31:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:26:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-resolution spectroscopy, typically operating at resolving powers R greater or equal to 25,000, has matured into one of the primary techniques for characterising the atmospheres of extrasolar planets. The ability of HRS to resolve individual rotational-vibrational lines of molecular bands, combined with the large Doppler shifts experienced by close-in planets during their orbits, allows planetary signals to be separated from quasi-stationary telluric and stellar contamination. Since the pioneering detection of carbon monoxide in the transmission spectrum of HD 209458b, HRS has enabled the identification of more than a dozen chemical species, including H2O, CH4, HCN, TiO, VO, Na, K, Li, H-alpha, He I, Mg, Ca, V, Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, and Ti, in a wide variety of transiting, non-transiting and directly imaged exoplanets . In addition to chemical abundances, HRS constrains the vertical temperature structure through the pressure dependence of line depths, and reveals atmospheric dynamics through Doppler shifts and asymmetries imprinted on the planetary cross-correlation function. This review synthesises observational and methodological progress from the past fifteen years with a focus on how current and forthcoming high-resolution facilities HARPS, ESPRESSO, NIRPS, CARMENES, CRIRES+, SPIRou, GIANO, and ultimately the ANDES, METIS and HARMONI instruments on the Extremely Large Telescope are reshaping our empirical view of exoplanet atmospheres."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 Pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00520","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:15Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00519","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00519","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00519","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingxu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jingxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangyu","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Liangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Convexity criterion and radial-profile response for off-shell Kerr geometries: a fuzzy-dark-matter profile as an analytic benchmark"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:30:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:26:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We establish a sufficient one-minimum criterion for the off-shell Kerr family $Δ(r) = r^2 - 2rm(r) + a^2$ with a positive, nondecreasing mass profile $m(r)$, showing that $1 - 2m'(r) - rm''(r) \u0026gt; 0$ ensures strict convexity and determines root counts for $Δ$. Using a fuzzy-dark-matter-inspired benchmark satisfying this bound, we derive first-order responses for the outer horizon, extremal branch, photon sphere, and shadow functional under general deformations $m/M_{\\text{ADM}} = 1 + \\varepsilon h$. We demonstrate that static horizon and photon responses are profile-controlled, spin-odd shadow displacements are completion-dependent, and scale-consistent weak-field limits render local profile-gradient effects negligible ($\\ll 10^{-20}$), confirming the strong-field box as a formal radial-profile benchmark rather than a self-consistent rotating scalar-field solution."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00519","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:13Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00518","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00518","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00518","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyang","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Yuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GuideGround: VLM-guided Semantic Understanding and Viewpoint-aware Reasoning for 3D Visual Grounding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:28:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:26:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"3D visual grounding aims to localize the target object in a 3D scene from a natural language query, requiring both fine-grained semantic understanding and viewpoint-dependent spatial reasoning. Existing methods typically formulate semantic understanding as an auxiliary closed-set object classification task and rely on multi-view feature aggregation for viewpoint reasoning, limiting semantic generalization and weakening viewpoint-specific evidence. We observe that vision-language models naturally provide complementary capabilities through open-vocabulary semantic understanding and global scene perception. Based on this insight, we propose GuideGround, a VLM-guided framework that complements rather than replaces task-specific grounding models by leveraging VLMs for semantic enhancement and viewpoint-specific hypothesis verification. Specifically, we replace auxiliary closed-set object classification with VLM-generated object semantic descriptions to enhance semantic understanding. Meanwhile, instead of directly aggregating multi-view representations, we preserve viewpoint-specific grounding hypotheses through per-view grounding and explicitly verify them using VLMs across candidate viewpoints. Extensive experiments on the ReferIt3D benchmark demonstrate that GuideGround consistently outperforms previous state-of-the-art methods. Comprehensive ablation studies further confirm the effectiveness of both the proposed semantic understanding and viewpoint reasoning strategies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00518","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:12Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00517","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00517","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00517","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Market Design Center, Graduate School of Economics, The University of Tokyo","Graduate School of Informatics and Engineering, The University of Electro-Communications"],"givenName":"Hiroaki","familyName":"Odahara","name":"Odahara, Hiroaki","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exact Budget Balance via Payment-Rule Ambiguity: Incentive Preservation, Transfer Capacity, and Participation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Theoretical Economics (econ.TH)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:27:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:26:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper asks whether the payment side of an incentive-compatible mechanism can be repaired so that every realized settlement balances exactly while the allocation and all report-by-report comparisons under the maintained worst-case evaluation remain unchanged. Before reports, the designer commits to a menu of payment rules and a report-blind selection protocol. A pointwise preservation condition then carries over dominant-strategy incentives. Under a full-support common reference, a precisely known label law permits repair only if the original rule already balances, whereas frequency uncertainty about even one non-worst label makes every non-deficit rule repairable. Under the common residual-ambiguity benchmark, every preserving, exactly balanced finite menu at a positive-surplus state requires more transfer capacity as certified frequency information becomes tighter. In the canonical menu, fixing the transfer cap makes the best attainable balance deteriorate toward the original surplus as ambiguity vanishes. When only the possible labels are certified, two labels suffice. Suitably balanced partitions can also retain the original cap for nonnegative payments and preserve individual rationality under every realized rule. This last guarantee is impossible in positive-revenue Vickrey states when each agent's preserving distribution has full support. Frequency information therefore changes feasibility, liquidity, and realized participation in distinct ways."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"54 pages, 1 figure; includes appendices and ancillary verification code"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00517","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:11Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00515","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00515","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00515","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nizam","familyName":"Kadir","name":"Kadir, Nizam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Auditable Release Control for Pedagogical Leakage in LLM Tutors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:24:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:26:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model tutors can be correct and helpful yet disclose an answer or decisive reasoning before that disclosure is authorized. We formalize this state- and action-dependent failure as pedagogical leakage and introduce an authorization-aware complete-mediation boundary. A selector emits one of five disclosure contracts, trusted policy gates privileged modes, and a renderer proposes language. A single release function applies inspectable checks, optional cumulative verification, and action-specific fallback; replayable traces separate selection, generation, verification, and enforcement failures.\n Matched component attribution exposes a safety-utility frontier. On 599 fixed Gemini 3.5 proposals, strict mediation reduces blinded three-model panel-majority leakage flags from 181 to 0 (paired problem-cluster difference -30.22 points, 95% CI [-35.00,-25.72]), while replacing 581 responses and lowering helpfulness. Checker-triggered fallback alone yields 11 majority flags; adding the semantic verifier yields 14 and no reliable marginal gain. A global A1 scaffold yields 0 majority and 54 any-judge flags, outperforming fitted Q on automatic safety and utility. In an externally timestamped replication over 40 unseen problem clusters and 480 attack sequences, high-assurance release reduces majority flags from 42 to 8 (-7.08 points, 95% CI [-13.13,-2.29]); seven failures persist, one is introduced, and mean helpfulness falls by .192. These results establish an auditable release boundary and failure attribution under declared contracts, not universal semantic safety or learning gains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 1 figure, 6 tables. Preprint; not peer reviewed"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00515","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:08Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00514","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00514","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00514","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["State Key Laboratory of Coal Combustion, School of Energy and Power Engineering, Huazhong University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, China"],"givenName":"Shuyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuyang","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["National Key Laboratory of Aerospace Physics in Fluids, Mianyang, China"],"givenName":"Weidong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weidong","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["National Key Laboratory of Aerospace Physics in Fluids, Mianyang, China"],"givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Ming","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Institute of Interdisciplinary Research for Mathematics and Applied Science, Huazhong University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, China"],"givenName":"Zhaoli","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Zhaoli","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mitigating ray effects in rarefied flow simulations using an ensemble-of-subproblems strategy with stochastic discrete velocities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:24:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:26:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, a ensemble-of-subproblems strategy with stochastic discrete velocities is extended to deterministic methods for mitigating ray effects in rarefied flow simulations. The strategy involves performing multiple independent subproblems, each using a small set of randomly sampled velocity points, and then averaging their solutions to obtain the final result. The core idea is to ensure that the distribution function at any velocity can contribute to the final result, approximating highly refined velocity-space resolution without increasing the memory requirement in any single subproblem. We incorporate this strategy within the DUGKS framework, and the resulting method is denoted as SDV-DUGKS. To evaluate the performance of the proposed method, we compare SDV-DUGKS with the original DUGKS on several test cases: (a) the Sod shock tube problem, (b) the one-dimensional Riemann problem, (c) the two-dimensional lid-driven cavity flow, and (d) the two-dimensional Riemann problem. The results show that, in the collisionless limit $\\mathrm{Kn} \\to \\infty$: (1) for one-dimensional compressible flows, SDV-DUGKS reduces memory usage by approximately 2/3 compared with that of the original DUGKS while achieving good agreement; (2) for two-dimensional compressible flows, SDV-DUGKS requires one to two orders of magnitude less memory than the original DUGKS while achieving good agreement. Based on these results, it can be concluded that the proposed method serves as a reliable and effective tool for mitigating ray effects in rarefied flow simulations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 26 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00514","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:07Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00513","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00513","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00513","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenya","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhenya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wendi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Wendi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongmei","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Hongmei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuguang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BayesSeg: A Bayesian Optimization Framework for State Segmentation of Electricity Consumption Time Series"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Analysis, Statistics and Probability (physics.data-an)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:22:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:26:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In Non-Intrusive Load Monitoring (NILM), adaptive segmentation of electricity consumption time series is critical for appliance recognition. However, prevailing methods face challenges including heuristic parameter tuning, boundary sensitivity, and metric saturation. This paper proposes BayesSeg, a unified framework integrating time-series segmentation, multidimensional evaluation, and automatic parameter optimization. The segmentation layer employs a dual steady-state criterion based on the tail value and mean of preceding subsequences, combined with a sequential extraction and complement-set parsing strategy, to achieve precise unsupervised partitioning of steady-state and transition-state segments. The evaluation layer maps segmentation results to binary state sequences and formulates a composite metric integrating an event-level F1 score (event_F1) with Normalized Mutual Information (NMI). The event_F1 quantifies switching-event precision and recall via tolerance matching, while NMI captures global structural consistency, jointly overcoming the boundary sensitivity and limited discriminability of point-wise metrics. In the optimization layer, the composite score serves as the objective function for Bayesian optimization, which constructs a TPE surrogate model for efficient global parameter-space exploration. Experiments on the SustDataED2 dataset demonstrate that Bayesian optimization requires only ~100 objective evaluations to locate a parameter region within 0.35% deviation of the exhaustive grid-search optimum. The framework achieves a weighted composite score of 0.7149 and an event_F1 of 0.9340 while reducing optimization latency from ~5300 seconds to under 1 second, a speedup exceeding 5700x. BayesSeg automates segmentation configuration and provides a scalable, efficient solution for time-series analysis in NILM and related domains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, 4 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00513","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:05Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00512","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00512","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00512","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guang-Yu","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Guang-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ren-Guo","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Ren-Guo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun-Chen","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Yun-Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang-hao","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Yang-hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"{\\it Ab initio} prediction of $d_{x^2-y^2}$-wave superconductivity in infinite-layer nickelates"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Superconductivity (cond-mat.supr-con)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:22:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:26:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Infinite-layer nickelates have recently emerged as a new family of potential unconventional high critical temperature ($T_c$) superconductors. However, fundamental questions such as their superconducting (SC) pairing mechanism and gap symmetry remain under intense debate. Here we present a fully {\\it ab initio} theoretical study on the SC properties of optimally doped nickelates $Re$$_{0.8}$Sr$_{0.2}$NiO$_2$ ($Re=$ La, Pr, Nd), based on the density functional theory for superconductors calculations with electron-phonon coupling (EPC), screened Coulomb repulsion and spin fluctuation (SF) interaction treated on an equal footing. We find that $Re_{0.8}$Sr$_{0.2}$NiO$_2$ are two-band superconductors with sign reversal $d_{x^2-y^2}(\\pm)$-wave gap functions on the different Fermi surface (FS) pockets. Interestingly, when the SF interaction is turned off, $T_c$ becomes negligibly small ($\\sim$0.01 K), thus demonstrating that the superconductivity in $Re_{0.8}$Sr$_{0.2}$NiO$_2$ is driven by SF interaction. Moreover, our {\\it ab initio} calculations reveal that the SF interaction is an order of magnitude stronger than both EPC and Coulomb repulsion on the large quasi-two-dimensional FS pocket around the Brillouin zone (BZ) center, thus leading to the SF-mediated pairing mechanism, although the EPC dominates on the small three-dimensional electron FS pockets at the BZ corners. The emergence of nodal $d_{x^2-y^2}(\\pm)$-wave gap structure is traced to the pronounced peaks in the Lindhard response function at the BZ corners. Our calculated FS, SC critical temperature, nodal gap structure and SC quasiparticle density of states are consistent with most available experiments. Furthermore, predicted unconventional SC properties such as scanning tunneling spectra of La$_{0.8}$Sr$_{0.2}$NiO$_2$ and Pr$_{0.8}$Sr$_{0.2}$NiO$_2$ are ready for immediate experimental verifications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 11 figures and 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00512","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:04Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00511","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00511","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00511","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siu-MIng","familyName":"Tam","name":"Tam, Siu-MIng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anders","familyName":"Holmberg","name":"Holmberg, Anders","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Total Statistical Error Framework for Comparing Census Data Collection Methods"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:13:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:26:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Population censuses increasingly rely on imputation to assign usual-residence addresses for non-responding dwellings, yet no formal statistical framework has existed for comparing competing imputation methods on their combined coverage and address-accuracy performance. We develop such a framework within a Total Statistical Error (TSE) paradigm that is not restricted to survey-based data collection and applies equally to register-based and administrative data sources. The central quantity is a unit-level binary correctness indicator, equal to one if and only if a person is both enumerated and assigned to the correct usual-residence address; its complement is the unit TSE, and aggregating over the population yields a correctness rate that serves as the basis for head-to-head method comparison. We distinguish two assessment paradigms: Paradigm I, in which a post-enumeration survey (PES) provides reference values for a probability sample, and Paradigm II, in which a complete benchmark makes the correctness rate directly computable. We illustrate the framework using 2021 Australian census microdata, comparing k-nearest-neighbour and random forest imputation under a not-missing-at-random (NMAR) mechanism. A key finding is that dependent nonresponse between the census and a simulated PES causes naive Paradigm I subgroup estimates to be severely biased when subgroup deletion rates are small. Augmenting the PES response propensity model with the census nonresponse indicator reduces this bias by approximately 90%; simulation experiments show that the nonresponse indicator alone drives the correction, with the additionally included imputed set A values contributing negligible further reduction. This is the preferred strategy whenever dependent nonresponse between the census and PES is a concern."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00511","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:03Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00510","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00510","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00510","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ling","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziming","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuecong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yuecong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test-time Adaptation of Pelvic Bone Segmentation Models via Dynamic Reliability-Guided"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:13:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:26:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reliable pelvic bone segmentation (PBS) from CT is essential for robot-assisted pelvic trauma surgery, yet deploying a source-trained model to a new hospital suffers from severe performance degradation due to cross-center domain shifts. While test-time adaptation (TTA) enables online model adaptation without accessing source data, existing methods show limited effectiveness for PBS, facing challenges including boundary degradation, anatomical inconsistency under domain shifts, and voxel-level class imbalance. To address these challenges, we propose a novel closed-loop dynamic Reliability-Guided TTA framework (ReGA) for PBS. Specifically, we introduce a pseudo-label reliability criterion termed Segmentation Inference Consistency Evaluation (SICE), which jointly measures region overlap and boundary deviation via dropout-based ensemble predictions. Based on SICE, a trust-weighted refinement module adaptively updates features to mitigate boundary errors in pseudo-labels. Furthermore, a confidence-weighted region-level contrastive learning strategy is proposed to enforce anatomical consistency. Finally, ReGA follows the teacher-student (TS) scheme to alleviate voxel-level class imbalance. Experiments on three heterogeneous 3D pelvic CT datasets demonstrate that ReGA consistently outperforms state-of-the-art TTA methods, enabling effective adaptation of the source-trained PBS model to unseen clinical domains. The code is available at https://github.com/Ren-ling/ReGA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Provisionally accepted for presentation at MICCAI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00510","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:01Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00509","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00509","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00509","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Wei","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Wei-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Two $q$-Supercongruences Related to Double Sums"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:12:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:26:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, by using $q$-identities and differential operator techniques, we establish two $q$-supercongruences modulo the square of a cyclotomic polynomial, which are associated with truncated double basic hypergeometric $q$-series."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00509","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:41:00Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:41:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:41:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00508","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00508","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00508","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Geissler","name":"Geissler, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laurens","familyName":"Müller-Groh","name":"Müller-Groh, Laurens","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hans","familyName":"Meine","name":"Meine, Hans","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RadYOLO: Computationally Efficient 3D Object Detection and Segmentation in CT and MRI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:02:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:26:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Object detection and segmentation in three-dimensional medical images is a very active area of research. However, most proposed deep learning models carry a high computational cost, and only few aim to be broadly applicable, achieve high detection performance, and remain fast to execute on resource-constrained hardware. To address this gap, we present RadYOLO, a 3D extension of YOLO11 tailored to medical images. We compare it with nnU-Net and nnDetection on five datasets comprising CT and MRI data with varying object sizes and prevalence. RadYOLO's detection performance surpasses that of nnDetection on four of five datasets and is comparable on one. Compared to nnU-Net, RadYOLO performs better on lesion detection tasks, while nnU-Net excels at detecting large organs when precise localization is required. When rough object localization is sufficient, RadYOLO matches or outperforms nnU-Net on all five datasets. Regarding inference time, RadYOLO is 8-46x faster than nnU-Net on a GPU. Compared to nnDetection the speedup is even higher. When executed on a CPU, RadYOLO's inference runs within seconds (still faster than nnU-Net on a GPU) offering a significant advantage for clinical and edge-device deployment.\n RadYOLO repository: https://github.com/FraunhoferMEVIS/RadYOLO"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00508","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:59Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00507","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00507","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00507","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sejin","familyName":"Yoo","name":"Yoo, Sejin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Learning Objective Governs Perceptual Narrowing: A Cross-Lingual, Layer-Wise, Ten-Seed Study of Self-Supervised Speech Encoders"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T08:02:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:26:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Perceptual narrowing---the developmental loss of non-native phoneme discrimination in the first year of life \\citep{werker1984}---is a canonical developmental finding, yet \\emph{what learning objective produces it} remains open. We train a \\(\\sim\\)7\\,M-parameter Transformer encoder on child-directed and read speech and evaluate phoneme ABX in English, French, and Mandarin over ten seeds, the seed as the unit of replication. Six results. \\textbf{(1)}~The objective sets the direction of cross-lingual transfer: reconstruction (masked mel-prediction) degrades non-native discrimination, prediction (frame-contrastive) improves it---a same-encoder, same-data gap of \\(+0.051\\) in first-layer Mandarin ABX (\\(p=3\\times10^{-8}\\)), unanimous in sign across twenty runs. \\textbf{(2)}~That decline combines a large arm-intrinsic difficulty gradient with a smaller language-specialization effect (matched vs.\\ mismatched \\(+0.022\\), \\(p=10^{-4}\\), all four layers). \\textbf{(3)}~Against a language-symmetric raw-mel floor, reconstruction pushes the first layer \\emph{below} the discriminability of its input; prediction pushes it \\emph{above}. \\textbf{(4)}~Read speech gives a \\(3.6\\times\\) steeper non-native decline than child-directed speech. \\textbf{(5)}~The customary three-seed budget cannot see this reliably: an effect unambiguous at ten seeds is called significant by as few as 70\\% of three-seed subsets. \\textbf{(6)}~Six objective configurations---sharpening, compression, consolidation, their composition, and word-level semantic grounding in two forms---fail to produce the full developmental signature (native improves \\emph{and} non-native declines): a single objective moves both languages the same way because it acts on a shared representation. We conclude that the objective, not the architecture, is the first-order determinant of narrowing-shaped representational change."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00507","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:57Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00505","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00505","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00505","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjian","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Benjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaishun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kaishun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Extremal cross $t$-intersecting families under $t$-covering number constraints for vector spaces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:56:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:25:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $V$ be an $n$-dimensional vector space over the finite field $\\mathbb{F}_q$, and ${V\\brack k}$ denote the family of all $k$-dimensional subspaces of $V$. The families $\\mathcal{F}\\subseteq {V\\brack k}$ and $\\mathcal{G}\\subseteq {V\\brack \\ell}$ are said to be cross $t$-intersecting if $\\dim(F\\cap G)\\geq t$ for all $F\\in\\mathcal{F}$ and $G\\in \\mathcal{G}$. In this paper, we determine the extremal structures when $|\\mathcal{F}||\\mathcal{G}|$ attains the maximum value under the conditions $\\dim\\left(\\cap_{F\\in \\mathcal{F}}F\\right)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00505","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:55Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00503","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00503","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00503","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moumita","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Moumita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dipanjan","familyName":"Ray","name":"Ray, Dipanjan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sourabh","familyName":"Bhattacharya","name":"Bhattacharya, Sourabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Recursive Gaussian Processes and the Bayesian Brain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:53:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:25:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Predictive coding offers a powerful framework for cortical computation, yet scalable implementations that respect both Bayesian exactness and neurobiological constraints remain scarce. We bridge this gap by formally connecting predictive coding to Recursive Gaussian Processes (RGPs). RGPs employ a single Gaussian process \\( g(t, \\cdot) \\) indexed by layer index and input value, preventing the representational collapse of standard deep Gaussian processes while allowing learnable cross-layer dependence via \\( r_{1g} \\). We demonstrate that RGPs intrinsically implement hierarchical Bayesian inference, uncertainty propagation, and precision-weighted prediction error. Critically, we map RGP components---the shared GP, spike-and-slab variable selection, and MCMC dynamics---onto the canonical cortical microcircuit, providing a neurobiological substrate for these computations. Drawing on the free energy principle, we show that RGP inference minimizes variational free energy, formally linking Bayesian mechanics to neuronal dynamics. Our synthesis positions RGPs as both a principled computational tool and a candidate model for the brain's predictive machinery, generating testable predictions for laminar-specific dynamics and spectral asymmetries between feedforward and feedback processing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"What is your thought process? The Bayesian Recursive Gaussian process?"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00503","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:52Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00502","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00502","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00502","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenlu","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Chenlu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donghui","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Donghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiaoxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hongwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SpatialAfford: Teaching Compact VLMs Where to Look and Where to Ground for Affordance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:53:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:25:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Affordance grounding aims to localize the functional region for interaction, such as the handle to grasp or the button to press, rather than the whole object. This makes it more challenging than generic visual grounding because the target region is smaller, more ambiguous, and more dependent on task context, especially for compact vision-language models (VLMs) used in embodied settings. Recent sequence-level supervision and reinforcement learning improve coordinate prediction quality, yet compact autoregressive VLMs still lack reliable affordance-aware visual focus before coordinate generation: the model can produce better coordinate tokens while its cross-modal attention remains diffuse and weakly anchored to the true affordance evidence. To address it, we propose SpatialAfford, a two-stage framework that first aligns attention to the ground-truth affordance region through Spatial Attention Alignment (SAA), then refines coordinate prediction with Spatial-Aware GRPO. By explicitly teaching the model where to look before optimizing where to ground, SpatialAfford turns affordance grounding from a purely output-constrained objective into attention-grounded spatial reasoning. Across ShareRobot-Bench, ReasonAff, and PartAfford, SpatialAfford consistently improves affordance grounding, with a compact 4B model outperforming stronger 7B+ baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00502","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:51Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00499","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00499","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00499","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wendi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wendi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weichao","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Weichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihang","familyName":"Ran","name":"Ran, Weihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujie","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinqiang","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yinqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optical Flow from Photons"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:45:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:25:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Optical flow remains challenging in high-speed and low-light scenes, where the limited frame rate and sensitivity of conventional cameras lead to motion blur and underexposure. Single-photon avalanche diode (SPAD) cameras offer single-photon sensitivity and extremely fine temporal sampling. However, individual slices in these high FPS binary photon streams are too sparse for dense correspondence. Temporal aggregation can provide the spatial cues required by optical flow, but accumulating photons at fixed coordinates blurs moving structures. Motion-aware aggregation can reduce this blur, yet it depends on the flow being estimated. To address this dependency, we propose QuantaFlow, the first method for dense optical flow directly from SPAD streams. Instead of constructing a fixed input representation, QuantaFlow embeds SPAD representation construction into iterative flow refinement. At each iteration, the current flow coarsely aligns the slices within the source and target sub-streams. A photon-flux transformation then constructs multi-scale representations containing intensity and structural cues, while adaptive multi-scale fusion balances photon noise and residual motion blur at each pixel. The fused representations drive a feature-warping flow update, and the refined flow guides representation construction in the next iteration. We further construct a synthetic dataset for SPAD optical-flow training and evaluation. Experiments on the synthetic dataset and real-world SPAD data demonstrate the effectiveness and generalization of QuantaFlow."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00499","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:47Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00498","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00498","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00498","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuminobu","familyName":"Takahashi","name":"Takahashi, Fuminobu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Nelson-Barr Inflation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:43:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:25:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Nelson-Barr mechanism solves the strong CP problem through spontaneous CP violation, but the resulting CP-conjugate vacua generically lead to stable domain walls. We propose that the Nelson-Barr scalar itself drives hilltop inflation. A CP-symmetric double-well potential for its imaginary component forms two CP-breaking valleys, with inflation proceeding along one of them. Since the lowest CP-invariant ridge is generically much higher than the energy available after inflation, transitions between the two branches are energetically inaccessible, and the domain walls cannot be regenerated after inflation. A small CP-preserving linear deformation raises the scalar spectral index of quartic hilltop inflation and yields CMB-compatible parameter regions. The Nelson-Barr sector necessarily provides a portal to visible-sector reheating. When coupled to right-handed neutrinos, it can also transmit the spontaneous CP phase to the lepton sector and, in an extension that allows a higher CP-breaking scale, even realize nonthermal leptogenesis. This establishes a unified cosmological realization of the Nelson--Barr mechanism in which the same CP-breaking dynamics solves the strong CP problem, can generate quark and lepton CP violation, drives inflation and reheating, and enables baryogenesis, while simultaneously eliminating the associated domain-wall problem."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00498","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:45Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00497","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00497","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00497","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Igor","familyName":"Buyanov","name":"Buyanov, Igor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Darya","familyName":"Yaskova","name":"Yaskova, Darya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danil","familyName":"Serenko","name":"Serenko, Danil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danil","familyName":"Shkereda","name":"Shkereda, Danil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrey","familyName":"Yaskov","name":"Yaskov, Andrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilya","familyName":"Sochenkov","name":"Sochenkov, Ilya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The methodology of Constructing the Large-Scale Dataset for Detecting Presuicidal and Anti-Suicidal Signals in Social Media Texts in Russian"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:41:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:25:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.15514/ispras-2025-37(6)-29","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The suicide is a terrifying act of a person who is misled by his own mental state. This problem arises across many countries. Sadly, Russia also has quite high number of persons who committed suicide. Luckily, a subset of these people writes their struggles in social media, allowing a way to find them and help. However, these valuable texts disappearing in many irrelevant texts which is considerably slowing down the decision process about person's suicidal risk. To tackle this problem, in this work we have presented a detailed methodology of building the dataset for detecting texts that describe presuicidal and anti-suicidal signals. This methodology describes the process of instruction and class table creation, the process of annotation, verification and post-annotation correction. Guiding by this methodology, we collect and annotate a large-scale Russian dataset with more than 50 thousand texts from social media. We provide a count statistic of the dataset as well as common problems in annotation. We also conduct basic experiments of building the classification models to show the on go performance on different levels of annotation. Furthermore, we make the dataset, code and all materials publicly available."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00497","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:44Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00496","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00496","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00496","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liwen","familyName":"He","name":"He, Liwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiachen","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Jiachen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SkinSpline: A Body-Attached Skeleton-Supported Haptic Interface for Continuous Skin Deformation through Physical Interpolation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:38:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:25:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3830727.3834841","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present SkinSpline, a body-attached skeleton-supported haptic interface that renders continuous skin deformation through physical interpolation of sparse mechanical actuation. SkinSpline combines a low-resolution array of rack-and-pinion linear actuators with an elastic interlocking skeleton that transforms discrete actuator motions into smooth surface deformation, enabling continuous cutaneous feedback without dense actuator arrays. The system includes a modular hardware architecture, a configurable control pipeline, and a visual interface supporting real-time configuration and actuation. We demonstrate SkinSpline through multiple scenarios, including wave rendering, video-synchronized rhythmic touch, visually driven water-wave feedback in VR, and sensor-based remote touch reproduction. SkinSpline explores an alternative approach to continuous on-body haptic rendering by leveraging structural coupling between sparse actuation and deformable surfaces."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"4 pages. To appear in the Proceedings of the 2026 ACM International Symposium on Wearable Computers (ISWC '26), October 11--15, 2026, Shanghai, China"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00496","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:42Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00495","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00495","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00495","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Youyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longtao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Longtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ruoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taolue","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Taolue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Di","name":"Di, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eduard","familyName":"Dragut","name":"Dragut, Eduard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sian","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Sian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Error-bounded Point Cloud Compression Using Truncated Octahedron Quantization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:37:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:25:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid advancement of large-scale scientific simulations, the massive volume of point cloud data generated has increasingly become a critical bottleneck for scientific storage systems and data management pipelines. Existing point cloud compression techniques integrated into scientific storage systems are designed for sparse geometry and rely on quantization schemes whose optimality assumptions do not hold for dense data. When applied at the compression layer to point clouds, this representation mismatch leads to fundamentally sub-optimal rate-distortion trade-offs that cannot be addressed through parameter tuning or framework-level adaptations. This mismatch increases storage overhead and limits efficient movement and downstream analysis of simulation outputs. This issue arises in scientific data management workflows handling large-scale dense particle datasets. State-of-the-art compression methods fail to fully exploit the redundancies inherent in such data.\n We address this limitation by developing a theory of point cloud compressibility for dense data, characterizing fundamental rate-distortion behavior at the representation layer. Guided by this analysis, we introduce XnYZip, an error-bounded lossy compressor based on provably optimal Truncated Octahedron quantization, combined with a locality-aware encoding pipeline using space-filling curves and run-length encoding. Experiments on large-scale scientific datasets demonstrate consistent storage and throughput improvements, achieving up to 3x higher compression ratios, 2.2x faster compression, and 1.2x faster decompression compared to state-of-the-art point cloud compressors under same distortion."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted by VLDB 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00495","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:41Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00494","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00494","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00494","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aofan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Aofan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingxiang","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Jingxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangxin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Fangxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongbiao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yongbiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TaPR: Test-Aware Policy Refinement for Feedback-Conditioned Code Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:35:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:25:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-turn code agents rely on execution feedback to repair incorrect programs, yet standard reinforcement learning paradigms optimize and evaluate policy performance primarily using single-shot outcome rewards. This misalignment conflates initial code generation with feedback-driven refinement, discards granular execution signals across intermediate turns, and fails to evaluate whether the policy actually acquires self-repair capabilities. We propose Test-aware Policy Refinement (TaPR), a framework that transforms execution feedback into a dense per-turn test-pass-ratio reward under a consistent multi-turn interaction protocol. Across six models on 219 code-generation problems from LiveCodeBench, TaPR improves the pooled three-turn success rate (Pass@3) by 2.44 percentage points. In the predefined 7B/8B high-headroom slice, pooled accuracy increases from 30.25% to 33.56% (+3.31 pp), with 42 improvements and 13 regressions in paired trials. On a matched Qwen3-8B ablation, the dense reward supplies nonzero feedback in all of the first ten steps and reaches a higher Hard-subset peak than outcome-only GRPO within the tested budget, although GRPO nearly matches pooled Pass@3 by step 300. Our primary contribution is a reward-decomposition framework and a turn-aware evaluation protocol that decouple first-shot generation quality from multi-turn repair competence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 3 figures, 3 tables. Aofan Liu and Jingxiang Meng contributed equally; Fangxin Liu and Yongbiao Chen are corresponding authors"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00494","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:40Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00493","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00493","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00493","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongbin","familyName":"Suo","name":"Suo, Hongbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haonan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Haonan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hidden-Domain Routing for All-Type Audio Deepfake Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:32:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:25:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3767308.3836630","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"All-type audio deepfake detection requires authenticity decisions across speech, environmental sound, singing voice, and music, while the audio type is unavailable at inference time. In AT-ADD Track2, this setting creates a hidden audio-domain condition: the binary real/fake label is shared across domains, but representation structure and detector-score behavior vary with audio type. We present a closed-condition routed system that first recovers the hidden audio domain and then interprets detector scores within the selected branch. The AudioType-BEATs-6s Router estimates audio type from a 6-second window; speech inputs are handled by the Speech-XLSR Expert, while sound, singing, and music rely on EAT-based general-audio experts with branch-local score interpretation. Development-set representation analysis, router-family comparisons, and component results show audio-domain separation and complementary detector strengths across audio types. On the official AT-ADD Track2 final evaluation, the system achieves 96.10% Track2 Macro-F1 and ranks first on the final leaderboard, with type-wise Macro-F1 scores of 88.07%, 98.18%, 99.07%, and 99.08% for speech, sound, singing, and music, respectively. These results support recovering the hidden audio domain before interpreting detector scores in all-type audio deepfake detection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACMMM 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00493","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:38Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00492","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00492","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00492","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sourabh","familyName":"Bhattacharya","name":"Bhattacharya, Sourabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Bayesian Reflex: A Predictive Coding Engine for Artificial Intelligence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:32:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:25:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Predictive coding offers a powerful theory of cortical computation, but corresponding scalable algorithmic implementations for artificial intelligence have remained elusive. This paper introduces the Bayesian reflex, a computational framework that directly instantiates predictive coding through three pillars: belief maintenance via hierarchical generative models, sequential Bayesian updating via prediction-error minimization, and uncertainty-driven action via active inference. We show that recent breakthroughs---ellipsoidal decomposition for exact $i.i.d.$ sampling, recursive Gaussian processes for deep hierarchical inference, and derivative-aware Bayesian optimization---provide the missing algorithmic ingredients. The resulting framework enables mathematically principled, scalable, and brain-inspired continual learning, perception, and decision-making. We illustrate its versatility through applications ranging from climate model evaluation to prime number discovery, offering a blueprint for truly adaptive artificial intelligence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"From the Bayesian Brain to Artificial Intelligence"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00492","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:37Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00491","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00491","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00491","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruichen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ruichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingxiang","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Jingxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Wenhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiaxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linsey","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Linsey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ravid","familyName":"Shwartz-Ziv","name":"Shwartz-Ziv, Ravid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yann","familyName":"LeCun","name":"LeCun, Yann","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuefan","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Yuefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HP-JEPA: Hierarchical Partitioning for Multi-Resolution Graph Joint-Embedding Predictive Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.5.1; G.2.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:28:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:25:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graph self-supervised learning aims to learn transferable representations from large-scale unlabeled graph data. Joint-embedding predictive architectures (JEPAs) avoid explicit negative-pair construction and raw-input reconstruction by predicting masked targets directly in latent space. However, existing graph JEPAs typically rely on a single predefined graph partition, biasing the learned representations toward one structural granularity and limiting their ability to capture complementary patterns at different graph scales. To address this limitation, we propose HP-JEPA, a hierarchical partitioning framework for multi-resolution graph joint-embedding prediction. HP-JEPA organizes each graph into an ordered bank of coarse-to-fine partition resolutions and performs context-target latent prediction separately at each resolution using an online encoder, an exponential-moving-average target encoder, and a latent predictor. The resulting resolution-specific graph representations are subsequently integrated through concatenation or task-specific resolution weighting, allowing downstream models to combine complementary local, regional, and global structural information. Experiments on seven graph classification benchmarks and one graph regression benchmark show that HP-JEPA outperforms the fixed-resolution Graph-JEPA baseline on 6 of 8 tasks, improving upon Graph-JEPA on most evaluated benchmarks. Size-stratified analyses further show that HP-JEPA achieves higher accuracy than Graph-JEPA in most evaluated graph-size quartiles on three representative datasets. These results highlight the effectiveness of hierarchical multi-resolution partitioning for transferable graph representation learning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 4 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00491","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:35Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00490","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00490","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00490","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Misora","familyName":"Sugiyama","name":"Sugiyama, Misora","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toya","familyName":"Oyama","name":"Oyama, Toya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hirokatsu","familyName":"Kataoka","name":"Kataoka, Hirokatsu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Image-Space Rule Discovery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:26:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:25:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Can image-editing models discover visual rules in image space and complete problem-solving end-to-end? We tackle this question in the spirit of a human worksheet test (e.g., an IQ test), using problems that require models to read image-based instructions, recognize the problem, infer the answer, bind it to the correct destination, control output count, suppress unnecessary edits, and preserve the input and format. We introduce WISRD, a Worksheet Image-Space Rule Discovery benchmark with 11 core tasks under eight information conditions, spanning localized marking, filling, copying, counting, and no-edit suppression, together with four supplementary reasoning-stress probes for multi-step spatial manipulation, abstract pattern reasoning, logical inference, and constraint-based problem solving. We identify three key findings as follows. (i) Among the frontier image-editing models evaluated, Nano Banana Pro achieves the highest score. On the shared V0--V3 no-reference subset, the Auto-Strict proxy pass rates are 48.7% for Nano Banana Pro, 13.4\\% for Qwen-Image-Edit, 11.5% for FLUX.2 Klein 4B API, 11.3% for FLUX.2 Klein 4B open-weight, and 0.0% for InstructPix2Pix. (ii) Analysis reveals that current image-editing models can partially rely on rendered in-image instructions even when the external prompt is absent or merely generic. (iii) In small supplementary diagnostics, Nano Banana Pro achieves 70.0% on 4-by-4 Sudoku and 22.9% on public RAVEN pattern-discovery items in image space."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00490","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:34Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00489","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00489","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00489","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wendi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wendi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujie","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zengxi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zengxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiyang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Haiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihang","familyName":"Ran","name":"Ran, Weihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinqiang","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yinqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Practical Noise Modeling for SPAD Intensity Imaging"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:25:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:25:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Single-photon avalanche diode (SPAD) cameras are promising for low-light and high-dynamic-range intensity imaging, but their practical use is limited by complex sensor-specific noise. Unlike time-correlated single-photon counting (TCSPC) systems, SPAD cameras record whether at least one detection occurred in each gate without photon timestamps in intensity imaging mode, making explicit noise decomposition difficult. We present a practical noise modeling and calibration framework for SPAD intensity denoising. Our forward model describes binary-frame accumulation with a Binomial observation process, models signal-independent dark noise as an exposure-dependent pure dark count term plus an exposure-independent dark-frame bias term, and incorporates pixel-wise response non-uniformity. We design a dedicated calibration procedure for the proposed model and use it to build a count-domain noise-synthesis pipeline for network training. For denoising, we further design a SPAD-specific dark-shading correction (SPAD-DSC) to remove most systematic noise before network training. We construct a real-world SPAD intensity dataset for testing. Experimental results demonstrate the superiority of the proposed noise model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00489","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:33Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00488","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00488","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00488","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L.","familyName":"Elesin","name":"Elesin, L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. L.","familyName":"Shilov","name":"Shilov, A. L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Kravtsov","name":"Kravtsov, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"X.","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, X.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Lukianov","name":"Lukianov, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Kuksov","name":"Kuksov, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Jana","name":"Jana, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"I.","familyName":"Iorsh","name":"Iorsh, I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R.","familyName":"Izmaylov","name":"Izmaylov, R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K.","familyName":"Shein","name":"Shein, K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"I.","familyName":"Gayduchenko","name":"Gayduchenko, I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T.","familyName":"Taniguchi","name":"Taniguchi, T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K.","familyName":"Watanabe","name":"Watanabe, K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K. S.","familyName":"Novoselov","name":"Novoselov, K. S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G. N.","familyName":"Goltsman","name":"Goltsman, G. N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Principi","name":"Principi, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D. A.","familyName":"Bandurin","name":"Bandurin, D. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Correlated Insulator Moiré Bolometer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Other Condensed Matter (cond-mat.other)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"J.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"NA","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:25:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:24:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Light incident on an insulator is generally not expected to turn it into a metal without invoking intense ultrafast excitation that leads to transient structural transitions. Here we show that magic-angle twisted bilayer graphene tuned to half filling of the moiré band provides a notable exception to this expectation. We find that weak beam of long-wavelength photons, with energies comparable to the flat-band width, selectively heat the low-heat-capacity electronic subsystem, thereby suppressing the correlated gap. This produces a giant resistance change governed not by a persistent photocarrier population, but by the extreme sensitivity of a many-body correlated gap to weak electronic heating. The resulting photon-driven insulator-to-metal transition produces a broadband low-noise photoresponse with voltage responsivity exceeding millivolts per nW of absorbed power. The mechanism is dual to superconducting hot-electron response: radiation-heated electrons suppress a many-body order, but in reverse the correlated insulator melts into a metal, providing robustness to magnetic fields of several tesla and a sharp insulator-to-metal resistive contrast. Our results establish correlated flat-band systems as a platform for ultra-sensitive detection of faint long-wavelength radiation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"37 pages and 20 figures (main text and SI together)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00488","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:31Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00487","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00487","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00487","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuvraj","familyName":"Sahni","name":"Sahni, Yuvraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiannong","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Jiannong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fu","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Fu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Collaborative Orbital Edge Intelligence: A Decentralized Paradigm for Energy-Efficient Computing in Space"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:24:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:24:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/mnet.2026.3700795","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, Low Earth Orbit (LEO) satellites have been increasingly deployed to enable connectivity in remote and disaster-prone areas. Researchers have proposed Orbital Edge Computing, which adds computational intelligence to LEO satellites to process data on orbit, providing edge intelligence close to space data sources. Existing work on Orbital Edge Computing typically assumes centralized control without collaboration among satellites from different providers, leading to limited connectivity, higher latency, and increased satellite battery depletion. They have not fully explored decentralized inter-satellite collaboration for energy-efficient intelligence under heterogeneous LEO constellations. This paper introduces a novel paradigm, Collaborative Orbital Edge Intelligence (COEI), that leverages decentralized collaboration among LEO satellites to enable energy-efficient on-orbit processing of space data. We describe the overall system architecture of COEI, including the issues related to networking, computing, and power management. COEI can help create a multi-party, multi-orbit megaconstellation of satellites that delivers better service quality by providing benefits, including global resilient connectivity, real-time intelligence, and energy-efficient services. To demonstrate the COEI benefits, we conduct a case study on decentralized energy-aware satellite task offloading to maximize the task success rate while minimizing the sum of the maximum battery depth-of-discharge across all satellites. Finally, we outline future directions for COEI that offer opportunities for further investigation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 4 figures, 2 tables. Accepted for publication in IEEE Network"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00487","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:30Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00486","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00486","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00486","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongsheng","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Tongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Boyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luyang","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Luyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DreamTraj: Generating 6-DoF Object Trajectories by Reading Unrendered Video Diffusion Latents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:24:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:24:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate prediction of object trajectories during manipulation is essential for closing the perception-action loop. Progress is limited on two fronts: available datasets lack fine-grained language-to-motion annotations, and existing predictors either rely on privileged inputs such as video, depth, or CAD models, or recover motion from fully generated videos through costly, error-prone perception pipelines. We close the supervision gap with the MOVE dataset, 5,038 object-centric egocentric trajectories, each paired with a fine-grained natural-language instruction rather than a coarse verb-noun label. We further propose DreamTraj, which predicts a 6-DoF object trajectory from a single RGB image and a task instruction, requiring no video, depth, or CAD model at inference: rather than generating a video, it reads motion from the internal representations of a frozen image-to-video diffusion model at an early denoising step. A lightweight flow-matching Reader decodes query-key attention tracks and pooled hidden states into relative 6-DoF poses. To our knowledge, this is the first approach to directly decode object 6-DoF trajectories from intermediate video diffusion representations rather than generated pixels. DreamTraj sets a new state of the art on both translation and rotation against forecasters that consume multi-frame or privileged inputs, and runs 4.6x faster than generate-then-extract pipelines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 8 figures, 11 tables. Project page: https://whathappen0.github.io/DreamTraj/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00486","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:28Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00483","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00483","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00483","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kasidis","familyName":"Arunruangsirilert","name":"Arunruangsirilert, Kasidis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heming","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Heming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiro","familyName":"Katto","name":"Katto, Jiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Streamable Neural Video Compression: A Mixed Precision Approach for Cross-Platform Deployment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:17:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:24:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural Video Codecs (NVCs) offer unprecedented rate-distortion performance, making them highly attractive for bandwidth-constrained environments like 5G cellular networks and emerging satellite direct-to-cell (D2C) links. However, deploying NVCs in real-world streaming applications is severely hindered by cross-platform floating-point non-determinism, which causes arithmetic entropy coders to desynchronize and crash across different GPU architectures. While recent integer-based quantization methods address this, they incur either massive degradation in compression efficiency (INT8) or severe computational bottlenecks by bypassing hardware acceleration (INT16). In this paper, we propose a streamable, client-server NVC architecture featuring a novel Mixed Precision (FP16/FP32) strategy. By strategically executing P-frames in hardware-accelerated FP16 for real-time throughput, while forcing I-frames and periodic feature-adapter resets to IEEE-754 compliant FP32, we guarantee deterministic synchronization at critical boundaries. Through extensive cross-encode/decode evaluations across 12 GPUs spanning four architectural generations, we demonstrate that our approach successfully eliminates intra-generation fragmentation and substantially broadens cross-die interoperability, achieving seamless cross-generation decodability for recent architectures at 1080p. Crucially, this is achieved with a negligible impact on compression efficiency. Furthermore, we evaluate the system's end-to-end latency across diverse real-world networks, including Wi-Fi 6, 5G NR (FDD/TDD), and Starlink D2C, proving the practical viability of streamable learned video compression while highlighting unique challenges in Non-Terrestrial Networks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"2026 IEEE Global Communications Conference (GLOBECOM 2026), 7-11 December 2026, Macau S.A.R., China"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00483","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:24Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00482","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00482","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00482","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathieu","familyName":"Besançon","name":"Besançon, Mathieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tung Quoc","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Tung Quoc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Iterate Convergence of AdaGrad for Generalized Smooth Convex Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:15:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:24:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove sequential convergence results for the AdaGrad algorithm family optimizing convex differentiable objectives. Specifically, we provide necessary and sufficient conditions for the convergence of iterates for the three main AdaGrad variants (AdaNorm, AdaDiag, AdaFull) when the objective is convex and locally Lipschitz-smooth, closing the question left open from the literature. We harness this general result to study the three variants under the generalized $(L_0,L_1)$-smoothness condition and show sequential convergence for sufficiently small constant step size. Moreover, under the so-called $(L_0,L_1)$-polynomially modifiable smoothness assumption, which is a relaxation of the $(L_0,L_1)$ generalized smoothness property and is satisfied by many function classes such as $L$-smooth functions or univariate polynomials, sequential convergence for these AdaGrad variants is proved for arbitrary learning rates. This result provides conditions under which AdaGrad presents adaptivity, i.e., does not require tuning the parameters based on the instance. Finally, we provide numerical illustrations of the behavior of AdaGrad on convex and nonconvex functions. In particular, we construct a counterexample empirically showing that smoothness alone is not sufficient for the sequential convergence of AdaGrad-type algorithms, and suggesting that additional geometric hypotheses (e.g., convexity as in this paper, or the Kurdyka-Łojasiewicz inequality) are indispensable for sequential convergence results."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00482","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:23Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00481","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00481","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00481","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of British Columbia"],"givenName":"Himanshu","familyName":"Mishra","name":"Mishra, Himanshu","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"F-WANDA: Fisher-Reweighted Post-Training Pruning for Sustainable Deployment of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:13:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:24:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"One-shot post-training pruning is the most energy-frugal compression strategy for largelanguage models (LLMs), yet existing approaches trade either quality (WANDA) or compute cost (SPARSEGPT). We introduce F-WANDA, a drop-in modification of WANDA that reallocates the per-row keep budget across output neurons in proportion to the empirical Fisher information of the pre-activation. The Fisher signal is collected in a single additional backward pass over the same calibration corpus WANDA already uses; no weights are updated. On LLAMA-2-7B at 50 % unstructured sparsity, F-WANDA attains WikiText-2 perplexity of 6.85, matches WANDA fluency, and improves 5-shot MMLU by +1.6 pp over WANDA and +1.1 pp over SPARSEGPT, while incurring only one-third of SPARSEGPT pruning wall-clock and energy. The headline trade-off is achieved without extra calibration data or fine-tuning, placing F-WANDA on the Pareto frontier of quality versus pruning cost for sustainable LLM compression."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00481","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:21Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00480","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00480","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00480","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deshui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Deshui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao-Ming","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Xiao-Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zishun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zishun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Dimensional Entropy for Vibration Data Quality Control in Wind Turbines: Properties, Deployment, and Industrial Implications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:11:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:24:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Erroneous vibration signals caused by sensor malfunction, shutdown transients, and abnormal acquisition conditions can degrade the reliability of automated industrial monitoring pipelines. This paper presents a deployment-oriented analysis of Multi-Dimensional Entropy (MDE) for vibration data quality control in wind turbines, focusing on computational efficiency, model-agnostic capability, physical interpretability, and robustness. Experiments on 57,643 labeled industrial vibration records from 12 wind farms and 14 turbine units, covering main bearings, gearboxes, and generators, together with cross-platform deployment validation and cross-turbine generalization tests on 4,152 unseen records from 3 additional wind farms, show that MDE provides a stable and discriminative feature representation across different classifiers and heterogeneous operating conditions while maintaining low computational and memory requirements. These results demonstrate that MDE can serve as a lightweight and deployment-ready feature layer for vibration data quality control, thereby improving the reliability of industrial monitoring pipelines and reducing the risk of error propagation into downstream diagnostic and prognostic tasks in wind energy applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 2 figures, 11 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00480","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:20Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00479","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00479","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00479","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vuong","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, Vuong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An optimal refinement-compatible bijection between singleton-free partitions and partitions without cyclic adjacencies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:10:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:24:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"It is well known that the number of partitions of $[n]$ without singletons equals the number of partitions of $[n]$ in which no block contains two cyclically adjacent elements $i,i+1\\pmod{n}$. Bernhart remarked that there might be no simple bijection between these two classes. Although Callan later constructed an algorithmic bijection proving the stronger equidistribution of singletons and adjacencies, his construction proceeds through multiple rounds of exchanges. Therefore, Bernhart's remark may still retain some validity, as suggested by Chen and Wang. In this article, we address this remark by giving a direct ``one-round'' bijection between the two classes. Unlike Callan's bijection, our map is closely compatible with the refinement order on partitions: in one direction it only decomposes blocks, while its inverse only merges blocks, with a single exceptional pair when $n\u0026gt;2$ is even. We further observe that this exception is unavoidable, establishing the optimality of the bijection with respect to the refinement order. The specific local form of these operations --- splitting off only singleton blocks and merging a singleton only with the block containing its cyclic neighbor --- also ensures that the construction restricts, without modification, to a bijection between the corresponding classes of noncrossing partitions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages; comments are welcome"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00479","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:18Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00478","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00478","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00478","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ekova: A Personality-Support Agent for Self-Discovery Dialogue"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:08:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:24:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Emotional Support (ES) systems have long optimized a single objective: alleviating the user's emotional distress in the moment. We argue that a complementary need, helping users see themselves more clearly, defines a distinct paradigm we call Personality Support (PS). PS is not counseling or clinical intervention: it targets cognitive clarity and self-articulation, not symptom relief or diagnosis. We instantiate this paradigm in three layers. First, we present DSD, a Chinese self-discovery PS Dataset of 8,590 samples collected through real longitudinal interaction across five minimal units, Coach, Warm, Tsukkomi, Real, and Gonzo. Second, we build DeepSupport, a multi-persona PS system trained with OrthoTune, a PS-tailored framework with style-specific adapters and a style-consistency regularizer. Third, we unify the five DeepSupport personas into Ekova, a persistent personality-support agent with a unified cross-session memory layer, supporting both adaptive routing and user-customized persona selection. Experiments show that OrthoTune-trained models outperform all baselines with an average relative gain of 16.3% across all metrics over the strongest prompt-based baseline. Code is available at https://github.com/Yukyin/Ekova."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the COLM 2026 Lifelong Agents Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00478","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:17Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00477","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00477","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00477","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiong-Yue","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiong-Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spinorial Div-Curl Structure and Bilinear Null-Form Estimates for Dirac Equations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:05:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:24:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We identify a physical-space balance-law mechanism underlying bilinear null forms for the free Dirac equation in three space dimensions. For each spatial direction, we decompose a spinor into the two eigenspaces of the directional Dirac symbol. The principal parts of the corresponding modes propagate in opposite directions, while the transverse derivatives and the mass term couple them. After integration over the transverse variables, their charge densities satisfy a pair of one-dimensional balance laws, and a div--curl interaction estimate controls the mixed product of these densities. The algebraic anticommutation condition defining the spinorial null form exchanges exactly the same two eigenspaces. This identifies the algebraic cancellation with the interaction selected by the balance laws. Combined with angular localization, the argument yields frequency-localized $L^2$ spacetime estimates while preserving the natural first-order formulation of the Dirac equation. In the massless case, the estimate has the same lower-frequency scaling as the three-dimensional wave null-form estimate and gains half a derivative over the direct product bound. In the massive case, we obtain a channel-dependent refinement. For the pseudoscalar interaction, anticommutation with the full massive Dirac Hamiltonian gives an additional frequency-to-mass factor $K/m$ for low-frequency interactions within the same energy branch, where $K$ is the frequency scale and $m$ is the mass. This gain is absent in the scalar channel. The bilinear estimates also yield factorized bounds for cubic spinorial null forms arising in nonlinear Dirac models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00477","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:15Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00476","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00476","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00476","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hsien-Chieh","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Hsien-Chieh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryuji","familyName":"Kondo","name":"Kondo, Ryuji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Makoto","familyName":"Arimoto","name":"Arimoto, Makoto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatsuro","familyName":"Kanenaga","name":"Kanenaga, Tatsuro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiro","familyName":"Otsuka","name":"Otsuka, Hiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shutaro","familyName":"Ueda","name":"Ueda, Shutaro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junko","familyName":"Hiraga","name":"Hiraga, Junko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daisuke","familyName":"Yonetoku","name":"Yonetoku, Daisuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatsuya","familyName":"Sawano","name":"Sawano, Tatsuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takanori","familyName":"Sakamoto","name":"Sakamoto, Takanori","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiroshi","familyName":"Tomida","name":"Tomida, Hiroshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akihiro","familyName":"Doi","name":"Doi, Akihiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiroshi","familyName":"Nakajima","name":"Nakajima, Hiroshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takaaki","familyName":"Tanaka","name":"Tanaka, Takaaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Hartmann","name":"Hartmann, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lothar","familyName":"Strüder","name":"Strüder, Lothar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Development and Evaluation of a CNN-Based Charged-Particle Event Rejection Algorithm for Soft X-ray Detection in a pnCCD-Based Satellite System"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T07:04:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:24:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"All-sky surveys in the soft X-ray band are essential for detecting transient objects such as high-redshift gamma-ray bursts (GRBs), which provide key insights into the early universe. HiZ-GUNDAM is a future satellite mission designed to detect and localize high-redshift GRBs. Its wide-field X-ray monitor, EAGLE, combines Lobster Eye Optics with a pnCCD imaging detector operating in the 0.4-4 keV band. Because of limited satellite telemetry, full-frame pnCCD images cannot be downlinked, requiring onboard event selection. Charged particles in the space environment produce background events that can be misidentified as X-ray photons, degrading detection sensitivity and potentially triggering false alerts. In this study, we developed a pnCCD readout system and evaluated charged-particle rejection using conventional grade methods and a convolutional neural network (CNN). Performance was evaluated using X-ray events from an Fe-55 source and electron events from a Sr-90 beta source. The CNN reduced the misclassification rate from 10.2-11.9% for conventional grade methods to 3.1% while maintaining a high acceptance rate for X-ray events. The improvement is particularly pronounced at higher deposited energies, reflecting the CNN's ability to distinguish track-like particle events from X-ray events by capturing detailed spatial features of charge distributions. Comparison with a thin-depletion-layer CMOS sensor further indicates that the thicker depletion layer of the pnCCD enhances discrimination performance. These results demonstrate that CNN-based event classification can substantially reduce charged-particle contamination while maintaining high X-ray acceptance, making it a promising approach for onboard event selection in future pnCCD-based wide-field X-ray missions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication in Nuclear Instruments and Methods in Physics Research Section A"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00476","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:14Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00475","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00475","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00475","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Dileep","name":"Dileep, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Patnaik","name":"Patnaik, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K.","familyName":"Sakthivel","name":"Sakthivel, K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Hasanov","name":"Hasanov, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Identification of thermal expansion coefficient in a thermoelastic plate from final time-measured displacement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"35R30, 74F05, 35B65","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T06:58:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:24:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate a coupled thermoelastic plate system consisting of a fourth-order displacement equation and a heat evolution equation linked through a spatially varying coupling factor $α(x)$. The model accounts for thermoelastic interactions through the operators $\\operatorname{div}(α(x)\\nabla θ)$ and $\\operatorname{div}(α(x)\\nabla u_t)$. We establish the well-posedness of the direct problem under homogeneous Neumann conditions for $u$ and Dirichlet conditions for $θ$, deriving optimal energy estimates and demonstrating continuous dependence of solutions on the given data. We further introduce an input-output operator corresponding to the considered inverse problem and show that it is compact and Lipschitz continuous, confirming the ill-posed nature of the associated inverse problem. Using these properties, the inverse problem is formulated as a minimization problem for the Tikhonov functional, and we establish the existence of a minimizer."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00475","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:13Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00474","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00474","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00474","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Junhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SDDMO-Bench: A Benchmark Suite for Streaming Data-Driven Dynamic Multi-Objective Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T06:56:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:24:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Streaming data-driven dynamic multi-objective optimization requires algorithms to track time-varying Pareto fronts using only sequential observations under concept drift. However, systematic evaluation remains difficult because real-world problems usually lack ground-truth optima, drift annotations, and controllable conditions, while existing benchmarks provide limited support for standardized comparison. This paper proposes SDDMO-Bench, a benchmark suite that transforms classical dynamic multi-objective test problems into streaming environments by combining intrinsic objective-mapping evolution, controllable distributional drift, and sequential data revelation. By combining five representative time-dependent base functions with six distributional drift patterns, SDDMO-Bench constructs 30 scenarios with diverse levels of non-stationarity, problem complexity, sample-distribution variation, and Pareto-front evolution. Experiments with representative evolutionary algorithms demonstrate that SDDMO-Bench provides challenging and discriminative test scenarios, offering a standardized, controllable, and reproducible benchmark for evaluating adaptability, robustness, and Pareto-front tracking in streaming data-driven dynamic multi-objective optimization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to IEEE MIND Conference. This submission contains main manuscript and supplementary materials. Corresponding author: Hui Bai"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00474","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:11Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00473","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00473","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00473","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaho","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kaho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengyu","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Pengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqin","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Yuqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianjing","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Tianjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CrossProjection: Geometric Grounding Beyond Viewpoint Change in Architectural Drawings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T06:51:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:23:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Architectural drawings violate the usual assumption behind multi-view reasoning: plans and sections are cuts, while elevations are facade projections, so corresponding components change appearance in ways camera motion cannot explain. We introduce CrossProjection, an anchor-grounded diagnostic of whether vision-language models preserve component identity and externalize geometry across heterogeneous architectural views. It evaluates Matching, Registration, and Geometric Grounding through categorical judgments, candidate selection, and free point, line, and region localization.\n Across 23 real drawing sets and 1,954 categorical conditions per model, GPT-5.5 scores 82.4%, Qwen3-VL-32B-Instruct 62.2%, and GLM-4.5V 57.2%. A matched 200-target study crosses natural and vector-text-suppressed drawings with closed-candidate and free-geometry outputs. Candidate-supported performance is often higher, but free localization remains fragile: on natural drawings, point/region PCK@.05 is 54-76% for GPT, 8-10% for Qwen, and 14-36% for GLM; line endpoint PCK@.05 is 22%, 4%, and 0%. A coordinate grid recovers some GPT point/region precision but not lines. Three architecture-trained participants reach 87.3-93.3% categorical accuracy and 76-92% GT-region hit, supporting task feasibility rather than a population-level human ceiling.\n Because the categorical families do not form a same-item Matching-Registration contrast and interface controls alter multiple burdens, we avoid mechanistic claims. The supported conclusion is narrower: closed-choice or marked-element success does not entail reliable explicit geometric grounding. For drawing-guided CAD/BIM systems, categorical correctness should not be treated as evidence of candidate-free spatial reliability. Reusable on-sheet anchors, fixed-denominator scoring, and hash-locked artifacts establish an audit trail for this gap."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Initial controlled diagnostic study on 23 natural drawing sets and three VLMs; broader model, building, repeated-inference, and human coverage is planned for a subsequent version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00473","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:10Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00472","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00472","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00472","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Brown U."],"givenName":"Kaleb","familyName":"Anderson","name":"Anderson, Kaleb","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Brown U."],"givenName":"Savvas M.","familyName":"Koushiappas","name":"Koushiappas, Savvas M.","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Kinetic backreaction cannot suppress axion quantum pressure"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T06:42:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:23:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ultralight axions from string compactifications carry non-canonical kinetic terms whose normalization is set by a coupling function that depends on an accompanying modulus field. As dark matter, such an axion forms a cold condensate whose quantum pressure resists gravitational collapse below a Jeans scale fixed by that coupling. One might hope that backreaction from the condensate's oscillations could shrink this Jeans length and thereby enhance structure growth. We show that it cannot. For any smooth, positive coupling function, the oscillation-averaged backreaction always drives the coupling to larger values, strengthening the quantum pressure and growing the Jeans length with time. We establish this no-go result analytically through an adiabatic- invariant analysis and confirm it by direct numerical integration. Kinetically coupled dark matter therefore cannot self-generate the conditions for enhanced gravitational collapse or the seeding of structure on sub-Jeans scales."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00472","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:08Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00471","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00471","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00471","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandip","familyName":"Dutta","name":"Dutta, Sandip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Filling the Shadow: A Propositional Model of Gravastar Accretion in $f(R, L_m, T)$ Gravity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"J.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"83C57, 85A30, 83C15","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T06:41:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:23:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While General Relativity remains our most rigorously tested framework for gravitation, the theoretical persistence of singularities within standard black hole solutions continues to motivate the exploration of mathematically regular alternatives. Gravitational vacuum stars (gravastars) offer a non-singular model, substituting the event horizon with a physical, ultra-stiff thin shell. Recent studies have demonstrated that extended theories, such as $f(R, L_m, T)$ gravity, can structurally support these objects by utilizing the non-minimal coupling between geometry and matter. Building upon these static foundations, this paper presents a phenomenological propositional model to explore the dynamic interactions between modified-gravity gravastars and equatorial accretion flows. By numerically solving the modified Tolman-Oppenheimer-Volkoff equations and applying a non-complexifying algorithm, we construct a mathematically regular rotating metric ansatz. We demonstrate that the modified gravity coupling parameter systematically alters the effective potential, shifting the location of the Innermost Stable Circular Orbit (ISCO). Furthermore, we explore the idealized thermodynamics of plasma colliding with the gravastar surface, suggesting a distinct thermal emission that could theoretically produce a ``filled-in'' central shadow in interferometric observations. While acknowledging the challenges of observational degeneracy and the deliberate omission of complex radiation pressure feedback, we offer these geometric and thermal signatures as a transparent conceptual baseline to motivate future general relativistic magnetohydrodynamic (GRMHD) campaigns."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00471","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:07Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00470","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00470","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00470","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanyou","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuanyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chenhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Arithmetic hypergeometric $\\mathcal {D}$-modules and exponential sums on reductive groups"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary 14F10, 14F30, Secondary 14M27, 11L07","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T06:37:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:23:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For a finite family of representations of a reductive group, we define a Laurent polynomial on the group. The exponential sum associated this Laurent polynomial is called a hypergeometric exponential sum. We introduce an arithmetic hypergeometric $\\mathcal D$-module to study the hypergeometric exponential sum. It is an overholonomic arithmetic $\\mathcal D$-module with a Frobenius structure so that the trace of the Frobenius at a rational point is the exponential sum. Over the locus where the Laurent polynomial is nondegenerate, the arithmetic hypergeometric $\\mathcal {D}$-module defines an $F$-isocrystal overconvergent along the degenerate locus. As an application, we get an estimation of the hypergeometric exponential sum."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00470","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:06Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00469","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00469","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00469","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubham","familyName":"Farswan","name":"Farswan, Shubham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reshma","familyName":"Kumawat","name":"Kumawat, Reshma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dipankar","familyName":"Sarkar","name":"Sarkar, Dipankar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deeksha","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Deeksha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md. Atif","familyName":"Hasan","name":"Hasan, Md. Atif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Devajyoti","familyName":"Mukherjee","name":"Mukherjee, Devajyoti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaushik","familyName":"Sen","name":"Sen, Kaushik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Negative thermal expansion, lattice dynamics, and complex magnetism in TbFeO$_3$"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T06:29:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:23:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We report a temperature-dependent investigation of orthoferrite TbFeO$_3$ using x-ray diffraction, DC magnetization, and Raman scattering, complemented by room-temperature x-ray photoelectron spectroscopy. X-ray diffraction reveals negative thermal expansion over 5-300 K, with a small but systematic increase in unit-cell volume upon cooling in the absence of any structural phase transition. Raman scattering measurements identify the Raman-active phonon modes and show clear deviations from the conventional Klemens anharmonic decay model, particularly in phonon frequencies, indicating the presence of spin-phonon coupling. Two modes of $A_g$ and $B_{1g}$ symmetry exhibit a crossover from Gaussian-dominated line shapes at low temperatures to mixed Gaussian-Lorentzian profiles at higher temperatures, reflecting a transition from inhomogeneous broadening to lifetime-driven dynamics. High-energy Raman spectra reveal two-magnon excitations associated with the Fe sublattice, consistent with linear spin-wave theory, whose spectral weight shows only weak temperature dependence. In addition, a broad Raman mode emerging below $\\sim 175$ K exhibits an order-parameter-like temperature evolution and coincides with the onset of phonon anomalies, while no corresponding strong anomaly is observed in the two-magnon response. Taken together, these results establish TbFeO$_3$ as a system with pronounced interplay among lattice dynamics, spin correlations, and emergent local magnetic-lattice anomalies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 19 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00469","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:04Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00468","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00468","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00468","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hideyasu","familyName":"Yamashita","name":"Yamashita, Hideyasu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What is electric charge?: Charge as a local observable in relativistic quantum field theory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"81T05 (Primary), 81P05 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T06:17:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:23:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"It is rather surprising that modern quantum physics does not appear to have provided any clear answer to the simple question ``what is electric charge?''. Even when the total charge operator $Q$ is well-defined, the non-locality of $Q$ implies that it is not an observable in the usual sense, which can be measured by a (local) experimental apparatus. A candidate for the ``local version'' of charge operator is the 4-current operator $j=(j^μ)_{μ=0,1,2,3}$. However, it is known that the rigorous definition of $j$ is difficult in $(3+1)$-dimensional Minkowski space. Although it was found that the current can be defined in $(1+1)$-dimensions (Carey et al.), I argue that even when $j$ can be suitably defined, the interpretability of $j$ as the ``local charge operator'' is dubious. Instead I return to Araki and Wyss (1964), and propose the concept of ``scope-local charge'' $Q_ζ(P)$ for a ``scope'' $P$, expressed by a finite-dimensional projection. I work in an abstract $C^{*}$-algebraic setting."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00468","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:03Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00467","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00467","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00467","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farid","familyName":"Labib","name":"Labib, Farid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guillaume","familyName":"Beutier","name":"Beutier, Guillaume","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Bombardi","name":"Bombardi, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc","familyName":"de Boissieu","name":"de Boissieu, Marc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryuji","familyName":"Tamura","name":"Tamura, Ryuji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Breakdown of Universal Whirling Order in a Heisenberg Tsai-Type Approximant"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T06:16:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:23:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Noncoplanar whirling magnetic order has recently been proposed as a universal magnetic state in non-Heisenberg Tsai-type quasicrystal approximants. Here, X-ray resonant magnetic scattering measurements on Heisenberg and non-Heisenberg Au-Al-R (R = Gd, Tb) 1/1 approximants reveal a clear breakdown of this universality in the Heisenberg limit. While the non-Heisenberg Au-Al-Tb system is well described by the previously identified whirling magnetic structure, the Heisenberg Au-Al-Gd counterpart exhibits qualitatively different azimuthal-angle dependences that are incompatible with the universal whirling-order model. Despite nearly identical crystal structures and comparable magnetic energy scales, the two systems stabilize distinct antiferromagnetic ground states with the same propagation vector k = (1, 0, 0), demonstrating that the apparent universality of whirling order is not a consequence of Tsai-cluster geometry alone but requires spin anisotropy. These results further reveal the emergence of a competing manifold of nearly degenerate magnetic states in the Heisenberg limit."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages; 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00467","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:01Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00466","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00466","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00466","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad A.","familyName":"Ayoubi","name":"Ayoubi, Mohammad A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sina","familyName":"Piramoon","name":"Piramoon, Sina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SPIRAL-PO: Symbolic Identification of Partially Observed Nonlinear Dynamics with Application to Rotating Machinery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T06:14:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:23:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many engineering systems have states that cannot be directly measured-tilt angles in rotating machines, internal flow variables, aeroelastic modes-yet these hidden states couple into the measured outputs. This paper addresses identifying the governing equations from such partial observations. We present SPIRA-PO (Symbolic Physics-Informed Residual Augmentation Loop-Partially Observed), a framework that treats hidden-state effects not as nuisances to be eliminated but as structured, physically interpretable signatures in the observable equations of motion. From a minimal physics seed in measured coordinates, the method fits a multi-output residual network to the projection residual, projects the learned structure onto a physics-constrained candidate library, and admits terms through a sequential statistical gating protocol. We give sufficient conditions for unique recovery of the hidden-coupling coefficients, a matching impossibility result showing that insufficiently rich excitation makes recovery impossible for any estimator, and a closed-form sample-complexity bound. These guarantees concern the projected hidden-coupling coefficients, not the hidden trajectory itself, which is reconstructed separately by a state estimator. The framework is demonstrated on a vertical flexible rotor with Duffing supports, where only the lateral displacements are measured while the tilt angles remain hidden. A constant-speed run is provably unidentifiable, whereas a speed sweep restores identifiability; from noisy coast-down data, SPIRAL-PO recovers the gyroscopic coupling, the Duffing nonlinearity, and the translation-tilt cross-coupling, each with a standard error and t-statistic. An Extended Kalman Filter built on the validated model then reconstructs the hidden tilt trajectory from observed displacements alone."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages, 6 figures. Submitted to Nonlinear Dynamics (Springer)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00466","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:40:00Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:40:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:40:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00464","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00464","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00464","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryo","familyName":"Hanai","name":"Hanai, Ryo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukiyasu","familyName":"Domaea","name":"Domaea, Yukiyasu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ixchel G.","familyName":"Ramirez-Alpizar","name":"Ramirez-Alpizar, Ixchel G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdullah","familyName":"Mustafa","name":"Mustafa, Abdullah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Floris","familyName":"Erich","name":"Erich, Floris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tetsuya","familyName":"Ogata","name":"Ogata, Tetsuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Predict Contact Force Distributions from Vision Leveraging Object Geometry Priors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T06:11:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:23:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1080/01691864.2026.2693560","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Based on vision and prior experience, humans can make rough physical predictions and adjust their manipulation strategies. This paper aims to endow robots with a similar ability. To collect paired data of vision and forces, we use a rigid-body simulator commonly adopted in robotics. However, unlike simulators that output noisy point forces, humans are able to make consistent predictions even in unfamiliar situations. Based on this observation, we hypothesize that predicting smooth force distributions rather than raw point forces can improve both force prediction itself and downstream task performance. To validate this hypothesis, we construct a model that predicts three-dimensional force distributions from a single RGB image of piled daily objects. The target distribution is generated by applying statistical smoothing to point forces obtained from the simulator. Moreover, by incorporating object geometry into the smoothing process, we aim to account for variations in contact states and achieve more consistent vision-based predictions. We conduct extensive evaluations in both simulation and real environments. Results show that our approach improves prediction accuracy, enhances downstream task performance through smoothing, and further benefits from geometry-guided smoothing. Remarkably, the trained model generalizes effectively to real-world scenes despite being trained solely in simulation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in Advanced Robotics, 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00464","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:57Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00463","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00463","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00463","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masaki","familyName":"Yoshida","name":"Yoshida, Masaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ren","familyName":"Togo","name":"Togo, Ren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takahiro","familyName":"Ogawa","name":"Ogawa, Takahiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miki","familyName":"Haseyama","name":"Haseyama, Miki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scene2Sound: Auditory-Grounded Soundscape Generation for 3D Gaussian Worlds"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T06:04:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:22:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) turns captured or generated imagery into photorealistic 3D world simulations that users can freely explore, yet these worlds remain silent. Because existing audio generation methods condition on a single image or viewpoint, their sound is tied to that observation and cannot stay consistent while a listener moves. We introduce the task of generating a spatially consistent soundscape for a given 3DGS world through auditory grounding, identifying which objects in the world should emit sound and anchoring each to a persistent 3D position, and present Scene2Sound, a training-free framework built on this grounding. From the input world alone, our pipeline selects viewpoints that jointly cover the scene, identifies sound-emitting objects with a vision-language model, and associates the multi-view detections into 3D instances through Gaussian set matching, which measures the overlap between the Gaussian sets that render each detection. Each source then receives generated audio that a standard object-based audio engine spatializes in real time at arbitrary listener poses. We further propose two spatial-consistency metrics, one testing whether rendered audio responds consistently to listener motion and one testing whether the claimed sources are supported by views held out from their placement. On a curated set of generated 3DGS worlds and on 3DGS scenes generated from real-world 360-degree captures, Scene2Sound preserves the audio quality of strong per-viewpoint baselines while remaining spatially consistent where per-viewpoint and single-panorama pipelines do not, and a user study confirms the perceptual benefit. Project page: https://masaki-lmd.github.io/scene2sound/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages. Project page: https://masaki-lmd.github.io/scene2sound/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00463","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:56Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00462","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00462","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00462","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xavier","familyName":"Blot","name":"Blot, Xavier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guido","familyName":"Carlet","name":"Carlet, Guido","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dimitrios","familyName":"Makris","name":"Makris, Dimitrios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey","familyName":"Shadrin","name":"Shadrin, Sergey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Triple Hodge integrals and constant Poisson brackets for rank-one Dubrovin-Zhang hierarchies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Exactly Solvable and Integrable Systems (nlin.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T06:04:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:22:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove a conjecture of Dubrovin, Liu, Yang, and Zhang that states that the Poisson bracket of the Dubrovin-Zhang hierarchy of a rank-one cohomological field theory is constant if and only if it is given by the triple Hodge class with the parameters satisfying the Calabi-Yau condition."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00462","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:54Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00461","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00461","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00461","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangbao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Guangbao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qishan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qishan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuefeng","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Xuefeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxing","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Wenxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wuming","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Wuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangjian","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Liangjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyuan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zeyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An eightfold equivalence-preserving speedup of the JUNO OMILREC vertex and energy reconstruction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Detectors (physics.ins-det)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Experiment (hep-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T06:02:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:22:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Jiangmen Underground Neutrino Observatory (JUNO) reconstructs each event's vertex and energy with OMILREC, a maximum-likelihood fit that scans all $17{,}612$ large photomultiplier tubes (LPMTs) in every Minuit function evaluation, about $470$ times per event. This inner loop dominates the reconstruction CPU cost. Profiling shows that the production algorithm is latency-bound, sustaining only $9.9%$ of scalar floating-point peak because of virtual-function dispatch, ROOT-histogram pointer chasing, and repeated computation. We apply staged \\emph{equivalence-preserving} optimizations: flattened data layouts, vectorizable geometry, hoisting of Minuit-invariant work, per-event precomputation, fit-phase loop splitting and indexing, and reduced-precision fast paths. Each stage is checked against a frozen reference from the unmodified code. The optimized implementation achieves single-thread speedups of $8.06\\times$ ($1524.8 \\rightarrow 189.2$~ms/event) on an Intel Xeon Platinum~8358P and $5.22\\times$ ($705.1 \\rightarrow 134.9$~ms/event) on an AMD~EPYC~9654, increasing to $8.6\\times$ ($177.7$~ms/event) after further optimization. The likelihood remains bit-identical through the first seven releases and later agrees within a relative drift of $1.3\\times10^{-14}$, below the $10^{-13}$ contract. For typical events, reconstructed vertex and energy agree with the baseline within $4$~mm and $7$~keV; a few boundary cases reach different valid minima owing to an improved minimizer seed. An eight-metric physics-acceptance test also passes on about $861{,}000$ $^{68}$Ge calibration events. Developed with assistance from an AI coding agent operating under these verification gates, this workflow offers a transferable template for accelerating likelihood-based reconstruction in large neutrino and collider detectors without changing physics output."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00461","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:53Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00460","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00460","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00460","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sylvain","familyName":"Courte","name":"Courte, Sylvain","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Delphine","familyName":"Moussard","name":"Moussard, Delphine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiuyu","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Qiuyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaozhou","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiaozhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multisections and bridge positions in arbitrary dimensions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Geometric Topology (math.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"57R65 57R40 57Q99","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T05:57:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:22:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multisections were defined by Ben Aribi--Courte--Golla--Moussard as a way to decompose closed manifolds into $1$-handlebodies, which is a generalization of Heegaard splittings and trisections. Previously, their existence was only known in dimensions up to $5$. We show that multisections exist for closed manifolds in arbitrary dimensions. We also show that submanifolds of codimension at least $2$ in multisected manifolds can always be put into an appropriate bridge position, generalizing the existence result on bridge trisections in dimension $4$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 9 figures in color"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00460","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:52Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00459","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00459","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00459","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanlai","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hanlai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiduo","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yiduo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A note on the saturation number for unions of three cliques"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"05C35","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T05:55:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:22:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A graph $G$ is $F$-saturated if $G$ contains no copy of $F$ but $G+e$ contains a copy of $F$ for every missing edge $e$ of $G$. The saturation number $\\sat(n,F)$ is the minimum number of edges in an $n$-vertex $F$-saturated graph. Motivated by a problem posed by Faudree, Ferrara, Gould, and Jacobson concerning $K_p\\cup K_q\\cup K_{q+1}$, we determine the saturation number and the unique extremal graph for $K_p\\cup K_q\\cup K_r$ whenever $2\\le p\\le q"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00459","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:50Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00458","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00458","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00458","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangwen","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Jiangwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanyu","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Wanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BANDMAS: Causality-Inspired Semantic Packet Scheduling for Bandwidth-Efficient Multi-Agent Collaboration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T05:51:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:22:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM-based multi-agent systems make decisions based on the aggregated information via exchanging messages across specialized agents. Forwarding every generated message among agents increases application-layer traffic. Yet, it introduces tremendous input tokens for agent processing, potentially raising inference latency and computational overhead. Existing approaches attempt to address the above issues by pruning agents or discarding redundant messages. Nevertheless, such agent-level or message-level optimization results in insufficient evidence supporting for final decisions or still containing redundant message transmissions. To address these challenges, we propose BANDMAS, a multi-agent collaboration framework that models inter-agent communications as task-oriented traffic, which enables efficient transmission via causality-inspired replay valuation. Specifically, we decompose messages into several data packets by analyzing their semantic features such as evidence and requests. The system only transmits these packets if their predicted replay-derived contribution exceeds their resource cost. Consequently, BANDMAS is able to adaptively schedule communication packets while adhering to bandwidth, latency, deadline, and receiver context constraints. On frozen Qwen3-4B traffic across SciFact, HotpotQA, and FanOutQA, our framework reduces application-layer bytes by 53.2\\% to 77.3\\% at selected caps and attains the highest mean task metric among constrained methods on all three workloads."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00458","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:49Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00457","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00457","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00457","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingsong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qingsong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangyang","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Fangyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Balanced Bismut torsion-parallel fourfold with constant holomorphic sectional curvature"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Differential Geometry (math.DG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"53C55","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T05:47:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:22:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"An old conjecture in non-Kähler geometry states that, if a compact Hermitian manifold has constant holomorphic sectional curvature, then the metric must be Kähler (when the constant is non-zero) or Chern flat (when the constant is zero). It is known to be true in complex dimension $2$ by the work of Balas and Gauduchon in 1985 (when the constant is negative or zero) and Apostolov, Davidov and Muskarov in 1996 (in general). In dimension $3$ or higher, the conjecture is only known in some special cases, such as for all twistor spaces by the work of Davidov, Grantcharov, and Muskarov, or for the locally conformally Kähler case (when the constant is negative or zero) by the work of H. Chen, L. Chen and Nie, or for all non-balanced Bismut torsion parallel (BTP) manifolds by the work of S. Chen and Zheng, where they also showed that the conjecture holds for all balanced BTP threefolds, utilizing a classification result by Zhao and Zheng. In this article, we show that the conjecture is valid for all balanced BTP manifolds in complex dimension $4$. The interesting part is that while balanced BTP manifolds are highly restrictive, the classification is still lacking in dimensions $4$ or higher, and this study might shed some light on the structure of balanced BTP manifolds in dimension $4$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00457","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:48Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00456","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00456","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00456","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiange","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tiange","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaohao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chaohao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuchen","familyName":"Shan","name":"Shan, Xuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meiqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Meiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolan","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Xiaolan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sub-40 nm resolution deep tissue imaging by image scanning emission saturation nanoscopy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T05:45:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:22:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The development of deep-tissue super-resolution imaging serves as an essential bridge toward non-invasive in vivo optical observation. However, there remain challenges to balance spatial resolution, imaging depth and phototoxicity. Here, we present a nanoscopy namely Image Scanning Emission Saturation (ISES) nanoscopy, achieving a lateral resolution of 37 nm, 1/25th of the excitation wavelength, at an imaging depth of 200 μm. Using a 976-nm doughnut-shaped excitation beam within an imaging-scanning microscopy configuration, we apply saturation-based point spread function (PSF) engineering and pixel-level confocal-pinhole enhancement to improve spatial resolution. As the high- and low-frequency components of the image OTF are concurrently acquired in a single scan via different camera pixels, Fourier-domain fusion can be employed with a single scanning dataset to further improve image quality. Compared with the traditional doughnut excitation beam-based adaptive pixel reassignment method, our strategy preserves the original frequency distributions and mitigates reconstruction artifacts in complex biological sample imaging. This strategy is generalizable and compatible with a variety of probes displaying saturation behavior. Beyond enabling a versatile and practical approach for deep tissue super-resolution imaging, it also informs the development of next-generation nanoprobes for imaging."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages,5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00456","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:46Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00455","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00455","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00455","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingqi","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Yingqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruida","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ruida","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqiang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Zhiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongkai","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Tongkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binhang","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Binhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AReaL-DTE: Sparse Policy-Weight Transfer for Online Agentic Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T05:34:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:22:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Online agentic reinforcement learning implemented with micro-services separates policy training from rollout generation, improving scalability and modularity while potentially making frequent policy-weight synchronization a critical systems overhead. Shared storage naturally connects these services across clusters, but vanilla dense policy weight synchronization could incur model-scale construction, transfer, and application costs. Sparse synchronization reduces transferred data, yet checkpoint-oriented approaches can still retain a previous model and materialize complete intermediates to bridge heterogeneous training and inference layouts. We present AReaL-DTE, a snapshot-free Delta Transfer Engine that translates inference-visible weight sparsity into end-to-end system efficiency. Across our evaluated workloads, fewer than 2% of BF16 weight elements change between consecutive policy versions. AReaL-DTE reconstructs overwritten weights on demand by inverting AdamW updates, streams reconstructed and current parameters through converter-aligned BF16 change detection, and remaps changed elements directly into receiver-local coordinates. AReaL-DTE supports manifest-committed sparse transfer through shared storage across clusters and a deadlock-safe two-round protocol within a cluster, followed by direct application to inference shards. We evaluate AReaL-DTE on Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-30B-A3B across four online RL workloads. AReaL-DTE achieves speedups of up to 19.9x over ByteCheckpoint and 3.2x over PULSE across clusters, and up to 7.6x and 7.4x, respectively, within a cluster. In the same-cluster Qwen3-30B-A3B experiments, it reduces peak GPU memory by approximately 41% and peak CPU memory by at least 87%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00455","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:45Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00454","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00454","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00454","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shoma","familyName":"Ansai","name":"Ansai, Shoma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasuo","familyName":"Okabe","name":"Okabe, Yasuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daisuke","familyName":"Kotani","name":"Kotani, Daisuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cache-Consistent Dynamic Load Balancing for Kubernetes Controllers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T05:31:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:22:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As Kubernetes clusters grow, the scalability of controllers can become a bottleneck for the performance of the system. Distributing the load dynamically across multiple controller instances, however, raises the following two problems, and a controller can therefore be run only as a single instance today. The first problem is the cost of reassignment. A controller retrieves objects on the basis of the Labels attached to them, so in a naive design in which the assigned instance is recorded in a Label on every object, the Labels must be rewritten in proportion to the total number of objects whenever instances are added or removed. The second problem is cache consistency. A controller consults only its own cache when it reads an object and never refers to the actual data, so the cache has to be updated explicitly at the time of a reassignment. Furthermore, a controller manages a cache independently for each kind of object, so cache updates have to be synchronized across the kinds of objects. We propose a method for scaling Kubernetes controllers horizontally that combines lightweight load balancing with cache synchronization. A two-level Hash maps objects to Virtual Nodes, records their identifiers in Labels, and assigns Virtual Nodes to instances by Consistent Hashing. Whenever instances are added or removed, it therefore suffices to update the Label value specified when objects are retrieved, and no Label on an object has to be rewritten. The cache is also locked until the reassignment has completed, which prevents any reference to a stale cache. The version identifier of the data store is used to synchronize the kinds of objects with one another. We implemented the proposed method on Kubernetes and evaluated it: the processing throughput rises with the number of instances, and the time required for reassignment remains within an acceptable range."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00454","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:44Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00453","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00453","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00453","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Support Contraction for Well-Supported Nash Equilibria"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T05:27:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:22:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce the support contraction, a structural procedure for computing well-supported Nash equilibria (WSNE) in bimatrix games. On a common action rectangle, the procedure alternately retains the support of a row maximin strategy for the row-payoff matrix and the support of a column maximin strategy for the column-payoff matrix. Each restriction preserves the value of the matrix game that selected it and can only increase the other value. At a stable rectangle, full support of the two maximizing strategies and complementary slackness make every surviving action tight. Crossing the two minimax strategies then gives an exact Nash equilibrium of the retained subgame, while the two zero-sum values bound deviations to deleted actions.\n Support contraction gives a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm that computes a $1/2$-WSNE of every rational bimatrix game with payoffs in $[0,1]$, with no additive slack, and a deterministic $O(ε^{-2}\\log^2 n)$-bit two-party protocol for a $(1/2+ε)$-WSNE. The same support-contraction certificate, preceded by randomized one-sided localization, gives an $O(ε^{-2}n\\log n)$ payoff-query algorithm for the same guarantee, improving the $ε^{-4}$ dependence to $ε^{-2}$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00453","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:42Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00452","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00452","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00452","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Kahirov","name":"Kahirov, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umesh","familyName":"Deshpande","name":"Deshpande, Umesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Swaminathan","familyName":"Sundararaman","name":"Sundararaman, Swaminathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CeQe: Grounding Lexical Retrieval in Semantic Evidence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T05:27:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:22:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Lexical retrieval (BM25) captures exact keyword matches and weights terms by corpus-wide significance, but it is blind to the semantic vocabulary gap: when a relevant document phrases an answer differently from the query, BM25 never retrieves it, and no amount of downstream reranking or fusion can recover a document that was never in the candidate set. We present Cross-Encoder Query Expansion (CE-QE), which reads the per-token relevance attributions of a cross-encoder applied to top semantic search results, selects the terms the cross-encoder treats as decisive, and appends them to the BM25 query. Unlike classical pseudo-relevance feedback, which reuses BM25's own (possibly wrong) top results, CE-QE seeds expansion from the semantic retriever's results, avoiding self-reinforcing query drift. Unlike recent generative query expansion (HyDE, Query2doc), which prompts a large language model to hallucinate text from its parametric knowledge, every CE-QE expansion term is copied verbatim from a retrieved passage, so it cannot introduce vocabulary the corpus does not contain, and its only added cost is attribution extraction on a cross-encoder a hybrid pipeline already runs for reranking. On seven BEIR datasets, CE-QE improves lexical recall substantially where query and answer vocabulary diverge (e.g., NQ Recall@100 from 0.32 to 0.47), and its score-fusion variant (SESF) beats cross-encoder score fusion by 2.5% on Recall@100 and beats SPLADEv2 and ColBERTv2 by 5.3% and 4.6% on nDCG@10, while leaving the underlying BM25 index completely unmodified."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00452","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:41Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00449","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00449","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00449","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ewan","familyName":"Davies","name":"Davies, Ewan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juspreet Singh","familyName":"Sandhu","name":"Sandhu, Juspreet Singh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"First-order phase transitions in the hard-core model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T05:17:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:22:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We give a rigorous proof that the hard-core model on a natural family of infinite graphs exhibits a first-order phase transition."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00449","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:31Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00448","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00448","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00448","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frédéric","familyName":"Bayart","name":"Bayart, Frédéric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maofa","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Maofa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingxing","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Xingxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Commutants of composition operators on function spaces of several complex variables"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Functional Analysis (math.FA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary 47B33, Secondary 46E15","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T05:16:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:22:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper is devoted to an in-depth study of the minimal commutant property for composition operators acting on Hilbert spaces of holomorphic functions in several complex variables, such as the Fock space on $\\mathbb{C}^d$, the Hardy space on the Euclidean ball, and the Hardy space on the unit polydisc."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00448","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:30Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00447","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00447","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00447","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takuya","familyName":"Fujimura","name":"Fujimura, Takuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gordon","familyName":"Wichern","name":"Wichern, Gordon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshiki","familyName":"Masuyama","name":"Masuyama, Yoshiki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph","familyName":"Boeddeker","name":"Boeddeker, Christoph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kohei","familyName":"Saijo","name":"Saijo, Kohei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julius","familyName":"Richter","name":"Richter, Julius","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takahiro","familyName":"Edo","name":"Edo, Takahiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan Le","familyName":"Roux","name":"Roux, Jonathan Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Anomalous Sound Detection Meets Noise-Aware Self-Supervised Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T05:14:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:22:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we introduce noise-aware self-supervised learning (NA-SSL) models for noise-aware anomalous sound detection (NA-ASD). NA-ASD is an ASD task with two-channel audio recordings, where one microphone is located close to the target machine and the other is located farther away to capture noise. For this task, we simulate two-channel recordings using diverse audio datasets and train NA-SSL models to extract clean SSL representations of the close-microphone signal by using the far-microphone recording dominated by background noise as auxiliary information. The NA-SSL models are then used as frontends in the standard ASD framework. Our experimental evaluation on the DCASE 2026 Challenge Task 2 development dataset demonstrates the effectiveness of the NA-SSL framework across three base SSL models (BEATs, EAT, and Dasheng), both with and without discriminative fine-tuning. Furthermore, the challenge results proved the effectiveness of the proposed approach, where the NA-BEATs system won the challenge by a large margin, achieving an official score of 70.24%, while the second-place system achieved 65.46%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00447","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:28Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00446","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00446","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00446","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huu Phong","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Huu Phong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Delower","familyName":"Hossain","name":"Hossain, Delower","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ehsan","familyName":"Saghapour","name":"Saghapour, Ehsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhandos","familyName":"Sembay","name":"Sembay, Zhandos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jake Y.","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jake Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structured Proxy Features for Multimodal NSCLC Survival Prediction from Pretreatment CT"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T05:10:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:22:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Lung cancer results in roughly 1.8 million fatalities annually worldwide, with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) comprising the majority of cases. Despite advancements in treatment, survival stratification remains challenging due to intratumoral heterogeneity inadequately captured by conventional descriptors. Standard radiomic and deep learning techniques regard imaging features as independent quantities, overlooking structured interactions between tumor characteristics. We evaluate whether structured proxy features can enhance multimodal NSCLC survival prediction by augmenting pretreatment computed tomography (CT) representations, radiomics, and clinical variables with six simulation-derived features designed to capture interactions between heterogeneity and morphology. A radiomic-parameterized cellular automaton generates growth-rate and necrosis-ratio proxy features from baseline CT by using entropy and sphericity to compute low-dimensional proxy parameters. The imaging backbone is a Transformer-based Masked Autoencoder (TMAE), which was chosen after a systematic evaluation with alternative encoders within the same pipeline and provides attention-based visualizations that highlight tumor regions receiving higher model attention. On the public Lung1 cohort (n = 390), the primary four-modality fusion attained a C-index of 0.641 (iAUC 0.731, log-rank p \u0026lt; 0.001). The primary result compares favorably with prior multimodal results on Lung1 (C-index 0.631; iAUC 0.592 [15]) under a comparable evaluation protocol, while a separate exploratory coefficient-optimization analysis achieved a best observed C-index of 0.662 (iAUC 0.748). These results indicate that, in addition to conventional radiomic, deep, and clinical representations within the Lung1 benchmark, simulation-derived proxy features may provide complementary predictive information within this fixed Lung1 benchmark."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00446","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:27Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00445","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00445","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00445","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuan","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Tuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exact detection threshold of the packing test"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T05:02:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:21:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Using Poisson approximation techniques, we derive the detection threshold of the packing test in \\cite{Jiang13} when testing spherical uniformity under high-dimensional Fisher--von Mises--Langevin (FvML) and Watson alternatives. Our result rigorously confirms the empirical observation that the packing test is strictly suboptimal for testing uniformity in these two popular models. In the high-dimensional FvML model, its detection threshold is precisely \\(κ=Θ\\lb p^{3/4}/(\\log n)^{1/4}\\rb\\). In the high-dimensional Watson model, its detection threshold is \\(p-2κ=Θ(\\sqrt{p\\log n})\\), or equivalently \\(κ=p/2-Θ(\\sqrt{p\\log n})\\). The non-null limiting distributions of the packing test under these two models are derived. We show that the limiting scalings of the largest squared inner product undergo a discontinuous phase transition in the Watson model, whereas no analogous phenomenon occurs in the FvML model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00445","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:25Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00444","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00444","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00444","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yibo","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Yibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyu","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Jinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yunhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Bin","name":"Bin, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"See-kiong","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, See-kiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reconstruction-Shift Discrimination via Mask-Guided Latent Diffusion for Medical Anomaly Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T04:56:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:21:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unsupervised medical anomaly detection learns normal anatomical patterns from healthy training images and identifies deviations at test time. Reconstruction-based and diffusion-based methods commonly use the difference between an input image and its reconstruction as anomaly evidence. However, this residual can be ambiguous. Expressive models may preserve pathological structures, while benign anatomical variation, imaging noise, and acquisition differences may also produce large reconstruction errors. We propose discriminative mask-guided diffusion (DMD), a medical anomaly detection framework that complements residual-based localization with reconstruction-shift discrimination. DMD first learns a compact quantized latent representation of normal images. Localized masks then perturb selected latent regions, and a latent diffusion model reconstructs the perturbed representations. The resulting reconstructions are paired with their original normal images to define a self-supervised classification task. At inference, the classifier provides a learned image-level anomaly score, while the residual between the input and its diffusion-based reconstruction yields a pixel-level anomaly map. Experiments on five datasets spanning brain MRI, breast ultrasound, and chest radiography show that DMD achieves the best overall performance among the state-of-the-art baseline methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00444","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:24Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00443","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00443","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00443","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alvan","familyName":"Arulandu","name":"Arulandu, Alvan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John M.","familyName":"Martyn","name":"Martyn, John M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isaac L.","familyName":"Chuang","name":"Chuang, Isaac L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trading Imaginary Time for Randomness in Ground State Preparation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T04:48:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:21:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Imaginary-time evolution (ITE) is a foundational method for ground state preparation on quantum computers. However, because ITE is non-unitary, existing implementations incur a sample complexity and/or classical cost that scales exponentially with the target imaginary time $β$. Moreover, the state itself converges slower than the energy, making accurate estimation of arbitrary ground state observables even more expensive. In this work, we improve upon standard ITE by introducing twirled imaginary-time evolution (TITE), which pairs ITE with real-time evolution applied for a random duration drawn from a carefully designed distribution. We prove that this randomization quadratically suppresses the trace distance to the ground state, and thus also the error of arbitrary observables, which allows roughly half of the imaginary time to be replaced with real-time evolution ($β\\mapsto β/2$) while maintaining the same level of accuracy. Because real-time evolution is unitary and does not incur an overhead in sample complexity or classical computation, this affords a quadratic reduction in the cost of any black-box ITE implementation, including Trotterization and quantum imaginary-time evolution. We demonstrate the efficiency of our algorithm in noisy circuit-level simulations of a non-integrable Ising chain, showing substantial improvements over standard ITE."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00443","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:23Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00441","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00441","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00441","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dana","familyName":"Makarova","name":"Makarova, Dana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dana","familyName":"Alina","name":"Alina, Dana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paolo","familyName":"Padoan","name":"Padoan, Paolo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baurzhan","familyName":"Kumarioldanov","name":"Kumarioldanov, Baurzhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mika","familyName":"Juvela","name":"Juvela, Mika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dilda","familyName":"Berdikhan","name":"Berdikhan, Dilda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naval","familyName":"Bhadari","name":"Bhadari, Naval","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hub-filament systems and the growth of massive stars: episodic accretion, clustered environments, and projection effects"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T04:45:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:21:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The processes controlling the early mass growth of future massive stars remain poorly understood, particularly the connection of this growth to star clustering and hub-filament systems (HFSs). This connection is difficult to establish observationally, because projection effects and line-of-sight confusion in position-position-velocity (PPV) data can distort the information about the intrinsic filamentary structure. To investigate this connection, we used a three-dimensional magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) simulation of star formation, where stars are represented by accreting sink particles. We identify clustered stellar environments, reconstruct time-dependent accretion histories, and investigate the relation between enhanced-accretion episodes and the locations of HFSs. We also use line radiative transfer modeling to produce synthetic molecular-line observations and examine how the same structures appear in projected PPV data. In our simulation, we find that 80% of future massive stars are associated with clustered environments. Their growth is also highly episodic: typically, about 40% of the accreted mass is gained during periods of enhanced accretion that occupy only about 10% of the total growth time. Periods of enhanced accretion occur slightly closer to three-dimensional HFS proxies, suggesting a possible link between HFS morphology and episodic accretion in future massive stars. Overall, our results suggest that the early growth of future massive stars is connected to both their clustered environment and the HFS structure of the surrounding gas, and projection effects must be considered when interpreting HFS in PPV data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 9 figures, 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00441","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:20Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00440","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00440","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00440","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhishan","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Zhishan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Poplar: A Scalable Pipeline for Human-Centric Image Dataset Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T04:39:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:21:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent image generators can synthesize convincing human-centric images, yet producing a useful collection remains different from producing a single successful image. A human-centric dataset must cover varied people and contexts, avoid implausible attribute combinations, preserve an everyday photographic character, and expose quality-control decisions at scale. We present Poplar, a reproducible Specify--Render--Inspect pipeline for human-centric image dataset synthesis. Specify samples structured attributes under commonsense constraints and verbalizes them as photography-oriented prompts. Render uses a realism-adapted image generator across composition-aware aspect ratios and retries obvious technical failures. Inspect applies a single structured vision--language review to each candidate, preserving the original prompt while rejecting intrinsic image defects or material prompt mismatches. Using Poplar, we construct Poplar-9K: 9,401 curated human-centric image--text pairs retained from 11,765 reviewed candidates (79.9\\% acceptance). We release the dataset together with the pipeline, configurations, immutable generation prompts, and auditable inspection records as a compact resource for building customizable human-centric collections."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"code: https://github.com/choucisan/poplar website: https://choucisan.github.io/publications/poplar"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00440","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:19Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00439","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00439","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00439","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luiz L.","familyName":"Lopes","name":"Lopes, Luiz L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Undergraduate Approach to the Quantum Hadrodynamics and the Physics of Neutron Stars Part II: Neutron Stars' Exotic Content"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Nuclear Theory (nucl-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T04:35:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:21:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.3390/universe12080222","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this second part, I discuss how to introduce and the role played by non-atomic degrees of freedom in neutron stars' core using the formalism developed in Part I."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published version is open access =)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00439","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:17Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00438","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00438","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00438","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guyu","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Guyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Homogenization of Viscous Sublinear Hamilton--Jacobi Equations with $u/ε$-Dependence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T04:33:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:21:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the periodic homogenization of a class of viscous Hamilton--Jacobi equations with fast dependence on the unknown. This problem combines features of first-order Hamilton--Jacobi equations with \\(u^ε/ε\\)-periodic Hamiltonians and semilinear heat equations with rapidly oscillating positive potentials. In this paper, we prove qualitative homogenization results for $H(y,s,p)=F(s,p)+ηW(y,s,p)$ when $|η|$ is sufficiently small, with the smallness threshold depending on the Lipschitz constant of the initial datum, and establish a large-time averaging result for a general class of evolutionary cell problems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"47 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00438","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:16Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00437","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00437","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00437","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faizan","familyName":"Faisal","name":"Faisal, Faizan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prem","familyName":"Devanbu","name":"Devanbu, Prem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toufique","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Toufique","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distilling Reasoning Traces into Advisory Prompts for Software Engineering Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T04:32:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:21:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language models are widely used for generating and otherwise processing code (e.g., identifying code hallucinations, possible inputs, or predicting outputs); however, LLMs can make mistakes, which can be serious. One key issue is that models are trained on (still) largely human-written, and thus imperfect, code; it's not easy to find sufficiently large code corpora that are entirely free of bugs. Thus, other inference-time ways of reducing LLM errors, without additional training, are desirable. \"Reasoning\" or \"thinking\" modes, exposed as a togglable feature by hybrid reasoning models, do reduce errors; however, reasoning consumes additional resources. This paper asks if better performance can be achieved without always incurring the cost of reasoning. Human students of programming learn to avoid mistakes by (a) identifying them, (b) reflecting upon the cognitive lapses that led to them (essentially, \"thinking through\" the errors), (c) inferring general rules or lessons from these reflections, and (d) internalizing these lessons into rules. In tutorial sessions with an instructor, this is a common Socratic interaction. Examples of such internalizable rules might include the nugget \"Before coding, restate the requirements to clarify them.\" Inspired by this process, this paper describes an approach where we first identify examples in which \"thinking mode\" in a (low-resource) LLM avoids errors. These errors, and their avoidance via \"thinking\" in the same LLM, are then examined by a bigger LLM to generate summary explanations; these are then summarized by a large LLM into brief advisory prompts. This approach works on many modest-sized models; in some cases, the \"advisory prompts\" thus learned can also be gainfully transferred to other models. We also present investigations into the nature of coding errors that language models make, and a characterization of when this approach can be helpful."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00437","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:14Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00436","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00436","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00436","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tia","familyName":"Pandit","name":"Pandit, Tia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bipin Singh","familyName":"Koranga","name":"Koranga, Bipin Singh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vivek Kumar","familyName":"Nautiyal","name":"Nautiyal, Vivek Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Matter-Induced CPT Violation and Earth-Density Stratification Effects in Long-Baseline Neutrino Oscillation Experiments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T04:27:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:21:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a unified analysis of two matter-potential systematics in long-baseline (LBL) neutrino oscillation experiments, treating matter-induced extrinsic CPT violation and Earth density stratification as correlated systematics within a single $χ^2$ framework. We study their joint impact on CP-phase reconstruction and mass-ordering dependence. Matter-induced extrinsic CPT violation produces a non-zero asymmetry $A_{μe}^{\\rm CPT}=(P_{μe}-\\bar P_{μe})/(P_{μe}+\\bar P_{μe})$, computed using exact three-flavour matrix exponentiation for T2K, NO$ν$A, DUNE, and Hyper-Kamiokande. The asymmetry ranges from 0.022 to 0.180 at the respective peak energies and differs by up to $9\\%$ between normal and inverted orderings. The surface $A_{μe}^{\\rm CPT}(E,δ_{\\rm CP})$ at DUNE reveals an interplay between extrinsic CPT and intrinsic CP violation in the high-$L/E$ regime requiring joint statistical treatment. We quantify the sensitivity of $A_{μe}^{\\rm CPT}$ to density uncertainties $ρ\\rightarrowρ(1+\\varepsilon)$ with $\\varepsilon=\\pm1\\%,\\pm2\\%,\\pm5\\%$, finding that a $\\pm5\\%$ uncertainty induces $|ΔA_{\\rm CPT}|\u0026lt;0.008$ at DUNE. Replacing the PREM profile with a constant path-averaged density yields a $δ_{\\rm CP}$ reconstruction bias below $0.3^\\circ$ for $L\\le5000$ km, increasing to $17.8^\\circ$ at $7000$ km and $172.2^\\circ$ at $12000$ km. Since both effects arise from the same matter Hamiltonian, they must be modelled jointly using a Poisson log-likelihood $χ^2$ with nuisance-parameter pulls."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00436","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:13Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00435","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00435","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00435","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Kane","name":"Kane, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikuláš","familyName":"Zindulka","name":"Zindulka, Mikuláš","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Negative bias in moments of the Legendre family of elliptic curves"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"11E41 (Primary) 11F27, 11F37, 11G05 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T04:19:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:20:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We determine the bias in higher moments of the Legendre family of elliptic curves in view of the Negative Bias Conjecture of S. J. Miller. We show that every lower order term is either zero or negative on average for both even and odd moments, and explicitly compute the third and fourth moment. The results about Hurwitz class numbers in arithmetic progressions which we prove in the process may be useful for other applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"36 pages, comments are welcome! Additional details contained in the source file"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00435","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:12Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00434","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00434","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00434","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqiang","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Ziqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Bowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Peiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengyin","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Shengyin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yankai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yankai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoli","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Haoli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichun","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Yichun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xue","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AdaMTP: An Adaptive Training Paradigm for Multi-Token Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T04:18:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:20:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-Token Prediction (MTP) has emerged as an effective paradigm that augments a shared Large Language Model backbone with auxiliary heads, training the model to predict several future tokens in parallel to enrich its supervision signal and accelerate inference. However, existing training frameworks adopt a rigid, fixed-length prediction horizon, disregarding the highly non-uniform information density of natural language and code. Forcing the auxiliary heads to predict across high-entropy semantic boundaries injects noisy, conflicting training signals; because these heads share the backbone's latent representations, the resulting gradients backpropagate and interfere with the model's core capabilities. We propose AdaMTP, an adaptive training paradigm that dynamically aligns the prediction horizon with the intrinsic predictability of the sequence. At its core, an entropy-based segmentation algorithm leverages the base model to detect sudden surges in uncertainty as semantic boundaries, partitioning sequences into variable-length groups. Each token is assigned an adaptive prediction depth, and a dynamically masked MTP objective suppresses the loss for predictions that cross these boundaries, attenuating the noisy gradients that degrade the backbone. Across mathematical reasoning, code generation, and general benchmarks on three backbones (Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen-2.5-7B, Gemma-3-12B), AdaMTP consistently outperforms standard MTP in both task performance and inference speedup."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00434","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:10Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00433","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00433","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00433","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruishi","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Ruishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Siyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Racquel","familyName":"Fygenson","name":"Fygenson, Racquel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dakuo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dakuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Correll","name":"Correll, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lace M.","familyName":"Padilla","name":"Padilla, Lace M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Seeing Through the Forecast Clutter: Communicating Climate Forecast Distributions with Weighted Multiple Forecast Visualizations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T04:17:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:20:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Forecasts often diverge because different models make varying assumptions to account for underlying uncertainty. Readers who consume forecasts may wish to survey the shape and spread of these multiple forecasts to get a full account of the different predictions. One approach to visualizing multiple forecasts is through Confidence Interval (CI) plots. However, while the summative CI plots can communicate uncertainty of an ensemble, they obscure attributes of individual forecasts that can lead to inaccurate perceptions of the distribution of these forecasts (e.g., implying a normal distribution when non-existent). To address this challenge, we investigate the use of multiple forecast visualization (MFV) in communicating nuanced forecast distributions through two preregistered experiments using climate forecast data. In Experiment 1 (480 participants), we compared how well MFV and CI plots can represent the distribution of multiple forecasts. We found that, compared to CI plots, MFV improved participants' ability to identify the underlying distribution of forecasts and reduced the likelihood of assuming normality. Building on Experiment 1, we examined in Experiment 2 (900 participants) whether a downsampled MFV showing 9 forecasts might be able to communicate additional forecast properties using linewidth and opacity without negatively impacting distribution perception. We found that visually weighting forecasts by linewidth or opacity preserves readers' perception of the underlying distribution. We discuss how these findings suggest the use of downsampled and weighted MFV to cut through forecast clutter by aligning perceived distribution with the underlying forecast distribution, while opening up design opportunities to use weighting to communicate additional forecast attributes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to IEEE VIS 2026 Full Paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00433","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:09Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00432","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00432","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00432","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tinghao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Tinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lilin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Deep Research Pretraining via Predictive Navigation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T04:17:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:20:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep research agents are often trained on expensive, environment-grounded tool-use trajectories that require repeated retrieval, document inspection, and report evaluation. We introduce Deep Research Pretraining (DRP), an offline framework that derives predictive navigation supervision from naturally occurring evidence structures. Given a citation-bearing or hyperlinked passage, DRP constructs a proxy research objective, recovers linked evidence and graph-related alternatives, and converts them into search-open-write trajectories. This teaches models what to search for, which documents to inspect, and how to synthesize evidence, without a live retrieval environment or executed policy rollout. We instantiate DRP on scholarly citation graphs (DRP-Paper) and Wikipedia hyperlinks (DRP-Web), continually pretrain separate Qwen3-14B-Base models on 1B tokens, and fine-tune them on controlled fractions of 13K agent trajectories. Across five independently sampled subsets at each low-data budget, both variants consistently outperform matched no-DRP models on DeepResearch Bench. With one quarter of the SFT data, DRP-Web even surpasses a fixed no-DRP full-data checkpoint, with gains transferring to ResearchQA, WebWalkerQA, and SimpleQA. Starting from matched low-data SFT checkpoints, the DRP-Web advantage also persists through subsequent agentic RL. Source-matched and evidence-mismatch controls indicate that these improvements arise from evidence-conditioned navigation rather than domain exposure or agent-format imitation. DRP thus provides a promising complementary approach to trajectory-based agent training."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"working in progress; correspondence to {ucaswu,maxwellyu}@tencent.com or wuxing@iie.ac.cn"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00432","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:07Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00431","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00431","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00431","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Adam","familyName":"Dombrowski","name":"Dombrowski, Muhammad Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mirror Variations on Catalan Multijections"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"05A18","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T04:15:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:20:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A Catalan multijection is a way to partition a set of $\\binom{2n}{n}$ objects into equivalence classes of size $n+1$, where each equivalence class has exactly one distinct Catalan object. Isaak and Langley investigated when two multijections produced the same partitions in an unpublished manuscript. We propose some new multijections for which we get different partitions from previously discovered multijections."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages, 17 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00431","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:06Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00430","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00430","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00430","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lizhe","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Lizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-similar solutions of the three-dimensional Muskat problem with surface tension"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"35R35, 35Q35, 35A01, 35A02","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T04:10:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:20:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We construct a one-parameter family of small self-similar solutions to the three-dimensional one-phase Muskat problem with surface tension. The solutions have the form $η_\\varepsilon(t,x) = t^{1/3}U_\\varepsilon(t^{-1/3}x)$ and emanate from the conical initial data $η_\\varepsilon(0,x)=\\varepsilon|x|$. The profiles are perturbations of the linear capillary regularization of the cone, and we identify the leading quadratic correction. The proof combines a raywise inverse estimate for the linear similarity operator, a favorable high--high-to-low cancellation in the quadratic term, and finite-order tame estimates for the Dirichlet--Neumann operator on asymptotically conical graphs. These estimates yield the solutions by a contraction argument and show that the conical singularity is instantaneously rounded for positive time."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"40 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00430","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:05Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00429","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00429","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00429","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Narendra","familyName":"Bhandari","name":"Bhandari, Narendra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Positive rational series for reciprocal powers of Catalan's constant and Dirichlet beta values"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary 11Y60, 40G05, Secondary 11M06, 33B30","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T04:08:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:20:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $β(s)=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}(-1)^k(2k+1)^{-s}$ and let $G=β(2)$ be Catalan's constant. We develop two families of positive series for reciprocal powers $β(s)^{-r}$. The first is obtained from the classical Euler transformation and is evaluated at $1/2$; it is valid for real $s\u0026gt;0$. A second transformation, valid for $s\\geq2$, gives a faster series evaluated at $1/3$. We derive finite-sum and integral formulas for the base coefficients, together with a positive recurrence and a composition formula for arbitrary reciprocal powers. When $s$ is an integer, all coefficients are rational. We also give explicit remainder estimates and determine the exact root-convergence rates of the two families. As applications, we obtain positive rational series for every reciprocal power of Catalan's constant and for reciprocal powers of $π$ arising from odd values of the Dirichlet beta function."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00429","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:03Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00428","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00428","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00428","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Shan","name":"Shan, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbin","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Wenbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qixian","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Qixian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Affine vertex algebras and an affine analog of Barbasch-Vogan's construction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Representation Theory (math.RT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:57:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:20:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This is an expository paper based on the authors' joint works. The goal is to explain the statements and the ideas behind two conjectures on associated varieties and simple modules of simple affine vertex algebras $L_k(\\mathfrak{g})$ for a simple and simply-laced Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{g}$ and a integer level $k$ above the critical level."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00428","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:39:02Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00426","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00426","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00426","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjun","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Wenjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yijin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiaqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangding","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Shangding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feiyu","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Feiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muning","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Muning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MAPLE-Guard: Memory-Aware Link Enforcement Against Memory-Link Poisoning in Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:55:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:20:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM-based multi-agent systems (MAS) increasingly rely on persistent private and shared memories for long-horizon coordination. This memory layer improves continuity, but it also gives attackers a durable channel: a poisoned memory can be written once, continuously retrieved in later tasks, promoted into shared memory, and reused by other agents. A single poisoned write can therefore steer many later decisions and contaminate agents that never saw the original attack, all while no malicious message crosses a visible communication edge at the moment of harm. Further, because existing safeguards mainly inspect prompts, actions, or communication edges, they can miss attacks whose content appears benign at write time but becomes harmful after retrieval. We introduce Memory-Aware Propagation and Link Enforcement Guard, MAPLE-Guard, a memory-link guard for memory-enabled MAS. MAPLE-Guard monitors the memory lifecycle and places gates at write, retrieval, promotion, and cross-agent reuse, so risky memories can be quarantined, unsafe retrievals filtered, and poisoned private memories blocked before they enter shared memory. In the main evaluation, MAPLE-Guard lowers attack success rate (ASR) from 38.2% to 0.9% on LongMemEval and from 34.7% to 0.2% on AppWorld; it also raises multi-agent defense success rate (MDSR) from 54.0% to 74.3% and from 42.5% to 99.8% on the same benchmarks. These results suggest that memory-aware link enforcement covers a gap left by prompt-level and topology-level defenses. Code is available at the link: https://github.com/xiong-wenjun/MAPLE-Guard."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 14 figures, 9 tables. Includes examples that may be misleading or harmful. Code: https://github.com/xiong-wenjun/MAPLE-Guard"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00426","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:59Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:39:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:39:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00425","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00425","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00425","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas H.","familyName":"Anderson","name":"Anderson, Nicholas H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael J.","familyName":"Mehl","name":"Mehl, Michael J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hagen","familyName":"Eckert","name":"Eckert, Hagen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Divilov","name":"Divilov, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiomara","familyName":"Campilongo","name":"Campilongo, Xiomara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Curtarolo","name":"Curtarolo, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The AFLOW Library of Crystallographic Prototypes: Part 5"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:54:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:20:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The AFLOW library of crystallographic prototypes has been updated to incorporate an additional 344 entries, which now reaches 2,127 prototypes. ICSD and CCDC numbers have been added to the website alongside improvements to the user interface. New tutorials covering the basics of crystallography in the context of materials science have also been added. Lastly, we covered the current known applications of AFLOW prototype labels and materials data across research software."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"43 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00425","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:58Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00424","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00424","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00424","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijun","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zhijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanjie","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Hanjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josef","familyName":"Ďurech","name":"Ďurech, Josef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Moskovitz","name":"Moskovitz, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Audrey","familyName":"Thirouin","name":"Thirouin, Audrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samantha","familyName":"Hemmelgarn","name":"Hemmelgarn, Samantha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen","familyName":"Bo","name":"Bo, Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian-Yang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jian-Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Non-Principal-Axis Rotation and Convex Shape Model of Earth Quasi-Satellite and the Target of China's Tianwen-2 Mission (469219) Kamo`oalewa"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:51:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:19:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"(469219) Kamo`oalewa is the most stable Earth quasi-satellite and the target of China's Tianwen-2 asteroid sample return mission. Due to its small size, fast rotation, and the limited observing geometry accessible from the ground, many physical properties of Kamo`oalewa remain poorly constrained, including the rotational status and shape. We obtained three epochs of high-cadence, high signal-to-noise photometric lightcurves of Kamo`oalewa with the Gemini North Telescope from 2026 April to May, supplemented by one lightcurve from the Lowell Discovery Telescope in 2026 May. Our analysis suggests that Kamo`oalewa is in a non-principal-axis rotation with an elongated shape. Four possible solutions exist, including a long-axis mode (LAM) solution and a short-axis mode (SAM) solution, as well as their corresponding mirrored angular momentum directions. The most preferable solution has a LAM model with a precession period $P_ϕ=27.65\\pm \\text{min}$, and a rotational period $P_ψ=50.49\\pm0.08\\text{min}$, and the angular momentum points to ecliptic coordinates $(λ, β) = (226^\\mathrm{o} \\pm 20^\\mathrm{o}, -39^\\mathrm{o} \\pm 15^\\mathrm{o})$, although we cannot rule out other solutions or other close-by periods due to aliasing. We also derived a convex shape inversion for LAM with consistent rotational parameters but could not find a satisfactory inversion for SAM. The non-principal-axis rotation provides additional constraints on the dynamic history or the internal structure of Kamo`oalewa."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to ApJL"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00424","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:57Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00423","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00423","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00423","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Liming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingfei","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Mingfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chijin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Chijin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongjun","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runzhe","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Runzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohai","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Xiaohai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heyuan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Heyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diagnose Before You Compress: Prediction-Independent Bottleneck Witness Refinement for LLM Serving Traces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:43:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:19:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Production LLM serving generates millions of diverse requests, making full-trace replay across serving configurations increasingly expensive. Existing trace reduction methods mainly preserve workload distributions or representative requests, but bottleneck-revealing workloads may be rare and non-representative. Moreover, evidence for one component cannot compensate for missing evidence in another, while using predicted bottlenecks as target truth creates circular evaluation. These limitations make it necessary to preserve evidence for every bottleneck component rather than rely on workload representativeness alone. We propose Bottleneck-Preserving Witnessing (BPW), a quality-constrained framework for compact and diagnostically reliable LLM serving replay suites. BPW first performs Workload Candidate Nomination using response-blind workload features and closed source-side measurements. This stage identifies workloads that may expose scheduler, prefill, decode, or KV-cache bottlenecks. Coverage-Priority Sequence Construction then organizes multi-component proposals as reusable hyperedges and prioritizes weak and uncovered dimensions. Finally, Bottleneck Truth Verification derives prediction-independent labels solely from direct target-system measurements. The verified results determine the earliest prefix satisfying the direct two-witness requirement for every component. Experiments on BurstGPT, ServeGen, and Mooncake show that BPW reaches the verified gate with a compact workload set and outperforms 16 policies, achieving relative improvements of 2.3% and 16.3% in Mean prefix Macro-F1 and WBRC-AUC, respectively. Stage-resolved and sensitivity analyses confirm the distinct contributions and local stability of its three stages. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/llmllmllm/BPW"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00423","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:55Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00421","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00421","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00421","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel","familyName":"Gray","name":"Gray, Gabriel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily","familyName":"Payne","name":"Payne, Emily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Holly","familyName":"Swisher","name":"Swisher, Holly","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ren","familyName":"Watson","name":"Watson, Ren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fixed Perimeter Analogues of Several Partition Results Related to Parity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"05A17, 05A19, 11P81","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:41:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:19:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In 2016, Straub proved that Euler's classic partition identity holds true for partitions with largest hook (perimeter) $n$. This inspired further study of the relationship between classical partitions and fixed perimeter partitions. We extend the study of parity bias inequalities, first introduced by Kim, Kim, and Lovejoy in 2020, to the fixed perimeter setting and show using combinatorial methods that fixed perimeter analogues of many classical parity bias results can be proven and generalized. We also extend these methods to prove similar inequalities for the fixed perimeter analogues of PED and POD partitions. We additionally develop recursive formulas for the number of perimeter $n$ partitions with odd parts distinct and even parts unrestricted and with even parts distinct and odd parts unrestricted."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00421","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:53Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00420","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00420","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00420","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongjia","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zongjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengjun","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Shengjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Weighted $L^p$ solutions of scalar BSDEs with general unbounded stochastic coefficients"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:40:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:19:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper is devoted to solving one-dimensional backward stochastic differential equations (BSDEs in short) with a general random terminal time $τ$ taking values in the extended nonnegative real numbers. The generator $g$ of BSDEs satisfies some stochastic growth/continuity conditions in the state variables $(y,z)$, featuring unbounded stochastic coefficients $μ_\\cdot\\in\\R$ and $ν_\\cdot\\in\\R_+$ satisfying $\\int_0^τ(|μ_t|+ν^2_t) {\\rm d}t\u0026lt;+\\infty$. For any given real $p\u0026gt;1$, let $ρ_\\cdot\\geq μ_\\cdot+\\fracθ{2(p-1)}ν_\\cdot^2$ (instead of $ρ_\\cdot\\geqμ_\\cdot+\\fracθ{2[1\\wedge(p-1)]}ν_\\cdot^2$ used in Zhang, Li, Hu and Fan [2026, arXiv:2603.13873v1]) be a real-valued process for some constant $θ\u0026gt;1$ such that $\\int_0^τ|ρ_t|{\\rm d}t\u0026lt;+\\infty$. We work within a weighted $L^p$ space with the weighting factor $e^{\\int_0^t ρ_r{\\rm d}r}$. Within this framework, we establish several innovative results on the weighted $L^p$ solutions of BSDEs: an existence result, an existence and uniqueness result, an existence and uniqueness result of the minimal (maximal) solution, and two comparison theorems. These findings unify and improve some existing results. Some novel ideas are employed to address the challenges posed by general unbounded stochastic coefficients and general weighted spaces."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00420","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:51Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00419","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00419","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00419","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Luna"],"givenName":"Muhammad Faizan","familyName":"Raza","name":"Raza, Muhammad Faizan","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Luna"],"familyName":"Shuo","name":"Shuo","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Organizational","name":"Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satish Mahadevan","familyName":"Srinivasan","name":"Srinivasan, Satish Mahadevan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joanna F.","familyName":"DeFranco","name":"DeFranco, Joanna F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unleashing the Potential of Large Language Models: A Blueprint for Real-Time, Enterprise-Ready Deployments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; H.3.3; C.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:40:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:19:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/mc.2026.3664470","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models deployed in real-time, regulated settings face knowledge staleness, catastrophic forgetting, hallucination, and weak feedback loops. We present a unified, pattern-driven LLMOps architecture integrating real-time data ingestion, continual learning, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), and human-in-the-loop feedback into a single operational pipeline. Four contributions map to established software design patterns: an adaptive ingestion pattern orchestrator (AIPO) evaluated with FreshStreamBench; STAR+FAR continual learning with sparse temporal adapter routing and freshness-aware replay; SAGE, an SLO-aware adaptive retrieval policy predicting a per-query passage budget to meet tail-latency targets; and an automated feedback-driven convergence stage with RLHF triggers. The result reduces latency-cost-accuracy trade-offs while supporting auditability and rollback for high-risk sectors such as health care and finance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 1 figure. Authors' accepted version of an article published in IEEE Computer. The version of record is available at the DOI below"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00419","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:50Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00418","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00418","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00418","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingze","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Mingze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hemanth","familyName":"Saratchandran","name":"Saratchandran, Hemanth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cameron","familyName":"Gordon","name":"Gordon, Cameron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Lucey","name":"Lucey, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mask-Based Priors Are More Persistent than Query-Key Initializations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:39:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:19:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers do not merely lack data on some Boolean extrapolation tasks; they generalize in a systematically wrong way. Recent work on generalization on the unseen has shown that, despite fitting the observed domain, Transformers often extrapolate according to a simpler minimum-degree interpolator rather than the true target function. These Boolean tasks are not practical applications, but controlled stress tests for understanding Transformer inductive bias. We ask whether this failure mode can be corrected by injecting explicit structural priors into attention. Existing structured-initialization methods alter Transformer inductive bias indirectly, by choosing query and key projections whose similarity scores approximate a desired attention pattern. However, we find that when applied to Boolean extrapolation, these QK-based priors can be rapidly overwritten during training and fail to change the learned extrapolation rule. We propose a simpler alternative: initialize the additive attention mask directly. Unlike standard hard masks used for causality or locality attention, our mask is a finite, learnable attention-logit bias initialized from task-level interaction structure. This separates the structural prior from content-dependent attention scores, allowing it to persist throughout optimization. On Boolean reasoning tasks, mask-based initialization achieves near-perfect extrapolation where vanilla and QK-initialized Transformers remain trapped by the default inductive bias. The same mechanism also improves low-data arithmetic performance and remains competitive on vision and language benchmarks. These results show that attention masks can serve not only as architectural constraints, but as a simple substrate for encoding persistent inductive bias in Transformers."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00418","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:48Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00416","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00416","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00416","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chanh","familyName":"Kieu","name":"Kieu, Chanh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A note on the shear-forced dynamics of tornadoes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Atmospheric and Oceanic Physics (physics.ao-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:35:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:19:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This note presents an axisymmetric model of a tornado-like vortex with internal vertical wind shear. By employing a prescribed incompressible circulation-strain flow that captures the horizontal variation of the vertical wind, we show that the model admits an exact viscous Feynman-Kac representation for the tornado structure. In the presence of vortex vertical shear, the tilting term induces radial phase mixing that rapidly deforms the vortex structure, thus causing the radius of the maximum azimuthal wind to broaden and shift upward. At the long-time limit, the vortex approaches the Burgers radial scale while both the wind and vorticity decay exponentially. This class of solutions may help explain why tornadoes often weaken rapidly once an internal vertical shear structure emerges after touchdown."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00416","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:46Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00415","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00415","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00415","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangjing","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Liangjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beilei","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Beilei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changjing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Changjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongliang","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Hongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Boosting Generalizable Depth Estimation in Endoscopy by Mixture of Lightweight Experts and Intrinsic Image Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:35:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:19:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Depth estimation is a significant task for 3D perception in endoscopic surgeries. However, illumination interference and feature diversity in various endoscopic scenes are still challenges for generalizable depth estimation and ego-motion estimation. Based on this, a novel self-supervised framework, EndoMINI, is proposed for depth estimation in endoscopic scenes. Specifically, mixture of low-rank experts (MiLoRE) is proposed to perform parameter-efficient fine-tuning, which can also boost the model adaptation to scenes with different characteristics. Meanwhile, an intrinsic image alignment (IIA) is introduced into the training loss to alleviate the influence of light reflectance in endoscopy with a novel intrinsic image decomposition network. The proposed method is evaluated on SCARED datasets for supervised depth estimation, and two endoscopic datasets, Hamlyn and SERV-CT, for zero-shot depth estimation, compared with state-of-the-art works as well. The experimental results demonstrate outstanding performance of the proposed model and the effects of the main contributions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by MICCAI 2026 @ The Efficient Medical AI (EMA4MICCAI) Workshop (Oral Presentation)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00415","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:44Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00414","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00414","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00414","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingquan","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Jingquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lvzhou","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lvzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sparse Quantum State Preparation with Sublinear T-Count"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:33:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:19:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the fault-tolerant cost of preparing sparse quantum states, measured by $T$-count in the Clifford+$T$ model. Here an $n$-qubit state is called $s$-sparse if it is supported on at most $s$ computational-basis states. For arbitrary $n$-qubit states, the optimal $T$-count is $Θ(\\sqrt{2^n\\log(1/ε)}+\\log(1/ε))$, but for $s$-sparse states the best previous upper bounds remained linear in $s$. We show that any $n$-qubit $s$-sparse state can be prepared up to error $ε$ using $\\widetilde{O}(\\min\\{s,\\ n^{3/4}\\sqrt{s}\\}+\\sqrt{s\\log(1/ε)}+\\log(1/ε))$ $T$ gates, giving the first sublinear dependence on $s$ once the support is sufficiently large. Our approach is based on a support-aware synthesis theorem for sparse Boolean functions, which may be of independent interest. We also prove that, for every $0\u0026lt;ε\\le 1/6$ and $2\\le s\\le 2^{n/2}$, sparse-state preparation requires $Ω(\\min\\{s,\\sqrt{ns}\\})$ $T$ gates, showing that linear dependence on $s$ is unavoidable in the small-support regime and substantially narrowing the gap between the known upper and lower bounds within this parameter range."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00414","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:43Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00413","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00413","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00413","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qunqiang","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Qunqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taozheng","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Taozheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Pan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Van Calster","name":"Van Calster, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jialiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Variance Estimation for the Polytomous Discrimination Index"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation (stat.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:22:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:18:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluating diagnostic accuracy for multi-category outcomes remains a significant challenge, primarily due to computational limitations in existing performance metrics. The Polytomous Discrimination Index (PDI) has emerged as an order-agnostic solution suitable for nominal classifications. However, its broader adoption has been constrained by the lack of efficient implementation, especially for its variance estimation, which typically would require computationally intensive bootstrapping procedures. In this work, we address this limitation by proposing a novel asymptotic variance estimator for the PDI. Our method integrates classical $U$-statistic theory with recent advances in combinatorics, offering a scalable and theoretically grounded alternative. To assess the performance of the proposed approach, we conduct extensive simulation studies and observe remarkable gain in computing time. We further apply our method to a real-world brain image analysis where deep neural networks are used as a diagnostic tool. We can efficiently report the accuracy of the neural networks with different depth specifications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00413","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:42Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00412","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00412","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00412","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanqing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lizhou","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Lizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiejun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tiejun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanzhi","familyName":"Xun","name":"Xun, Hanzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianmin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jixuan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuchao","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Xuchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"C2P-Cache: Scalable GPU L1 Cache Sharing via Concurrent Candidate Pruning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:21:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:18:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern GPUs rely on private per-SM L1 caches and a shared L2 cache, but this organization obscures cross-SM reuse: an L1 miss is typically forwarded to L2 even when the requested line already resides in a peer L1 cache, leading to redundant L2 access. Prior GPU L1-sharing designs attempt to recover such reuse through exact or broad remote-hit searches, which become increasingly difficult to scale and can interfere with the critical L1 miss path under high concurrency. %miss handling as more caches participate and more misses arrive concurrently. We observe that eliminating redundant L2 accesses does not require exact, chip-wide knowledge of private L1 contents. Instead, it requires only sufficient visibility to sharply narrow down a small set of candidate caches, leaving exact confirmation to a much smaller number of L1s.\n Based on this insight, we propose C2P-Cache, a scalable GPU L1-sharing mechanism that transforms remote-hit discovery from a chip-wide exact search problem into a lightweight filtering-and-confirmation process. C2P-Cache maintains compact Bloom-filter-based snapshots of private L1 tags, performs parallel chip-wide candidate filtering, and selectively probes only a small number of likely peer caches. To sustain high concurrency, C2P-Cache organizes filtering as bit-sliced matching over a banked and replicated snapshot matrix, enabling efficient, parallel processing of many concurrent misses without interfering with normal L1 accesses. Across a wide range of GPU workloads, C2P-Cache improves instructions per cycle (IPC) by up to 49.7\\% and by 23.5\\% on average for applications with high remote-L1 reuse and strong sensitivity to L2 latency, demonstrating that lightweight, scalable filtering can effectively unlock cross-SM reuse with modest overhead."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the 59th IEEE/ACM International Symposium on Microarchitecture (MICRO 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00412","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:40Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00411","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00411","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00411","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijun","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Lijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ya Yan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Ya Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Robustness of Propagating Bound States in the Continuum"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:19:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:18:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Bound states in the continuum (BICs) are localized eigenmodes with their frequencies in the radiation continuum of scattering states. The existence of a BIC implies the loss of uniqueness for scattering problems with given incident waves. Perturbed wave systems close to the ideal ones with a BIC exhibit strong resonance effects that are essential to numerous practical applications. A question of fundamental importance is whether a BIC is robust, i.e., whether it can continue its existence when the structure is slightly perturbed. In an earlier work [Yuan and Lu, Optics Letters, Vol.~42, pp.~4490-4493, 2017], for a class of BICs governed by the two-dimensional (2D) Helmholtz equation, which are not trivially protected by symmetry, we uncovered the conditions that ensure robustness and formally constructed the BIC in perturbed systems using a perturbation method. In this paper, we present a rigorous theory on the robustness of BICs in 2D dielectric structures with a single periodic direction. Specifically, we analyze the solvability and provide estimates for each order in the perturbation series, and prove the convergence of the series."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00411","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:39Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00409","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00409","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00409","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aohan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Aohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryota","familyName":"Ariyoshi","name":"Ariyoshi, Ryota","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikio","familyName":"Hasegawa","name":"Hasegawa, Mikio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miao","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Miao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomoaki","familyName":"Ohtsuki","name":"Ohtsuki, Tomoaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhu","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Zhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Design and Implementation of Schwarz Information Criterion-Aided Intelligent Decentralized Resource Allocation in Dynamic LoRa Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:12:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:18:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper proposes a lightweight distributed learning method for selecting transmission parameters in Long-Range (LoRa) networks that adapts to dynamically changing communication environments. In the proposed method, the Thompson Sampling (TS) is adopted for transmission parameter selection, whereas the Schwarz Information Criterion (SIC) is employed for environmental change detection. TS is a reinforcement learning approach that effectively balances exploration and exploitation by updating parameters based on probability distributions. Additionally, it demonstrates stable performance even with a small number of trials, thereby making it well-suited for LoRa end devices (EDs) with limited memory capacity and computational resources. Furthermore, to address the issue that TS-based methods strongly depend on past learning histories and therefore adapt slowly to abrupt changes in communication environments, a statistical change detection mechanism based on the SIC is integrated into our proposed method. SIC is adopted because it can detect environmental changes with low computational cost and is suitable for implementation on resource-constrained LoRa EDs. When a change in the communication environment is detected by SIC, the learning history of TS is reset, thereby enabling rapid re-learning under new environmental conditions. Moreover, to achieve fully distributed communication parameter selection while enhancing transmission reliability and energy efficiency, the proposed method relies solely on Acknowledgment (ACK) feedback and the selected transmission parameters. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed method improves the transmission success rate from 64.0% to 71.1% and increases energy efficiency from 293.9 bit/J to 328.3 bit/J compared with the conventional Upper Confidence Bound (UCB)1-tuned scheme under high-density dynamic LoRa networks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00409","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:36Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00408","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00408","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00408","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyong","familyName":"Xi","name":"Xi, Xiaoyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chong","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongsheng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Dongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The complete Boolean algebra of regular open sets in real line is not sober"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Topology (math.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary 06B35, Secondary 54A05, 54D99","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:11:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:18:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Whether the Scott space of the complete Boolean algebra $\\RO(\\bbR)$ of regular open subsets in the real line is sober was explicitly recorded as an open problem by Xu and Zhao and was reiterated in a later survey. We give a negative answer to this problem in this paper."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00408","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:35Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00405","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00405","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00405","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoming","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Xiaoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengkun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chengkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoang","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Haoang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liyang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Liyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gene Ontology-Guided Hierarchical Spatial Gene Expression Prediction from Histopathology Images"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Genomics (q-bio.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T03:01:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:17:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Predicting spatial gene expression from histopathology images enables large-scale transcriptomic profiling without the cost of direct measurement. Existing methods decode the target gene set as a flat, unstructured vector, ignoring the inter-gene dependencies arising from shared biological pathways and regulatory programs. Without explicit structural guidance, models must infer these dependencies entirely from limited paired data, constraining prediction quality. We propose MSGR (Multi-Scale Gene Refiner), which bridges this gap by incorporating the Gene Ontology (GO), a curated functional hierarchy of genes, as an explicit structural prior. MSGR organizes target genes into a four-level GO tree. Its GO-guided decoder then progressively refines predictions from coarse functional domains to fine individual genes via residual corrections under scale-weighted supervision. Operating solely on the gene side, the GO-guided decoder serves as a seamless plug-in replacement that consistently improves existing architectures without requiring any image-side modifications. Extensive experiments on nine datasets from the HEST-1k benchmark provide empirical evidence for two central claims: GO-structured decoding consistently outperforms flat decoding, even against a state-of-the-art generative baseline, and the gain is attributable to biological ontology structure rather than hierarchical decomposition per se, as confirmed by a +0.027 margin over a structurally equivalent random hierarchy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACM MM 2026. Code: https://github.com/NozomiMizore/MSGR"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00405","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:30Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00404","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00404","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00404","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daeyoung","familyName":"Ham","name":"Ham, Daeyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sihan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Sihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Debiased inference for proximal dose-response function"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T02:58:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:17:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we study nonparametric inference for the causal dose-response curve of a continuous-treatment under unmeasured confounding by leveraging treatment- and outcome-inducing confounding proxies. To estimate the curve, we introduce a novel proximal doubly robust pseudo-outcome whose conditional mean given treatment equals the dose-response curve whenever either bridge function is correctly specified, thereby addressing a key gap in proximal causal inference for continuous-treatments. Furthermore, we derive an influence function for its smoothed causal estimand, and construct a cross-fitted debiased local-linear estimator with a proper local-quadratic bias correction. We establish pointwise and finite-dimensional asymptotic normality and a uniform Gaussian approximation over compact treatment intervals. Both smoothing bandwidths may have the mean-squared-error-optimal order without undersmoothing, while cross-fitting accommodates flexible bridge estimators under a product convergence rate conditions without fitted-class entropy restrictions. We also develop practical bandwidth selectors, pointwise confidence intervals, and simultaneous confidence bands. Extensive simulations and a data analysis highlight the practical performance of the proposed method under latent confounding and multiple proxies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00404","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:29Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00403","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00403","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00403","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdelrahman","familyName":"Zaky","name":"Zaky, Abdelrahman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiare","familyName":"Feuchtner","name":"Feuchtner, Tiare","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Visualizing Placement Proposals for Window Arrangement in Mixed Reality: A Comparative User Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T02:55:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:17:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Adaptive mixed reality (MR) interfaces typically optimize window layouts on behalf of the user, with limited consideration for individual preferences. A promising alternative keeps users in the loop by presenting layout proposals for them to select from, but how these proposals should be visualized remains underexplored. We compare three proposal-visualization techniques for window placement, Situated Icon Preview, Situated Window Preview, and 3D Preview, against a Manual Positioning baseline. The techniques differ in level of detail and degree of interaction-space context. In a within-subjects user study, 24 participants completed a multi-stage trip-planning task in VR, individually placing seven sequentially introduced windows using each technique. We thereby focus on single-window placement under predefined proposal positions. We measured layouting time, number of layout changes, task load, user experience, and preference, complemented by semi-structured interviews. Although Situated Icon Preview and Situated Window Preview reduced layouting time compared to Manual Positioning - with Situated Window Preview also faster than 3D Preview - participants preferred direct manual control. We discuss the factors shaping this preference (perceived control, cognitive cost, familiarity, and informativeness of the proposal) and outline implications for hybrid approaches, as a promising combination of proposal-based suggestions with manual refinement."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00403","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:28Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00402","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00402","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00402","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanming","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Yanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingkun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lingkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruipeng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Ruipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyue","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xinyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DSETA: A Dual-Stage Continual Learning Framework for Travel Time Prediction in Dynamic Traffic Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T02:55:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:17:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Estimated Time of Arrival (ETA) prediction is a core component of intelligent transportation systems. As traffic congestion patterns become increasingly dynamic in large cities, maintaining high prediction accuracy poses a major challenge for ride-hailing platforms. Existing methods either fail to adapt to irregular traffic patterns and sudden congestion, or suffer from new distributions without disentangling long-term trends from short-term fluctuations, thereby degrading model performance in real-world scenarios. To address this challenge, we propose DSETA, an incrementally updated Dual-Stage ETA prediction framework. Specifically, the continual learning process is divided into \\textit{inter-day} and \\textit{intra-day} stages. We first design the \\textit{intra-day} learning stage, which relies entirely on real-time data to enable dynamic adaptation to short-term traffic patterns caused by events like holidays or accidents. Next, we develop the \\textit{inter-day} learning stage, which leverages aggregated historical data from a short time window to capture knowledge of long-term distribution shifts, such as seasonal trends and traffic network evolution. Subsequently, to prevent catastrophic forgetting and preserve knowledge of regular patterns, we explore a \\textit{Historical Traffic Knowledge Consolidation} module. Finally, we validate DSETA's effectiveness and robustness through extensive offline and online experiments conducted on real-world datasets from DiDi's platform. Online A/B tests across three major cities including Beijing, Wuhan, and Xi'an consistently demonstrated performance gains, achieving MAE reductions of 6.62\\%, 0.73\\%, and 2.40\\% respectively. This framework has been successfully deployed in DiDi's production environment, processing hundreds of millions of daily requests and validating its strong performance in industrial applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00402","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:26Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00401","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00401","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00401","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanbaihui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yanbaihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ravi","familyName":"Prakash","name":"Prakash, Ravi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li-Yu","familyName":"Lo","name":"Lo, Li-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nils","familyName":"Roede","name":"Roede, Nils","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Boyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Embodied Passive Aeroacoustic Perception Enables Relative Sensing and Pursuit Between Aerial Robots"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T02:52:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:17:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Aerial robots generate structured aeroacoustic fields during flight, yet these signals have been underexplored as a source of onboard relative perception, particularly under the strong ego-acoustic interference generated during simultaneous flight in various outdoor conditions. We introduce embodied passive aeroacoustic perception, a sensing paradigm in which an aerial robot infers actionable relative-state information from the naturally generated sound of flight while operating within its own evolving aeroacoustic field. We present SonicFly, a passive aeroacoustic perception framework that enables one unmanned aerial vehicle to estimate and follow another using only the leader's intrinsic flight sound, without active acoustic signaling, inter-robot communication, GPS sharing, or external sensing infrastructure. The system uses a lightweight four-microphone array, rotorcraft-informed acoustic representations, a neural bearing-range estimator, and confidence-gated filtering for closed-loop flight. Through acoustic characterization, onboard localization, and outdoor pursuit experiments, we show that multirotor aeroacoustic signals contain sufficient information to support relative perception despite strong ego-acoustic interference, environmental variability, and changing flight geometry. During acoustic-only pursuit, SonicFly achieved a mean distance-maintenance error of 1.34 m across diverse outdoor trajectories and operating conditions. Analysis of the acoustic channel reveals design principles governing embodied passive aeroacoustic perception, including the roles of harmonic structure, spectral separability, and spatial acoustic cues in determining observability. Our results establish the feasibility of embodied passive aeroacoustic perception for aerial robots and suggest that naturally generated behavioral signals can serve as information for robotic perception and coordination."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Our project website is at: http://generalroboticslab.com/SonicFly"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00401","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:25Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00400","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00400","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00400","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongshu","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhongshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongbin","familyName":"Xiu","name":"Xiu, Dongbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modeling Unknown Nonlocal PDE Systems via Flow Map Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Dynamical Systems (math.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T02:46:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:17:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Nonlocal partial differential equations arise in many applications but are often difficult to model and learn because of the presence of nonlocal operators. We present a flow-map learning (FML) framework for modeling unknown nonlocal PDEs directly from solution data. Rather than learning or approximating the underlying nonlocal operators, the proposed approach learns the finite-time evolution operator in either modal or nodal space. Two complementary formulations are developed for spectral and grid-based solution representations. Numerical experiments on one- and two-dimensional fractional diffusion and wave equations demonstrate accurate and stable long-time prediction using only short observation windows. The proposed approach provides an effective data-driven framework for learning unknown nonlocal dynamics without explicit evaluation of nonlocal operators."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00400","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:23Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00399","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00399","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00399","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omer","familyName":"Angel","name":"Angel, Omer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Hermon","name":"Hermon, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuliang","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yuliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Heavy-tailed Frog Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"60K35 (Primary) 60G50, 60J10, 05C81 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T02:42:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:17:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the frog model on $\\mathbb Z^d$ and on the discrete tori $\\mathbb T_L^d$, $d\\ge 2$, with a symmetric, translation-invariant, and heavy-tailed transition kernel satisfying \\[ Q(x,y)\\asymp |x-y|^{-(d+α)}, \\qquad α\u0026gt;0. \\] Starting from an i.i.d. Poisson$(λ)$ number of sleeping particles per site and one active particle at the origin. Active particles perform independent $Q$-random walks and activate the particles they encounter.\n We first determine the timescale for activating distant vertices. When $α\\in(0,d)$, the time required to activate all vertices within distance $L$ of the origin is, with high probability, \\[ (\\log L)^{Δ+o(1)}, \\qquad Δ^{-1}:=\\log_2\\left(\\frac{2d}{d+α}\\right), \\] as $L\\to\\infty$. This polylogarithmic spreading contrasts sharply with the linear spreading of the classical frog model driven by simple random walks; see Alves, Machado, and Popov (2002) and Ramírez and Sidoravicius (2004). When $α\u0026gt;d$, we recover this classical linear behavior by proving matching linear upper and lower bounds; at $α=d$, we prove a linear upper bound.\n Finally, we consider the finite-lifespan model on $\\mathbb T_L^d$, in which each particle is removed after taking $\\ell$ steps. We show that the cover lifespan, defined as the smallest $\\ell$ for which the torus is entirely activated, is asymptotic to the cover time of a Poisson$(λL^d)$ cloud of independent stationary random walkers."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00399","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:22Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00398","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00398","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00398","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matheus G. C.","familyName":"Cunha","name":"Cunha, Matheus G. C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Douglas D.","familyName":"Novaes","name":"Novaes, Douglas D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel","familyName":"Ponce","name":"Ponce, Gabriel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Infinite-Piecewise Expanding Maps: Chaos, Ergodicity and Invariant-Set Complexity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Dynamical Systems (math.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"37E99, 37A99, 26A18, 28A78, 28A80, 34A36, 37C29","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T02:36:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:17:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we study a class of one-dimensional piecewise maps defined by infinitely many smooth expanding branches. This class arises naturally in the context of non-smooth dynamical systems and includes the first-return maps locally defined near sliding Shilnikov connections. By means of the theory of conformal iterated function systems (CIFS), we investigate several dynamical properties of these maps as well as the topological complexity of their invariant sets. In particular, we show that the dynamics restricted to the invariant set is topologically conjugate to the shift on $\\mathbb{N}^{\\mathbb{N}}$. We also establish the existence of a unique conformal measure that is invariant and ergodic under the map."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Version 01"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00398","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:21Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00397","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00397","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00397","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianbo","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Jianbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carmela","familyName":"Scalone","name":"Scalone, Carmela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structure-Preserving Dynamical Low-Rank Approximations for Stochastic Vlasov--Poisson Equations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T02:30:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:17:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose structure-preserving dynamical low-rank methods for stochastic Vlasov--Poisson equations with transport noise. We first derive the continuous low-rank evolution equations and show that, by including fixed velocity modes associated with the conserved quantities, the low-rank dynamics inherits the mass, momentum, and energy balance laws of the original stochastic model. We then develop two augmented basis-update Galerkin (BUG) integrators based on two stochastic discretizations: an Euler--Maruyama scheme applied to the equivalent Itô formulation and a Heun scheme applied directly to the Stratonovich formulation. These two choices allow us to study how the stochastic time discretization interacts with the conservative low-rank framework. In both cases, basis augmentation and conservative rank truncation retain the relevant moment spaces and provide robustness under rank reduction. Numerical experiments demonstrate the conservation properties of the proposed methods and compare the augmentation requirements, stochastic correction terms, and momentum and energy behavior induced by the two stochastic formulations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00397","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:19Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00396","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00396","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00396","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Tafese","name":"Tafese, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Milad","familyName":"Hooshyar","name":"Hooshyar, Milad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sam","familyName":"Bayless","name":"Bayless, Sam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nick","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Nick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arie","familyName":"Gurfinkel","name":"Gurfinkel, Arie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Verifiable Checks for Business Rule Consistency"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T02:22:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:17:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Maintaining consistency between natural language documentation of business rules and their evolving internal implementations is a significant challenge in large-scale systems. We present SIRNA, a tool and framework for checking such consistency using SMT solvers. Using the case study of cost calculations in tax domains, we demonstrate a three-part system that combines large language models (LLMs) with formal verification methods. SIRNA translates natural language documentation into candidate SMT formulas using LLMs, followed by checks to validate the translations. Then, corresponding business rules are converted into equivalent SMT representations and validated against the natural language formalizations. Our method is generalizable to domains where business logic exists in both natural language documentation and programmatic implementation. Compared to baseline evaluations, SIRNA significantly reduces the number of false positives and false negatives while offering explainability for its findings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Formal Methods in Computer-Aided Design 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00396","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:18Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00395","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00395","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00395","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On bricks of one-point extension algebras"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Representation Theory (math.RT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T02:21:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:17:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $Λ=B[E]$ be the one-point extension algebra of $B$ by the extension module $E$. Using the standard triple description of $Λ$-modules, we characterize mixed bricks through an injectivity condition and a scalar-stabilizer condition. Under the assumption that $B$ is $E$-visible finite, we interpret mixed bricks as orbits in Grassmannians and derive a necessary and sufficient criterion for the brick-finiteness of $Λ$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"38 pages. Comments are welcome"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00395","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:17Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00394","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00394","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00394","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanhao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Xuanhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruichen","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Ruichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhizhen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhizhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingzhe","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Mingzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Channel-Agnostic Semantic Compression for Bandwidth-Limited Visual Communication"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T02:18:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:17:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Bandwidth-limited visual communication systems require efficient transmission of high-dimensional data under dynamic wireless conditions. Existing approaches either rely on joint source-channel coding, which tightly couples representation learning with channel models and lacks flexibility across varying environments, or adopt generative reconstruction techniques that may introduce semantically inconsistent outputs. In this paper, we propose RQ-NAC, a channel-agnostic semantic compression framework for visual communication. The proposed method leverages residual quantization to produce scalable discrete semantic representations, enabling fine-grained and predictable control over the rate-distortion tradeoff. To further enhance compression efficiency, we integrate an n-gram-driven arithmetic coding module that exploits contextual dependencies among latent indices for lossless entropy coding. Extensive experiments demonstrate that RQ-NAC achieves over 600$\\times$ compression relative to uncompressed visual data while preserving high perceptual quality. The results show that our approach enables efficient, flexible, and reliable semantic transmission under bandwidth-constrained conditions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by IEEE GLOBECOM 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00394","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:15Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00393","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00393","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00393","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingyang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Dingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanxiang","familyName":"Chao","name":"Chao, Hanxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olexandr","familyName":"Isayev","name":"Isayev, Olexandr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huamin","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Huamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanna","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MolecularCanvas: LLM-assisted Small-Molecule Drug Discovery via Structure-Guided Constraints"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T02:08:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:16:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Small-molecule drug discovery relies on iterative molecular optimization, where chemists repeatedly modify candidate compounds to balance multiple competing properties such as efficacy, toxicity, and solubility. Recent advances in generative AI (GenAI) have shown promise in accelerating this process by automatically proposing new molecular structures or targeted modifications. However, existing GenAI-based molecular design tools remain poorly aligned with experts' real-world workflows. Specifically, they offer limited support for specifying structure-level modification intents on molecules, provide insufficient transparency into model-generated modifications, and lack integrated support for downstream property evaluation with external computational tools. To address these challenges, we introduce MolecularCanvas, an interactive system that enables users to iteratively construct an optimization context by integrating high-level goals, structure-level annotations, property constraints, and reference-based preferences. This context guides the generation of candidate molecules across diverse molecular structures. MolecularCanvas further enhances transparency by providing evidence for AI-generated suggestions and streamlines molecular evaluation by integrating commonly used computational tools for property assessment into a unified interface. Finally, a user study with 12 participants demonstrates the usefulness and effectiveness of MolecularCanvas in helping users optimize candidate molecules."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00393","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:14Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00392","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00392","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00392","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vrej","familyName":"Zarikian","name":"Zarikian, Vrej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unique Pseudo-Expectations for Dynamical $C^*$-Inclusions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Operator Algebras (math.OA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"46L07, 46L55 (Primary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T02:02:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:16:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $(\\mathcal{A},G,α)$ be a $C^*$-dynamical system. Unique extension properties for the $C^*$-inclusion $\\mathcal{A} \\subseteq \\mathcal{A} \\rtimes_{α,r} G$ have been studied extensively. In this paper, we investigate unique extension properties for some other natural ``dynamical'' $C^*$-inclusions, namely:\n $\\bullet$ $C_r^*(G) \\subseteq \\mathcal{A} \\rtimes_{α,r} G$;\n $\\bullet$ $\\mathcal{A}^G \\subseteq \\mathcal{A}$, where $\\mathcal{A}^G$ is the fixed-point subalgebra;\n $\\bullet$ $Z(\\mathcal{A})^c \\subseteq \\mathcal{A} \\rtimes_{α,r} G$, where $Z(\\mathcal{A})^c = Z(\\mathcal{A})' \\cap (\\mathcal{A} \\rtimes_{α,r} G)$ is the relative commutant of the center.\n Our hope is that a larger selection of examples will enable progress on some lingering open problems, in particular (1) the relationship between aperiodicity and the unique pseudo-expectation property and (2) the relationship between the faithful unique pseudo-expectation property and norming."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages, comments welcome"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00392","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:12Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00391","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00391","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00391","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuaijun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shuaijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunlin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kunlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ningxing","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Ningxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongsheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Gate, Not the Cache: Gate Provenance Bounds the Closed-Loop Reliability of Training-Free VLA Token Skipping"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T01:59:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:16:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Token skipping is a widely used training-free way to accelerate vision--language--action (VLA) models by bypassing computation for most visual tokens at each control step according to a gate. When the next gate is harvested from the previous accelerated forward, however, the tokens skipped at one step are also the ones least visible to the next gate, and the damage can compound across control steps until the task fails. We study the two mechanisms this class is built on, reuse and deletion, crossing each against where its gate signal comes from on identical episodes. At a skip ratio of 0.9 on LIBERO-Object, both collapse when the gate comes from the model's own accelerated forwards, to 0.68 under reuse and to 0.31 under deletion against a dense 1.00, and the collapse is invisible to the action-level detectors we evaluate. What separates collapse from dense-level operation is not the mechanism but whether the gate is clean, computed by a forward that skipped nothing. We therefore propose actuation-slack refresh, one dense pass run while the robot executes its current action chunk, off the critical path, that hands the next step a clean gate and a fresh KV base. Since the measured detectors do not reliably reveal the failure, the refresh is unconditional rather than triggered. Both mechanisms then recover to 0.98, keeping the speed of skipping and the information of a dense pass. We then integrate the refresh into state-of-the-art caching and pruning methods across two VLA policies, 4 LIBERO suites, and 4 SIMPLER tasks, where it repairs every collapse caused by using a self-harvested gate. Serve latency drops 18--22\\% below dense, measured both in simulation and on a physical robot. Where the gate signal comes from, not how tokens are skipped, decides closed-loop reliability for accelerated VLAs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00391","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:11Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00390","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00390","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00390","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jincheng","familyName":"An","name":"An, Jincheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ajit C.","familyName":"Balram","name":"Balram, Ajit C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ganpathy","familyName":"Murthy","name":"Murthy, Ganpathy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Skyrmion Excitations in the $ν=-1$ Quantum Hall state in Monolayer Graphene"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T01:58:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:16:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate the skyrmion excited states atop the $SU(4)$ quantum Hall ferromagnetic ground state at filling factor $ν=-1$ in monolayer graphene. The competition among short-range anisotropic interactions, the Zeeman coupling, and sublattice symmetry-breaking potential gives rise to four distinct spin-valley-ordered ground-state phases. Combining effective field theory with the Hartree--Fock approximation, we develop a variational framework that incorporates both the long-range Coulomb interaction and the symmetry-breaking terms on an equal footing. Variational minimization determines the optimal skyrmion texture, including both its spatial radial profile and internal $SU(4)$ spinor structure. We establish the phase diagram of skyrmion excitations, identify thirteen distinct skyrmion phases under different external-field conditions, and determine their spin-valley textures, energies, and characteristic sizes. The robustness of the results against different radial ansätze demonstrates the reliability of our variational approach. Our work provides a systematic framework for studying skyrmion excitations in multicomponent quantum Hall ferromagnets and can be naturally extended to more general $SU(4)$ quantum Hall systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00390","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:10Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00389","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00389","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00389","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Ya","name":"Ya, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Na","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Na","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rong","familyName":"An","name":"An, Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Constraining tensor force terms with the charge radii difference of mirror-pair nuclei"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Nuclear Theory (nucl-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T01:58:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:16:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/c983-bg79","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Charge radii differences of mirror partner nuclei provide an alternative probe to pin down the interaction components in asymmetric nuclear matter. In this work, the differences in the charge radii of almost spherical mirror-paired nuclei $^{54}$Ni-$^{54}$Fe and $^{36}$Ca-$^{36}$S are used to constrain the magnitude of tensor terms in the Skyrme interactions. The calculated results suggest that a linear correlation can be found between the difference of charge radii of mirror partner nuclei and the adopted strengths of the triplet-odd and triplet-even tensor components. Besides, it suggests that charge radii differences of mirror-paired nuclei are more sensitive to the adopted strengths of the triplet-odd parameter $U$ rather than the triplet-even parameter $T$. Combining the quantitative constraint strengths of the triplet-even tensor part obtained from the magnetic dipole (M1) excitations, the charge-exchange Gamow-Teller (GT) states, and the spin-dipole (SD) excitations, the triplet-odd strengths are further constrained for the SLy5 as well as SGII effective interactions. This provides an alternative approach to constrain the appropriate magnitude of tensor force."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 5 figures. To appear in Physical Review C"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00389","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:08Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00388","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00388","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00388","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robin","familyName":"Msiska","name":"Msiska, Robin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cynthia J. O.","familyName":"Reichhardt","name":"Reichhardt, Cynthia J. O.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charles","familyName":"Reichhardt","name":"Reichhardt, Charles","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Fullerton","name":"Fullerton, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avadh","familyName":"Saxena","name":"Saxena, Avadh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Planar Interfaces for Transmission of Chiral Spin Textures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T01:56:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:16:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Lateral magnetic interfaces provide a direct way to test whether skyrmions remain robust when driven across abrupt changes in material parameters and magnetic order. Here we study skyrmion transmission across planar ferromagnet-ferromagnet (FM-FM), antiferromagnet-antiferromagnet (AFM-AFM), ferromagnet-antiferromagnet (FM-AFM), and antiferromagnet-ferromagnet (AFM-FM) interfaces using micromagnetic simulations and analytic reduced-coordinate criteria. The outcomes are organized into phase diagrams according to the morphology formed in the receiving region, distinguishing compact transmission from deformed skyrmions, stripe-domain states, amorphous textures, and relaxation into the background. Same-order FM-FM and AFM-AFM skyrmion transmission is captured by an analytically defined range of the reduced Dzyaloshinskii-Moriya interaction, identifying the wall-softening regime that supports compact transmission without stripe formation. Mixed-order FM-AFM and AFM-FM interfaces are directionally distinct, requiring conversion between ferromagnetic magnetization and antiferromagnetic Néel textures. These results show that planar interfaces act as active transport elements and provide reduced design criteria for heterogeneous skyrmion tracks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 5 figures, research article"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00388","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:07Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00387","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00387","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00387","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuwu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xiuwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Asymptotic independence of class-group 4-ranks in correlated pairs of imaginary quadratic fields"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"11R29, 11R11, 15B52, 60B20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T01:55:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:16:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fix a squarefree integer $d_0\u0026gt;1$, and let $d$ range over the positive squarefree integers coprime to $2d_0$. Although $\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{-d})$ and $\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{-d_0d})$ share all variable ramified primes, we prove that their class-group $4$-ranks are asymptotically independent. Over the subfamily $d\\le X$, their joint distribution converges in total variation to the product of two copies of the Cohen--Lenstra--Gerth distribution, with error bounded by a negative power of $\\log\\log X$. We further conjecture that the corrected $2$-primary groups $2\\operatorname{Cl}_{\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{-d})}[2^\\infty]$ and $2\\operatorname{Cl}_{\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{-d_0d})}[2^\\infty]$ are asymptotically independent, each with the Cohen--Lenstra distribution.\n Suppose in addition that the class number of $\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{d_0})$ is odd. For a density-one subset of this family, we prove that extension of ideals to $K(d)=\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{d_0},\\sqrt{-d})$ induces $4\\operatorname{Cl}_{K(d)}[2^\\infty]\\cong 2\\operatorname{Cl}_{\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{-d})}[2^\\infty]\\oplus 2\\operatorname{Cl}_{\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{-d_0d})}[2^\\infty]$. Together with this decomposition, the group-valued conjecture predicts that $4\\operatorname{Cl}_{K(d)}[2^\\infty]$ is distributed as the direct sum of two independent Cohen--Lenstra $2$-groups, giving a corrected Cohen--Lenstra--Martinet distribution for the biquadratic family. Unconditionally, the $8$-rank of $\\operatorname{Cl}_{K(d)}$ has limiting distribution given by the convolution of two copies of the Cohen--Lenstra--Gerth distribution. The proof combines Smith's box method with quantitative truncated Gaussian-binomial moment inversion for diagonally coupled, fixed-width bordered Rédei matrices."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"52 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00387","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:05Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00386","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00386","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00386","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Magee","name":"Magee, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Roig-Sanchis","name":"Roig-Sanchis, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joe","familyName":"Thomas","name":"Thomas, Joe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Near optimal spectral gaps for line bundles on hyperbolic three-manifolds"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Group Theory (math.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Operator Algebras (math.OA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Representation Theory (math.RT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Spectral Theory (math.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"46L54, 22D10, 58J50, 20F36","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T01:55:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:16:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove that there exists a sequence of two-torsion Hermitian line bundles on closed hyperbolic three-manifolds, with volume tending to infinity, and with asymptotically optimal smallest eigenvalue of the Laplacian. The proof stems from recent advances in the program of strongly convergent unitary representations of discrete groups."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00386","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:04Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00385","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00385","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00385","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianbiao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qianbiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengxiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhengxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijun","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Lijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Universal scaling of the Anomalous Hall voltage of magnetic layer with the adjacent conducting layer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T01:54:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:16:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The anomalous Hall voltage of magnetic heterostructures plays a key role as the indicator for the magnetization state and its interplay with a variety of spintronic effects. In this letter, we report the observation, mechanism, and impact of the universal, dramatic scaling of the anomalous Hall voltage of magnetic heterostructures with the thickness and resistivity of the nonmagnetic conducting layers (e.g., normal metal) by combining transport experiments, analytical derivation, and finite-element analyses. We identify that the mechanism is the serial resistor effect of the magnetic and non-magnetic layers within the magnetic heterostructures and irrelevant to any Fermi surface variation or angular momentum injection from the nonmagnetic layer to the magnetic layer. We also show that the accuracy of the harmonic Hall voltage analyses of magnetic heterostructures are unaffected by the under-measuring of the anomalous Hall voltage and other transverse voltages due to the serial resistor effect. These findings provide crucial information for understanding a variety of spintronic phenomena involving the anomalous Hall and other transverse voltages."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00385","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:02Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00384","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00384","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00384","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng-Long","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Cheng-Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin-Yu","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Xin-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shui-Hua","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Shui-Hua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong-Liang","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Hong-Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mauro","familyName":"Antezza","name":"Antezza, Mauro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Anomalous enhancement of thermal radiation transport by quasidisorder"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Applied Physics (physics.app-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T01:52:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:16:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/ftcg-p85b","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The transition from order to disorder is conventionally regarded as detrimental to solid-state heat transfer in classical wave and quasiparticle systems. In striking contrast, we show that in near-field thermal radiation, breaking long-range order-shifting from periodic to quasiperiodic configurations-induces a counterintuitive enhancement of energy transport. This effect arises from delocalized interactions within quasiperiodic elements, where this quasiperiodicity relays and amplifies thermal electromagnetic energy transfer across large spatial separations, surpassing even corresponding near-field scenarios. The extraordinary transport properties induced by quasidisorder effect of near-field thermal radiation could unlock exciting opportunities for heat flow manipulation, offering transformative implications for thermal science and advancing the fundamental understanding of collective excitations in non-ordered systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00384","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:01Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00383","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00383","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00383","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yin Jun","familyName":"Phua","name":"Phua, Yin Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pretrain on Small Synthetic Data, Scale Large for Free: Symmetry-Aware Foundation Model for Logic Rule Induction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T01:49:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:16:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Logical rule induction seeks interpretable rules that transfer across propositional schemas. This requires respecting symmetries: atom naming, example order, polarity flips, and label swap. Enforcing exact symmetry by construction lets one trained inducer scale beyond its training schemas. Our central contribution is a canonical export that decodes a discrete rule from literal scores. It needs no retraining and is exactly equivariant whenever those scores respect the symmetries. We instantiate it on the Neural Rule Inducer, a disjunctive-normal-form (DNF) foundation model that natively respects only example order. We restore the remaining symmetries through architecture, inference, and training. On synthetic stress tests, accuracy on the support labels stays stable at much larger schemas, and rule fidelity on fresh inputs remains above the unmodified model. On real data, accuracy improves most on larger schemas. The exported rule is exact on synthetic full-group tests and on schema-valid real-data tests. This is a mathematical property of the export rather than of a specific model, and we validate it empirically only on the NRI. Enforcing symmetry by construction turns this small-data pretrained model into a reusable, interpretable inducer that transfers to larger schemas."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at 20th Conference on Neurosymbolic Learning and Reasoning (NeSy 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00383","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:38:00Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:38:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:38:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00382","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00382","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00382","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiuan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jiuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoxia","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Zhaoxia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TI-StegoAlign: Channel-Guided Post-Training for Generative Text Steganography under Tokenization Inconsistency"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T01:45:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:16:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative text steganography enables LLM agents to exchange secret information through task-relevant messages. Yet most methods evaluate recovery on sender-side tokens, whereas the receiver observes only surface text. Detokenization and receiver-side retokenization can alter token boundaries, desynchronize coding states, and cause such evaluation to overestimate receiver-side recovery. Existing remedies rely on inference-time filtering or verification, correcting individual outputs without adapting the generation policy to the receiver-side channel. To address these limitations, we propose TI-StegoAlign, a channel-guided post-training framework. The Bit-Consistent Supervised Objective (BCSO) enlarges local coding margins at realized sender-side embedding positions. Channel-Conditioned Preference Optimization (CCPO) then aligns complete stegotexts using receiver-realistic recovery, text quality, and anti-steganalysis feedback. TI-StegoAlign updates only LoRA parameters and requires no tokenization-specific correction during communication. Experimental results show 100% receiver bit accuracy. Compared with the strongest baselines, TI-StegoAlign achieves a 21.6% reduction in normalized perplexity deviation and a 6.3% relative improvement in anti-steganalysis performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00382","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:58Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00381","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00381","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00381","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuxin","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Shuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neil Qiang","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Neil Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reduced Density Matrix Functional Theory Across Molecules and Periodic Systems: Screening and Coupled Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T01:45:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:16:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reduced density matrix functional theory (RDMFT) provides a rigorous framework for treating strong electron correlation, yet its application to periodic systems has long been hindered by two fundamental challenges: the lack of transferable approximate functionals and the poor efficiency of orbital-occupation optimization. Here we show that these two longstanding obstacles can be overcome simultaneously. We develop a periodic implementation of RDMFT based on a coupled optimization framework that enables the efficient simultaneous optimization of natural orbitals and occupation numbers under periodic boundary conditions. Using this implementation, we demonstrate that physically motivated short-range screening transforms the Power functional into a transferable functional applicable to both molecules and periodic solids. Remarkably, short-range screening is found to play a dual role: besides substantially improving energetic accuracy, it fundamentally reshapes the optimization landscape, producing robust optimization step sizes and dramatically accelerating convergence. The coupled optimization framework consistently requires substantially fewer optimization iterations than conventional decoupled optimization for periodic calculations, while the screened $ω$P22 functional outperforms semilocal and hybrid density functionals in predicting representative surface reaction barriers. These results establish a computationally efficient periodic RDMFT framework and identify short-range screening as a promising design principle for developing transferable one-body reduced-density-matrix functionals."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00381","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:57Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00380","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00380","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00380","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Afsoon Alidadi","familyName":"Shamsabadi","name":"Shamsabadi, Afsoon Alidadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Animesh","familyName":"Yadav","name":"Yadav, Animesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Halim","familyName":"Yanikomeroglu","name":"Yanikomeroglu, Halim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GNN-RSMA: An Interference Management Framework for a Large-Scale HAPS Network"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T01:34:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:16:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Integrating non-terrestrial networks (NTN) with terrestrial infrastructure is a key enabler of next-generation wireless systems, providing ubiquitous connectivity while meeting stringent rate and latency requirements. In particular, high altitude platform stations (HAPS) can complement terrestrial networks and jointly form vertical heterogeneous networks (vHetNets), extending coverage while delivering high-capacity, reliable, and low-latency connectivity for user equipments (UEs) including ground users and uncrewed aerial vehicles (UAVs). However, the high altitude deployment of HAPS establishes strong line-of-sight (LoS) links to UEs, creating highly correlated channels among UEs. Moreover, the wide coverage footprint of HAPS enables it to serve a large number of UEs, forcing limited radio resources to be shared among many UEs and resulting in significant intra-resource block (RB) interference. To address this challenge, we propose an interference management scheme based on UE clustering and rate-splitting multiple access (RSMA). Specifically, the network is modeled as a heterogeneous graph, and a graph neural network (GNN) is developed to efficiently allocate the common and private RSMA powers, maximizing the minimum spectral efficiency (SE) in a fast and scalable manner. Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed GNN-RSMA interference management algorithm outperforms conventional multiple access schemes while achieving fairness and worst-user performance comparable to successive convex approximation (SCA)-based optimization at only a fraction of its computational cost."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00380","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:56Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00379","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00379","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00379","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialin","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Jialin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ziyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Hanson","name":"Hanson, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Discovering Explicit Magnetic Core Loss Equations via Learnable Symbolic Sparse Identification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T01:30:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:16:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Explicit magnetic core loss equations with simple expressions and physical interpretability are significant tools in the design of high-frequency power magnetics. Traditional fits to empirical data like the Steinmetz Equation (SE) often struggle with accuracy, whereas modern machine learning approaches improve precision but deviate from physics. To fill this gap, this paper proposes a Learnable Symbolic Sparse Identification (LSSI) framework for data-driven equation discovery. Specifically, LSSI reformulates magnetic core loss equations for sinusoidal drives as a symbolic regression problem derived directly from experimental data. Building upon the SE, an expanded library of candidate functions are introduced and a sparse identification framework is implemented to select the dominant ones. More importantly, crucial parameters like exponents and coefficients of candidate functions are treated as learnable ones, simultaneously achieving equation simplicity and high expressiveness of the underlying fractional power laws. Experimental results demonstrate that LSSI achieves superior accuracy with a state-of-the-art $\\mathbf{R^2}$ of $\\mathbf{0.9999}$ and a MAPE of $\\mathbf{1.04\\%}$ through a highly compact explicit equation containing only $\\mathbf{4}$ active terms. Furthermore, it drastically reduces the parameter count from $\\mathbf{4417}$ in neural network methods to $\\mathbf{15}$, showcasing exceptional compactness and efficiency. The LSSI framework thus provides a physically transparent and highly accurate solution suitable for complex modern magnetic characterization and design."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00379","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:54Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00378","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00378","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00378","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biruk Asmare","familyName":"Muse","name":"Muse, Biruk Asmare","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lionel","familyName":"Briand","name":"Briand, Lionel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keheliya","familyName":"Gallaba","name":"Gallaba, Keheliya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CASPER-Change-Aware Slice Prioritization for Efficient Regression Testing of LLM-based systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T01:21:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:16:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Regression testing for LLM-based systems poses unique challenges because individual regression instances provide limited information about system-level regressions. A failure in a single instance does not necessarily indicate a meaningful regression or provide sufficient information to diagnose affected behaviors. Conversely, detecting regressions based only on overall system performance changes is too coarse-grained, as it does not identify which behaviors are affected. This motivates analyzing regression instances at an intermediate level through test suite slices.\n To address this challenge, we propose CASPER, a change-aware slice prioritization framework for efficient regression testing of prompt-level and model-level changes in LLM-based systems. CASPER first identifies regression slices containing semantically related instances with consistent performance characteristics using an evolutionary slice identification approach. Given a change to an LLM-based application, CASPER prioritizes slices according to their likelihood of regression using behavioral information extracted from execution logs. We instantiate CASPER in the software issue resolution domain and evaluate slice identification against clustering-based baselines and regressed slice prioritization against a random ranking baseline. Results show that CASPER generates more consistent slices while maintaining comparable or better semantic coherence and improves regressed slice prioritization across different LLM-based system changes and testing budgets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00378","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:53Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00375","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00375","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00375","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Chiang","name":"Chiang, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trimming: Decoupling Multiplicative Depth from Modulus Chains in RNS-CKKS via Rational Levels"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T01:11:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:16:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent work on Grafting decouples scale factors from ciphertext moduli, enabling more flexible precision management in RNS-CKKS. However, the multiplicative depth remains fundamentally constrained by the modulus chain structure.\n In this paper, we propose \\emph{Trimming}, a novel fine-grained level management mechanism that decouples multiplicative depth from modulus chains in RNS-CKKS via rational levels. The key idea is to introduce an auxiliary trimming modulus chain composed of smaller NTT-friendly modulus factors, which enables partial modulus transitions instead of directly discarding an entire modulus factor. By replacing conventional discrete level reductions with fine-grained modulus factor refinement, Trimming provides a rational-level abstraction beyond the traditional integer-based modulus chain representation.\n Our approach preserves the compatibility with existing RNS-CKKS arithmetic while enabling more flexible depth management and adaptive modulus transitions. Similar to Grafting, which addresses the precision bottleneck, Trimming targets the depth bottleneck in RNS-CKKS and contributes toward a fully decoupled RNS-CKKS architecture.\n The proposed framework will be further validated through concrete implementation and experimental evaluation to investigate its practical performance and computational overhead in real-world homomorphic encryption applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00375","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:48Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00374","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00374","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00374","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianlin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jianlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nick","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Nick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhou","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tensor Probabilistic Model Checking of Finite-Horizon Markov Chains (Extended Version)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Software (cs.MS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T00:57:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:15:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-3-032-32537-2_24","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We reexamine the problem of verifying Markov chains with respect to step-bounded reachability probabilities. Prevailing approaches rely on encoding the state-transition matrix using either explicit or symbolic representations. While these approaches are effective for sparse transition dynamics, they scale less favorably in the dense regime.\n Our insight is to cast probabilistic model checking of Markov chains as computations over dense tensors. This methodology enables the use of off-the-shelf compiler toolchains for optimized execution of these tensor computations on hardware accelerators. We prove the soundness of the methodology of mapping probabilistic model checking to tensor computations. We implement our approach in a tool called Tessa . Empirical evaluation shows that Tessa unlocks massive speedups over state-of-theart methods on selected benchmarks from the literature."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Extended version of a CAV 2026 paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00374","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:47Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00373","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00373","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00373","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ellen","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Ellen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Chun","name":"Chun, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Connelley","name":"Connelley, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruihan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olivier","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Olivier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony","familyName":"Denault","name":"Denault, Tony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Rayner","name":"Rayner, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Baeten","name":"Baeten, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arjo","familyName":"Bos","name":"Bos, Arjo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matias","familyName":"Kidron","name":"Kidron, Matias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fred","familyName":"Kamphues","name":"Kamphues, Fred","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Kuiper","name":"Kuiper, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wouter","familyName":"Jonker","name":"Jonker, Wouter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alan","familyName":"Ryan","name":"Ryan, Alan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philip","familyName":"Hinz","name":"Hinz, Philip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Facility integration of the NASA IRTF adaptive secondary mirror"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T00:57:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:15:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"IRTF-ASM-1 has been functioning well since its first light in 2024. This adaptive secondary mirror (ASM) was primarily developed to be an on-sky demonstration of the new hybrid variable reluctance actuator technology at the NASA Infrared Telescope Facility (IRTF). However, due to its physical robustness and our previous demonstrations of sensitivity enhancements with the ASM, we are interested in using it to optimize telescope image quality on a nightly basis. This will directly benefit science observations. However, as IRTF does not currently have adaptive optics expertise, we have been developing the system to be used with minimal human intervention. We present our progress in developing software for active optics mode with IRTF-ASM-1 using the single conjugate, facility 2x2 Shack-Hartmann wavefront sensor Felix. We also present techniques for removing low-order, large amplitude non-common path aberrations between Felix and our science instruments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 6 figures. In SPIE Adaptive Optics Systems X"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00373","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:46Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00372","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00372","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00372","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"San Lam","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, San Lam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georgii","familyName":"Grechko","name":"Grechko, Georgii","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladislav","familyName":"Bushmakin","name":"Bushmakin, Vladislav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rainer","familyName":"Stöhr","name":"Stöhr, Rainer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vadim","familyName":"Vorobyov","name":"Vorobyov, Vadim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roman","familyName":"Kolesov","name":"Kolesov, Roman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jörg","familyName":"Wrachtrup","name":"Wrachtrup, Jörg","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Electrically Reconfigurable Silicon Carbide Nanophotonic Cavities on Thin-Film Lithium Niobate"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T00:54:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:15:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Interfacing integrated photonics with solid-state spin defects holds great promise for future quantum networks, but the scaling of spin-photon architectures is hindered by frequency mismatches arising from fabrication-induced variations in photonic cavity resonances and the inhomogeneous optical transition frequencies of individual spins. These challenges call for a photonic platform with deterministic and wide-range tunability. Here, we demonstrate a hybrid nanophotonic platform based on direct bonding of silicon carbide photonic crystal nanocavity arrays onto thin-film lithium niobate on insulator, enabling deterministic electrical tuning of multiple SiC nanocavities into mutual spectral resonance. By exploiting the strong electro-optic response of lithium niobate, we achieve continuous and wide-range cavity tuning of 380 GHz ($\\sim$1.1 nm), sufficient to compensate both cavity disorder and spin inhomogeneity. The nanocavities balance strong optical confinement with electrical tunability, exhibiting a theoretical Purcell factor of approximately 400. This hybrid platform enables electrically reconfigurable spin-photon interfaces for large-scale integrated quantum photonics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00372","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:44Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00370","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00370","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00370","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenxin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhenxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lixin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Continuity of measure-theoretic entropy for stochastic differential equations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Dynamical Systems (math.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"28D20, 37A10, 37A35, 60H10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T00:43:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:15:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For stochastic differential equations, we establish a relationship between the measure-theoretic entropy of the stochastic flow and the rate of volume growth of stable submanifolds under iteration. By combining the result of Kifer and Yomdin (1988), we show that under a suitable integrability condition, for systems with $C^{\\infty}$ coefficients, the measure-theoretic entropy is upper semicontinuous with respect to the coefficients of SDEs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, no figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00370","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:42Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00369","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00369","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00369","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Van","familyName":"Remenar","name":"Remenar, Van","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anatol","familyName":"Gogoj","name":"Gogoj, Anatol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomas","familyName":"Verardi","name":"Verardi, Tomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matei","familyName":"Mandoiu","name":"Mandoiu, Matei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor Jimenez","familyName":"Santiago","name":"Santiago, Victor Jimenez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mihai","familyName":"Duduta","name":"Duduta, Mihai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Robotic System for Automated Manufacturing of Dielectric Elastomer Actuators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T00:40:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:15:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This letter presents an automated robotic manufacturing system for soft capacitors which operate as actuators and sensors. Emphasis is placed on the two processes that most directly govern device quality, dielectric layer formation by spin coating and carbon nanotube (CNT) electrode application by stamping. Twenty multilayer DEAs, each comprising 12 dielectric layers with a mean thickness of 55.37 +- 2.04 um and 11 alternating CNT electrodes, were fabricated reducing total process time by 14.2% and removing the operator from 56.1% of it."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00369","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:40Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00368","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00368","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00368","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuoran","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Zhuoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihang","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohua","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiaohua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agent-Native Task-Oriented Communication with Joint Token Compression Coding and Modulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T00:38:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:15:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large foundation models empower agents to become pervasive across industries and emerge as central actors in intelligent systems, a fundamental rethinking of communication paradigms toward AI-native, agent-centric designs in the post-Shannon era becomes inevitable. One essential shift is that tokens, which are the minimal semantic units natively processed by large language models (LLMs), should replace bits as the fundamental unit of communication. However, existing works in the LLMs field assume lossless token transmission over high-speed wired links and largely neglect the air-interface overhead and channel distortions inherent in wireless environments, lacking a native design for wireless token communication systems. To bridge this gap, we propose an innovative design for a token transmitter-receiver architecture that facilitates task-oriented token transmission. Specifically, we propose JTCM (Joint Token Coding and Modulation), an AI-native semantic communication framework that jointly optimizes token representation, channel coding, and modulation to maximize downstream task performance directly. Correspondingly, we propose a two-stage training scheme. In the first stage, the token encoder-decoder pair is pre-trained to enable semantic-preserving compression and reconstruction. In the second stage, it is fine-tuned end-to-end with a multi-modal foundation model under specific downstream tasks to achieve task-aware optimization. Extensive experiments demonstrate that JTCM significantly reduces transmission overhead while enhancing task accuracy and robustness compared to state-of-the-art baselines in bandwidth- and SNR-constrained wireless channels."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper is accepted by IEEE Globecom 2026, to appear"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00368","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:39Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00367","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00367","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00367","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James M.","familyName":"Hyman","name":"Hyman, James M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sylvester's Theorem and Reduced Linear Systems: From Three-Factor Products to Small Dynamical Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"History and Overview (math.HO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Dynamical Systems (math.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"15A18 (Primary), 15A15, 15A23, 34A30, 65F55, 97H60 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T00:37:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:15:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large systems of linear equations often contain far fewer active degrees of freedom than their ambient dimension suggests. A particularly transparent instance occurs when a large matrix factors as A = TSW, where T is m x n, S is n x n, and W is n x m, and m \u0026gt;\u0026gt; n. The action of A then passes through an n-dimensional intermediate space. Sylvester's theorem relates the nonzero eigenvalues of a product XY to those of the reversed product YX. Applied cyclically, it shows that the nonzero spectrum of the large matrix A is completely determined by either of the small matrices B = (WT)S, C = S(WT). This article develops the theorem from first principles and explains the geometry behind the factorization. The result yields exact reduced systems, both for linear algebraic equations and for first-order linear ordinary differential equations. Worked examples carry this through in detail: spectral reduction, reconstruction of full-space eigenvectors, reduced solution of shifted systems, and exact integration of a three-dimensional ODE through a two-dimensional model. A separate section takes up singular values. Those of A, B, and C do not agree in general, though they do when the bases are orthonormal, a case the same theorem settles once it is applied to A*A. The closing sections separate exact factorization from projection-based approximation, and flag what the theorem leaves undetermined: zero-eigenvalue structure, conditioning, and transient behavior. The material is classical. What this article adds is one continuous development, from first principles through to the limits of the theorem, pitched for an upper-level undergraduate course."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"36 pages, 3 figures, 15 exercises with selected solutions. Lecture notes for an upper-level undergraduate course in numerical analysis"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00367","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:37Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00366","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00366","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00366","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niclas","familyName":"Feldkamp","name":"Feldkamp, Niclas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philippe J.","familyName":"Giabbanelli","name":"Giabbanelli, Philippe J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Istvan","familyName":"David","name":"David, Istvan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Artificial Intelligence and Modeling \u0026amp; Simulation: An Overview"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T00:36:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:15:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Artificial intelligence (AI) and Modeling \u0026amp; Simulation (M\u0026amp;S) are increasingly intertwined, reflecting converging research needs across both communities, rapid technological advances such as the rise of generative AI, and the growing availability of data and computational resources. This report provides a structured overview of the intersections of AI and M\u0026amp;S. The relationship goes both ways: AI can support, augment, or even replace components of simulation studies, while simulations can serve as data generators, training environments, and evaluation platforms for AI. We organize this landscape along the stages of M\u0026amp;S from model specification and input modeling to execution, experimentation, verification and validation, and output analysis. Selected studies at each stage illustrates how techniques such as Large Language Models have reshaped simulation practices, while highlighting limitations and open challenges. This report also provides a conceptual roadmap that helps readers navigate a rapidly changing ecosystem."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00366","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:36Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00363","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00363","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00363","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandro","familyName":"Cabo-Bizet","name":"Cabo-Bizet, Alejandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vasil","familyName":"Dimitrov","name":"Dimitrov, Vasil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dario","familyName":"Martelli","name":"Martelli, Dario","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Ruggeri","name":"Ruggeri, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Projecting Gravitational Fluctuations onto Near-Horizon Throats"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T00:24:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:15:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We show that, for stationary geometries whose linearized fluctuation equations separate into radial and angular Heun equations, a pair of diffeomorphisms projects the full problem onto two coupled, isospectral Gauss hypergeometric problems in complementary spacetime regions. This projection requires Robin boundary conditions to be imposed at the intersection between the complementary regions, which are uniquely fixed by requiring the two diffeomorphisms to match there. The method provides an independent derivation of recent results obtained from the correspondence between Heun connection coefficients and instanton partition functions in the Nekrasov-Shatashvili limit. As an application, we identify a subset of modes in Kerr-de Sitter and Kerr-anti-de Sitter black holes that, in the near-extremal limit, continuously reduce to a basis of the Schwarzian/Jackiw-Teitelboim functional space of gravitational fluctuations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"41 pp"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00363","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:32Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00362","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00362","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00362","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Wensrich","name":"Wensrich, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giles","familyName":"Parkes","name":"Parkes, Giles","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladimir","familyName":"Luzin","name":"Luzin, Vladimir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Daly","name":"Daly, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Floriana","familyName":"Salvemini","name":"Salvemini, Floriana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An essay on $d_0$ in neutron-based strain measurement techniques: equilibrium-based methods for non-destructive $d_0$ estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T00:23:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:15:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Determination of the stress-free reference lattice spacing, $d_0$, is a central and often underestimated difficulty in diffraction-based strain measurement. Although commonly treated as a material constant, $d_0$ often varies with position and measurement direction as a result of composition, phase, texture and residual stress at the sub-bulk scale. While direct measurement of $d_0$ from coupons is usually preferable, this is sometimes impractical or even impossible. This paper reviews the $d_0$ problem in general and develops a hierarchy of non-destructive alternatives based on mechanical equilibrium. This ranges from established techniques based on force-balance, to the application of boundary conditions and finally an approach based on point-wise equilibrium within a sample, implemented through eigenstrain analysis. In all cases, examples are provided in the form of real samples including an additively manufactured Inconel cube, ancient Roman bronze medical probes and an ancient bronze dagger of purported Persian origin. These latter examples demonstrate that equilibrium can provide a practical physical constraint for estimating $d_0$ when destructive reference measurements are entirely inappropriate."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00362","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:31Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00361","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00361","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00361","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simiao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Simiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenneth","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, Kenneth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lynn","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Lynn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Astrid","familyName":"Harth","name":"Harth, Astrid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asami","familyName":"Odate","name":"Odate, Asami","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aggelos","familyName":"Katsaggelos","name":"Katsaggelos, Aggelos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuel Ballester","familyName":"Matito","name":"Matito, Manuel Ballester","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Eastaugh","name":"Eastaugh, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc","familyName":"Walton","name":"Walton, Marc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Artificial Intelligence for the Characterization of Particles and Fibers by Optical Microscopy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T00:18:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:15:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Optical microscopy of particle and fiber dispersions involves interpreting subtle visual cues influenced by specimen morphology, chemical composition, magnification, and illumination conditions. We introduce an artificial intelligence (AI) distillation framework that extracts semantically rich image embeddings from microscopy images using semantic anchors. A multimodal teacher combines each image's visual embedding with three text embeddings representing illumination modality, magnification, and specimen identity and morphology. Generated by LongCLIP's extended-context text encoder, this yields a 2304-dimensional block-structured teacher vector whose component blocks remain physically interpretable throughout training and inference. A student vision transformer (ViT) with a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) decoder is trained to reconstruct this teacher vector from the image alone, minimizing a mean absolute error (L1) loss that enforces coordinate-level fidelity to the teacher's block structure. A cross-entropy term over pseudo-classes derived from HDBSCAN clustering of the teacher embedding space acts as a collapse-prevention regularizer, enforcing inter-cluster separation without requiring contrastive negative mining. At inference, the student operates on image input alone, producing compact embeddings that recover the full semantic content of the teacher vector. The framework achieves approximately 80% pseudo-class validation accuracy and 75% Recall@1 on fine-grained specimen description labels under leave-one-out nearest-neighbor retrieval. These results demonstrate that semantic anchoring enables a vision-only student to acquire richer and more interpretable representations than image-only training, with direct applicability to retrieval, classification, and exploratory analysis of heterogeneous particle and fiber dispersions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00361","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:29Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00360","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00360","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00360","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanner","familyName":"Campbell","name":"Campbell, Tanner","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Battle","name":"Battle, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bill","familyName":"Gray","name":"Gray, Bill","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan A.","familyName":"Sanchez","name":"Sanchez, Juan A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Cantillo","name":"Cantillo, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucille","familyName":"LeCorre","name":"LeCorre, Lucille","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishnu","familyName":"Reddy","name":"Reddy, Vishnu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Physical Characterization of Moon Impactor 2025-010D"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T00:15:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:15:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Renewed interest in lunar exploration is creating a growing population of poorly tracked cislunar objects, some of which will ultimately impact the Moon$^{1}$. The ultimate fate of rocket upper stages or failed mission payloads is often unknown and unplanned. Determining the origin and physical properties of such objects will become increasingly important as sustained lunar exploration places humans and infrastructure on the surface$^{2;3;4;5}$. Here we present a ground-based physical and dynamical characterization of 2025-010D, a Falcon 9 upper stage predicted to impact the lunar farside on 2026 August 05. Backward propagation of its orbit independently links the object to the ``Ghost Riders in the Sky'' launch, while visible and near-infrared spectroscopy distinguishes it from natural objects and reveals absorption bands consistent with spacecraft thermal control materials. Photometric lightcurves confirm its elongated shape and reveal an unexpected change of more than 8 s in its $\\sim$7 min rotation period during observations. We predict an impact between Bell and Einstein craters at $\\sim$2.4 km s$^{-1}$, producing a crater $\\sim$40 m in diameter. Since its provenance is independently known, 2025-010D provides a benchmark for identifying and characterizing future cislunar objects of uncertain origin."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00360","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-04T03:37:28Z","registered":"2026-08-04T03:37:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T03:37:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00358","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.00358","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.00358","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wesley","familyName":"Bland","name":"Bland, Wesley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiago","familyName":"Antunes","name":"Antunes, Tiago","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lars Paul","familyName":"Huse","name":"Huse, Lars Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chidambaram","familyName":"Muthu","name":"Muthu, Chidambaram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adel","familyName":"Abouchaev","name":"Abouchaev, Adel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rabib","familyName":"Alam","name":"Alam, Rabib","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdullah","familyName":"Alperen","name":"Alperen, Abdullah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexey","familyName":"Andronov","name":"Andronov, Alexey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose Anto","familyName":"Akkara","name":"Akkara, Jose Anto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vineet","familyName":